 'Rounding Cell Structure Explanation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly defines rounding cells I_m = [(m⁻+m)/2, (m+m⁺)/2) with midpoint symmetry, while the SxS winner uses notation ψ(s) = fl(s)−s without explaining the cell structure that creates the cancellation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = cells mentioned implicitly; 3-4 = cells defined with notation; 5-6 = full geometric/structural explanation of cells and symmetry', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'First-Order Cancellation Mechanism', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly identifies that the integral of −ξ over a symmetric cell is zero, explaining why round-to-nearest-even achieves O(ρ²) rather than O(ρ), while the SxS winner states ∫ψ=0 without explaining the symmetry mechanism.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = cancellation mentioned; 3-4 = symmetry of integrand noted; 5-6 = explicit demonstration that odd integrand over symmetric interval yields zero', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Density Variation as Error Source', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 clearly identifies that the residual O(ρ²) error arises from density variation (f′ term) across cells, via Taylor expansion f(m+ξ)=f(m)+f′(m)ξ+O(ξ²), while the SxS winner presents the calculation without this conceptual framing.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = Taylor expansion used without interpretation; 3-4 = f′ identified as source; 5-6 = explicit conceptual link between density gradient and residual error', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Summation by Parts Technique', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly mentions 'discrete summation by parts' as the technique for bounding the sum over cells, while the SxS winner performs the summation without naming the method.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = summation performed correctly; 3-4 = technique named; 5-6 = technique named and its role in controlling boundary terms explained', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Handling of Nondifferentiability Points', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly addresses the finitely many points where ρ is discontinuous, bounding their contribution separately using |m−y|≤Δ_m/2 and ‖f‖_∞<∞, while the SxS winner implicitly assumes smoothness everywhere.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = discontinuities ignored; 3-4 = mentioned in passing; 5-6 = explicit separate treatment with concrete bounds', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Conditional Expectation Structure', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly introduces the filtration (F_n) and uses the tower property to structure the proof as E[η_n]=E[E[η_n|F_n]], while the SxS winner uses E[η_n|X̄_n=x] without formalizing the conditioning structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = conditioning used informally; 3-4 = conditional expectation notation; 5-6 = full filtration/tower property machinery', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Verification Section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated '## Verification' section that summarizes the key mechanisms and assumptions used, while the SxS winner ends with the proof conclusion only.", 'scale': "1-6 scale: 1-2 = no verification; 3-4 = brief summary; 5-6 = structured verification with bullet points explaining each assumption's role", 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit Tie-Breaking Discussion', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly notes that tie events have probability zero under a density, so round-to-nearest-even's tie-breaking does not affect the expectation, while the SxS winner does not address this subtlety.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = tie-breaking ignored; 3-4 = mentioned; 5-6 = explicit probability-zero argument', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Affine Transformation of Density', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly writes S_n = α_n + β_n Z̃_n and identifies the conditional density as an affine transformation of ρ with the scaling factor 1/|β_n|, while the SxS winner writes the density g_x(s) without explaining its origin from the affine structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = density stated; 3-4 = transformation noted; 5-6 = explicit α_n, β_n decomposition with clear scaling', 'rating': 4}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemin