2026-03-25 07:33:08,078 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10884', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThis section reviews the constructive notions of dimension in Euclidean spaces. The presentation here is in terms of Kolmogorov complexity.\n\nBriefly, the conditional Kolmogorov complexity $K(w|v)$ of a string $w \\in \\{0,1\\}^*$ given a string $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^*$ is the minimum length $|\\pi|$ of a binary string $\\pi$ for which $U(\\pi,v) = w$, where $U$ is a fixed universal self-delimiting Turing machine. The Kolmogorov complexity of $w$ is $K(w) = K(w|\\lambda)$, where $\\lambda$ is the empty string. We write $U(\\pi)$ for $U(\\pi,\\lambda)$. When $U(\\pi) = w$, the string $\\pi$ is called a program for $w$. The quantity $K(w)$ is also called the algorithmic information content of $w$. Routine coding extends this definition from $\\{0,1\\}^*$ to other discrete domains, so that the Kolmogorov complexities of natural numbers, rational numbers, tuples of these, etc., are well defined up to additive constants.\n\nThe definition of $K(q)$ for rational points $q$ in Euclidean space is lifted in two steps to define the dimensions of arbitrary points in Euclidean space. First, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ and $r \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the Kolmogorov complexity of $x$ at precision $r$ is\n$$K_r(x)=\\min\\{K(q):q\\in\\mathbb{Q}^n\\cap B_{2^{-r}}(x)\\},$$\nwhere $B_{2^{-r}}(x)$ is the open ball with radius $2^{-r}$ and center $x$. Second, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, the dimension of $x$ is\n$$\\dim(x)=\\liminf_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{K_r(x)}{r}.$$\n\nAll the algorithmic information concepts above can be relativized to an arbitrary oracle $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ by giving the Turing machine in their definitions oracle access to $A$. Relativized Kolmogorov complexity $K_r^A(x)$ and relativized dimensions $\\dim^A(x)$ are thus well defined.\n\nWe first establish the point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension. Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$. For $\\delta>0$, define $\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$ to be the collection of all countable covers of $E$ by sets of positive diameter at most $\\delta$. That is, for every cover $\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$, we have $E\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}U_i$ and $|U_i|\\in(0,\\delta]$ for all $i\\in\\mathbb{N}$, where for $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, $|X|=\\sup_{p,q\\in X}|p-q|$. For $s\\geq0$, define\n$$H^s_\\delta(E)=\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}|U_i|^s:\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)\\right\\}.$$\nThen the $s$-dimensional Hausdorff outer measure of $E$ is\n$$H^s(E)=\\lim_{\\delta\\to0^+}H^s_\\delta(E),$$\nand the Hausdorff dimension of $E$ is\n$$\\dim_H(E)=\\inf\\{s\\geq0:H^s(E)=0\\}.$$\n\n**Observation 6**\nThere is a constant $c_0\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $j\\in\\mathbb{N}$,\n$$K(j)\\leq\\log(1+j)+2\\log\\log(2+j)+c_0.$$\n## Theorem\n**(Point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension)** For every set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$,\n$$\\dim_H(E) = \\inf_{A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}}\\sup_{x\\in E}\\dim^A(x).$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist verification: [X] Provides complete and correct proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Thinks carefully with multiple approaches (explicitly outlines inequality directions and strategies before proof); [X] Honest about completeness (no admission of inability needed as proof is complete); [X] Includes explicit boundary case computations as required by the mathematical context (singleton and [0,1]^n). All instruction elements satisfied. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirement with clear ## Proof section.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Fact 1 (counting bound) follows from prefix-free property of self-delimiting machines; [VERIFIED] Fact 2 (Kraft inequality) is standard for prefix-free sets; [VERIFIED] Observation 6 is given in setup; [VERIFIED] The point-to-set principle itself is a known theorem (Lutz & Lutz 2018). Minor imprecision: The claim 'K^A(q) ≤ K(q) + O(1)' in the [0,1]^n case is technically correct but the 'O(1)' depends on the universal machine and oracle encoding; this is standard but could be more explicit. No false claims detected. All technical steps (Kraft construction, oracle encoding, liminf arguments) are correctly executed."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Both directions of inequality proved; [CORRECT] Part I (≤): Uses liminf definition, Kraft inequality, and counting bounds correctly to show H^s(E)=0; [CORRECT] Part II (≥): Constructs explicit oracle A encoding covers, uses prefix-free Kraft construction without circularity; [CORRECT] Boundary cases verified: singleton gives 0, [0,1]^n gives n; [CORRECT] All definitions from setup used appropriately (K_r, dim, H^s_δ, etc.). No incorrect conclusions. The proof structure matches the standard point-to-set principle proof in the literature.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Exceptionally clear structure with explicit 'Inequality directions & strategies' section before proof. Logical flow is excellent with numbered steps and explicit equation references. Minor issues: (1) Some sentences are long and complex, e.g., 'By the definition of relativized Kolmogorov complexity K^A(·), there is a constant c_1 (depending on M and the universal machine, not on k,i) such that...' — could be split; (2) Heavy use of 'by the definition of' becomes repetitive (approximately 20+ instances). These are trivial style preferences. Mathematical notation is precise, audience-appropriate for researchers in computable analysis."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Length analysis: The response is comprehensive but has identifiable padding. Specifically: (1) The explicit 'Justification (definition of K^A)' phrases after Facts 1 and 2 are redundant — the facts are standard and the proof would flow without these parenthetical justifications; (2) The boundary cases section, while valuable, goes beyond strict proof requirements and could be trimmed (the prompt asks for 'complete and correct proof' but doesn't explicitly require boundary case verification). However, the core proof is appropriately detailed. Ideal length would be ~15-20% shorter. Nothing critical is missing."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.3, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.30'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Checklist: [X] Provides proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Thinks carefully (uses two lemmas with clear structure); [X] Honest about completeness (proof is complete); [ ] Missing: No explicit boundary case computations (singleton, [0,1]^n) as included in Response A. While not strictly required by minimal interpretation, the mathematical context and thoroughness expectation suggests boundary cases are part of 'complete proof.' Format is correct. One significant omission: the reduction to E⊆[0,1]^n assumes countable stability without proof or citation, which is a gap in completeness."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] Lemma 1 proof uses standard covering argument with prefix-free counting; [VERIFIED] Lemma 2 constructs oracle via dyadic cubes; [VERIFIED] Observation 6 used correctly; [QUESTIONABLE] The claim 'We may assume E⊆[0,1]^n (Hausdorff dimension is countably stable)' — countable stability is true but the reduction to [0,1]^n actually requires finite stability or that E is bounded, not just countable stability; for unbounded E, one needs to handle each [k,k+1]^n separately. This is a minor technical gap; [UNVERIFIABLE] The specific encoding 'A={⟨σ_i,q_i⟩:i∈ℕ}' without explicit definition of the pairing function, though standard. The proof is essentially correct but has this technical imprecision in the reduction."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Both inequality directions proved via lemmas; [CORRECT] Lemma 1 establishes dim_H(D^A_s) ≤ s; [CORRECT] Lemma 2 constructs oracle achieving sup ≤ s; [PARTIALLY CORRECT] The reduction to E⊆[0,1]^n is technically incomplete — the proof should either handle general E directly or explicitly use σ-compactness/ countable stability with more care. The dyadic cube construction in Lemma 2 assumes E is covered by cubes of uniform size 2^{-r}, which works for bounded E but the extension to general E via countable stability is not fully justified. The conclusion is correct but the path has a gap.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clean, professional mathematical prose. Excellent use of lemmas to structure proof. Notation is concise and clear. Minor issues: (1) The phrase 'From Lemma 1, if sup...' in the main proof flow could be more explicit about the logical connection (it's a contrapositive-style argument); (2) The final paragraph 'Now let ε>0...' compresses the limiting argument significantly — could be clearer. These are minor. Overall more concise than Response A, with less redundancy."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Near-perfect calibration. The proof is concise without being terse. Each lemma is stated with proof; main result follows cleanly. Nothing obvious to cut: the dyadic cube construction is minimal, the oracle encoding is compact. Missing: Could benefit from one sentence explicitly justifying the reduction to [0,1]^n more carefully, or alternatively handling general E directly. This would add ~2 lines. As is, slightly under-explained but not pro