2026-03-25 07:33:11,519 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10884', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThis section reviews the constructive notions of dimension in Euclidean spaces. The presentation here is in terms of Kolmogorov complexity.\n\nBriefly, the conditional Kolmogorov complexity $K(w|v)$ of a string $w \\in \\{0,1\\}^*$ given a string $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^*$ is the minimum length $|\\pi|$ of a binary string $\\pi$ for which $U(\\pi,v) = w$, where $U$ is a fixed universal self-delimiting Turing machine. The Kolmogorov complexity of $w$ is $K(w) = K(w|\\lambda)$, where $\\lambda$ is the empty string. We write $U(\\pi)$ for $U(\\pi,\\lambda)$. When $U(\\pi) = w$, the string $\\pi$ is called a program for $w$. The quantity $K(w)$ is also called the algorithmic information content of $w$. Routine coding extends this definition from $\\{0,1\\}^*$ to other discrete domains, so that the Kolmogorov complexities of natural numbers, rational numbers, tuples of these, etc., are well defined up to additive constants.\n\nThe definition of $K(q)$ for rational points $q$ in Euclidean space is lifted in two steps to define the dimensions of arbitrary points in Euclidean space. First, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ and $r \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the Kolmogorov complexity of $x$ at precision $r$ is\n$$K_r(x)=\\min\\{K(q):q\\in\\mathbb{Q}^n\\cap B_{2^{-r}}(x)\\},$$\nwhere $B_{2^{-r}}(x)$ is the open ball with radius $2^{-r}$ and center $x$. Second, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, the dimension of $x$ is\n$$\\dim(x)=\\liminf_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{K_r(x)}{r}.$$\n\nAll the algorithmic information concepts above can be relativized to an arbitrary oracle $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ by giving the Turing machine in their definitions oracle access to $A$. Relativized Kolmogorov complexity $K_r^A(x)$ and relativized dimensions $\\dim^A(x)$ are thus well defined.\n\nWe first establish the point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension. Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$. For $\\delta>0$, define $\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$ to be the collection of all countable covers of $E$ by sets of positive diameter at most $\\delta$. That is, for every cover $\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$, we have $E\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}U_i$ and $|U_i|\\in(0,\\delta]$ for all $i\\in\\mathbb{N}$, where for $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, $|X|=\\sup_{p,q\\in X}|p-q|$. For $s\\geq0$, define\n$$H^s_\\delta(E)=\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}|U_i|^s:\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)\\right\\}.$$\nThen the $s$-dimensional Hausdorff outer measure of $E$ is\n$$H^s(E)=\\lim_{\\delta\\to0^+}H^s_\\delta(E),$$\nand the Hausdorff dimension of $E$ is\n$$\\dim_H(E)=\\inf\\{s\\geq0:H^s(E)=0\\}.$$\n\n**Observation 6**\nThere is a constant $c_0\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $j\\in\\mathbb{N}$,\n$$K(j)\\leq\\log(1+j)+2\\log\\log(2+j)+c_0.$$\n## Theorem\n**(Point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension)** For every set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$,\n$$\\dim_H(E) = \\inf_{A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}}\\sup_{x\\in E}\\dim^A(x).$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instructions satisfied: (1) Complete and correct proof provided under ## Proof heading; (2) Two matching inequalities proven separately as required; (3) Multiple approaches not needed as proof succeeds directly; (4) No extraneous content added. The proof structure follows standard mathematical convention with clear directional labels (i) and (ii). No unrequested elements present.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: (1) Kraft inequality application in part (i) is correct—sum of 2^{-|p|} over programs producing balls yields valid cover measure bound; (2) The pruning to prefix-free subfamily in part (ii) is standard and correctly applied; (3) Observation 6 used correctly for K(m) and K(i_m) bounds; (4) The counting argument N_{m,k} ≤ 2^{ks-m} follows from the Hausdorff measure condition. One minor unverifiable: the constant c_M in 'K(m)+K(i_m)+O(1)' is not explicitly bounded but follows from standard Turing machine simulation overhead. No false claims identified."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Every requirement correctly addressed: (1) Point-to-set principle equality proven with both directions; (2) Direction (i) shows dim_H(E) ≤ sup_{x∈E} dim^A(x) for all A, hence ≤ inf_A sup; (3) Direction (ii) constructs explicit oracle A achieving sup_{x∈E} dim^A(x) ≤ s for any s > dim_H(E); (4) All definitions from setup used correctly: K_r(x), dim(x), H^s_δ(E), dim_H(E), relativized versions. The proof correctly handles the liminf in dimension definition and the cover construction from Hausdorff measure.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with minor issue: (1) Clear structure with labeled parts (i) and (ii); (2) Appropriate use of LaTeX formatting; (3) Logical flow is easy to follow; (4) Technical terms used correctly. Minor issue: some sentences are dense (e.g., 'If p has length ℓ, the associated ball has diameter 2^{-r} with (2^{-r})^s ≤ 2^{-ℓ}')—could benefit from slightly more explicit intermediate steps. The notation 'r'=⌈|p|/s⌉≤r' reusing r is slightly confusing though technically correct. Overall excellent clarity for expert audience."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect calibration: (1) Each inequality proven with sufficient detail—no gaps requiring external knowledge beyond standard results; (2) The Kraft inequality and prefix-free pruning mentioned without excessive elaboration, appropriate for expert audience; (3) The O(log k_m) and O(1) terms handled concisely; (4) No redundant examples or filler. Minor trim possible: the explicit 'Consequently' restatement before combining inequalities is slightly redundant but aids clarity. Nothing essential missing."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Critical failure: Missing explicit ## Proof heading as required by instructions. The response uses '## Proof Strategy' and '## Proof' but the latter is buried after a lengthy strategy section, and the final proof is split with '## Boundary Cases' added—unrequested content. The prompt asked for proof under ## Proof heading with honesty if unable; instead we get: (1) Unrequested 'Proof Strategy' section with verbose directional descriptions; (2) Unrequested 'Boundary Cases' section with two concrete instances; (3) The actual proof is present but surrounded by excessive framing. This violates 'cleaned up proof under heading ## Proof' and adds substantial unrequested material."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Multiple questionable/unverifiable claims: (1) 'By universally shifting our sequence of covers to start at index k = ⌈s⌉ + 1'—this shift is unmotivated and the claim that this 'normalizes' the sum is imprecise; the sum bound 2^{s-k} ≤ 1/2 is trivial for large k anyway. (2) The oracle construction via 'deterministic mapping from (k, w_{k,i}) to q_{k,i}' lacks explicit verification that this is well-defined (unique decoding). (3) The claim 'M checks A bit-by-bit to safely decode w = w_{k,i}' is vague—how does M know when w_{k,i} ends without circularity? (4) In Direction 2, 'fewer than 2^{sr+1} valid rational points q'—should be ≤ 2^{⌊sr⌋+1} or similar, the +1 placement is sloppy. (5) The Borel-Cantelli argument in Concrete Instance 2 is compressed to incomprehensibility—'tracking the series ∑V_n 2^{-r^{1/2}} < ∞ falls into Borel-Cantelli bounds' is not a complete argument. Several technical gaps make claims unverifiable."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Significant issues: (1) The proof in Direction 1 has a critical error in the limit argument: 'liminf_{k→∞} K_{m_{k,i}}^A(x)/m_{k,i}' depends on i which varies with k, making the limit ill-defined as written—the index i(k) is not controlled; (2) The conclusion 'dim^A(x) ≤ s' requires showing liminf over r, not just the subsequence m_{k,i(k)}; (3) Direction 2 correctly proves the inequality but with excessive length; (4) The Boundary Cases section contains errors: in Concrete Instance 2, the claim that 'fewer than 2^{nr-r^{1/2}} rational coordinate centers' is not justified—the argument about 'aggregate tally of valid encoded outputs' is garbled and unverifiable. The core theorem is approximately correct but with technical gaps in execution."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 2, 'reason': "Multiple major clarity issues: (1) Severe verbosity with redundant phrasing ('rigorously guarantees', 'mathematically guarantee', 'definitively yielding', 'perfectly outputs', 'safely decode', 'strictly inside', 'aggressively as grid size', 'solidifying', 'mechanically fetched'); (2) Overuse of passive and convoluted constructions; (3) Inconsistent tone—mix of formal mathematics with flowery language ('fires the distance condition', 'lands safely inside', 'crushes 2^{-m(t-s)}→0'); (4) Run-on sentences throughout, e.g., 'By definition of conditional Kolmogorov complexity K(w|v), we envision a prefix-free Turing machine M equipped with oracle A. Given the empty string λ, M dynamically reads a valid program p_k to extract integer k (using the standard self-delimiting mechanism of U). It subsequently reads string w.'—this is procedural rather than mathematical; (5) Poor paragraph organization with excessive single-sentence paragraphs in Boundary Cases. The writing impedes comprehension of the mathematics."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 2, 'reason': "Severely miscalibrated: (1) The 'Proof Strategy' section (~200 words) is entirely unnecessary per instructions; (2) The 'Proof of no circularity' paragraph is self-indulgent padding; (3) The 'Bounding Complexity' subsection uses ~150 words for a standard simulation argument that needs ~30; (4) Direction 1 proof is ~3× longer than necessary due to procedural description of Turing machine operation; (5) The Boundary Cases section (~400 words) is completely unrequested; (6) Multiple filler phrases: 'It's important to note that' (implied), 'We explicitly define', 'By universally shifting', 'This normalizes our sum'. The response could be cut by 60% without losing mathematical content. Critical missing element: the clean ## Proof heading with immediate proof start."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 2.7, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 2.70'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': ''}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) provides a correct, rigorous proof with clean mathematical structure following standard proof conventions in algorithmic information theory, while Gemini 3 Pro's response contains significant technical errors and verbosity issues. For Instruction Following: SxS winner precisely addresses both directions of the inequality with appropriate mathematical notation and structure; Gemini 3 Pro adds unnecessary 'Proof Strategy' and 'Boundary Cases' sections not requested, violating the prompt's instruction for a cleaned up proof under '## Proof' heading only. For Truthfulness: SxS winner correctly implements the Kraft inequality argument with proper indexing (using programs of length ℓ with r = ⌈ℓ/s⌉); Gemini 3 Pro makes a critical error in Direction 2 where it claims 'fewer than 2^{sr+1} valid rational points q'—this bound is wrong because multiple programs can produce the same q, and more seriously, the argument that 'tightening δ_m = 2^{-m+1} → 0 precisely mimics the limit to 0+' is incorrect since the cover sum must be shown to go to zero for fixed t > s, not just for t > s with t approaching s. For Prompt Correctness: SxS winner correctly uses Observation 6 as stated (K(j) ≤ log(1+j) + 2loglog(2+j) + c_0); Gemini 3 Pro misapplies it as K(k) ≤ log k + 2loglog k + c_0, missing the +1 and +2 offsets. For Writing Style: SxS winner uses concise, professional mathematical prose; Gemini 3 Pro exhibits excessive verbosity ('rigorously guarantees,' 'fundamentally non-circular,' 'perfectly outputs') and redundant commentary that obscures the mathematics. For Verbosity: SxS winner is appropriately concise (~400 words of proof); Gemini 3 Pro is excessively verbose (~1200 words) with unnecessary meta-commentary and tangential 'Boundary Cases' that were not requested."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides a correct, complete, and elegantly structured proof with clear mathematical notation and rigorous argumentation, while GPT 5.2's response is excessively verbose with redundant 'definition' citations, contains a critical error in Part I's strategy (claiming to prove by contrapositive but actually using direct proof), and introduces unnecessary complexity through its elaborate Kraft coding construction that obscures the core insight. The SxS winner's Part (i) cleanly uses the Kraft inequality with a direct covering argument, and Part (ii) efficiently encodes cover indices via Observation 6; GPT 5.2's Part I contains a flawed claim that 'for every x∈E, dim^A(x)<s' allows uniform epsilon_x, when the liminf definition only guarantees infinitely many r per x without uniformity, and its elaborate prefix-free coding in Part II, while ultimately salvageable, is unnecessarily circuitous compared to the SxS winner's straightforward oracle construction."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Proof strategy section', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a de