ualities shown below:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}= \\left \\{h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h(i)-\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j)\\le r(i,a)-\\lambda^k, \\forall i \\in S, \\forall a\\in A(i) \\right\\},$$\nwhich is equivalent to $H_{\\lambda^k} = \\{ h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h \\le T_{\\lambda^k} h \\}$.\n\nThe Gauss-Seidel variant of the operator, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$, is defined by its application $h^{k+1}=T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h^k}$ where:\n$$h^{k+1}(i)=\\min_{a\\in{A(i)}}\\left\\{r(i,a)-\\lambda^k+\\sum_{j<i} p_{ij}(a) h^{k+1}(j)+ \\sum_{j \\geq i}^{n-1} p_{ij}(a) h^k(j) \\right\\}, \\forall i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\n\nLet the set $H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$ be defined as:\n$$H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}=\\{h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\mid h\\le T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h}\\}.$$\n\nThe following lemmas are required to prove the main theorem.\n\n**Lemma 19 (Monotonicity)**\n1. If $h \\ge g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h \\ge T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h \\ge T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n2. If $h > g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h > T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h > T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n\n**Lemma 15**\nThe following relation holds:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}\\subset{H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}}.$$\n\n**Lemma 17**\n$$T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}( H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}.$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 18**\nThe set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ is invariant under $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$. That is, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}(H_{\\lambda^k}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All instruction elements checked: [X] Provide complete and correct proof; [X] Think carefully (evidenced by clear strategy); [X] Try multiple approaches if stuck (not needed here as direct approach works); [X] Give cleaned up proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Be honest if unable to complete (not applicable, proof completed). The response follows the exact format requested with '## Proof' heading. No unrequested content added. The proof strategy is clearly stated before the formal proof. All required lemmas are referenced correctly."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All claims verified: [VERIFIED] Lemma 15 states H_{λ^k} ⊂ H_{GS_{λ^k}} — correctly cited; [VERIFIED] Lemma 17 states T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{GS_{λ^k}}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} — correctly cited; [VERIFIED] The logical chain 'h ∈ H_{λ^k} ⇒ h ∈ H_{GS_{λ^k}} ⇒ T_{GS_{λ^k}}h ∈ H_{λ^k}' is valid by transitivity of subset relations; [VERIFIED] The conclusion T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{λ^k}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} correctly follows. No unverifiable technical claims. The verification section correctly notes that equality is not claimed, which is accurate."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Theorem 18 asks to prove H_{λ^k} is invariant under T_{GS_{λ^k}}, i.e., T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{λ^k}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} — fully proven; [CORRECT] Proof correctly uses Lemma 15 to establish H_{λ^k} ⊂ H_{GS_{λ^k}}; [CORRECT] Proof correctly uses Lemma 17 to establish T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{GS_{λ^k}}) ⊂ H_{λ^k}; [CORRECT] The composition of these inclusions yields the desired result. The proof is logically sound and complete. No incorrect or missing elements.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clarity: excellent, logical flow is transparent; Structure: clear two-step proof with labeled steps; Tone: appropriate mathematical formality; Grammar: correct. One minor issue identified: The phrase 'the chosen h' in Step 1 is slightly informal for a formal proof ('the arbitrary h' or simply 'h' would be more standard). The verification section is helpful but slightly redundant with the proof itself. These are trivial style preferences."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof is short-to-medium (direct proof using two lemmas). The response is concise and well-calibrated. Nothing can be cut because: each sentence serves a purpose; the proof strategy aids reader comprehension; the verification section clarifies scope lim