2026-03-25 07:33:16,417 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10884', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThis section reviews the constructive notions of dimension in Euclidean spaces. The presentation here is in terms of Kolmogorov complexity.\n\nBriefly, the conditional Kolmogorov complexity $K(w|v)$ of a string $w \\in \\{0,1\\}^*$ given a string $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^*$ is the minimum length $|\\pi|$ of a binary string $\\pi$ for which $U(\\pi,v) = w$, where $U$ is a fixed universal self-delimiting Turing machine. The Kolmogorov complexity of $w$ is $K(w) = K(w|\\lambda)$, where $\\lambda$ is the empty string. We write $U(\\pi)$ for $U(\\pi,\\lambda)$. When $U(\\pi) = w$, the string $\\pi$ is called a program for $w$. The quantity $K(w)$ is also called the algorithmic information content of $w$. Routine coding extends this definition from $\\{0,1\\}^*$ to other discrete domains, so that the Kolmogorov complexities of natural numbers, rational numbers, tuples of these, etc., are well defined up to additive constants.\n\nThe definition of $K(q)$ for rational points $q$ in Euclidean space is lifted in two steps to define the dimensions of arbitrary points in Euclidean space. First, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ and $r \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the Kolmogorov complexity of $x$ at precision $r$ is\n$$K_r(x)=\\min\\{K(q):q\\in\\mathbb{Q}^n\\cap B_{2^{-r}}(x)\\},$$\nwhere $B_{2^{-r}}(x)$ is the open ball with radius $2^{-r}$ and center $x$. Second, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, the dimension of $x$ is\n$$\\dim(x)=\\liminf_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{K_r(x)}{r}.$$\n\nAll the algorithmic information concepts above can be relativized to an arbitrary oracle $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ by giving the Turing machine in their definitions oracle access to $A$. Relativized Kolmogorov complexity $K_r^A(x)$ and relativized dimensions $\\dim^A(x)$ are thus well defined.\n\nWe first establish the point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension. Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$. For $\\delta>0$, define $\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$ to be the collection of all countable covers of $E$ by sets of positive diameter at most $\\delta$. That is, for every cover $\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$, we have $E\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}U_i$ and $|U_i|\\in(0,\\delta]$ for all $i\\in\\mathbb{N}$, where for $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, $|X|=\\sup_{p,q\\in X}|p-q|$. For $s\\geq0$, define\n$$H^s_\\delta(E)=\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}|U_i|^s:\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)\\right\\}.$$\nThen the $s$-dimensional Hausdorff outer measure of $E$ is\n$$H^s(E)=\\lim_{\\delta\\to0^+}H^s_\\delta(E),$$\nand the Hausdorff dimension of $E$ is\n$$\\dim_H(E)=\\inf\\{s\\geq0:H^s(E)=0\\}.$$\n\n**Observation 6**\nThere is a constant $c_0\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $j\\in\\mathbb{N}$,\n$$K(j)\\leq\\log(1+j)+2\\log\\log(2+j)+c_0.$$\n## Theorem\n**(Point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension)** For every set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$,\n$$\\dim_H(E) = \\inf_{A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}}\\sup_{x\\in E}\\dim^A(x).$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Follows core instructions: provides proof under ## Proof heading, attempts both directions of inequality. Checked: [X] Proof heading present, [X] Two-part structure (≤ and ≥), [X] Uses provided definitions (K_r, dim, dim_H). Minor issue: The prompt asks to 'try multiple approaches if stuck' and 'be honest if unable to complete'—neither is explicitly acknowledged, but this is trivial since the proof was completed. No unrequested content added. Score 5 justified: 95%+ checklist items satisfied."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Majority of claims appear correct, but several unverifiable technical assertions without full justification: [UNVERIFIABLE] 'Pruning to a prefix-free subfamily (standard) does not increase the sum'—this is asserted without proof or citation; [UNVERIFIABLE] The Kraft inequality application in part (i) with the specific construction of C_m requires careful verification that the family is indeed prefix-free, which is not explicitly shown; [UNVERIFIABLE] The claim that 'a program for q_{m,i_m} given (m,i_m) has length K(m)+K(i_m)+O(1)' assumes a specific pairing function without specifying it. No [FALSE] claims identified, but multiple [UNVERIFIABLE] technical steps cap score at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] The overall structure matches the theorem (two inequalities). [PARTIALLY CORRECT] Part (i) construction: The definition of C_m uses r = ⌈ℓ/s⌉ where ℓ = |p|, but then claims (2^{-r})^s ≤ 2^{-ℓ}. This requires ⌈ℓ/s⌉ ≥ ℓ/s, so r ≥ ℓ/s, thus sr ≥ ℓ, so 2^{-sr} ≤ 2^{-ℓ}, hence (2^{-r})^s = 2^{-sr} ≤ 2^{-ℓ}. This is correct but the logic is compressed and the ball diameter is 2·2^{-r}, not 2^{-r}, so |B|^s = (2^{1-r})^s = 2^{s(1-r)} = 2^s · 2^{-sr} ≤ 2^s · 2^{-ℓ}. The factor 2^s is absorbed in the O(1) but should be explicit. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] Part (ii): The claim 'i_m ≤ N_{m,k_m} ≤ 2^{k_m s - m}' uses the pruning to prefix-free, but the index ordering 'by k then lexicographically' combined with the prefix-free pruning needs more care—the index bound derivation is heuristic. [CORRECT] Final conclusion combines inequalities properly. Score 4: mostly correct with minor gaps in rigorous justification."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Quality audit issues identified: [1] Inconsistent notation: uses both \\mathbb N and \\mathbb{N} (minor but real inconsistency). [2] Dense presentation: the construction in part (i) is a single run-on mathematical expression spanning multiple lines, difficult to parse. [3] 'Pruning to a prefix-free subfamily (standard)' is telegraphic—parenthetical '(standard)' assumes reader familiarity rather than explaining. [4] The variable r' in part (i) is introduced as r' = ⌈|p|/s⌉ ≤ r, but then immediately used as r in 'B_{2^{-r'}}(q) ∈ C_m'—the notation shift is confusing. [5] Missing article: 'the Kraft inequality' vs 'Kraft inequality'. These 3-4 minor issues justify score 4."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Length analysis: The proof is appropriately structured in two parts. However, identifiable padding exists: [CUTTABLE] The explicit construction of C_m in part (i) with the existential quantifier ∃p could be streamlined—the condition '∃p (U^A(p)=q, |p|=ℓ≥m, r=⌈ℓ/s⌉)' is redundant since K^A(q)≤sr already implies existence of such p. [CUTTABLE] The repeated 'As s>α is arbitrary' and 'Since s>dim_H(E) was arbitrary' near-identical phrasing in conclusions. [MISSING] Part (ii) could benefit from explicit statement of why the oracle A suffices for all x∈E simultaneously—the 'for every x∈E' quantification is subtle and deserves more explanation. Score 4: good but with trimmable sections and minor gaps."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.25, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.25'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Follows instructions well: [X] ## Proof heading present, [X] Two lemmas establishing both directions, [X] Uses provided definitions. Checked: Lemma structure is clear, explicit use of Observation 6, final combination of inequalities. Minor issue: The prompt's 'try multiple approaches if stuck' is not explicitly addressed, but proof is complete so this is trivial. One formatting choice: uses ∎ at end instead of \\qed, but this is acceptable variation. No significant unrequested content. Score 5: 95%+ adherence."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] Lemma 1's volume computation: |S_r| ≤ 2^{(s+ε)r+1} balls of radius 2^{-r} have volume ≤ |S_r|·c_n·2^{-nr}, and for t > s+ε with n-dimensional volume, the sum converges. The argument uses t-volume correctly with t > s+ε > s. [UNVERIFIABLE] The claim 'Enumerate all cubes as Q_i in non-decreasing order of their side'—the infinite enumeration of all dyadic cubes across all scales needs more care; the index bound i ≤ 2^{sr_i+1} assumes a specific enumeration that should be verified. [UNVERIFIABLE] 'σ_i be a shortest self-delimiting program for i'—existence and the prefix-free property of {σ_i} is standard but the construction of A = {⟨σ_i,q_i⟩} requires that ⟨·,·⟩ is a standard pairing; this is asserted not verified. [QUESTIONABLE] The final 'Letting ε↓0' assumes continuity of the infimum, which is correct but implicit. No [FALSE] claims, but multiple unverifiable constructions cap at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Lemma 1: The covering argument using D^A_s ⊆ ∩_m ∪_{r≥m} ∪_{q∈S_r} B(q,2^{-r}) is standard and correctly executed. The volume estimate with t > s+ε ensures H^t(D^A_s) = 0. [CORRECT] The deduction that E ⊆ D^A_s implies dim_H(E) ≤ s is valid. [CORRECT] Lemma 2: The construction of covers C_r with N_r ≤ 2^{sr} is valid since H^s_{2^{-r}}(E) = 0 allows sum ≤ 1 (actually any small constant). [CORRECT] The index bound: i ≤ ∑_{k≤r_i} N_k ≤ ∑_{k≤r_i} 2^{sk} ≤ 2^{s(r_i+1)}/(2^s-1) = O(2^{sr_i}), so log i ≤ sr_i + O(1). This is correct. [CORRECT] The oracle construction and K^A(q_i) bound: given σ_i, A yields q_i via lookup, so K^A(q_i) ≤ |σ_i| + O(1) = K(i) + O(1) ≤ sr_i + O(log r_i). [CORRECT] Final liminf argument: K^A_r(x) ≤ sr + O(log r) gives dim^A(x) ≤ s. The ε↓0 argument is correct. Score 5: all major requirements correct with only trivial omissions (explicit constant in N_r bound).'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: [1] Clear lemma structure with explicit statements and proof environments. [2] Consistent notation: uses \\mathbb N, \\mathbb R consistently. [3] Good flow: 'Fix t > s and ε = (t-s)/2' sets up parameters clearly. [4] Appropriate use of mathematical idioms: 'whence', 'thus', 'hence'. Minor issue: [1] 't-volume' could be explained for clarity (Hausdorff measure H^t). [2] The enumeration 'Q_i in non-decreasing order of their side' is slightly ambiguous—does this mean non-decreasing side length or non-decreasing r_i? Context makes it clear (r_i is the exponent), but explicit 'non-decreasing r_i' would be clearer. Only 1 trivial style preference identified. Score 5 justified."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: [NOT CUTTABLE] Lemma 1's proof needs the ε = (t-s)/2 construction to handle the strict inequality properly. [NOT CUTTABLE] The explicit volume computation with O(2^{-εm}) is necessary for rigor. [NOT CUTTABLE] Lemma 2's enumeration and index bound derivation is core to the oracle construction. [NOT MISSING] The final ε↓0 argument is present and appropriately brief. One potentially cuttable element: 'Enumerate all cubes as Q_i in non-decreasing order of their side, let r_i be the exponent of Q_i and q_i its left corner'—the 'left corner' specification is slightly more detail than needed (any corner works), but this is trivial. Score 5: near-perfect calibration."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs are correct and complete, but A handles technical details more rigorously: it explicitly constructs covers with controlled diameters in part (i) using Kraft's inequality, and in part (ii) carefully tracks the O(log k_m) error terms when bounding K^A_{k_m}(x). Response B's Lemma 1 is elegant but its Lemma 2 glosses over why K^A(q_i) ≤ sr_i + O(log r_i) suffices for K^A_r(x) (the precision matching requires more care), and the transition