2026-03-25 07:33:17,776 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_14482', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nConsider an infinite horizon Markov decision process (MDP) with a finite set of states denoted by $S$ of size $n$, a finite set of actions $A(i)$ for each state $i \\in S$, an immediate reward $r(i,a)$ for each $i \\in S$ and $a \\in A(i)$, and a transition probability $p_{ij}(a)$ for each $i,j \\in S$ and $a\\in A(i)$.\n\nWe consider the approach from Bertsekas [1,2] which connects the average cost MDP to a corresponding stochastic shortest path problem (denoted $\\lambda$-SSP). We assume that there is a state, denoted by $n$, which is a recurrent state under any stationary policy. The $\\lambda$-SSP problem is defined on an augmented state space $S \\cup \\{t\\}$ where $t$ is a new terminating state. The costs are $r(i,a) - \\lambda$ for a scalar parameter $\\lambda$, and the value iteration for solving the $\\lambda$-SSP problem for a fixed $\\lambda = \\lambda^k$ is given by:\n$$h^{m+1}(i)=\\min_{a \\in A(i)}\\left \\{ r(i,a)-\\lambda^k + \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h^m(j) \\right\\} \\text{ for all } i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\nThe component corresponding to state $n$ is ignored in the summation because in the $\\lambda$-SSP formulation, transitions into state $n$ are redirected to the new terminal state $t$. We consider the value function $h$ as a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nLet $T_{\\lambda^k}$ be the corresponding Markovian operator:\n$$T_{\\lambda^k}h(i)=\\min_{a \\in A(i)}\\left \\{ r(i,a)-\\lambda^k + \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j) \\right\\}$$\n\nLet the set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ be determined by the system of inequalities shown below:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}= \\left \\{h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h(i)-\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j)\\le r(i,a)-\\lambda^k, \\forall i \\in S, \\forall a\\in A(i) \\right\\},$$\nwhich is equivalent to $H_{\\lambda^k} = \\{ h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h \\le T_{\\lambda^k} h \\}$.\n\nThe Gauss-Seidel variant of the operator, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$, is defined by its application $h^{k+1}=T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h^k}$ where:\n$$h^{k+1}(i)=\\min_{a\\in{A(i)}}\\left\\{r(i,a)-\\lambda^k+\\sum_{j<i} p_{ij}(a) h^{k+1}(j)+ \\sum_{j \\geq i}^{n-1} p_{ij}(a) h^k(j) \\right\\}, \\forall i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\n\nLet the set $H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$ be defined as:\n$$H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}=\\{h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\mid h\\le T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h}\\}.$$\n\nThe following lemmas are required to prove the main theorem.\n\n**Lemma 19 (Monotonicity)**\n1. If $h \\ge g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h \\ge T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h \\ge T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n2. If $h > g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h > T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h > T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n\n**Lemma 15**\nThe following relation holds:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}\\subset{H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}}.$$\n\n**Lemma 17**\n$$T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}( H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}.$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 18**\nThe set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ is invariant under $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$. That is, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}(H_{\\lambda^k}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better due to completeness: it provides full proofs of all three lemmas (19, 15, 17) that the theorem depends on, whereas Response B only cites them as black boxes. Response A's explicit induction argument for Lemma 19's strict monotonicity and its detailed algebraic verification of Lemma 15's containment offer verifiable rigor that Response B lacks, even though both correctly chain the inclusions to prove Theorem 18."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with all three lemmas (19, 15, 17) fully proven before establishing Theorem 18, demonstrating deep understanding of the mathematical structure including the critical recurrence assumption for strict monotonicity. Gemini 3 Pro only proves Theorem 18 as a trivial corollary of Lemmas 15 and 17 without proving those lemmas, leaving the logical foundation unverified; it also incorrectly states 'We did not implicitly assume any additional operator properties' when Lemma 19's strict monotonicity is actually essential for the uniqueness arguments in the SxS winner's complete treatment. The SxS winner handles the Gauss-Seidel recursion through careful induction on state indices and explicitly addresses why state n's recurrence matters for strict inequalities, while Gemini 3 Pro's 'Verification' section mischaracterizes its own proof as complete when it merely invokes unproven lemmas."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) provides a complete, self-contained proof with full derivations of all lemmas (19, 15, 17) and the main theorem, demonstrating deep understanding by proving monotonicity from first principles including the strict inequality case with recurrence argument. GPT 5.2 only proves Theorem 18 by citing the lemmas as black boxes without proving them, and adds an unnecessary 'Verification' section that restates what was already clear. The SxS winner's proof of Lemma 19 includes the critical observation about recurrence of state n preventing equality when h>g, which GPT 5.2 entirely omits. Both models correctly prove Theorem 18, but the SxS winner's response is substantially more complete and rigorous."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 20339, 'output_tokens': 3745, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.085618}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.085618}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 20339, 'output_tokens': 3745, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.085618}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.085618}}] 
