2026-03-25 07:33:19,570 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: tasks1_response---------------------------[{'index': 0, 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$ be the theory of parametric bit-vectors and $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$ be the theory of uninterpreted functions and integer arithmetic. A parametric bit-vector formula $\\varphi$ is a formula over a signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ containing parametric bit-vector variables (from a set $X^\\ast$) and parametric bit-vector constants (from a set $Z^\\ast$).\n\nA mapping $\\omega = (\\omega^b, \\omega^N)$ assigns to each symbol $\\mathsf{s} \\in X^\\ast \\cup Z^\\ast$ a bit-width term $\\omega^b(\\mathsf{s})$ and for $\\mathsf{s} \\in Z^\\ast$ an integer value term $\\omega^N(\\mathsf{s})$. The formula $\\varphi$ is well-sorted under $\\omega$ if for every term, its bit-width is well-defined.\n\nWe define a translation function $\\mathcal{T}_A$ from parametric bit-vector formulas to formulas in $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$, parameterized by an axiomatization mode $A$. The translation is defined as:\n$\\mathcal{T}_A(\\varphi, \\omega) \\equiv \\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega) \\implies Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$\n\nThe auxiliary function $Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$ converts parametric bit-vector expressions into integer expressions with uninterpreted functions:\n- Parametric bit-vector variables $\\mathsf{x} \\in X^\\ast$ are replaced by unique integer variables $\\chi(\\mathsf{x})$.\n- Parametric bit-vector constants $\\mathsf{z} \\in Z^\\ast$ are replaced by the term $\\omega^N (\\mathsf{z} ) \\mod \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}} (\\omega^b (\\mathsf{z} ))$.\n- An operator $\\bvsymbolf{f}$ from the signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ is replaced by a corresponding uninterpreted function ${f}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}$. For example, $\\bvsymbolf{+}$ with sort $\\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\times \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\to \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n}$ is replaced by $\\natsymbol{+}$ of sort $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\to \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}}$, where the first argument is the bit-width.\n- Most of these uninterpreted functions are then eliminated by replacing them with equivalent expressions in integer arithmetic. For example, ${+}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}(k,x,y)$ is replaced by $(x+y) \\mod \\mathsf{pow2}(k)$.\n- After elimination, the only remaining uninterpreted functions are $\\{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}, \\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}\\}$.\n\nThe formula $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ provides axioms for these functions. For the `full` axiomatization mode ($A=\\mathrm{full}$), $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {\\mathrm{full}}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ is the conjunction of:\n1.  Range constraints for variables: For each parametric bit-vector variable $\\mathsf{x}$ in $\\varphi$, the axiom $0 \\le \\chi(\\mathsf{x}) < \\mathsf{pow2}(\\omega^b(\\mathsf{x}))$ is added.\n2.  Positivity of bit-widths: For each free variable $v$ in $\\omega$, the axiom $v > 0$ is added.\n3.  Axioms for the uninterpreted functions, as defined in Table 2 from the paper.\n\n**Full Axiomatization from Table 2:**\nLet $i, j, k$ be bit-widths and $x, y$ be integers. We use $\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{i}{x}$ as shorthand for $(x \\div \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)} {i}) \\mod 2$, where $\\twotothef(k)$ is an uninterpreted function for $2^k$.\n\n- **Axioms for $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\mathsf{pow2}}}$):**\n  - $\\forall k. k \\le 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 1$\n  - $\\forall k. k > 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 2 \\cdot \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise AND, $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\&}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intandf(1, x, y) = \\min(x, y)$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intandf(k, x, y) = \\intandf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\min(\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x}, \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y})$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise XOR, $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\oplus}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intxorf(1, x, y) = \\lvert x-y \\rvert$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intxorf(k, x, y) = \\intxorf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\lvert \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x} - \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y} \\rvert$\n\nThe axiomatization of $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}$ is omitted and is dual to that of $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$.\n\n**Notation from Appendix:**\n- Let $\\mathcal{J}$ be a mapping of the variables in $\\vec{v}=\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{FV}\\kern-.15em(#1)} {\\omega}$ to positive integers.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\fsbvtheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$.\n- For a bit-vector constant $a$, let ${\\brackets{a}}_{\\mathbb{N}}$ denote its value as an integer.\n- For a parametric $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$-term $t$, let $t|_{\\omega, \\mathcal{J}}$ denote the concrete bit-vector term obtained by instantiating the parameters in $t$ according to $\\omega$ and $\\mathcal{J}$.\n\nWe use $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ as shorthand for $\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\varphi, \\omega)$.\n## Theorem\nLet $\\varphi$ be a parameteric bit-vector formula that is well sorted under $\\omega$ and has no occurrences of bit-vector extract or concrete bit-vector constants. Then: $\\varphi$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}} $-valid under $\\omega$ if and only if $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}} $-valid.', 'status': 'ACCEPT', 'result': {'status': 'ACCEPT', 'rejection_reason': None, 'justification': 'The prompt is a well-formed academic request asking for a proof of a theorem in formal logic/theorem proving. It provides complete reference text including setup definitions, translation functions, axiomatization details, and the theorem statement to prove. No rejection criteria apply.'}, 'accepted': True, 'error': None, 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 2411, 'output_tokens': 81, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.005794}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.005794}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 2411, 'output_tokens': 81, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.005794}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.005794}}] 
