2026-03-25 07:34:23,875 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_15079', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nConsider a flat Universe with a cosmological constant ($\\Omega_0 + \\lambda_0 = 1$), where $\\Omega_0$ is the present-day dimensionless mass density and $\\lambda_0$ is the present-day dimensionless dark energy. The Hubble parameter at a redshift $z_i$ is related to its present-day value $H_0$ by:\n$$ H_i^2 = H_0^2\\left[\\Omega_0(1+z_i)^3 + (1-\\Omega_0)\\right] $$\nWe model the growth of a structure using the spherical collapse model. Consider a spherical shell of matter at a very early time $t_i$ (corresponding to redshift $z_i \\gg 1$) at radius $r_i$. The mass enclosed within the shell is given by\n$$ M = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\left (1+\\delta_i \\right) \\rho_i r_i^3 $$\nwhere $\\rho_i = \\rho_0(1+z_i)^3$ is the mean mass density of the Universe at time $t_i$, and $\\delta_i$ is the relative overdensity within radius $r_i$ at time $t_i$. We assume the overdensity evolves according to linear theory, such that $\\delta_i = \\delta_0/(1+z_i)$, where $\\delta_0$ is the linearly extrapolated present-day overdensity.\n\nThe equation of motion of the shell of matter is\n$$ \\frac{{\\rm d}^2 r}{{\\rm d}t^2} = - \\frac{G M}{r^2} + \\frac{\\Lambda}{3}r $$\nwhere we assume no shell crossing, so the mass $M$ is constant. The dimensionless mass density and dark energy at any epoch are defined as:\n$$ \\Omega \\equiv \\frac{8\\pi G\\rho}{3H^2} \\quad , \\quad \\lambda \\equiv \\frac{\\Lambda}{3H^2} $$\nWe introduce a dimensionless radial growth factor $u(t) = r(t)/r_i$. The initial velocity of the shell is not a pure Hubble flow, but incorporates the peculiar motion, given by:\n$$ \\dot u_i = H_i\\left (1-{\\delta_i \\over 3} \\right ) $$\nThe comoving radius of the initial perturbation is $r_0 = r_i(1+z_i)$. We define a scaled radius $y = r/r_0 = u/(1+z_i)$. The turnaround point is the first apocenter of the shell, where its radial velocity is zero. We denote the radius and time at turnaround by $r_\\mathrm{ta}$ and $T_\\mathrm{ta}$ respectively, and the corresponding dimensionless radii by $u_\\mathrm{ta}$ and $y_\\mathrm{ta}$.\n## Theorem\nIn a flat $\\Lambda$CDM cosmology ($\\Omega_0 + \\lambda_0 = 1$), a spherical overdense shell of matter, with a linearly extrapolated present-day overdensity $\\delta_0$, will reach its turnaround radius $r_\\mathrm{ta}$ at time $T_\\mathrm{ta}$. Let $y_\\mathrm{ta} = r_\\mathrm{ta}/r_0$ be the ratio of the physical turnaround radius to the initial comoving radius. Then $y_\\mathrm{ta}$ is the smallest real positive solution to the cubic equation:\n$$ \\frac{1-\\Omega_0}{\\Omega_0}y_\\mathrm{ta}^3 - \\frac{5}{3}\\delta_0y_\\mathrm{ta} + 1 = 0 $$\nFor $\\delta_0 \\geq \\frac{9}{10} 2^{1/3} \\left(\\frac{(1-\\Omega_0)^2}{\\Omega_0^2}\\right)^{1/3}$, the solution is given by:\n$$ y_\\mathrm{ta} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\delta_0\\Omega_0}{1-\\Omega_0}}\\cos \\left (\\frac{\\phi+\\pi}{3} \\right ) $$\nwhere\n$$ \\phi = \\cos^{-1} \\sqrt{\\frac{729}{500}\\frac{1-\\Omega_0}{\\delta_0^3\\Omega_0}} $$\nThe time of turnaround $T_\\mathrm{ta}$ is given by the integral:\n$$ H_0T_\\mathrm{ta} = \\int_0^{y_\\mathrm{ta}} \\frac{\\sqrt{y}{\\rm d}y}{\\sqrt{\\Omega_0 - \\frac{5}{3}\\delta_0\\Omega_0y + (1-\\Omega_0)y^3}} $$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading, (2) Multiple approaches not needed as direct derivation succeeded, (3) All three components of theorem proved: cubic equation derived (eq. 4), trigonometric solution obtained with correct condition, turnaround integral derived. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirements with clean mathematical presentation.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'All major mathematical claims verified: [VERIFIED] Equation (3) for ẏ² correctly derived from first principles; [VERIFIED] Cubic equation (4) matches theorem exactly; [VERIFIED] Cardano solution with discriminant condition correctly applied; [VERIFIED] Trigonometric solution form matches theorem with k=1 giving smallest positive root; [VERIFIED] Turnaround integral correctly derived. One minor issue: The condition in theorem uses δ₀ ≥ (9/10)2^(1/3)[(1-Ω₀)²/Ω₀²]^(1/3) = (9/10)(4(1-Ω₀)²/Ω₀²)^(1/3), while response derives δ₀³ ≥ (729/500)(1-Ω₀)/Ω₀. These are equivalent: (9/10)³·2 = 729/500, confirming consistency. No false claims; all derivations checkable.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All requirements [CORRECT]: (1) Cubic equation for y_ta derived exactly as stated; (2) Trigonometric solution with correct φ and coefficient 2√5/3 √(δ₀Ω₀/(1-Ω₀)) verified; (3) Condition on δ₀ correctly interpreted and satisfied; (4) Turnaround time integral derived with correct integrand √(y)/√(Ω₀ - (5/3)δ₀Ω₀y + (1-Ω₀)y³). Physical interpretation consistent with spherical collapse model. No missing elements.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Exceptionally clear mathematical writing: equations numbered and referenced, logical flow from EOM to first integral to turnaround condition, compact notation (ε for 1+zᵢ, A and B for coefficients). Minor issue: 'eq.(2)' and 'eq.(3)' style slightly inconsistent (period after eq vs not), and 'O(ε)' notation in (2) could be clearer. Otherwise publication-ready for physics context."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Nothing can be cut: each equation serves purpose—(1) first integral, (2) expansion showing cancellation, (3) final ẏ² equation, (4) cubic. The Cardano solution section is minimal but complete. Nothing is 