ting issues (missing spaces after periods, run-together equations) and occasionally dense prose. Overall, the SxS winner provides a more reliable, pedagogically sound, and physically accurate derivation."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) and GPT 5.2 both derive the correct results, but the SxS winner is more concise and pedagogically clearer while GPT 5.2 contains a subtle error in its threshold condition. Specifically, GPT 5.2 states the discriminant condition as δ₀³ ≥ (729/500)(1-Ω₀)/Ω₀ in equation (13), but the theorem requires δ₀ ≥ (9/10)2^(1/3)[(1-Ω₀)²/Ω₀²]^(1/3) — GPT 5.2's condition has (1-Ω₀)/Ω₀ instead of (1-Ω₀)²/Ω₀², missing the square on the ratio. The SxS winner correctly identifies this as a 'typographical error' in the theorem statement and derives the proper condition in equation (7). Both models correctly derive the cubic equation (6/11), the trigonometric solution (8), and the time integral (9/18). The SxS winner's presentation is more streamlined with better physical interpretation sections, while GPT 5.2 is more verbose with excessive algebraic detail that doesn't add clarity. The SxS winner's Einstein-de Sitter limit check is cleaner and correctly notes the standard result H₀T_ta = 1/3 for δ₀ ≈ 1.686, whereas GPT 5.2's equivalent calculation in equation (19) is correct but less directly connected to the physical interpretation."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit initial condition expansion', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly expands and substitutes the initial conditions at time t_i to derive the integration constant E, showing the full algebraic manipulation including the expansion of (1-δ_i/3)² and the grouping of terms by Ω_0(1+z_i)³ and (1-Ω_0).', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of initial conditions; 3-4 = partial expansion shown; 5-6 = complete step-by-step derivation with all algebraic steps visible', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Physical interpretation of integ