2026-03-25 07:35:09,273 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_8371', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nWe consider the relativistic formulation of radiative transfer in moving media. The one-particle distribution function for photons is denoted by $f(p, x)$, where $p^{\\mu}$ is the four-momentum of a photon and $x^\\mu$ is its location in space-time.\n\nFor a gray medium, where the photon mean free path $\\varepsilon$ is assumed to be constant, we can write an expansion for the distribution function in powers of a small parameter $\\epsilon$ (a typical value of $\\varepsilon$):\n$$\nf=\\sum_{m=0}^{\\infty}f_{(m)}\\epsilon^m\n$$\nThe terms in the expansion satisfy the relations:\n$$\nf_{(0)} ={\\cal S}\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal{S}$ is the source function, and for higher orders:\n$$\nf_{(m+1)}=-n^{\\mu} f_{(m),\\mu}\n$$\nwhere $p^\\mu \\partial_{\\mu}{f}$ is the substantive derivative, $n^\\mu$ is a null vector, and summation over repeated indices is understood. The null vector is defined as $n^{\\mu} = p^{\\mu}/\\tilde\\nu$, where $\\tilde\\nu = u^\\mu p_\\mu$ is the photon frequency in the local rest frame of the matter, which has four-velocity $u^\\mu$. The derivative of the null vector is given by:\n$$\nn^\\mu_{ ,\\nu}=-n^\\mu n^\\rho u_{\\rho,\\nu}\n$$\nThe expansion coefficients $f_{(m)}$ have the general form:\n$$\nf_{(m)} = \\sum_{k=m}^{2m} (-1)^k {\\cal F}^{(mk)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_k} n^{\\mu_1}\\cdots n^{\\mu_k}\n$$\nwhere the quantities ${\\cal F}$ are functionals of ${\\cal S}$.\n\nThe frequency-integrated specific intensity is defined as:\n$$\nI=\\int_0^{\\infty}\\tilde\\nu^3 f d\\tilde\\nu\n$$\nIts expansion in powers of $\\epsilon$ is:\n$$\nI = \\sum_{m=0}^\\infty \\epsilon^m I_{(m)}\n$$\nwhere the coefficients are given by introducing the expansion for $f$:\n$$\nI_{(m)}=\\sum_{k=m}^{2m}(-1)^k Y_{\\mu_1\\cdots \\mu_k}^{(m,k)} n^{\\mu_1}\\cdots n^{\\mu_k}\n$$\nwith the tensors $Y$ defined as:\n$$\nY_{\\mu_1\\cdots \\mu_k}^{(m,k)}=\\int\\tilde{\\nu}^3 {\\cal F}_{\\mu_1\\cdots \\mu_k}^{(mk)} d\\tilde{\\nu}\n$$\nThe following conventions are adopted:\n$$\n{\\cal F}^{(mk)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_k} = 0 \\quad \\text{for } k<m \\text{ and } k>2m.\n$$\n$$\nY^{(m,k)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_k} = 0 \\quad \\text{for } k<m \\text{ and } k>2m.\n$$\nMoreover, for either $m<0$ or $k<0$, we define $Y^{(m,k)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_k}$ to be zero.\n## Theorem\nThe coefficients $Y^{(m+1,k)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_k}$ in the expansion of the frequency-integrated intensity can be calculated from the coefficients of the previous order, $Y^{(m,k')}$, via the following recursion formula:\n$$\nY^{(m+1,k)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_k} = Y^{(m,k-1)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_{k-1},\\mu_k} + (k+2) Y^{(m,k-2)}_{\\mu_1 \\cdots \\mu_{k-2}}u_{\\mu_{k-1},\\mu_k}\n$$\nThe sequence is built up from the zeroth order term, which is the integrated source function:\n$$\nY^{(0,0)} = S = \\int^\\infty_0\\tilde\\nu^3{\\cal S}(\\tilde\\nu,T)d\\tilde\\nu\n$$", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: [X] Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading; [X] Multiple approaches considered (preparatory identities, recursion for F, then Y); [X] Honest completion - no admission of inability needed as proof succeeds; [X] Cleaned up final proof structure with clear steps. No unrequested content added, format matches requirement exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Homogeneity claim (eq. hom): $\\tilde\\nu\\partial_{\\tilde\\nu}\\mathcal{F}^{(mk)} = -(k+2)\\mathcal{F}^{(mk)}$ follows from dimensional analysis and