2026-03-25 07:36:47,784 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_7662', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$ be the theory of parametric bit-vectors and $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$ be the theory of uninterpreted functions and integer arithmetic. A parametric bit-vector formula $\\varphi$ is a formula over a signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ containing parametric bit-vector variables (from a set $X^\\ast$) and parametric bit-vector constants (from a set $Z^\\ast$).\n\nA mapping $\\omega = (\\omega^b, \\omega^N)$ assigns to each symbol $\\mathsf{s} \\in X^\\ast \\cup Z^\\ast$ a bit-width term $\\omega^b(\\mathsf{s})$ and for $\\mathsf{s} \\in Z^\\ast$ an integer value term $\\omega^N(\\mathsf{s})$. The formula $\\varphi$ is well-sorted under $\\omega$ if for every term, its bit-width is well-defined.\n\nWe define a translation function $\\mathcal{T}_A$ from parametric bit-vector formulas to formulas in $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$, parameterized by an axiomatization mode $A$. The translation is defined as:\n$\\mathcal{T}_A(\\varphi, \\omega) \\equiv \\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega) \\implies Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$\n\nThe auxiliary function $Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$ converts parametric bit-vector expressions into integer expressions with uninterpreted functions:\n- Parametric bit-vector variables $\\mathsf{x} \\in X^\\ast$ are replaced by unique integer variables $\\chi(\\mathsf{x})$.\n- Parametric bit-vector constants $\\mathsf{z} \\in Z^\\ast$ are replaced by the term $\\omega^N (\\mathsf{z} ) \\mod \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}} (\\omega^b (\\mathsf{z} ))$.\n- An operator $\\bvsymbolf{f}$ from the signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ is replaced by a corresponding uninterpreted function ${f}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}$. For example, $\\bvsymbolf{+}$ with sort $\\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\times \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\to \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n}$ is replaced by $\\natsymbol{+}$ of sort $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\to \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}}$, where the first argument is the bit-width.\n- Most of these uninterpreted functions are then eliminated by replacing them with equivalent expressions in integer arithmetic. For example, ${+}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}(k,x,y)$ is replaced by $(x+y) \\mod \\mathsf{pow2}(k)$.\n- After elimination, the only remaining uninterpreted functions are $\\{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}, \\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}\\}$.\n\nThe formula $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ provides axioms for these functions. For the `full` axiomatization mode ($A=\\mathrm{full}$), $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {\\mathrm{full}}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ is the conjunction of:\n1.  Range constraints for variables: For each parametric bit-vector variable $\\mathsf{x}$ in $\\varphi$, the axiom $0 \\le \\chi(\\mathsf{x}) < \\mathsf{pow2}(\\omega^b(\\mathsf{x}))$ is added.\n2.  Positivity of bit-widths: For each free variable $v$ in $\\omega$, the axiom $v > 0$ is added.\n3.  Axioms for the uninterpreted functions, as defined in Table 2 from the paper.\n\n**Full Axiomatization from Table 2:**\nLet $i, j, k$ be bit-widths and $x, y$ be integers. We use $\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{i}{x}$ as shorthand for $(x \\div \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)} {i}) \\mod 2$, where $\\twotothef(k)$ is an uninterpreted function for $2^k$.\n\n- **Axioms for $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\mathsf{pow2}}}$):**\n  - $\\forall k. k \\le 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 1$\n  - $\\forall k. k > 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 2 \\cdot \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise AND, $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\&}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intandf(1, x, y) = \\min(x, y)$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intandf(k, x, y) = \\intandf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\min(\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x}, \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y})$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise XOR, $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\oplus}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intxorf(1, x, y) = \\lvert x-y \\rvert$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intxorf(k, x, y) = \\intxorf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\lvert \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x} - \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y} \\rvert$\n\nThe axiomatization of $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}$ is omitted and is dual to that of $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$.\n\n**Notation from Appendix:**\n- Let $\\mathcal{J}$ be a mapping of the variables in $\\vec{v}=\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{FV}\\kern-.15em(#1)} {\\omega}$ to positive integers.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\fsbvtheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$.\n- For a bit-vector constant $a$, let ${\\brackets{a}}_{\\mathbb{N}}$ denote its value as an integer.\n- For a parametric $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$-term $t$, let $t|_{\\omega, \\mathcal{J}}$ denote the concrete bit-vector term obtained by instantiating the parameters in $t$ according to $\\omega$ and $\\mathcal{J}$.\n\nWe use $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ as shorthand for $\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\varphi, \\omega)$.\n## Theorem\nLet $\\varphi$ be a parameteric bit-vector formula that is well sorted under $\\omega$ and has no occurrences of bit-vector extract or concrete bit-vector constants. Then: $\\varphi$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}} $-valid under $\\omega$ if and only if $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}} $-valid.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under '## Proof' heading. It attempts to prove the theorem using multiple approaches (contrapositive via satisfiability equivalence). However, it uses 'Lemma 0' without defining it in the proof, which is a minor deviation from complete self-containment. The proof structure is cleaned up as requested. No unrequested content added. Score capped at 5 due to undefined 'Lemma 0' reference."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof contains unverifiable claims: 'Lemma 0 (proved by induction on k...)' is cited but not provided, making the inductive argument uncheckable. The claim that 'Lemma 0' gives specific semantic interpretations for pow2, intandf, intxorf is presented as fact but lacks the actual proof. The semantic equations for bitwise operations are plausible but not derived from the given axioms in the visible proof. No false claims detected, but multiple unverifiable claims (Lemma 0's content, the inductive proof) cap score at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof correctly addresses the theorem: equivalence of T_fsbv-validity and T_ufia-validity for the translation. Both directions of the equivalence are covered (bit-vectors to integers, integers to bit-vectors). The restriction about no extract or concrete constants is addressed in the conclusion. The proof correctly uses the definitions of trfullf, AX_full, and Conv. Minor issue: the handling of OR (|) is mentioned as 'dual' but not explicitly verified, though this is acceptable given the prompt's statement. Score 5 as the core argument is correct and complete."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear section structure. Uses standard proof notation and formatting. Minor issues: 'Lemma 0' is undefined, which is a clarity gap; the LaTeX formatting has some awkwardness (e.g., '\\T_{\\!{\\rm fsbv}}' with excessive escaping). The flow is logical and readable. One minor redundancy: the conclusion restates what was already proven. Overall high quality with one minor clarity issue."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof is concise and well-calibrated. Each section serves a purpose: semantics setup, forward direction, backward direction, conclusion. Nothing appears extraneous. The restriction discussion at the end is brief and relevant. Could potentially include slightly more detail on the 'Lemma 0' proof, but the current length is appropriate for a cleaned-up proof. Score 5 as length is near-ideal."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response provides a proof under '## Proof' heading and attempts multiple approaches. However, it has significant formatting issues: 'Wewill', 'Thus, the theoremreduces', 'Thesetup', 'Weconstruct' show missing spaces throughout. The 'Necessity of Restrictions' section adds explanatory content not strictly required by the proof structure, though relevant. The proof is complete but the formatting errors and minor structural deviations cap score at 4."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Multiple questionable claims: (1) 'The axioms generated by AX_full only depend on the signature and bit-widths of terms, so AX_full(φ,ω) = AX_full(ψ,ω)' - this is asserted without proof and is non-obvious since φ and ψ may have different variables/constants. (2) '¬Conv(φ,ω) is logically equivalent to Conv(¬φ,ω)' - this is false; Conv is not necessarily homomorphism for negation, it's a term translation. The claim that 'intandf(1,x,y)=min(x,y)' works for 'x,y ∈ {0,1}' is verified, but the general claim about equivalence is unsupported. These errors significantly impact reliability."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "The proof attempts both directions but contains critical errors. The claim that ¬Conv(φ,ω) ≡ Conv(¬φ,ω) is incorrect - Conv translates terms to integer expressions, and negation of the formula is not preserved by term-level translation. This invalidates the core proof strategy. The verification of axioms is partially correct but relies on the incorrect equivalence. The 'Necessity of Restrictions' section is correct about extract and concrete constants. The fundamental logical error in the proof strategy significantly lowers correctness."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Multiple formatting errors: missing spaces ('Wewill', 'theoremreduces', 'Thesetup', 'Weconstruct', 'T_ufia-satisfiabilitySuppose', 'T_fsbv-satisfiabilitySuppose', 'Because I^nat', 'uniquely determined for valid parameterized widths:-pow2'). Inconsistent bold formatting. The prose is often awkward ('seamlessly identical', 'strictly behaves', 'terminates smoothly'). Paragraph breaks are sometimes missing. The structure is present but execution has significant quality issues."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof is somewhat longer than necessary. The 'Necessity of Restrictions' section, while relevant, could be more concise. Some verification sections repeat points (e.g., pow2 verification appears in both directions). The explanation of why extract doesn't work is verbose. However, no major sections are completely extraneous. The length is acceptable but has identifiable padding."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 3.4, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 3.40'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better. Both proofs establish the same biconditional via contrapositive/equivalent satisfiability formulation, but A is significantly more concise and mathematically elegant, using a single Lemma 0 and clean equation (eq:term) for the term correspondence, while B is verbose with redundant explanations ('strictly', 'seamlessly', 'natively') and buries the core induction argument under excessive commentary. A's structure with clear 'From bit-vectors to integers' and 'From integers to bit-vectors' sections is superior to B's over-detailed verification subsections that restate obvious facts (e.g., '2^k = 2 · 2^{k-1} natively holds')."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': None, 'comparison_comment': ''}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) demonstrates superior concision and mathematical elegance while maintaining complete correctness; GPT 5.2 is more verbose with unnecessary structural overhead. The SxS winner establishes the core equivalence (*) immediately, then proves both directions through a unified term-level correspondence (equation \\eqref{eq:term}) that handles all operators simultaneously, whereas GPT 5.2 proliferates lemmas (Lemma 1, 2, 3) with redundant base-case/recursive-case analysis that the SxS winner subsumes in 'Lemma 0'. The SxS winner's 'Semantics of the axioms' paragraph achieves in 4 lines what GPT 5.2 takes 30+ lines across Lemma 1 and Lemma 2. However, GPT 5.2 provides more explicit justification for the restrictions (concrete constants, extract) in its final section, which the SxS winner only briefly mentions; this is GPT 5.2's sole advantage. The SxS winner's bidirectional proof structure—using the same equation (\\ref{eq:term}) for both directions with 'roles exchanged'—is mathematically cleaner than GPT 5.2's separated (⇒) and (⇐) sections with duplicated logic. Both proofs are truthfully correct, but the SxS winner's writing style achieves research-paper quality concision while GPT 5.2 reads like expanded lecture notes with excessive signposting ('Proof strategy', 'Proof sketch', 'Why the restrictions are needed')."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit restriction justification', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated section explicitly explaining why the restrictions (no extract, no concrete constants) are necessary for the theorem to hold, with specific technical reasoning for each restriction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = brief mention, 3 = explains one restriction, 5-6 = detailed technical justification for both restrictions', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Structured proof organization with clear directional headings', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses explicit hierarchical headings (### Direction 1, ### Direction 2) to demarcate the two directions of the if-and-only-if proof, making the proof structure immediately scannable.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no headings, 3 = some structural markers, 5-6 = clear numbered/labelled directional sections', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Detailed verification subsections per direction', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes explicit 'Verification' subsections within each direction that systematically check axioms, range constraints, and function definitions against the constructed interpretation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no verification details, 3 = some checking mentioned inline, 5-6 = dedicated structured verification with itemized checks', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Base case and recursive case analysis for function axioms', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly analyzes both base cases (k=1) and recursive cases (k>1) when verifying that the axiomatized functions match standard bitwise operations.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no case analysis, 3 = mentions cases briefly, 5-6 = explicit base/recursive case structure with technical details', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit termination argument for recursive definitions', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that recursive definitions terminate because k decreases by 1 per step until reaching k=1.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no termination argument, 3 = implicit in structure, 5-6 = explicit termination claim with justification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Construction steps enumerated with numbered lists', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses numbered lists (1, 2, 3) to enumerate the construction steps for building interpretations in each direction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = prose construction, 3 = some list usage, 5-6 = consistent numbered enumeration for interpretant construction', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit mention of standard integer interpretation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that satisfiability in T_ufia is evaluated assuming the standard interpretation of integer arithmetic (domain is standard integers Z).', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no mention, 3 = implicit assumption, 5-6 = explicit statement of standard interpretation', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit lemma statements with formal hypotheses', 'description': "GPT 5.2 states Lemma 1, Lemma 2, and Lemma 3 with explicit formal hypotheses and conclusions, whereas the SxS winner uses informal 'Lemma 0' without stating its hypotheses or conclusions explicitly.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = marginally more formal, 3-4 = noticeably more structured with clear hypotheses, 5-6 = substantially more rigorous with fully formalized lemma statements', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Complete base case and inductive step for Lemma 1', 'description': "GPT 5.2 provides a complete mathematical induction proof for Lemma 1 (pow2 correctness) with explicit base case (k=1) and inductive s