2026-03-25 07:36:55,039 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_7662', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$ be the theory of parametric bit-vectors and $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$ be the theory of uninterpreted functions and integer arithmetic. A parametric bit-vector formula $\\varphi$ is a formula over a signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ containing parametric bit-vector variables (from a set $X^\\ast$) and parametric bit-vector constants (from a set $Z^\\ast$).\n\nA mapping $\\omega = (\\omega^b, \\omega^N)$ assigns to each symbol $\\mathsf{s} \\in X^\\ast \\cup Z^\\ast$ a bit-width term $\\omega^b(\\mathsf{s})$ and for $\\mathsf{s} \\in Z^\\ast$ an integer value term $\\omega^N(\\mathsf{s})$. The formula $\\varphi$ is well-sorted under $\\omega$ if for every term, its bit-width is well-defined.\n\nWe define a translation function $\\mathcal{T}_A$ from parametric bit-vector formulas to formulas in $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$, parameterized by an axiomatization mode $A$. The translation is defined as:\n$\\mathcal{T}_A(\\varphi, \\omega) \\equiv \\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega) \\implies Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$\n\nThe auxiliary function $Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$ converts parametric bit-vector expressions into integer expressions with uninterpreted functions:\n- Parametric bit-vector variables $\\mathsf{x} \\in X^\\ast$ are replaced by unique integer variables $\\chi(\\mathsf{x})$.\n- Parametric bit-vector constants $\\mathsf{z} \\in Z^\\ast$ are replaced by the term $\\omega^N (\\mathsf{z} ) \\mod \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}} (\\omega^b (\\mathsf{z} ))$.\n- An operator $\\bvsymbolf{f}$ from the signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ is replaced by a corresponding uninterpreted function ${f}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}$. For example, $\\bvsymbolf{+}$ with sort $\\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\times \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\to \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n}$ is replaced by $\\natsymbol{+}$ of sort $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\to \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}}$, where the first argument is the bit-width.\n- Most of these uninterpreted functions are then eliminated by replacing them with equivalent expressions in integer arithmetic. For example, ${+}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}(k,x,y)$ is replaced by $(x+y) \\mod \\mathsf{pow2}(k)$.\n- After elimination, the only remaining uninterpreted functions are $\\{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}, \\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}\\}$.\n\nThe formula $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ provides axioms for these functions. For the `full` axiomatization mode ($A=\\mathrm{full}$), $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {\\mathrm{full}}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ is the conjunction of:\n1.  Range constraints for variables: For each parametric bit-vector variable $\\mathsf{x}$ in $\\varphi$, the axiom $0 \\le \\chi(\\mathsf{x}) < \\mathsf{pow2}(\\omega^b(\\mathsf{x}))$ is added.\n2.  Positivity of bit-widths: For each free variable $v$ in $\\omega$, the axiom $v > 0$ is added.\n3.  Axioms for the uninterpreted functions, as defined in Table 2 from the paper.\n\n**Full Axiomatization from Table 2:**\nLet $i, j, k$ be bit-widths and $x, y$ be integers. We use $\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{i}{x}$ as shorthand for $(x \\div \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)} {i}) \\mod 2$, where $\\twotothef(k)$ is an uninterpreted function for $2^k$.\n\n- **Axioms for $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\mathsf{pow2}}}$):**\n  - $\\forall k. k \\le 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 1$\n  - $\\forall k. k > 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 2 \\cdot \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise AND, $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\&}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intandf(1, x, y) = \\min(x, y)$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intandf(k, x, y) = \\intandf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\min(\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x}, \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y})$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise XOR, $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\oplus}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intxorf(1, x, y) = \\lvert x-y \\rvert$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intxorf(k, x, y) = \\intxorf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\lvert \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x} - \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y} \\rvert$\n\nThe axiomatization of $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}$ is omitted and is dual to that of $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$.\n\n**Notation from Appendix:**\n- Let $\\mathcal{J}$ be a mapping of the variables in $\\vec{v}=\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{FV}\\kern-.15em(#1)} {\\omega}$ to positive integers.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\fsbvtheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$.\n- For a bit-vector constant $a$, let ${\\brackets{a}}_{\\mathbb{N}}$ denote its value as an integer.\n- For a parametric $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$-term $t$, let $t|_{\\omega, \\mathcal{J}}$ denote the concrete bit-vector term obtained by instantiating the parameters in $t$ according to $\\omega$ and $\\mathcal{J}$.\n\nWe use $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ as shorthand for $\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\varphi, \\omega)$.\n## Theorem\nLet $\\varphi$ be a parameteric bit-vector formula that is well sorted under $\\omega$ and has no occurrences of bit-vector extract or concrete bit-vector constants. Then: $\\varphi$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}} $-valid under $\\omega$ if and only if $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}} $-valid.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under '## Proof' heading with both directions of the equivalence. It includes the required proof strategy, lemmas, and detailed verification for both directions. Minor issue: uses '## Proof Strategy' and '###' subheadings rather than a single '## Proof' heading as literally requested, but this is a trivial formatting variance that doesn't impede readability. All required elements (setup understanding, bidirectional proof, explanation of restrictions) are present."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof contains several unverifiable technical claims stated as fact without hedging. Specifically: (1) Lemma 1 claims uniqueness of axiomatized functions with 'we have for every positive integer k' — this is a strong uniqueness claim that requires proof of that no other models exist, which is not established; the response only shows by induction that the standard interpretation satisfies the axioms, not that it's the unique model. (2) The claim that 'termination is guaranteed' for the recursive definitions is stated without justification — while k decreases, this doesn't automatically guarantee termination in all models of T_ufia. (3) The duality claim for OR axiomatization ('omitted and is dual to that of AND') is mentioned but not verified. These are substantive gaps in a formal proof context, capping the score at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof correctly identifies the theorem statement and attempts both directions. However, there are correctness issues: (1) The proof of Lemma 1 only shows that the standard interpretation satisfies the axioms, not that the axioms uniquely characterize the functions as claimed — this is a significant logical gap for a soundness/completeness proof. (2) The handling of 'hextractf' is problematic: the proof claims extract is 'not axiomatized' as the reason for excluding it, but hextractf actually appears in the AND/XOR axioms (in the recursive clauses), so the explanation is misleading. (3) The proof doesn't explicitly verify that all eliminated operators (like +, -) are correctly handled — it asserts Lemma 2 without proving it. These partial correctness issues warrant a 4."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is clear, professional, and well-structured with explicit section headers. Mathematical notation is consistent with the prompt. Minor issues: (1) Some sentences are slightly dense (e.g., 'Since each step strictly decreases k, termination is guaranteed' — this is vague about what 'termination' means in a model-theoretic context). (2) The use of 'fsbv' and 'ufia' subscripts in LaTeX is inconsistent with the prompt's 'T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}' formatting. These are trivial style preferences; the response is publication-ready with only minor polish possible."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The length is appropriate for a formal proof of this complexity. Nothing can be cut: the lemmas are essential, both directions require their full construction, and the restriction explanation is necessary. Nothing is missing: the proof includes strategy, detailed constructions, verification, and explanation of restrictions. Minor observation: the 'Why the restrictions are needed' section could be slightly more detailed about why concrete constants specifically break the construction (the current explanation is somewhat brief), but this is not a significant gap."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.55, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.55'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the instruction with a clear '## Proof' heading and complete bidirectional proof. It includes both directions, explicit construction of interpretations, and explanation of restrictions. Minor issue: uses 'Proof strategy' as a subheading and '---' separators rather than clean '## Proof' followed by content, but this is a trivial formatting variance. All required elements are present and properly organized."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Similar to Response A, there are unverifiable claims: (1) The induction proofs for AND and XOR claim correctness but don't rigorously establish that the axioms uniquely determine these functions — the response shows the standard interpretation satisfies the axioms but doesn't prove no other interpretations exist. (2) The claim 'termination follows because k decreases and positivity ensures k>0' is imprecise — decreasing alone doesn't guarantee termination in non-standard models of arithmetic. (3) The statement 'the bitwise axioms hold because the concrete definitions satisfy the base and recursive clauses' assumes what needs to be shown (that satisfying the recursive clauses implies being the bitwise operation). These are substantive gaps in a formal proof, capping at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof correctly identifies the theorem and provides both directions. Issues: (1) The induction for AND/XOR shows that the standard interpretation satisfies the axioms, but doesn't prove that any model of the axioms must coincide with the standard interpretation — this is crucial for the 'if and only if' claim. (2) The handling of eliminated operators (like +, -) is hand-waved: 'Conv replaces fⁿᵃᵗ(k,x,y) by the corresponding arithmetic expression...which matches the semantics' — this needs explicit verification that modular arithmetic in T_ufia corresponds to bit-vector arithmetic. (3) The restriction explanation for extract is clearer than Response A (noting that extract would require additional axioms), though it still understates that hextractf already appears in the axioms. These partial correctness issues warrant a 4."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear, professional writing with good use of structural markers. The response uses consistent notation and explains the induction steps explicitly. Minor issues: (1) Some notation mixing (e.g., 'fⁿᵃᵗ' for superscript 'nattheory') is slightly informal. (2) The phrase 'the same inductive argument as above, now in the forward direction' is slightly vague about what 'forward direction' means. These are trivial; the writing is professional quality with only minor polish possible."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length is well-calibrated. Nothing can be cut: the explicit induction proofs for AND/XOR are necessary for a complete proof, both directions need their full construction, and the restriction explanation is appropriately detailed. Nothing is missing: all operators are covered, both directions are complete, and restrictions are explained. The explicit base and recursive cases for the induction add appropriate length without padding.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.55, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.55'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better due to clearer structure and more rigorous handling of key technical details. Response A explicitly states and proves Lemma 1 (uniqueness of axiomatized functions) and Lemma 2 (correspondence of Conv), providing a cleaner separation of concerns. Response B embeds similar reasoning inline but omits formal lemma statements, making the proof harder to follow. Response A also correctly notes that extract uses uninterpreted function hextractf which lacks axioms, while Response B incorrectly states Conv 'does not handle' extract—actually Conv does handle extract via hextractf, but the issue is the lack of axioms for it. Response B's handling of bitwise AND/XOR induction is more detailed but buries the key insight about termination and well-definedness that Response A elevates to lemma status."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': None, 'comparison_comment': ''}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) provides a cleaner, more focused proof with better logical flow, while GPT 5.2 is more verbose with redundant explanations. Both models correctly prove the theorem with proper bidirectional structure and necessary lemmas. SxS winner's Lemma 1 elegantly combines all three function correctness claims (pow2, AND, XOR) with a unified induction, whereas GPT 5.2 separates Lemma 1 (pow2 only) from Lemma 2 (AND/XOR), creating unnecessary fragmentation. SxS winner's construction in the (⇒) direction explicitly builds the fsbv interpretation from the ufia model with clear verification steps, while GPT 5.2's corresponding section buries the construction inside a longer narrative. However, GPT 5.2 provides more explicit detail in Lemma 3's term-level correspondence with structural induction cases, and its contrapositive approach for (⇐) is technically sound though less direct than SxS winner's constructive approach. SxS winner's explanation of restrictions is more concise and precise, noting that extract would break Lemma 2's correspondence and concrete constants would fail range constraints, while GPT 5.2's restriction explanation is slightly muddled regarding concrete constants (the issue is their fixed width, not modulo reduction per se). Both handle the core mathematical content correctly, but SxS winner's superior organization and conciseness give it a modest edge."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Lemma 1 explicit pow2 proof', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a complete, explicit proof of Lemma 1 showing that pow2(k) = 2^k for all k under the full axioms, with clear base case and inductive step using the axioms directly.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = brief mention of pow2 behavior, 3-4 = partial proof with gaps, 5-6 = complete rigorous proof with explicit base case and induction', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Lemma 2 detailed AND/XOR correctness', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a detailed, step-by-step proof of Lemma 2 showing that the axiomatized AND and XOR functions match standard k-bit bitwise operations, including explicit handling of lower bits, top bits, and reassembly.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = states correctness without proof, 3-4 = outlines structure but skips key steps, 5-6 = complete proof with bit-level decomposition and induction', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Lemma 3 term-level correspondence statement', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly states Lemma 3 as a formal lemma with precise conditions about term-level correspondence between Conv(t,ω) and instantiated bit-vector terms, including the structural induction proof sketch.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = no explicit lemma, just informal claim, 3-4 = mentions correspondence without formal statement, 5-6 = fully formalized lemma with induction structure', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Explicit contrapositive for reverse direction', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly uses proof by contrapositive for the (⇐) direction, clearly stating the assumption of non-validity and constructing a countermodel, rather than direct model construction.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = direct proof without strategic clarity, 3-4 = mentions contrapositive informally, 5-6 = explicit contrapositive structure with clear falsifying instance', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Concrete countermodel construction', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs a concrete T_ufia interpretation with standard integer operations as the countermodel, defining pow2(k)=2^max(k,0) and standard k-bit operations for &, |, ⊕.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = abstract claim of countermodel existence, 3-4 = partial construction, 5-6 = fully explicit construction with all function interpretations specified', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Detailed restriction justification for extract', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a detailed technical explanation of why extract is excluded, specifically noting that multi-bit slices require additional translation machinery and axioms beyond the given Conv elimination and Table-2 axioms.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = states extract is excluded without reason, 3-4 = vague reference to missing axioms, 5-6 = precise explanation about multi-bit slices and reassembly machinery', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Detailed restriction justification for concrete constants', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a specific technical explanation of why concrete bit-vector constants are excluded, noting that SMT-style numerals denote values reduced modulo 2^n and that Conv would need explicit modulo reduction for literals (unlike parametric constants).', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = states concrete constants excluded without reason, 3-4 = vague reference to width issues, 5-6 = precise explanation about modulo reduction and literal semantics', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit valuation definitions in both directions', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly defines the valuations α and β in both proof directions with precise domain/codomain specifications and construction rules for each variable type.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = implicit or missing valuations, 3-4 = mentions valuations without full specification, 5-6 = complete explicit definitions for all variable types in both directions', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Use of standard integer structure', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly references and uses 'the standard integer structure' where remaining uninterpreted functions are interpreted as standard k-bit operations, providing semantic grounding for the correspondence.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = no mention of standard structure, 3-4 = implicit use without naming, 5-6 = explicit naming and use of standard integer structure with standard operations', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Termination argument for recursive axioms', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly states the termination condition for recursive axioms, noting that each recursive use decreases k to k-1, guaranteeing finite steps to base case.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = no termination argument, 3-4 = implicit understanding, 5-6 = explicit termination argument with decreasing measure', 'rating': 5}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 44537, 'output_tokens': 4789, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.146542}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.146542}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 44537, 'output_tokens': 4789, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.146542}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.146542}}] 
