2026-03-25 07:37:09,213 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_14482', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nConsider an infinite horizon Markov decision process (MDP) with a finite set of states denoted by $S$ of size $n$, a finite set of actions $A(i)$ for each state $i \\in S$, an immediate reward $r(i,a)$ for each $i \\in S$ and $a \\in A(i)$, and a transition probability $p_{ij}(a)$ for each $i,j \\in S$ and $a\\in A(i)$.\n\nWe consider the approach from Bertsekas [1,2] which connects the average cost MDP to a corresponding stochastic shortest path problem (denoted $\\lambda$-SSP). We assume that there is a state, denoted by $n$, which is a recurrent state under any stationary policy. The $\\lambda$-SSP problem is defined on an augmented state space $S \\cup \\{t\\}$ where $t$ is a new terminating state. The costs are $r(i,a) - \\lambda$ for a scalar parameter $\\lambda$, and the value iteration for solving the $\\lambda$-SSP problem for a fixed $\\lambda = \\lambda^k$ is given by:\n$$h^{m+1}(i)=\\min_{a \\in A(i)}\\left \\{ r(i,a)-\\lambda^k + \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h^m(j) \\right\\} \\text{ for all } i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\nThe component corresponding to state $n$ is ignored in the summation because in the $\\lambda$-SSP formulation, transitions into state $n$ are redirected to the new terminal state $t$. We consider the value function $h$ as a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nLet $T_{\\lambda^k}$ be the corresponding Markovian operator:\n$$T_{\\lambda^k}h(i)=\\min_{a \\in A(i)}\\left \\{ r(i,a)-\\lambda^k + \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j) \\right\\}$$\n\nLet the set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ be determined by the system of inequalities shown below:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}= \\left \\{h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h(i)-\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j)\\le r(i,a)-\\lambda^k, \\forall i \\in S, \\forall a\\in A(i) \\right\\},$$\nwhich is equivalent to $H_{\\lambda^k} = \\{ h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h \\le T_{\\lambda^k} h \\}$.\n\nThe Gauss-Seidel variant of the operator, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$, is defined by its application $h^{k+1}=T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h^k}$ where:\n$$h^{k+1}(i)=\\min_{a\\in{A(i)}}\\left\\{r(i,a)-\\lambda^k+\\sum_{j<i} p_{ij}(a) h^{k+1}(j)+ \\sum_{j \\geq i}^{n-1} p_{ij}(a) h^k(j) \\right\\}, \\forall i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\n\nLet the set $H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$ be defined as:\n$$H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}=\\{h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\mid h\\le T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h}\\}.$$\n\nThe following lemmas are required to prove the main theorem.\n\n**Lemma 19 (Monotonicity)**\n1. If $h \\ge g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h \\ge T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h \\ge T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n2. If $h > g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h > T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h > T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n\n**Lemma 15**\nThe following relation holds:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}\\subset{H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}}.$$\n\n**Lemma 17**\n$$T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}( H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}.$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 18**\nThe set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ is invariant under $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$. That is, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}(H_{\\lambda^k}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All instruction elements checked: [X] Complete and correct proof provided; [X] Multiple approaches considered (outline + detailed proof); [X] Cleaned up proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Honest verification section added; [X] Used all provided lemmas (15, 17, 19 mentioned in strategy); [X] Mathematical notation preserved exactly. No unrequested content added. Fault considered: Could have been more explicit about Lemma 19 usage, but the proof strategy mentions it and the core proof doesn't strictly require it given Lemmas 15+17 suffice."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All claims verified: [VERIFIED] Lemma 15 gives H_{λ^k} ⊂ H_{GS_{λ^k}} — correctly cited; [VERIFIED] Lemma 17 gives T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{GS_{λ^k}}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} — correctly cited; [VERIFIED] The logical chain h ∈ H_{λ^k} → h ∈ H_{GS_{λ^k}} → T_{GS_{λ^k}}h ∈ H_{λ^k} — valid deduction; [VERIFIED] Clarification that only subset (not equality) is claimed — accurate. One minor issue: The proof strategy mentions Lemma 19 but the main proof doesn't use it; this is not false but slightly imprecise in the outline. No false claims detected."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Theorem 18 statement correctly understood as T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{λ^k}) ⊂ H_{λ^k}; [CORRECT] Proof establishes invariance via subset relation; [CORRECT] All mathematical objects (T_{λ^k}, T_{GS_{λ^k}}, H_{λ^k}, H_{GS_{λ^k}}) used consistently with definitions; [CORRECT] State n handling and summation limits respected; [CORRECT] No logical gaps — the proof is complete. The verification section explicitly addresses potential confusion about subset vs. equality, showing deep understanding.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clear structure with numbered steps; professional mathematical tone; proper LaTeX formatting. Minor issue identified: The phrase 'we never claim or need that every element of H_{λ^k} is attainable as T_{GS_{λ^k}}h (which would be equality)' in the verification section is slightly awkward — 'attainable' is not the standard term (would prefer 'is the image of'). Also, the verification section, while valuable, slightly repeats what was already clear in the proof. These are trivial style preferences."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The response includes a proof strategy outline, the formal proof with three clear steps, and a verification section. Ideal length for a ma