alities shown below:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}= \\left \\{h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h(i)-\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j)\\le r(i,a)-\\lambda^k, \\forall i \\in S, \\forall a\\in A(i) \\right\\},$$\nwhich is equivalent to $H_{\\lambda^k} = \\{ h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h \\le T_{\\lambda^k} h \\}$.\n\nThe Gauss-Seidel variant of the operator, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$, is defined by its application $h^{k+1}=T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h^k}$ where:\n$$h^{k+1}(i)=\\min_{a\\in{A(i)}}\\left\\{r(i,a)-\\lambda^k+\\sum_{j<i} p_{ij}(a) h^{k+1}(j)+ \\sum_{j \\geq i}^{n-1} p_{ij}(a) h^k(j) \\right\\}, \\forall i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\n\nLet the set $H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$ be defined as:\n$$H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}=\\{h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\mid h\\le T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h}\\}.$$\n\nThe following lemmas are required to prove the main theorem.\n\n**Lemma 19 (Monotonicity)**\n1. If $h \\ge g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h \\ge T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h \\ge T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n2. If $h > g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h > T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h > T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n\n**Lemma 15**\nThe following relation holds:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}\\subset{H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}}.$$\n\n**Lemma 17**\n$$T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}( H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}.$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 18**\nThe set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ is invariant under $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$. That is, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}(H_{\\lambda^k}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading [X], (2) All required lemmas (19, 15, 17) proved before main theorem [X], (3) Theorem 18 proved as requested [X], (4) Honest completion - no admission of inability needed [X], (5) Mathematical notation preserved and used correctly [X]. No unrequested content added. The response follows the exact structure implied by the prompt: proving the lemmas then the theorem.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major mathematical claims verified correct. The monotonicity proof for strict inequality uses the recurrent state assumption correctly: 'The set {j:h(j)=g(j)} would then be closed under a stationary policy, contradicting the assumption that n is recurrent under any policy.' This is a valid argument. Lemma 15's induction is correctly structured. Lemma 17's inequality chain: 'Since h≤y, the right–hand side is ≤ r(i,a_i)-λ+∑_j p_ij(a_i)y(j)' is valid because replacing h(j) with larger y(j) for j≥i and with y(j) for j<i (where y(j)≥h(j) by construction) gives an upper bound. One minor issue: the proof claims 'base i=1 is the T_λ case' for T_GS monotonicity, but strictly speaking i=1 for T_GS uses only h(j) terms (no y(j) terms since j<1 is empty), so it's identical to T_λ, which is correct. No false claims found."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All prompt requirements correctly addressed: (1) Theorem 18 (invariance of H_λ^k under T_GS_λ^k) proved correctly [CORRECT], (2) Lemma 19 (Monotonicity) proved with both weak and strict versions [CORRECT], (3) Lemma 15 (H_λ ⊂ H_GS) proved by induction [CORRECT], (4) Lemma 17 (T_GS(H_GS) ⊂ H_λ) proved correctly [CORRECT]. The proof structure matches the required logical dependencies: Lemma 15 + Lemma 17 → Theorem 18. Mathematical details align with Bertsekas's framework (state n ignored in sums, recurrent state assumption used)."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear structure. Strengths: (1) Logical flow with explicit lemma/theorem headings, (2) Proper use of LaTeX formatting, (3) Clear induction structure in Lemma 15, (4) Explicit variable definitions. Minor issue: The phrase 'the right–hand side is ≤' in Lemma 17 could be more explicit about which terms are being bounded; the reader must infer that h(j)≤y(j) for j≥i is used. Also, '∑_j' in Lemma 17 should technically be '∑_{j=1}^{n-1}' for consistency