ualities shown below:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}= \\left \\{h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h(i)-\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}p_{ij}(a)h(j)\\le r(i,a)-\\lambda^k, \\forall i \\in S, \\forall a\\in A(i) \\right\\},$$\nwhich is equivalent to $H_{\\lambda^k} = \\{ h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid h \\le T_{\\lambda^k} h \\}$.\n\nThe Gauss-Seidel variant of the operator, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$, is defined by its application $h^{k+1}=T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h^k}$ where:\n$$h^{k+1}(i)=\\min_{a\\in{A(i)}}\\left\\{r(i,a)-\\lambda^k+\\sum_{j<i} p_{ij}(a) h^{k+1}(j)+ \\sum_{j \\geq i}^{n-1} p_{ij}(a) h^k(j) \\right\\}, \\forall i\\in \\overline{1,n}.$$\n\nLet the set $H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$ be defined as:\n$$H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}=\\{h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\mid h\\le T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}{h}\\}.$$\n\nThe following lemmas are required to prove the main theorem.\n\n**Lemma 19 (Monotonicity)**\n1. If $h \\ge g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h \\ge T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h \\ge T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n2. If $h > g$, then for any $\\lambda^k$, $T_{\\lambda^k}h > T_{\\lambda^k}g$ and $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} h > T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}} g$.\n\n**Lemma 15**\nThe following relation holds:\n$$H_{\\lambda^k}\\subset{H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}}.$$\n\n**Lemma 17**\n$$T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}( H_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}.$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 18**\nThe set $H_{\\lambda^k}$ is invariant under $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}$. That is, $T_{GS_{\\lambda^k}}(H_{\\lambda^k}) \\subset H_{\\lambda^k}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements checked: [X] Provide complete and correct proof - satisfied; [X] Think carefully with multiple approaches if stuck - the proof strategy section shows clear thinking; [X] Give cleaned up proof under ## Proof heading - satisfied with clear ## Proof section; [X] Be honest if unable to complete - not needed as proof was completed. No unrequested content added, format matches requirements. The verification section is appropriate commentary, not extraneous content.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All claims verified: [VERIFIED] Lemma 15 states H_{λ^k} ⊂ H_{GS_{λ^k}} - correctly cited and used; [VERIFIED] Lemma 17 states T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{GS_{λ^k}}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} - correctly cited and used; [VERIFIED] The composition yields T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{λ^k}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} - logically valid; [VERIFIED] The proof establishes subset relation not equality - correctly noted. No false, questionable, or unverifiable claims. Mathematical logic is sound.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Theorem 18 statement correctly understood - invariance as subset relation; [CORRECT] Proof structure follows logical requirement: h ∈ H_{λ^k} → h ∈ H_{GS_{λ^k}} (by Lemma 15) → T_{GS_{λ^k}}h ∈ H_{λ^k} (by Lemma 17); [CORRECT] Conclusion matches theorem statement exactly; [CORRECT] No incorrect claims about equality or fixed points. The proof correctly establishes T_{GS_{λ^k}}(H_{λ^k}) ⊂ H_{λ^k} using the given lemmas.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clear structure with Proof strategy, numbered steps, and Verification section. Mathematical notation is correct and consistent. Minor issue: 'The composition yields the desired invariance' in proof strategy is slightly informal compared to the formal proof that follows, but this is appropriate for a strategy section. Otherwise publication-ready with excellent flow between steps."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Nothing can be cut because each section serves distinct purpose - strategy provides intuition, proof provides rigor, verification clarifies scope. Nothing is missing because all logical steps are explicit and the verification addresses potential confusion about subset vs. equality. The response is near-perfect length; only trivial trimming possible (e.g., 'be arbitrary' could be 'arbitrary' but this is truly minor)."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.7, 'reason'