) - f(x)}{s}.\n$$\n## Theorem\nAssume that $f$ is weakly lower semismooth. Then Algorithm 2 terminates.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading '## Proof'. It includes a proof strategy section before the formal proof as requested. Minor formatting variance: the proof strategy is labeled '**Proof strategy.**' rather than exactly matching the requested format, but this is trivial. The proof is cleaned up and complete. All required elements from the setup (Algorithm 2, weak lower semismoothness definition, the theorem) are addressed. No unrequested content added."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All mathematical claims appear correct and verifiable. Key claims checked: (1) The chain rule for Clarke subdifferentials: if φ(t) = f(x₀ + tṽ) + linear term, then ∂φ(t) = ⟨∂f(x₀ + tṽ), ṽ⟩ + c̃‖ṽ‖² — this is standard and correct. (2) The bisection invariant: the proof correctly establishes φ(aⱼ) ≤ 0 < φ(bⱼ) by induction. (3) The limit behavior: aⱼ ↑ t* with bⱼ fixed at b₁ is correctly derived. (4) The weak lower semismoothness application: the inequality limsup ⟨ηⱼ, 1⟩ ≤ liminf (φ(t*+s)-φ(t*))/s is correctly applied. One minor issue: the proof claims 'the right endpoint never moves: bⱼ ≡ b₁' — this is correct given the update rule and the sign of the subgradient, but the reasoning could be more explicit about why step 4 always chooses aⱼ₊₁ = tⱼ. However, this is verifiable from the context. No false claims identified."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The theorem is correctly addressed: Algorithm 2 terminates when f is weakly lower semismooth. The proof correctly identifies the two cases (finite termination vs. infinite sequence) and derives a contradiction in the infinite case. The contradiction correctly uses weak lower semismoothness to compare the limsup of subgradient inner products with the liminf of difference qu