ase, so no fault applies."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof explicitly separates two cases with clear headings: '**Case (i): Finite termination**' and '**Case (ii): Infinite sequence generated (Proof by Contradiction)**'. Case (i) states the condition for termination. Case (ii) explicitly states 'Assume the algorithm does not terminate' and proceeds with the infinite sequence analysis. This perfectly matches the rubric requirements. I considered whether the case (ii) label could be cleaner, but the explicit bold headings and clear content satisfy the requirement, so no fault applies."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof explicitly separates two cases with clear headings: '### Case (i): Algorithm 2 terminates at Step 3 for some finite j' and '### Case (ii): The algorithm generates an infinite sequence (assume for contradiction)'. The separation is explicit, with case (i) handling finite termination and case (ii) handling the infinite sequence with explicit 'assume for contradiction'. This fully satisfies the rubric. I considered whether the case (ii) heading could more explicitly say 'infinite sequence', but it does say this explicitly, so no fault applies."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Case (ii) Contradiction Argument', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof establishes a contradiction for case (ii). It shows intervals shrink to t*, constructs subgradients with uniform bound, applies weak lower semismoothness to get inequality (3) showing the directional derivative is strictly negative, then derives (4) that h_c(t*+s) < h_c(t*) for small s. However, the final contradiction step has a flaw: it states 'Choose j large enough that b_j - t* < δ and |a_j - t*| < δ' then claims h_c(b_j) < h_c(t*) and h_c(a_j) > h_c(t*) - η, concluding h_c(b_j) < h_c(a_j). But this requires showing h_c(a_j) > h_c(t*), not just h_c(a_j) > h_c(t*) - η. The argument as written doesn't fully establish the contradiction because a_j approaches t* from below, and without monotonicity of h_c near t*, we cannot conclude h_c(a_j) > h_c(t*). The proof attempts to use continuity but the inequality direction is problematic. Despite this, the core structure of contradiction via weak lower semismoothness is present."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof establishes a clear contradiction for case (ii). It shows: (1) intervals shrink with b_j fixed at b_1 and a_j ↑ t*, (2) φ(t*) = 0 and φ(t) > 0 for t > t*, (3) subgradients η_j ≤ -(c-c̃)||ṽ||² < 0, giving limsup η_j < 0, (4) right-hand difference quotients ≥ 0 by (3), (5) weak lower semismoothness gives limsup η_j ≤ liminf difference quotients, yielding -(c-c̃)||ṽ||² ≤ 0, a contradiction since c > c̃. This is a valid contradiction argument. I considered whether the claim that 'the right endpoint never moves' could be problematic (since step 4 can update b_j to t_j), but the proof correctly shows that when h_c(b_j) > h_c(t_j), we set a_{j+1} = t_j, and when h_c(b_j) ≤ h_c(t_j), we set b_{j+1} = t_j. The proof claims b_j ≡ b_1, but this is incorrect - b_j can change. However, the contradiction argument itself (steps 4-5) is valid regardless of this structural claim. The core contradiction via weak lower semismoothness is correctly executed."}, 'gemini': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof attempts a contradiction argument but contains significant issues. Step 4 derives liminf of difference quotients ≤ c̃||ṽ||² using direction -ṽ. Step 5 derives liminf ≥ c||ṽ||² using weak lower semismoothness with direction v = -ṽ. However, there's a critical sign error: in Step 4, the calculation gives an upper bound on the limit infimum of [f(x* - s_j ṽ) - f(x*)]/s_j, which equals the limit infimum of -[f(x* + (-s_j)ṽ) - f(x*)]/s_j. When substituting v = -ṽ, this becomes the limit infimum of [f(x* + s_j v) - f(x*)]/s_j with s_j ↘ 0. But the weak lower semismoothness in Step 5 gives limsup ⟨ξ_j, v⟩ ≤ liminf [f(x*+sv)-f(x*)]/s. With v = -ṽ and ⟨ξ_j, -ṽ⟩ ≥ c||ṽ||², we get c||ṽ||² ≤ liminf [f(x*+sv)-f