2026-03-25 07:50:46,598 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_9856', 'prompt': 'Prove the following: \\n\\nIf \\(f\\) is integrable and \\(\\left\\{f_{n}\\right\\}\\) is an approximating sequence of step maps, then \\(|f|\\) is integrable, and \\(\\left\\{\\left|f_{n}\\right|\\right\\}\\) approximates \\(|f|\\). In particular,\n\n\\[\n\\int_{X}|f|=\\lim \\int_{X}\\left|f_{n}\\right|=\\lim \\left\\|f_{n}\\right\\|_{1}\n\\]', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist verification: [X] Prove |f| is integrable using approximating sequence of step maps; [X] Show {|f_n|} approximates |f|; [X] Establish the limit formula ∫|f| = lim ∫|f_n| = lim ||f_n||_1; [X] Address complex-valued case. All instruction elements satisfied. No unrequested content added. Structure follows logical progression matching the prompt's three-part structure. Fault considered: Could have been more explicit about 'step map' definition—rejected because context makes this clear and the proof is self-contained."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All factual claims verified: [VERIFIED] Reverse triangle inequality ||a|-|b|| ≤ |a-b| for real and complex numbers; [VERIFIED] |∑c_k 1_{E_k}| = ∑|c_k|1_{E_k} for step maps; [VERIFIED] Definition of integrable function via L^1-approximation by step maps; [VERIFIED] Monotonicity of integral used correctly; [VERIFIED] Definition of ||·||_1 as ∫|·|. No unsourced statistics, no outdated information, no missing caveats. The proof is standard measure theory found in texts like Rudin or Folland.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] |f| integrability—correctly shows {|f_n|} is Cauchy in L^1 by using reverse triangle inequality; [CORRECT] Approximation claim—correctly proves |||f_n|-|f|||_1 → 0; [CORRECT] Limit formula—correctly applies definition of integral for integrable functions and identifies ∫|f_n| = ||f_n||_1; [CORRECT] Complex case—correctly notes |c_k| ∈ [0,∞) and reverse triangle inequality holds. All mathematical reasoning sound, no gaps, no incorrect conclusions.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clear mathematical exposition with good structure (numbered stages, visual separation). Notation is consistent and precise. Minor issue identified: The phrase 'The **only place** the inequality (1) is used' in Stage 2 is slightly informal emphasis for a mathematical proof; could be stated as 'The key estimate is' or similar. Otherwise professional quality: proper LaTeX formatting, logical flow, appropriate theorem-proof style. No run-on sentences, no jargon without explanation, no passive voice overuse."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof is medium-to-detailed given the need to establish three distinct results plus complex case. Nothing can be cut because: each stage is essential (integrability, approximation, limit formula); the complex case extension is explicitly requested by 'in particular' scope. Nothing is missing because: all steps justified, inequality cited, limit properties used correctly. Minor trimming possible: the 'only place' commentary in Stage 2 could be condensed without loss. Overall ±10% of ideal length."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.7, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.70'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist verification: [X] Prove |f| is integrable; [X] Show {|f_n|} approximates |f|; [X] Establish limit formula; [X] Address complex-valued case. All elements satisfied. Response uses slightly different notation (Σ for σ-algebra vs M) but equivalent. Structure matches prompt requirements. Fault considered: Stage 1 says 'To conclude |f| is integrable we must show...in Stage 2' which creates forward reference—rejected because this is a valid proof structure and all claims are eventually fulfilled."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All factual claims verified: [VERIFIED] Same mathematical facts as Response A—all standard measure theory; [VERIFIED] Step map definition correct; [VERIFIED] Reverse triangle inequality correctly stated for reals and extended to complexes; [VERIFIED] Monotonicity of integral applied correctly; [VERIFIED] ||f_n||_1 = ∫|f_n| correctly identified. No questionable claims, no unverifiable technical assertions, no outdated information.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Stage 1 correctly sets up {|f_n|} as step maps; [CORRECT] Stage 2 correctly proves approximation via reverse triangle inequality and integral monotonicity; [CORRECT] Stage 3 correctly derives limit formula; [CORRECT] Complex case correctly handled with two explicit adjustments. Mathematical reasoning is sound. One observation: Stage 1 defers the approximation proof to Stage 2, which is logically valid but creates slight dependency—still correct.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clear, professional mathematical writing. Good use of structural markers (---, ###). Notation is consistent (Σ for σ-algebra, explicit index sets). Minor issue: The forward reference in Stage 1 ('this is done in Stage 2') slightly disrupts self-contained flow; a brief indication that the proof is coming would be smoother. Otherwise excellent: precise language, proper LaTeX, appropriate formality for mathematical proof."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Comparable to ideal length. Nothing can be cut because: explicit formula for |f_n| in Stage 1 aids clarity; pointwise inequality explicit statement aids rigor; complex case adjustments are usefully itemized. Nothing is missing because: all proof steps present, complex case covered, limit formula fully derived. Minor possible trim: the parenthetical '(the reverse triangle inequality)' in Stage 2 is redundant given the displayed name above it. Overall well-calibrated length."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.7, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.70'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both responses are essentially equivalent in mathematical rigor, structure, and completeness. Both correctly prove |f| is integrable via the reverse triangle inequality, establish {|f_n|} approximates |f|, derive the limit formula, and address the complex case. Response A uses slightly more formal notation (L^1 convergence definition) and labels stages identically, while Response B uses 'Stage 1' as preparatory rather than conclusive. Neither contains errors or significant gaps; the differences are stylistic, not substantive."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) is better on Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner correctly uses the definition that f is integrable iff there exists a sequence of step maps with L^1 convergence to f, which matches the standard completion definition in measure theory. Gemini 3 Pro incorrectly states that approximating sequences must satisfy 'f_n converges to f almost everywhere'—this is not part of the definition used in the prompt's context (Lang's Real and Functional Analysis or similar texts), where integrability is defined purely through L^1-Cauchy sequences of step maps without requiring a.e. convergence. The SxS winner's Stage 1 correctly shows |f| is integrable by proving {|f_n|} approximates |f| in L^1, while Gemini 3 Pro redundantly proves Cauchy-ness of {|f_n|} separately when the prompt only requires showing {|f_n|} approximates |f|. The SxS winner's proof is more direct and elegant, using the reverse triangle inequality once to establish both integrability of |f| and the approximation property simultaneously. Gemini 3 Pro's Stage 3 unnecessarily uses the inequality |∫g - ∫h| ≤ ∫|g-h| when the definition of the integral already gives ∫|f| = lim ∫|f_n| directly from the approximation property, making its argument circular and less clean. Both handle complex-valued functions adequately, but the SxS winner's treatment is more concise."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and structural clarity compared to GPT 5.2. Gemini 3 Pro correctly identifies that Stage 1 and Stage 2 are essentially the same argument—using the reverse triangle inequality to show {|fₙ|} approximates |f|, which immediately implies |f| is integrable by definition. GPT 5.2's Stage 1 contains a logical error: it proves |f| has finite integral but does not prove |f| is integrable under the definition used (which requires approximation by step maps, not just finiteness). GPT 5.2's argument that ∫|f| ≤ 1 + ∫|fₙ₀| < ∞ only shows |f| ∈ L¹, but integrability in this context requires exhibiting an approximating sequence of step maps—precisely what Stage 2 accomplishes. Gemini 3 Pro avoids this redundancy by unifying the argument. Additionally, Gemini 3 Pro explicitly notes that |s| is a step map when s is, a crucial verification GPT 5.2 omits. Both handle complex-valued functions correctly, but Gemini 3 Pro's exposition is cleaner with explicit coefficient handling."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent LaTeX delimiters ($...$ for inline, $$...$$ for display) throughout, while the SxS winner mixes $$...$$ and \\[...\\] with occasional formatting inconsistencies', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor inconsistency, 3-4 noticeable improvement, 5-6 substantially cleaner LaTeX', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit Cauchy sequence definition', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the Cauchy condition (lim_{m,n→∞} ∫|f_n - f_m| = 0) as part of the definition of approximating sequence, which the SxS winner omits', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor addition, 3-4 meaningful clarification, 5-6 critical missing piece', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Squeeze theorem explicit citation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names and applies the Squeeze Theorem in Stage 2, while the SxS winner uses the same logic without naming the theorem', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor naming, 3-4 clearer mathematical reasoning, 5-6 substantially improved rigor', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Integral property explicit statement', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the foundational property |∫g - ∫h| ≤ ∫|g-h| before applying it in Stage 3, while the SxS winner jumps directly to the conclusion', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor addition, 3-4 clearer derivation, 5-6 critical gap filled', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Complex modulus terminology', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly distinguishes 'complex modulus' from 'absolute value' in the verification section, while the SxS winner uses 'absolute value' ambiguously for complex case", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor terminology, 3-4 improved precision, 5-6 critical mathematical distinction', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Structural heading hierarchy', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent hierarchical headings (### Stage 1, ### Stage 2, etc.) with clear visual separation, while the SxS winner uses inconsistent formatting (bold headers, horizontal rules, varying styles)', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor formatting, 3-4 improved readability, 5-6 substantially better organization', 'rating': 4}, {'name': "Explicit 'almost everywhere' mention", 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly mentions 'almost everywhere (a.e.)' convergence in Stage 1, while the SxS winner omits this measure-theoretic qualification entirely", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor technicality, 3-4 important precision, 5-6 critical omission in measure theory context', 'rating': 5}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit finite bound for integrability', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs a finite upper bound for ∫|f| by choosing n₀ such that ∫|f−fₙ₀|<1, yielding ∫|f|≤1+∫|fₙ₀|<∞, whereas the SxS winner only shows convergence in L¹ without establishing finiteness of the integral directly.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit use of integral continuity estimate', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly invokes and states the standard estimate |∫g−∫h|≤∫|g−h| to justify the limit interchange in Stage 3, while the SxS winner appeals to a generic definition without showing the error bound.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Clear separation of integrability from approximation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 proves |f| is integrable in Stage 1 using a pointwise triangle inequality and explicit finite bound, logically separating this from the approximation proof in Stage 2; the SxS winner conflates these by using the reverse triangle inequality for both purposes simultaneously.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit mention of measure notation dμ', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 consistently includes the measure notation dμ in integral expressions (e.g., ∫_X |f_n-f| dμ), reinforcing the measure-theoretic context, while the SxS winner omits this throughout.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Structural heading hierarchy with clear stage labels', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses consistent hierarchical section markers (## Stage 1, ## Stage 2, etc.) with visual separators (---) between major sections, creating clearer navigational structure than the SxS winner's mixed use of ### **Stage X** and horizontal rules.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginall