2026-03-25 07:50:49,008 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_9856', 'prompt': 'Prove the following: \\n\\nIf \\(f\\) is integrable and \\(\\left\\{f_{n}\\right\\}\\) is an approximating sequence of step maps, then \\(|f|\\) is integrable, and \\(\\left\\{\\left|f_{n}\\right|\\right\\}\\) approximates \\(|f|\\). In particular,\n\n\\[\n\\int_{X}|f|=\\lim \\int_{X}\\left|f_{n}\\right|=\\lim \\left\\|f_{n}\\right\\|_{1}\n\\]', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) proves |f| is integrable with explicit construction using g_N and monotone convergence, (2) proves {|f_n|} approximates |f| by verifying both conditions (convergence a.e. and summable L^1 differences), (3) establishes the limit formula ∫|f| = lim ∫|f_n| = lim ||f_n||_1. No unrequested content added. Format is appropriate mathematical proof with clear structure.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: (1) construction of g_N as dominating function for |f| is standard technique in measure theory, (2) triangle inequality ||a|-|b|| ≤ |a-b| correctly applied, (3) monotone convergence theorem correctly invoked for g_N ↑ g, (4) Cauchy criterion for (∫|f_n|) correctly established. One minor issue: the claim 'the series on the right converges absolutely a.e.' follows from the construction but could be more explicitly justified—however this is verifiable from the given bounds. No false claims detected."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Every requirement correctly addressed: [CORRECT] |f| integrable—proved via dominating function g ∈ L^1; [CORRECT] {|f_n|} approximates |f|—verified both conditions (|f_n| → |f| a.e. by continuity, and Σ∫||f_{n+1}|-|f_n|| < ∞ via the key inequality); [CORRECT] limit formula—proved with explicit identification of L = lim ∫|f_n| and showing ∫|f| = L. The proof uses standard definition of approximating sequence (convergence a.e. + summable differences) consistent with Lang's Real and Functional Analysis."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear logical flow. Strengths: explicit definition of g_N, clear statement of monotone convergence application, systematic verification of approximating sequence conditions. Minor issue: the phrase 'the series on the right converges absolutely a.e.' appears without immediate justification—reader must infer this from the g_N construction. This is a trivial gap in exposition, not affecting correctness. Otherwise, notation is consistent, paragraphs well-structured, and the proof reads smoothly."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut because: (1) the g_N construction is essential for establishing |f| ≤ g, (2) the double inequality bounding |∫|f| - ∫|f_n|| is necessary for the limit conclusion, (3) the final identification of ||f_n||_1 = ∫|f_n| is required for the last equality. Nothing is missing because: (1) integrability of |f| is fully proved, (2) approximating sequence conditions are both verified, (3) limit formula is established with explicit limit identification. Only trivial trimming possible: the explicit Cauchy argument for (∫|f_n|) could be slightly condensed since L^1 convergence already implies this.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) |f| integrable—proved in part (i), (2) {|f_n|} approximates |f|—established in part (i), (3) limit formula—proved in parts (ii)-(iii). However, one minor issue: the proof introduces L^1 convergence ∫||f|-|f_n|| → 0 in part (ii), which while true and useful, is not strictly required by the prompt and represents a slight addition beyond the requested conclusions. The structure with labeled parts (i)-(iii) is clear and appropriate.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Major claims verified but with one significant issue. Verified: (1) the key inequality ||a|-|b|| ≤ |a-b| correctly applied throughout, (2) {|f_n|} satisfies approximating sequence conditions, (3) the limit identification L = ∫|f| via sandwiching is correct. However, [UNVERIFIABLE/QUESTIONABLE]: In part (ii), the claim 'Letting m→∞ and using Σ∫|f_{k+1}-f_k| < ∞ we obtain ∫|f-f_n| → 0' requires justification—this uses that f_m → f in L^1, which follows from the approximating sequence definition but should be explicitly noted. More critically, the step 'If 0 ≤ s ≤ |f| then s ≤ |f_n| + |f-f_n|' in part (iii) is correct, but the subsequent supremum argument for ∫|f| ≤ L assumes the monotone convergence or approximation property for non-negative functions that may need more explicit justification in this context. These are not errors but gaps in rigor that make some claims less immediately verifiable."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'All requirements correctly addressed: [CORRECT] |f| integrable—proved via approximating sequence conditions; [CORRECT] {|f_n|} approximates |f|—verified; [CORRECT] limit formula—established with explicit L = lim ∫|f_n| and showing L = ∫|f|. Minor issue: the proof in part (iii) uses the characterization ∫g = sup{∫s : s ≤ g, s step} for non-negative g, which is correct but introduces a different approach than the standard definition being used. The sandwiching argument is valid but slightly more convoluted than necessary. The conclusion is correct but the path has some unnecessary complexity.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear professional mathematical writing with good structure. Strengths: explicit labeling of parts (i)-(iii), clear statement of the key inequality at the outset, logical progression. Minor issue: the transition in part (ii) from ∫|f_m - f_n| bound to ∫|f - f_n| → 0 is somewhat abrupt—'Letting m→∞' skips over why f_m → f in L^1, which while true could be more explicit. The final paragraph is slightly repetitive in stating the conclusion. Overall very readable with consistent notation."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Good but has identifiable padding. Could be trimmed: (1) the L^1 convergence result in part (ii) (∫||f|-|f_n|| → 0) is not required by the prompt—while elegant, it's extra; (2) the explicit Cauchy argument for {∫|f_n|} in part (ii) is redundant since L^1 convergence of |f_n| to |f| already gives this; (3) part (iii)'s supremum characterization, while valid, adds length when the sandwiching could be done more directly as in Response A. Nothing is missing: all required conclusions are established. The response is approximately 15-20% longer than ideal due to these additional elements."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.6, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.60'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': ''}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and completeness, while Gemini 3 Pro contains significant technical errors and gaps. The SxS winner correctly uses the defining property of approximating sequences (summable differences: $\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\int|f_{k+1}-f_k|<\\infty$) to construct a dominating function $g$ via monotone convergence, proving $|f|$ is integrable through absolute convergence of the telescoping series—this is the standard completion construction. Gemini 3 Pro incorrectly conflates 'Cauchy in $L^1$' with the stronger 'summable differences' condition; it states '$\\lim_{m,n \\to \\infty} \\int_X |f_n - f_m|= 0$' as the definition, but then never establishes the crucial summability condition for $|f_n|$ that would make it a valid approximating sequence per the actual definition used in the prompt. The SxS winner explicitly proves $\\sum_n\\int||f_{n+1}|-|f_n||<\\infty$ using the reverse triangle inequality, whereas Gemini 3 Pro only shows Cauchy-ness, which is insufficient for the limit formula $\\int|f|=\\lim\\int|f_n|$ without additional work. The SxS winner also provides the careful two-sided estimate showing $|\\int|f|-\\int|f_n||\\to0$, while Gemini 3 Pro's Stage 3 argument has a subtle circularity—it assumes $\\int|f|$ exists as a number before properly establishing the convergence. Writing style favors Gemini 3 Pro's structured exposition, but mathematical correctness decisively favors the SxS winner."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) demonstrates superior Instruction Following and Truthfulness by correctly using the standard definition of 'approximating sequence' in measure theory (convergence a.e. plus absolutely summable differences in L¹-norm), whereas GPT 5.2 incorrectly assumes the definition ∫|fₙ−f|→0, which is convergence in L¹ rather than the technical definition required. The SxS winner rigorously constructs the dominating function g via monotone convergence and verifies the Cauchy criterion for ∫|fₙ|, proving lim∫|fₙ| = ∫|f| with explicit error bounds using tail series estimates; GPT 5.2's Stage 3 conclusion that ∫|fₙ|→∫|f| follows from ∫||fₙ|−|f||→0 is circular reasoning since this assumes what needs to be proven about the limit of integrals. The SxS winner's Writing Style is more concise and mathematically precise, avoiding GPT 5.2's verbose section headers and redundant 'Complex-valued f' addendum that addresses an unstated generalization. GPT 5.2's response contains a critical logical gap: it asserts ∫|f| < ∞ by bounding with a single n₀, but this only shows finiteness without establishing integrability in the measurable sense or connecting to the approximating sequence property for |f|."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Structured section headers', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses explicit markdown headers (### Stage 1, ### Stage 2, ### Stage 3) to demarcate logical proof phases, while the SxS winner presents an unbroken prose proof.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor formatting difference, 3-4 = clear structural improvement, 5-6 = dramatically superior organization enabling easier verification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit statement of proof goals per stage', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro begins each stage with a clear declaration of what will be proven (e.g., 'To show that |f| is integrable...', 'To prove that the sequence {|f_n|} approximates |f|...'), whereas the SxS winner proceeds without such explicit goal statements.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = occasional goal mentions, 3-4 = consistent explicit goals, 5-6 = comprehensive goal framing that guides reader through proof', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Reverse triangle inequality explicitly named', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names and labels the 'reverse triangle inequality' when applying the key bound ||a|-|b|| ≤ |a-b|, while the SxS winner uses the inequality without naming it.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = inequality present but unnamed, 3-4 = named once, 5-6 = prominently featured with clear conceptual framing', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Complex-valued extension section', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated ### Verification for Complex-Valued Functions section addressing the modulus generalization, while the SxS winner treats only the real-valued case.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention, 3-4 = substantive extension with key points covered, 5-6 = thorough treatment matching or exceeding main proof rigor', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Squeeze theorem explicitly invoked', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names the 'Squeeze Theorem' as justification for the limit conclusion, while the SxS winner derives the same result through Cauchy sequence estimates without naming this theorem.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = theorem present but implicit, 3-4 = explicitly named once, 5-6 = prominently featured with clear application', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'LaTeX display equation formatting', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses dedicated display math environments with $$ delimiters for key inequalities and results, while the SxS winner embeds most mathematics in inline $...$ or $$...$$ without consistent structural emphasis.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor differences, 3-4 = noticeable improvement in visual hierarchy, 5-6 = substantially clearer mathematical presentation', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 17449, 'output_tokens': 4113, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.084254}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.084254}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 17449, 'output_tokens': 4113, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.084254}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.084254}}] 
