2026-03-25 08:01:41,170 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_13498', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $(\\rho, u)$ be a smooth solution to the Cauchy problem for the one-dimensional compressible Navier-Stokes equations on the interval $(x,t) \\in {\\mathbb R}\\times(0,T]$ for any $T>0$. The system of equations is given by:\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\n\\rho_t + (\\rho u)_x = 0 & \\text{(S1)} \\\\\n(\\rho u)_t + (\\rho u^2)_x + p_x = (\\mu(\\rho)u_x)_x & \\text{(S2)}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nwhere $\\rho$ is the density, $u$ is the velocity, the pressure $p$ is given by $p(\\rho) = \\rho^\\gamma$ for a constant $\\gamma > 1$, and the viscosity $\\mu$ is given by $\\mu(\\rho) = 1 + \\rho^\\beta$ for a constant $\\beta \\ge 0$.\n\nThe initial conditions are:\n$$\n(\\rho(x,0), u(x,0)) = (\\rho_0(x), u_0(x)) \\quad \\text{for } x \\in {\\mathbb R} \\quad \\text{(S3)}\n$$\n\nWe assume the following standard energy estimate holds for the solution:\n**Lemma 4 (Energy Estimate)**\nLet $(\\rho,u)$ be a smooth solution to (S1)-(S3). Then for any $T>0$, it holds\n$$\n\\int_{{\\mathbb R}}(\\rho u^{2}+\\rho^{\\gamma})(t)dx+\\int_{0}^{T}\\int_{{\\mathbb R}}(1+\\rho^\\beta)(u_{x})^{2}dx\\leq C. \\quad \\text{(S4)}\n$$\nwhere $C$ is a constant depending on the initial data. This implies in particular that for any $t \\in [0, T]$, $\\|\\sqrt{\\rho}u(t)\\|_{L^2}$ and $\\|\\rho(t)\\|_{L^1}$ are bounded by a constant $C$.\n## Theorem\n**Lemma 5 (Uniform upper bound of the density)**\nSuppose that $(\\rho,u)$ is a smooth solution to the Cauchy problem (S1)-(S3). Then for any $T>0$, there exists an absolute constant $C>0$ which depends on the initial data and $\\beta\\ge 0$ such that\n$$\n\\rho(x,t)\\leq C, \\quad (x,t)\\in {\\mathbb R}\\times(0,T].\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All instructions followed: (1) Complete and correct proof provided under '## Proof of Lemma 5' heading; (2) Multiple approaches not needed as proof succeeds; (3) Cleaned up proof structure with numbered sections; (4) Honesty condition not triggered since proof completed. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirements with clear section headers and logical flow."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All mathematical claims verified: [VERIFIED] Definition of Φ(ρ) and its properties; [VERIFIED] Evolution equation D_tΦ = -μ(ρ)u_x derived correctly from (S1); [VERIFIED] Effective flux F = μu_x - p and its relation to B_t + ρu²; [VERIFIED] Key inequality D_t(Φ+B) = -p(ρ) ≤ 0; [VERIFIED] Bound on |B| using Cauchy-Schwarz: |B| ≤ ‖√ρ u‖_{L²}‖√ρ‖_{L²} ≤ C; [VERIFIED] Final step: Φ(ρ) ≥ ρ^β/β for β>0, ρ≥1 and Φ(ρ)=ln ρ for β=0 implies uniform bound on ρ. One minor issue: [UNVERIFIABLE] The claim that 'the integration constant is 0 because ρu, ρu² ∈ L¹' — while physically reasonable given the energy estimates, the rigorous justification that boundary terms vanish at x→-∞ is not fully explicit. This is standard in this literature but technically unverified without additional decay assumptions."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All requirements [CORRECT]: (1) Proves Lemma 5 (uniform upper bound of density) — [CORRECT]; (2) Uses given setup (S1)-(S3) and Lemma 4 — [CORRECT]; (3) Constructs appropriate auxiliary functions (Φ, B/F) — [CORRECT]; (4) Derives key differential inequality along characteristics — [CORRECT]; (5) Applies energy estimates to bound auxiliary quantities — [CORRECT]; (6) Concludes uniform bound via properties of Φ — [CORRECT]. The proof correctly handles both cases β>0 and β=0. No incorrect statements identified.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 