al data and $\\beta\\ge 0$ such that\n$$\n\\rho(x,t)\\leq C, \\quad (x,t)\\in {\\mathbb R}\\times(0,T].\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under '## Proof' heading. It uses the standard mathematical structure expected. Minor formatting variance: uses '## Proof of Lemma 5' rather than exactly '## Proof' as specified, but this is trivial. The proof is complete and honest (no false claims of completion). Checklist: [X] Complete proof provided; [X] Cleaned up proof under appropriate heading; [X] Honest about completion (no 'unable to complete' needed). Score 5 due to trivial heading variance."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check process: [VERIFIED] The definition of Φ(ρ) and its properties; [VERIFIED] The derivation D_tΦ = -μ(ρ)u_x from (S1); [VERIFIED] The effective flux F = μu_x - p and its spatial derivative; [VERIFIED] The definition B(x,t) = ∫_{-∞}^x ρu dy; [VERIFIED] The ODE D_t(Φ+B) = -p(ρ) ≤ 0; [VERIFIED] The bound on |B| using Cauchy-Schwarz and Lemma 4; [VERIFIED] The final conclusion that Φ bounded implies ρ bounded. [UNVERIFIABLE] The claim that 'the integration constant is 0 because ρu, ρu² ∈ L¹' — while plausible for finite energy solutions, this is stated without rigorous justification of decay at infinity. This is a technical gap in the rigorous justification of the boundary condition. Due to one unverifiable technical claim, score capped at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: The response correctly proves Lemma 5. [CORRECT] All key steps: evolution of Φ, effective flux construction, closed ODE along characteristics, bound on B, conclusion from Φ to ρ. [CORRECT] Mathematical derivations align with standard proofs in compressible Navier-Stokes literature (similar to Hoff, Kazhikhov-Shelukhin methods). [CORRECT] The conclusion ρ(x,t) ≤ C matches the theorem statement. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] The boundary condition at -∞ for the integrated flux is asserted but not fully justified — this is standard in the literature but technically requires additional decay assumptions. Overall 95%+ correct with only trivial omission of rigorous decay justification. Score 5.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: The writing is professional, clear, and mathematically precise. Structure follows logical flow with numbered sections. Notation is consistent. Minor issue identified: The use of '\\medskip\\noindent\\textbf{...}' formatting is slightly idiosyncratic but does not impede readability. One minor redundancy: 'This completes the proof of Lemma 5. ∎' — the ∎ symbol and verbal completion are slightly redundant. Otherwise publication-ready mathematical writing. Score 5 for one trivial style preference."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The proof is concise and well-calibrated for expert readers in PDEs. Nothing can be cut because: each step (Φ evolution, flux definition, ODE construction, bound on B, conclusion) is essential to the logical chain. Nothing is missing because: the energy estimate is invoked correctly, the characteristic method is fully specified, and the final bound is derived. Minor observation: The case β=0 could be slightly more explicit in the final step, but this is handled adequately. Score 5 for near-perfect calibration.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response provides a proof under '## Proof' heading as requested. However, it adds substantial unrequested content: a '## Problem Analysis' section before the proof and a '## Verification' section after. The instruction asks for 'a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof' with honesty if unable to complete. The extra sections violate the 'no unrequested content' rul