h given data', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Construction of auxiliary function Ψ(ρ)', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs the monotone function Ψ(ρ) = ∫₁ᵖ μ(s)/s ds with its piecewise definition for β>0 and β=0, including the verification that Ψ(ρ)→+∞ as ρ→+∞, which is essential for the Zlotnik argument to yield a finite bound.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions Ψ without definition, 3-4 = defines Ψ but omits key properties, 5-6 = complete construction with all properties verified', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear distinction between assumed and derived bounds', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 carefully labels which bounds come from Lemma 4 (S4) versus which would require additional assumptions, specifically marking (7)-(8) as from S4 and (9)-(13) as requiring further estimates, whereas the SxS winner conflates these.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = some labeling present, 3-4 = clear separation of sources, 5-6 = meticulous tracking of what each estimate depends on', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Precise Zlotnik lemma conditions', 'description': "GPT 5.2 states the exact condition needed for Zlotnik's lemma (bounded variation of b, equivalently ∫₀ᵀ‖F‖∞ < ∞) and explains why this reduces to estimate (9), while the SxS winner applies Zlotnik without verifying its hypotheses are satisfied by available data.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions Zlotnik vaguely, 3-4 = states conditions, 5-6 = explicit verification of applicability and identification when condition fails', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Mathematical rigor in flux bound derivation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 derives the bound (11)-(12) on ‖F‖∞ through ‖F‖∞ ≤ ‖ρ‖₁¹/²‖√ρ ḋu‖₂ with explicit Cauchy-Schwarz application, showing exactly how (13) would imply (9), while the SxS winner asserts ‖F‖∞ ≤ C₃ without showing the dependence on unavailable estimates.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = asserts bound without derivation, 3-4 = some derivation with gaps, 5-6 = complete derivation exposing exact dependence on unverified estimate', 'rati