2026-03-25 08:05:59,727 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10884', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThis section reviews the constructive notions of dimension in Euclidean spaces. The presentation here is in terms of Kolmogorov complexity.\n\nBriefly, the conditional Kolmogorov complexity $K(w|v)$ of a string $w \\in \\{0,1\\}^*$ given a string $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^*$ is the minimum length $|\\pi|$ of a binary string $\\pi$ for which $U(\\pi,v) = w$, where $U$ is a fixed universal self-delimiting Turing machine. The Kolmogorov complexity of $w$ is $K(w) = K(w|\\lambda)$, where $\\lambda$ is the empty string. We write $U(\\pi)$ for $U(\\pi,\\lambda)$. When $U(\\pi) = w$, the string $\\pi$ is called a program for $w$. The quantity $K(w)$ is also called the algorithmic information content of $w$. Routine coding extends this definition from $\\{0,1\\}^*$ to other discrete domains, so that the Kolmogorov complexities of natural numbers, rational numbers, tuples of these, etc., are well defined up to additive constants.\n\nThe definition of $K(q)$ for rational points $q$ in Euclidean space is lifted in two steps to define the dimensions of arbitrary points in Euclidean space. First, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ and $r \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the Kolmogorov complexity of $x$ at precision $r$ is\n$$K_r(x)=\\min\\{K(q):q\\in\\mathbb{Q}^n\\cap B_{2^{-r}}(x)\\},$$\nwhere $B_{2^{-r}}(x)$ is the open ball with radius $2^{-r}$ and center $x$. Second, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, the dimension of $x$ is\n$$\\dim(x)=\\liminf_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{K_r(x)}{r}.$$\n\nAll the algorithmic information concepts above can be relativized to an arbitrary oracle $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ by giving the Turing machine in their definitions oracle access to $A$. Relativized Kolmogorov complexity $K_r^A(x)$ and relativized dimensions $\\dim^A(x)$ are thus well defined.\n\nWe first establish the point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension. Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$. For $\\delta>0$, define $\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$ to be the collection of all countable covers of $E$ by sets of positive diameter at most $\\delta$. That is, for every cover $\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$, we have $E\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}U_i$ and $|U_i|\\in(0,\\delta]$ for all $i\\in\\mathbb{N}$, where for $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, $|X|=\\sup_{p,q\\in X}|p-q|$. For $s\\geq0$, define\n$$H^s_\\delta(E)=\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}|U_i|^s:\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)\\right\\}.$$\nThen the $s$-dimensional Hausdorff outer measure of $E$ is\n$$H^s(E)=\\lim_{\\delta\\to0^+}H^s_\\delta(E),$$\nand the Hausdorff dimension of $E$ is\n$$\\dim_H(E)=\\inf\\{s\\geq0:H^s(E)=0\\}.$$\n\n**Observation 6**\nThere is a constant $c_0\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $j\\in\\mathbb{N}$,\n$$K(j)\\leq\\log(1+j)+2\\log\\log(2+j)+c_0.$$\n## Theorem\n**(Point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension)** For every set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$,\n$$\\dim_H(E) = \\inf_{A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}}\\sup_{x\\in E}\\dim^A(x).$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete and correct proof provided under ## Proof heading [X], (2) Two inequalities established with clear strategy statements [X], (3) Detailed rigorous construction with explicit oracle definition [X], (4) Boundary cases computed explicitly for singleton and [0,1]^n [X], (5) Honest completion - no admission of inability needed [X]. No unrequested content added; format matches requirements exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified against standard references (Lutz & Lutz point-to-set principle). Key verifiable claims: Lemma 1 counting bound follows from Kraft inequality [VERIFIED]; Lemma 2 construction uses valid Hausdorff measure properties [VERIFIED]; oracle construction (14) is explicit and non-circular [VERIFIED]; prefix-free coding fact is standard [VERIFIED]. One minor issue: the claim 'by shrinking the rational approximation slightly we can ensure inclusion' in Step 2.2 is hand-waved without full detail, though the enlargement to radius 2^{-(r_{m,i}-1)} is correctly handled. No false claims identified."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Every requirement correctly addressed: (1) Equality proven via two inequalities [CORRECT], (2) Inequality (I) uses F_s^A construction with proper liminf analysis [CORRECT], (3) Inequality (II) constructs explicit oracle encoding covers [CORRECT], (4) All definitions from Setup used correctly: K_r(x), dim(x), H^s_δ, dim_H [CORRECT], (5) Observation 6 applied correctly in (18) [CORRECT]. Boundary cases verify equality for singleton (0=0) and [0,1]^n (n=n) [CORRECT]. No incorrect elements.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear structure. Strengths: explicit strategy statements before each part, consistent notation, proper use of ∎ symbols, numbered equations for key steps. Minor issue identified: occasional redundancy - 'This is Inequality (I). ∎' followed by section break then '## Part 2' is slightly verbose; the geometric argument in Step 2.2 could be more concise. One grammar observation: 'w.l.o.g.' used without expansion. These are trivial style preferences; overall publication-ready quality."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal proof for this result requires substantial detail due to oracle construction complexity. Response A is ~2800 words. Identifiable padding: (1) Boundary cases section (600+ words) exceeds minimal verification needs - singleton case could be 3 sentences not a full subsection, (2) Step 2.2 geometric justification is over-detailed with multiple radius adjustments when the standard 'enlarge by constant factor' suffices, (3) Explicit prefix-free coding fact proof is included though it's standard material. Missing: nothing critical. Could trim ~20% without losing rigor. Score 4 due to identifiable padding in boundary cases and geometric step."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.3, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.30'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Core instructions satisfied: (1) Proof under ## Proof [X], (2) Two inequalities established [X], (3) Complete proof with oracle construction [X]. Minor issues: (a) 'We may assume E⊆[0,1]^n' adds unrequested content - the general case reduction is standard but not explicitly requested, and the countably stable justification is slightly misleading (the principle holds for all E without this assumption), (b) Boundary cases not computed explicitly as in Response A - while not strictly required, the prompt's 'complete and correct' suggests thorough verification. Score capped at 5 due to unrequested assumption and missing explicit boundary verification."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Major claims verified: Lemma 1 follows standard argument [VERIFIED]; Lemma 2 oracle construction is valid [VERIFIED]. Issues identified: (1) 'Enumerate all cubes as Q_i in non-decreasing order of their side' - this enumerates infinitely many cubes across all scales, but the indexing and the claim 'i ≤ 2^{sr_i+1}' requires careful justification not fully provided; the bound on i assumes a specific enumeration order that affects the K(i) bound [QUESTIONABLE]. (2) 'K^A(q_i) ≤ |σ_i| + O(1)' - the O(1) hides the cost of parsing the pair ⟨σ_i, q_i⟩, which depends on the encoding; this is standard but should be noted [UNVERIFIABLE without encoding details]. (3) The dyadic cube construction is less general than Response A's ball construction but works for the restricted [0,1]^n case; the extension to general E is hand-waved via 'countably stable'. Score capped at 4 due to questionable enumeration bound and unverifiable encoding claim."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': 'Requirements assessment: (1) Equality proven [CORRECT], (2) Inequality directions correct [CORRECT], (3) Key issue: The proof assumes E⊆[0,1]^n without full justification for general E [PARTIALLY CORRECT] - while Hausdorff dimension is countably stable, the point-to-set principle requires handling unbounded E via countable union, which is not explicitly carried out. The oracle construction uses dyadic cubes which are natural for [0,1]^n but the extension to R^n requires additional argument not provided. (4) Observation 6 applied [CORRECT]. The response is correct for the restricted case but the general case reduction is incomplete.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Concise professional mathematical writing. Strengths: efficient lemma structure, minimal redundancy, clear logical flow. Minor issues: (1) 'medskip\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 1.}' uses LaTeX formatting that doesn't render in plain text, slightly awkward, (2) The enumeration argument in Lemma 2 is compressed and harder to follow than Response A's explicit construction - 'i ≤ ∑_{k≤r_i} N_k ≤ 2^{sr_i+1}' requires reader to fill in steps, (3) No explicit strategy statements before lemmas unlike Response A. These are minor; overall clean and professional."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: ~900 words, significantly more concise than Response A. Assessment: (1) Nothing can be cut: the dyadic cube construction is already minimal, lemma statements are tight, (2) Minor gap: the enumeration bound justification could use one more sentence for clarity, (3) The restriction to [0,1]^n makes the proof shorter but less general - this is a trade-off. Near-perfect calibration for the restricted case; slight gap in generality justification. Score 5 for near-ideal length with minor clarity gap in enumeration argument.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.55, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.55'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better due to superior rigor, explicitness, and structural clarity. Response A carefully justifies each step with citations to definitions (e.g., 'definition of H^t_δ(E)', 'definition of K_r^A(x)'), proves Lemma 1 explicitly, handles the Kraft coding construction with full detail including the prefix-free coding fact proof, and explicitly addresses circularity concerns in oracle construction. Response B compresses critical steps: it omits proof of the counting bound (just asserting 'prefix-freeness gives'), skips the Kraft inequality justification entirely, uses dyadic cubes without explaining why this suffices for arbitrary covers, and has a subtle error in Lemma 2 where it claims N_r ≤ 2^{sr} from the sum bound when actually N_r could be larger—Response A correctly handles this by working directly with the cover's t-cost. Response B's oracle construction also elides how the enumeration ordering interacts with the complexity bound, while Response A's explicit (m, σ_{m,i}, q_{m,i}) encoding is fully verifiable."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner demonstrates superior Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with explicit lemma statements (Lemma 1, Lemma 2), careful attention to definitional dependencies, and explicit verification of non-circularity in oracle construction (Step 2.4: 'No circularity'). The SxS winner correctly handles the inequality direction in Part 2, showing sup_{x∈E} dim^A(x) ≤ t for constructed A, then taking inf_A. Gemini 3 Pro has critical errors: in Direction 1, the inequality sign is reversed (claims dim_H(E) ≥ inf_A sup dim^A(x) but proves the opposite), and contains a nonsensical claim that 'k - K(k) - c_1 - 1 → ∞ (making the subtracted fraction positive)' when this quantity actually goes to +∞, making the fraction negative and the bound invalid. Gemini 3 Pro also lacks explicit lemma structure, has compressed formatting errors ('highlyefficient', 'thisgives'), and omits verification that the oracle construction is non-circular. The SxS winner's boundary cases are more thorough, explicitly computing both sides for singletons and [0,1]^n with proper lower bound arguments, while Gemini 3 Pro's [0,1]^n case uses an advanced Borel-Cantelli argument without establishing the necessary measure-theoretic framework from the given Setup."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both responses are mathematically identical, word-for-word reproductions of the same proof. Using Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, and Verbosity metrics: the SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) provides a complete, rigorous proof with explicit citations to definitions from the Setup (e.g., 'definition of K_r^A(x)', 'definition of dim^A(x)', 'Observation 6'), clear lemma structure, detailed boundary cases for singletons and [0,1]^n, and careful attention to non-circularity in the oracle construction. The external model (GPT 5.2) contains the exact same content with identical structure, notation, equation numbering, and even the same explicit citations to definitions; every sentence, tag, and formatting choice matches precisely. Since the responses are verbatim duplicates with no discernible differences in correctness, completeness, clarity, or style, they perform equally on all evaluation criteria."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses \\( \\) and \\[ \\] delimiters consistently throughout mathematical expressions, 