2026-03-25 08:06:02,331 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10884', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThis section reviews the constructive notions of dimension in Euclidean spaces. The presentation here is in terms of Kolmogorov complexity.\n\nBriefly, the conditional Kolmogorov complexity $K(w|v)$ of a string $w \\in \\{0,1\\}^*$ given a string $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^*$ is the minimum length $|\\pi|$ of a binary string $\\pi$ for which $U(\\pi,v) = w$, where $U$ is a fixed universal self-delimiting Turing machine. The Kolmogorov complexity of $w$ is $K(w) = K(w|\\lambda)$, where $\\lambda$ is the empty string. We write $U(\\pi)$ for $U(\\pi,\\lambda)$. When $U(\\pi) = w$, the string $\\pi$ is called a program for $w$. The quantity $K(w)$ is also called the algorithmic information content of $w$. Routine coding extends this definition from $\\{0,1\\}^*$ to other discrete domains, so that the Kolmogorov complexities of natural numbers, rational numbers, tuples of these, etc., are well defined up to additive constants.\n\nThe definition of $K(q)$ for rational points $q$ in Euclidean space is lifted in two steps to define the dimensions of arbitrary points in Euclidean space. First, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ and $r \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the Kolmogorov complexity of $x$ at precision $r$ is\n$$K_r(x)=\\min\\{K(q):q\\in\\mathbb{Q}^n\\cap B_{2^{-r}}(x)\\},$$\nwhere $B_{2^{-r}}(x)$ is the open ball with radius $2^{-r}$ and center $x$. Second, for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, the dimension of $x$ is\n$$\\dim(x)=\\liminf_{r\\to\\infty}\\frac{K_r(x)}{r}.$$\n\nAll the algorithmic information concepts above can be relativized to an arbitrary oracle $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ by giving the Turing machine in their definitions oracle access to $A$. Relativized Kolmogorov complexity $K_r^A(x)$ and relativized dimensions $\\dim^A(x)$ are thus well defined.\n\nWe first establish the point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension. Let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$. For $\\delta>0$, define $\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$ to be the collection of all countable covers of $E$ by sets of positive diameter at most $\\delta$. That is, for every cover $\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)$, we have $E\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}U_i$ and $|U_i|\\in(0,\\delta]$ for all $i\\in\\mathbb{N}$, where for $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, $|X|=\\sup_{p,q\\in X}|p-q|$. For $s\\geq0$, define\n$$H^s_\\delta(E)=\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}|U_i|^s:\\{U_i\\}_{i\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\in\\mathcal{U}_\\delta(E)\\right\\}.$$\nThen the $s$-dimensional Hausdorff outer measure of $E$ is\n$$H^s(E)=\\lim_{\\delta\\to0^+}H^s_\\delta(E),$$\nand the Hausdorff dimension of $E$ is\n$$\\dim_H(E)=\\inf\\{s\\geq0:H^s(E)=0\\}.$$\n\n**Observation 6**\nThere is a constant $c_0\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $j\\in\\mathbb{N}$,\n$$K(j)\\leq\\log(1+j)+2\\log\\log(2+j)+c_0.$$\n## Theorem\n**(Point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension)** For every set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$,\n$$\\dim_H(E) = \\inf_{A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}}\\sup_{x\\in E}\\dim^A(x).$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instructions followed: (1) complete and correct proof provided under ## Proof heading [X], (2) two inequalities proved separately as is standard for this theorem [X], (3) constructive approach using Kolmogorov complexity machinery as specified in setup [X], (4) Observation 6 used explicitly in part (ii) [X], (5) no request for multiple approaches was made when stuck—none needed as proof succeeds [X]. No unrequested content added. Format matches mathematical proof conventions with clear structure using \\medskip and bold headings for parts (i) and (ii).'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: (1) Kraft inequality application in part (i) is correct—sum of 2^{-|p|} over prefix-free programs is ≤1, and scaling by s gives the stated bound [VERIFIED]. (2) The construction of C_m covers E because for any x∈E with dim^A(x)<s, infinitely many r satisfy K^A_r(x)≤sr, and the witness q at precision r has a program p with |p|≤sr, so r'=⌈|p|/s⌉≤r and x∈B_{2^{-r'}}(q)∈C_m [VERIFIED]. (3) The pruning to prefix-free subfamily in part (ii) is standard and correctly applied [VERIFIED]. (4) The bound N_{m,k}≤2^{ks-m} follows from ∑N_{m,k}2^{-ks}≤2^{-m} [VERIFIED]. (5) The use of Observation 6 for K(m) and K(i_m) is correct [VERIFIED]. Minor issue: the claim 'r' = ⌈|p|/s⌉ ≤ r' in the proof of (i) has slightly confusing notation—the proof intends r' ≤ r which holds since |p| ≤ sr. This is verifiable but notation could be clearer; no false claims found."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Every requirement answered correctly: (1) The point-to-set principle for Hausdorff dimension is proved, showing equality of dim_H(E) and inf_A sup_{x∈E} dim^A(x) [CORRECT]. (2) Both directions of inequality established: (i) dim_H(E) ≤ sup_{x∈E} dim^A(x) for all A, and (ii) for s>dim_H(E), exists A with sup_{x∈E} dim^A(x) ≤ s [CORRECT]. (3) All definitions from setup used correctly: K_r(x), dim(x), dim^A(x), H^s_δ(E), H^s(E), dim_H(E) [CORRECT]. (4) The relativized oracle construction in part (ii) correctly builds A encoding cube centers [CORRECT]. (5) The limiting arguments and liminf handling for dimension definitions are mathematically correct [CORRECT].'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing quality. Strengths: clear structure with labeled parts (i) and (ii), appropriate use of LaTeX formatting, logical flow maintained throughout. Minor issue identified: the sentence 'If p has length ℓ, the associated ball has diameter 2^{-r} with (2^{-r})^s ≤ 2^{-ℓ}' could be clearer—actually (2^{-r})^s = 2^{-sr} and since r = ⌈ℓ/s⌉ ≥ ℓ/s, we have sr ≥ ℓ so 2^{-sr} ≤ 2^{-ℓ}. The inequality direction is correct but the reasoning is slightly compressed. Another minor issue: 'r' = ⌈|p|/s⌉ ≤ r' in part (i) has confusing variable naming. These are trivial style issues that don't impede understanding."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length calibration. Nothing can be cut because: (1) The Kraft inequality justification is essential for the covering argument in (i); (2) The explicit construction of C_m with all three conditions is necessary for the proof to work; (3) The pruning to prefix-free subfamily and the counting argument with N_{m,k} in (ii) are essential; (4) The application of Observation 6 with explicit bounds is required for the O(log k_m) error term. Nothing is missing because: (1) Both directions of the inequality are fully proved; (2) The oracle construction is explicit; (3) The limiting arguments are complete. Minor trimming possible: the phrase 'standard' after 'Pruning to a prefix-free subfamily' could be slightly expanded for clarity, but this is trivial."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Instructions mostly followed: (1) complete proof under ## Proof heading [X], (2) two directions proved via Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 [X], (3) Observation 6 used in Lemma 2 [X]. Minor issue: the prompt asks to 'try multiple approaches if you are stuck'—this was not needed, but Response B adds an assumption 'We may assume E⊆[0,1]^n' that, while standard, was not explicitly requested. This is a small addition of unrequested content (justification for countable stability). The proof structure is clear with explicit lemmas."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Major claims verified with one unverifiable element: (1) Lemma 1 proof: the covering argument using S_r with |S_r|≤2^{(s+ε)r+1} is correct [VERIFIED], and the t-volume calculation showing H^t(D^A_s)=0 is correct [VERIFIED]. (2) The claim 'dim_H(E)≤s' from E⊆D^A_s follows from Lemma 1 [VERIFIED]. (3) Lemma 2 construction: the bound N_r≤2^{sr} from ∑|Q|^s≤1 is correct [VERIFIED]. (4) The enumeration and index bound i≤2^{sr_i+1} is correct [VERIFIED]. (5) Observation 6 application for K(i) is correct [VERIFIED]. Unverifiable claim: 'By standard coding we may assume E⊆[0,1]^n'—the 'standard coding' is not explained and while Hausdorff dimension is indeed countably stable, the specific coding construction is not provided. This is a technical gap that could hide issues. Additionally, the final step 'Letting ε↓0 yields the opposite inequality' is slightly fast—strictly speaking one needs to argue that the infimum over A achieves the limit, but this is standard."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Requirements mostly correct: (1) Point-to-set principle proved with both directions [CORRECT]. (2) Lemma 1 establishes dim_H(D^A_s)≤s which gives the first inequality [CORRECT]. (3) Lemma 2 constructs oracle A for the second inequality [CORRECT]. (4) All definitions used appropriately [CORRECT]. Partial issue: The reduction to E⊆[0,1]^n is presented without explicit justification of why this is valid for the general statement. While true by countable stability, the prompt asks for a 'complete and correct proof' and this step is slightly hand-waved. The oracle construction in Lemma 2 uses A={⟨σ_i,q_i⟩} which is elegant but the verification that this gives K^A(q_i)≤|σ_i|+O(1) assumes the pairing is computable from σ_i alone, which is correct but slightly compressed."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional quality with clear lemma structure. Strengths: explicit lemma statements with \textbf{Lemma 1} and \textbf{Lemma 2}, good use of mathematical notation, clear separation of proof parts. Minor issue: the phrase 'By standard coding' is vague—what coding? The reduction to [0,1]^n deserves a sentence of explanation. Another minor issue: 'the t-volume of the right side' in Lemma 1 uses informal terminology (should be 'the t-dimensional Hausdorff measure' or similar). The final '∎' symbol is used which is fine but slightly inconsistent with the 'qed' in Response A."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut because: (1) Lemma 1 requires the full ε-construction to show dim_H(D^A_s)≤s; (2) The explicit enumeration and index calculation in Lemma 2 is essential; (3) The oracle definition A={⟨σ_i,q_i⟩} is the core construction. Nothing is missing because: (1) Both directions proved; (2) Oracle explicitly constructed; (3) Limits handled. Minor gap: The reduction to [0,1]^n could use one sentence of explanation. The final 'Letting ε↓0' step could explicitly note that the infimum over A is achieved in the limit, though this is standard."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs are correct, but A handles technical details more rigorously: it explicitly constructs prefix-free families (crucial for Kraft inequality), carefully tracks the relationship between program length ℓ and precision r via r=⌈ℓ/s⌉, and properly justifies the covering argument with H^s_{2^{-m+1}}(E)≤2^{-m+1}. Response B's Lemma 2 has a subtle issue: it claims K^A(q_i)≤|σ_i|+O(1) but the oracle A contains ⟨σ_i,q_i⟩, so extracting q_i requires parsing this pair, which needs additional bits; A avoids this by having A directly code (m,i)↦q_{m,i} as a computable function."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with correct mathematical structure and clean execution; Gemini 3 Pro's response contains significant errors in Direction 1. For Instruction Following: SxS winner follows the prompt exactly, providing a cleaned proof under ## Proof with two clear inequality directions. Gemini 3 Pro adds unnecessary 'Strategies' and 'Boundary Cases' sections not requested, violating the prompt's explicit instruction. For Truthfulness: SxS winner's proof is mathematically sound; Direction 1 correctly uses dyadic cubes and Kraft inequality, Direction 2 correctly bounds Kolmogorov complexity. Gemini 3 Pro contains a critical error in Direction 1 where it claims 'k - K(k) - c_1 - 1 → ∞' making 'the subtracted fraction positive'—this is backwards: as k→∞, (k-K(k))/r_k → 1, so the bound becomes K^A_{r_k}(x)/r_k ≤ s + o(1) - (1+o(1)) ≈ s-1, which is wrong; the inequality sign should reverse or the logic collapses. For Prompt Correctness: SxS winner correctly identifies the standard point-to-set principle proof structure; Gemini 3 Pro's Direction 1 incorrectly tries to prove dim_H(E) ≥ inf_A sup_x dim^A(x) by assuming s > dim_H(E) and constructing A, which is the correct approach for the reverse inequality, but then botches the limit analysis. For Writing Style: SxS winner uses precise, professional mathematical prose; Gemini 3 Pro is verbose with awkward phrasing ('highlyefficient', 'totally free of circularity') and formatting errors like missing spaces. For Verbosity: SxS winner is appropriately concise; Gemini 3 Pro is excessively long with redundant sections."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides a correct, complete proof with superior mathematical rigor and concision, while GPT 5.2's response, though also correct, suffers from excessive verbosity and minor technical imprecision. The SxS winner establishes inequality (i) elegantly using Kraft's inequality with a clean covering argument, correctly handling the liminf definition of dimension by ensuring infinitely many scales are captured. GPT 5.2'