2026-03-25 08:20:19,440 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: tasks1_response---------------------------[{'index': 0, 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $R$ be a commutative ring and let $\\Omega = R[x_1, \\dots, x_n]$ be the polynomial ring in $n$ variables over $R$. For any polynomial $f \\in \\Omega$, $\\mathrm{supp}(f)$ denotes the set of exponent vectors of its non-zero terms. For any $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}^n$, we define $\\nabla(A) = \\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} (\\alpha + \\mathbb{N}^n)$.\n\nLet $S_1, \\dots, S_n$ be finite subsets of $R$. Let $\\Lambda$ be a finite set, and let $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda) \\in \\Omega^\\Lambda$ be a family of monic polynomials. For any $\\lambda \\in \\Lambda$, let $\\theta(\\lambda)$ be the greatest element of $\\mathrm{supp}(g(\\lambda))$ with respect to the standard partial order on $\\mathbb{N}^n$. Let $D = \\{\\theta(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda\\}$. We assume that $\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(D)$ is finite.\n\nLet $(B_a \\mid a \\in \\prod_{i=1}^n S_i)$ be a family of subsets of $\\mathbb{N}^n$ such that for each $a$, $\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(B_a)$ is finite.\n\nLet $Q$ be the ideal of $\\Omega$ defined as\n$$Q \\triangleq \\{f \\in \\Omega \\mid \\mathrm{supp}(f(x_1+a_1, \\dots, x_n+a_n)) \\subseteq \\nabla(B_a) \\text{ for all } a \\in \\prod_{i=1}^n S_i\\}.$$\nWe assume that $\\{g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda\\} \\subseteq Q$.\n\nLet $\\zeta_1$ and $\\zeta_2$ be two quantities defined as:\n$$\\zeta_1 \\triangleq \\sum_{a \\in \\prod_{i=1}^n S_i} |\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(B_a)|$$\n$$\\zeta_2 \\triangleq |\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(D)|$$\nWe assume that $\\Omega/Q$ is a finitely generated free $R$-module with $\\mathrm{rank}_R(\\Omega/Q) = \\zeta_1$.\n\n**Definition (Condition (D) and (F))**\nA finite subset $X \\subseteq R$ satisfies **Condition (D)** in $R$ if for any distinct elements $c, d \\in X$, the difference $d-c$ is not a zero divisor of $R$.\nA finite subset $X \\subseteq R$ satisfies **Condition (F)** in $R$ if for any distinct elements $c, d \\in X$, the difference $d-c$ is a multiplicative unit of $R$.\n\n**Definition (Gröbner basis)**\nThe family $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a **Gröbner basis** of $Q$ if for any non-zero polynomial $\\tau \\in Q$, there exists $\\alpha \\in \\mathrm{supp}(\\tau)$ and $r \\in \\Lambda$ such that $\\theta(r) \\le \\alpha$.\n\n**Required Lemmas:**\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (1)** Suppose that for any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, $S_i$ satisfies Condition (F) in $R$. Then, we have $\\zeta_1 \\le \\zeta_2$. Moreover, if $\\zeta_1 = \\zeta_2$, then $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a Gröbner basis of $Q$.\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (2)** Suppose that $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a Gröbner basis of $Q$. Then, we have $\\zeta_2 \\le \\zeta_1$.\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 4.1 (1)** Suppose that for any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, $S_i$ satisfies Condition (D) in $R$. Then, it holds that $\\zeta_1 \\le \\zeta_2$. Moreover, $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a Gröbner basis of $Q$ if and only if $\\zeta_1 = \\zeta_2$.', 'status': 'ACCEPT', 'result': {'status': 'ACCEPT', 'rejection_reason': None, 'justification': 'The prompt is a complex mathematical proof request in commutative algebra. It provides complete setup with definitions, lemmas, and a theorem to prove. All necessary reference material is included within the prompt itself. No rejection criteria apply.'}, 'accepted': True, 'error': None, 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cos