ps to residue classes, while the SxS winner uses a direct pigeonhole argument without modular reduction.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if external model merely mentions moduli, 3-4 if moduli are clearly defined and used, 5-6 if the modular construction is central and enables the clean divisibility argument', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Divisibility forcing exact zero', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly proves L_j(x)=0 by combining the divisibility condition L_j(x) ≡ 0 (mod M_j) with the size bound |L_j(x)| < M_j, while the SxS winner only obtains equality through direct difference without this forcing mechanism.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if mentioned briefly, 3-4 if clearly stated, 5-6 if this is a distinctive proof technique not present in the SxS winner', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Floor vs ceiling bound choice', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses B = ⌊Q⌋ with B+1 > Q to enable the strict inequality (B+1)^{n-m} > A_1...A_m, while the SxS winner uses H = ⌈B⌉ requiring a more complex estimation to prove the pigeonhole inequality.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if different choice noted, 3-4 if it simplifies the argument, 5-6 if it leads to cleaner inequality verification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit codomain size calculation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly computes the codomain size as (B+1)^m(A_1...A_m) and contrasts it with domain size (B+1)^n, while the SxS winner uses a product of (2A_jH+1) terms requiring additional estimation.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if sizes mentioned, 3-4 if clearly computed and compared, 5-6 if this enables direct inequality comparison without extra bounding', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX display for key definitions', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses dedicated display math for Q, B, M_j, and Φ with proper alignment and spacing, while the SxS winner embeds definitions inline or with less structural separation.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if slightly better, 3-4 if noticeably clearer, 5-6 if substantially improves readability of the proof structure', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Step 