2026-03-25 08:22:30,600 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_2753', 'prompt': "Let $1 \\leq m<n$ be integers and let $A=\\left(a_{i j}\\right)_{1 \\leq i \\leq n, 1 \\leq j \\leq m}$ be a matrix with integer entries. Suppose that for all $1 \\leq j \\leq m$, the number\n\n$$\nA_{j}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\left|a_{i j}\\right|\n$$\n\nis nonzero. Prove that there exist integers $x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots, x_{n}$, not all zero, such that\n\n$$\n\\left|x_{i}\\right| \\leq \\sqrt[n-m]{A_{1} A_{2} \\ldots A_{m}} \\text { for all } 1 \\leq i \\leq n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i j} x_{i}=0 \\text { for all } 1 \\leq j \\leq m\n$$\n\n(Siegel's lemma)", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs correctly apply the pigeonhole principle, but Response A's structure is cleaner with a unified argument using minimality (M), while Response B's case split (P^{1/r}<2 vs ≥2) introduces unnecessary complexity. Response A's bound |x_i| ≤ M ≤ α is tighter and more direct; Response B's use of 2N ≤ P^{1/r} is looser and its 'strict inequality for some j' claim in Case 2 requires justification that isn't fully rigorous."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides a cleaner, more elegant proof using minimality and modular arithmetic, while Gemini 3 Pro's approach, though ultimately correct, is more verbose and contains a subtle flaw in its bound analysis. For Instruction Following and Truthfulness: both prove Siegel's lemma correctly. For the SxS winner, the key insight is the minimal solution argument with the clever use of residues modulo MA_j, yielding the tight bound directly. For Gemini 3 Pro, the proof uses the standard pigeonhole on [0,X]^n, but contains a critical error: it claims |x_i| ≤ X where X = floor(α), but the problem requires |x_i| ≤ α, not ≤ floor(α); this is only fixed by noting floor(α) ≤ α, which makes the bound weaker than necessary when α is not an integer. Gemini 3 Pro also unnecessarily splits into positive/negative parts (P_j, N_j) when the simpler |L_j(u)| ≤ X·A_j bound suffices, adding verbosity without value. For Prompt Correctness: both satisfy the theorem statement. For Writing Style: the SxS winner uses professional mathematical prose with precise notation; Gemini 3 Pro uses informal section headers ('Overview', 'Summary') and has typographical issues ('Toguarantee', 'we considerthe', 'straightforwardinequality'). For Verbosity: Gemini 3 Pro is significantly longer with redundant explanations and a superfluous final summary section."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner (internal) response is superior in Instruction Following and Truthfulness. The SxS winner correctly proves the bound with |x_i| ≤ (A_1...A_m)^{1/(n-m)} exactly, using a minimal counterexample argument with M = min{||x||_∞} that cleanly establishes M ≤ α without floor/ceiling complications. GPT 5.2's response uses B = ⌊Q⌋, which only yields |x_i| ≤ B ≤ Q, but this creates a subtle gap: when Q is not an integer, B < Q, so GPT 5.2 actually proves a slightly weaker bound than required. The SxS winner's modulus choice MA_j with M being the minimal infinity-norm is more elegant and directly attains the exact bound. In Writing Style, both are clear, though GPT 5.2's verbose 'Overview' and 'Summary' sections add unnecessary length. For Prompt Correctness, both correctly apply Siegel's lemma, but GPT 5.2's modulus M_j = A_j(B+1) versus the SxS winner's MA_j reveals that GPT 5.2's approach needs the +1 in (B+1) to make the pigeonhole inequality work, which is less tight than the SxS winner's direct optimization."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'LaTeX equation rendering quality', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper display math delimiters ($$) with correct spacing and alignment, while the SxS winner uses plain text math notation without proper LaTeX formatting', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better formatting, 3-4 noticeably better with clear visual distinction, 5-6 substantially better rendering that significantly aids readability', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Structural organization with clear sections', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes clearly demarcated sections titled 'Overview', 'Proof', and 'Summary' with visual separators (---), while the SxS winner presents as a continuous unbroken text block", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor structural improvement, 3-4 clear organizational advantage, 5-6 dramatically superior structure that transforms readability', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit enumeration of proof conditions', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly lists and verifies all three required conditions (system of equations, not all zero, bounded) with numbered labels, while the SxS winner embeds these verifications implicitly in prose', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 slightly more explicit, 3-4 clearly more verifiable, 5-6 makes conditions unmistakably explicit and checkable', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed derivation of output bounds', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides complete step-by-step derivation showing how the maximum and minimum values of y_j are computed using P_j and N_j (positive and negative entry sums), while the SxS winner uses a modular arithmetic approach without explaining the bound derivation', 'scale