icitly uses the quasimodular form structure theorem from the setup to justify why E_2(z)^2 can be expressed as a linear combination of E_4(z) and DE_2(z), rather than invoking Ramanujan's differential identity as a known fact.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Coefficient matching for identity derivation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro derives the key identity E_2(z)^2 = E_4(z) + 12DE_2(z) by computing explicit q-expansions and matching coefficients, rather than assuming or stating the Ramanujan differential identity without derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Complete algebraic expansion of E_2(z)^2', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro fully expands E_2(z)^2 = (1 - 24∑σ_1(n)q^n)^2 and identifies the 576S_1(n) - 48σ_1(n) coefficient structure explicitly, showing the intermediate step of the Cauchy product.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Fraction simplification shown explicitly', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly shows the fraction simplifications (240/576 = 5/12, 288/576 = 1/2, 48/576 = 1/12) rather than leaving them implicit or already simplified.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'LaTeX formatting consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper LaTeX delimiters ($...$) consistently throughout mathematical expressions, while the SxS winner uses a mix of \\( ... \\) and bare text, with some formatting inconsistencies.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Structural organization with clear section headers', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent ## Proof and ## Verification headers with proper markdown formatting, while maintaining logical flow between the theoretical framework, coefficient derivation, and final formula extraction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit basis decomposition justification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly invokes the quasimodular form space decomposition theorem to justify why E_2^2 can be written as A·E_4 + B·DE_2, whereas the SxS winner assumes the Ramanujan identity without derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions decomposition briefly, 3-4 = clearly states the theorem and applies it, 5-6 = rigorously derives the specific form from first principles with coefficient comparison', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Systematic coefficient comparison for undetermined coefficients', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 solves for the unknown constants A and B by comparing constant terms and q^1 coefficients explicitly, while the SxS winner states the Ramanujan identity as known without derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = states values without work, 3-4 = shows some coefficient work, 5-6 = complete systematic derivation from space decomposition with two independent equations', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear section structure with numbered steps', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses explicit numbered sections (1) and (2) with descriptive headers, while the SxS winner uses informal paragraph breaks without clear structural demarcation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor formatting difference, 3-4 = clear section headers present, 5-6 = rigorous hierarchical structure that aids logical flow', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit handling of S_1(1)=0 edge case in generating function', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly notes that S_1(1)=0 due to no valid (a,b) pairs when defining the generating function, while the SxS winner mentions this only in verification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = me