g arguments with clear logical flow, whereas the SxS winner combines these in a more compressed direct sum decomposition.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor structural difference, 3-4 if noticeably clearer construction, 5-6 if substantially more pedagogically effective', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX formatting consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro maintains consistent LaTeX formatting with proper spacing around operators and relations, whereas the SxS winner has occasional formatting issues like missing spaces after commas and inconsistent equation alignment.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor formatting issues, 3-4 if noticeably more consistent, 5-6 if substantially superior presentation', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit use of integral domain property', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly invokes that A is an integral domain (no zero divisors) to conclude c·a_k = 0 implies c = 0, whereas the SxS winner mentions this more briefly in the direct sum argument without explicit citation of the domain property.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor difference, 3-4 if noticeably more explicit, 5-6 if substantially clearer logical step', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Section organization with clear headers', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent hierarchical section headers (## Setup and Strategy, ## Inductive Construction, ## Verification, ## Formulas) with clear visual separation, whereas the SxS winner uses similar headers but with less consistent visual weight and some redundancy.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor difference, 3-4 if noticeably better structure, 5-6 if substantially superior organization', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Handles infinite rank case', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly addresses that the proof works for modules of arbitrary rank (not just finitely generated), while the SxS winner incorrectly restricts to finitely generated free modules from the outset.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor mention of generality, 3-4