not identically vanishing. Modular forms are quasimodular forms of depth 0.\n\nThe Eisenstein series $E_2$ is a quasimodular form of weight 2 and depth 1 on $\\Gamma_0(1)$. The Eisenstein series $E_4$ is a modular form (depth 0) of weight 4 on $\\Gamma_0(1)$.\n\nLet $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{k}^{\\leq s}(N)$ denote the space of quasimodular forms of weight $k$ and depth less than or equal to $s$ on $\\Gamma_0(N)$. Let $\\mathrm{M}_{k}(N)$ be the space of modular forms of weight $k$ on $\\Gamma_0(N)$.\nIt is known that $\\mathrm{M}_{2}(1)=\\{0\\}$ and that $\\mathrm{M}_{4}(1)$ is the one-dimensional space spanned by $E_4$.\n\nLet $D$ be the differential operator $D = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\frac{d}{dz}$. If $f(z) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\widehat{f}(n) e(nz)$, then $D f(z) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n \\widehat{f}(n) e(nz)$. The operator $D$ maps $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{k}^{\\leq s}(N)$ to $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{k+2}^{\\leq s+1}(N)$.\n\nFor $k \\ge 2$ even, the space of quasimodular forms can be expressed as:\n$$ \\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{k}^{\\leq k/2}(N) = \\bigoplus_{i=0}^{k/2-1}D^i\\mathrm{M}_{k-2i}(N)\\oplus \\mathbb{C} D^{k/2-1}E_2 $$\nFor the case $k=4, N=1$, this gives:\n$$ \\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{4}^{\\leq 2}(1) = \\mathrm{M}_{4}(1) \\oplus D\\mathrm{M}_{2}(1) \\oplus \\mathbb{C} D E_2 $$\nSince $\\mathrm{M}_{2}(1)=\\{0\\}$, the space simplifies to $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{4}^{\\leq 2}(1) = \\mathrm{M}_{4}(1) \\oplus \\mathbb{C} D E_2$. Thus, any form in $\\widetilde{\\mathrm{M}}_{4}^{\\leq 2}(1)$ is a linear combination of $E_4$ and $DE_2$.\n## Theorem\nLet $n\\geq 1$. Then,\n$$ S_1(n) = \\sum_{\\substack{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}^2 \\\\ a+b=n}} \\sigma_1(a)\\sigma_1(b) = \\frac{5}{12}\\sigma_3(n) - \\frac{n}{2}\\sigma_1(n) + \\frac{1}{12}\\sigma_1(n) $$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 