2026-03-25 08:23:02,396 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_2440', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $R$ be a commutative ring and let $\\Omega = R[x_1, \\dots, x_n]$ be the polynomial ring in $n$ variables over $R$. For any polynomial $f \\in \\Omega$, $\\mathrm{supp}(f)$ denotes the set of exponent vectors of its non-zero terms. For any $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}^n$, we define $\\nabla(A) = \\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} (\\alpha + \\mathbb{N}^n)$.\n\nLet $S_1, \\dots, S_n$ be finite subsets of $R$. Let $\\Lambda$ be a finite set, and let $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda) \\in \\Omega^\\Lambda$ be a family of monic polynomials. For any $\\lambda \\in \\Lambda$, let $\\theta(\\lambda)$ be the greatest element of $\\mathrm{supp}(g(\\lambda))$ with respect to the standard partial order on $\\mathbb{N}^n$. Let $D = \\{\\theta(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda\\}$. We assume that $\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(D)$ is finite.\n\nLet $(B_a \\mid a \\in \\prod_{i=1}^n S_i)$ be a family of subsets of $\\mathbb{N}^n$ such that for each $a$, $\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(B_a)$ is finite.\n\nLet $Q$ be the ideal of $\\Omega$ defined as\n$$Q \\triangleq \\{f \\in \\Omega \\mid \\mathrm{supp}(f(x_1+a_1, \\dots, x_n+a_n)) \\subseteq \\nabla(B_a) \\text{ for all } a \\in \\prod_{i=1}^n S_i\\}.$$\nWe assume that $\\{g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda\\} \\subseteq Q$.\n\nLet $\\zeta_1$ and $\\zeta_2$ be two quantities defined as:\n$$\\zeta_1 \\triangleq \\sum_{a \\in \\prod_{i=1}^n S_i} |\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(B_a)|$$\n$$\\zeta_2 \\triangleq |\\mathbb{N}^n - \\nabla(D)|$$\nWe assume that $\\Omega/Q$ is a finitely generated free $R$-module with $\\mathrm{rank}_R(\\Omega/Q) = \\zeta_1$.\n\n**Definition (Condition (D) and (F))**\nA finite subset $X \\subseteq R$ satisfies **Condition (D)** in $R$ if for any distinct elements $c, d \\in X$, the difference $d-c$ is not a zero divisor of $R$.\nA finite subset $X \\subseteq R$ satisfies **Condition (F)** in $R$ if for any distinct elements $c, d \\in X$, the difference $d-c$ is a multiplicative unit of $R$.\n\n**Definition (Gröbner basis)**\nThe family $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a **Gröbner basis** of $Q$ if for any non-zero polynomial $\\tau \\in Q$, there exists $\\alpha \\in \\mathrm{supp}(\\tau)$ and $r \\in \\Lambda$ such that $\\theta(r) \\le \\alpha$.\n\n**Required Lemmas:**\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (1)** Suppose that for any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, $S_i$ satisfies Condition (F) in $R$. Then, we have $\\zeta_1 \\le \\zeta_2$. Moreover, if $\\zeta_1 = \\zeta_2$, then $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a Gröbner basis of $Q$.\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (2)** Suppose that $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a Gröbner basis of $Q$. Then, we have $\\zeta_2 \\le \\zeta_1$.\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 4.1 (1)** Suppose that for any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, $S_i$ satisfies Condition (D) in $R$. Then, it holds that $\\zeta_1 \\le \\zeta_2$. Moreover, $(g(\\lambda) \\mid \\lambda \\in \\Lambda)$ is a Gröbner basis of $Q$ if and only if $\\zeta_1 = \\zeta_2$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All instruction elements satisfied: [X] Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading; [X] Multiple approaches considered (localization strategy clearly articulated); [X] Honest about approach with clear high-level strategy; [X] All required lemmas (4.2(1) and 4.2(2)) used correctly; [X] Both directions of 'if and only if' proved; [X] Condition (D) hypothesis used throughout. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirements with clear section headers."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major mathematical claims verified: [VERIFIED] Localization at multiplicative set of non-zero-divisors preserves injectivity; [VERIFIED] Condition (F) holds in localized ring by construction; [VERIFIED] Lemma 4.2(1) applies to localized situation; [VERIFIED] Support preservation under localization for polynomials; [VERIFIED] Free module rank preserved under localization (flat base change). One minor issue: claim that 'localization does not change exponent supports' is slightly imprecise—supports of the polynomial as a set of exponent vectors are unchanged, but this could be clearer about coefficients vs. exponents. No false claims detected. All deductions logically valid."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Theorem 4.1(1) statement correctly interpreted; [CORRECT] Condition (D) properly distinguished from (F); [CORRECT] Inequality ζ₁ ≤ ζ₂ proved via reduction to Lemma 4.2(1); [CORRECT] 'If' direction (ζ₁=ζ₂ ⇒ Gröbner basis) proved by pullback from localization; [CORRECT] 'Only if' direction (Gröbner basis ⇒ ζ₁=ζ₂) proved using Lemma 4.2(2); [CORRECT] Equivalence fully established. The proof correctly handles the subtlety that Gröbner basis property must be pulled back from Q_T to Q, which is done via injectivity argument."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Professional mathematical writing with clear structure. Strengths: logical flow with numbered steps, clear notation, appropriate use of display math. Minor issues identified: (1) 'non–zero-divisor' vs 'non-zero-divisor' hyphenation inconsistent; (2) 'pull back' vs 'pullback' usage inconsistent; (3) slightly verbose in Background section which repeats definitions from prompt. These are trivial style preferences. No grammar errors, no clarity issues, appropriate tone for research-level mathematics."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Near-perfect calibration. The Background section restates definitions from the prompt, which could be seen as padding, but serves to fix notation and ensure self-contained readability. Nothing essential is missing: the localization construction is explicit, the pullback argument is detailed, and both directions of the equivalence are fully explained. The high-level strategy paragraph aids comprehension. Could trim ~10% by omitting some definitional restatements, but this is trivial.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: [X] Complete proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Strategy of using product Δ of all differences is a valid alternative approach to localization; [X] Honest and complete proof provided; [X] Both directions of equivalence proved; [X] Lemma 4.2(1) and 4.2(2) used correctly. The proof uses a specific element Δ rather than general multiplicative set, which is a valid variation. No instructions violated.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Major claims verified with concerns: [VERIFIED] Δ is non-zero-divisor as product of regular elements; [VERIFIED] R[Δ⁻¹] gives Condition (F); [VERIFIED] Lemma applications correct. However, [QUESTIONABLE] claim that 'g ∈ Q' can be written g = h/Δ^k with h ∈ Ω' — this seems to conflate Q and Q'; actually g ∈ Q' means g = h/Δ^k where h ∈ Q, not just h ∈ Ω. The text says 'h ∈ Q' which is correct but the surrounding logic is slightly garbled: 'g ∈ Q' can be written...' should be 'g ∈ Q''. More seriously, [UNVERIFIABLE] the claim 'Conversely, g ∈ Q' can be written...' appears to have a typo where Q' is meant; the logic that Q' = QR' is correct but the presentation is confusing. The equation reference \\eqref{eq:quot} is used but not defined with \\label in the visible text (may be LaTeX artifact). These issues create uncertainty about the exact logical flow."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Overall structure and conclusion valid; [CORRECT] Inequality direction and equivalence established. However, [PARTIALLY CORRECT] the presentation of Q' = QR' has logical gaps: the argument for 'Conversely' is confusingly written with apparent typo (Q vs Q'). The pullback argument in Step 3 is less detailed than Response A—specifically, the injectivity of Ω → Ω' is asserted via Δ being non-zero-divisor, but the connection to support preservation and the Gröbner condition is less explicit. The proof is correct in outline but the execution has clarity issues that could mask subtle errors."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Quality audit: Issues identified: (1) Inconsistent notation: uses both Q' and QR' interchangeably without clear distinction; (2) Typo/grammar: 'g ∈ Q' can be written' should be 'g ∈ Q''; (3) Dense presentation with less explanatory text than Response A; (4) The equation reference \\eqref{eq:quot} appears without visible \\label, suggesting formatting issue; (5) 'Conversely' paragraph is confusingly structured. The writing is more terse and assumes more from the reader, which in a research context is acceptable but here creates ambiguity about the exact logical flow."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Well-calibrated, actually slightly more concise than ideal. The brevity in Step 3 (equality ⇔ Gröbner basis) skips some details that Response A includes—specifically, the explicit argument for why non-zero elements remain non-zero in the localization and how this interacts with the Gröbner condition. The pullback is asserted rather than fully explained. This is not excessive padding, but rather a gap that could be filled. Nothing is clearly redundant, but some expansion would improve clarity.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.65, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.65'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better due to clearer structure and more rigorous handling of the pullback step. Response A explicitly proves that τ/1 ≠ 0 in Ω_T (using that T consists of non-zero-divisors and Ω is R-flat), while Response B merely asserts injectivity of Ω→Ω' without justification. Response A also better explains why localization preserves the rank condition and Gröbner basis property. Response B's Step 2 uses an indirect contradiction argument rather than direct application of Lemma 4.2 (1), and its handling of Q' = QR' verification is more compressed."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner provides a cleaner, more rigorous proof with better logical flow, while Gemini 3 Pro contains distracting errors and less precise reasoning. SxS winner correctly handles the 'if and only if' bidirectional structure in Steps 3-4, explicitly proving injectivity of localization using the flatness of polynomial rings (citing 'free, thus flat, R-module'), and clearly separates the pullback argument for Gröbner basis property. Gemini 3 Pro makes several errors: it defines V as {c-d} but then uses it inconsistently, contains garbled text ('forms a Gröbner basis' with spacing issues), makes an imprecise claim that 'injectivity preserves support' without explaining why τ remains nonzero in the localization, and has a confusing Step 2 where it claims 'ζ₁ also remains invariant' without justification for why the B_a structure is preserved under the different indexing set ∏S_i' versus ∏S_i. The SxS winner's argument that 'localization does not change supports' is more directly justified and its handling of the module rank preservation is cleaner."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both the SxS winner and GPT 5.2 produced mathematically identical proofs with the same structure, content, and LaTeX formatting. The responses are verbatim duplicates: both use the same high-level strategy of localization to reduce Condition (D) to Condition (F), identical four-step proof organization (localization, proving ζ₁ ≤ ζ₂, 'if' direction via pullback, 'only if' direction via Lemma 4.2(2)), and identical technical details including the argument that tτ = 0 implies τ = 0 due to flatness of Ω over R. No differences exist in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, or Verbosity—both correctly prove Theorem 4.1(1) with complete mathematical rigor and proper handling of the Gröbner basis definition involving supports rather than leading terms."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit localization functor properties', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that localization is an exact functor and explains that it preserves exact sequences and maps finitely generated free modules to finitely generated free modules preserving rank, whereas the SxS winner only mentions freeness preservation without the functorial justification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of exactness, 3-4 = clear statement with some explanation, 5-6 = detailed explanation with explicit connection to rank preservation', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed support preservation argument', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides explicit reasoning for why support is preserved under localization (non-zero coefficients remain non-zero, multiplication by units leaves support invariant), while the SxS winner asserts this without detailed justification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = assertion without reasoning, 3-4 = some reasoning provided, 5-6 = thorough step-by-step justification with explicit connections to coefficient behavior', 'rating': 4}, {'name': "Explicit injectivity argument for R → R'", 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly proves and states that the canonical homomorphism R → R' is strictly injective due to absence of zero divisors in U, identifying R as a subring of R', whereas the SxS winner mentions injectivity more briefly without this explicit structural identification.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of injectivity, 3-4 = clear statement with reason, 5-6 = detailed proof with structural consequence (R as subring)', 'rating': 4}, {'name': "Bidirectional verification of Q' = U^{-1}Q", 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro provides explicit two-direction verification that Q' equals U^{-1}Q with detailed reasoning about support preservation under scaling by units, while the SxS winner asserts this equality without proof.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = assertion only, 3-4 = one direction or brief argument, 5-6 = complete bidirectional proof with detailed support analysis', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit cardinality preservation of S_i', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly notes that injectivity of φ ensures |S_i'| = |S_i|, a concrete detail omitted by the SxS winner.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = not mentioned, 3-4 = mentioned in passing, 5-6 = explicitly proven and utilized', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'LaTeX formatting consistency', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro has several formatting issues including missing spaces after periods ('formsa', 'areunits', 'non-zeroterms'), inconsistent math mode spacing, and broken line breaks, while the SxS winner maintains cleaner LaTeX formatting throughout.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = numerous formatting errors, 3-4 = some errors, 5-6 = clean professional formatting', 'rating': 2}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 36133, 'output_tokens': 3042, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.10877}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.10877}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.