cit transfinite induction machinery, making it more direct. The construction of N_i = {x ∈ N | x_j = 0 for all j < i} is clever and cleaner than Response A's F_α intersection approach. The generation argument using least index and iterative subtraction is correct and well-explained. The independence argument using minimal index is correct and explicitly uses the domain property. The final isomorphism N ≅ A^(B) is correct. Every requirement is satisfied with clear, correct reasoning."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear, well-structured mathematical writing with good signposting. Strengths: explicit 'Proof.' opening; 'First suppose... Now let...' guides reader through structure; 'Generation.' and 'Independence.' as explicit section headers aid navigation; 'Hence' and 'Thus' provide logical connectives. Minor issues: (1) 'Let B = {b_i | a_i ≠ 0}' — should specify this is a set indexed by I, though clear from context; (2) 'the support of x is finite' is parenthetical but could be more prominent; (3) 'A^{(B)}' notation for free module might be unfamiliar to some readers (means direct sum of |B| copies). These are trivial. The writing is professional and accessible."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Nothing can be cut because: the finite rank base case is essential; the case analysis (a = 0 vs a ≠ 0) is necessary for completeness; the definition of N_i requires the explicit coordinate condition; the generation and independence arguments need their full detail; the final boxed conclusion provides appropriate closure. Nothing is missing because: the domain property is explicitly used where needed; the rank bound ≤ |I| is stated; both generation and independence are fully proved. The length is ideal: shorter than Response A while being equally complete, with better efficiency.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.35, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.35'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 2, 'overall_comment': "Response B is moderately better. Both proofs correctly establish the theorem using transfinite induction, but B has clearer structure and exposition: it explicitly separates the finite case (with cleaner handling of the a=0 vs a≠0 cases) and uses more intuitive notation (N_i as 'elements with support starting at i' vs F_α/N_α construction). B's 'Generation' and 'Independence' subsections make the argument more readable, while A's dense presentation with ordinal notation κ and nested subscripts creates unnecessary cognitive load. A also contains a minor notational awkwardness (using β<α for indexing when α is the current stage) and less explicit justification for why the sum is direct in the limit case."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and completeness, while Gemini 3 Pro contains critical errors and limitations. Instruction Following: SxS winner correctly handles both finite and infinite-rank free modules via transfinite induction with well-ordering; Gemini 3 Pro incorrectly assumes F has finite rank ('standard basis {e_1, ..., e_n}'), failing the general case. Truthfulness: SxS winner's proof is fully correct; Gemini 3 Pro's proof has a subtle error in linear independence—claiming 'v_k is linearly independent' rather than the correct statement that {v_k} generates a rank-1 free submodule, and its 'Formulas' section is redundant padding. Prompt Correctness: SxS winner directly proves the stated theorem; Gemini 3 Pro adds unnecessary restrictive assumptions. Writing Style: SxS winner is concise and elegant; Gemini 3 Pro is excessively verbose with redundant sections ('Formulas') and conversational filler ('The theorem we aim to prove...'). Verbosity: SxS winner is appropriately terse for expert readers; Gemini 3 Pro is ~3x longer with minimal added value."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) is better on Instruction Following and Truthfulness, while GPT 5.2 is more verbose but contains a critical error. The SxS winner correctly proves the theorem for arbitrary free modules (any rank, possibly infinite) using transfinite induction with a well-ordering, which is the standard and complete proof. GPT 5.2 incorrectly restricts to finite rank n throughout, stating 'let F be a free A-module of finite rank n' in its setup—this is a significant limitation since the theorem holds for all free modules, not just finitely generated ones. While GPT 5.2's finite case proof is correct and well-structured with clear formulas, its restriction makes it incomplete. The SxS winner's proof handles both the finite and infinite cases rigorously, with proper handling of the general case via well-ordering. GPT 5.2's verbosity (extensive formulas section, redundant verification section) does not compensate for its scope limitation."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit projection map definition', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines the projection maps π_i with a clear formula showing how they extract the i-th coordinate from a general element of F, whereas the SxS winner only mentions projection without defining it formally.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = formula present but minimal, 3-4 = clear explicit definition with proper notation, 5-6 = comprehensive definition with explanation of well-definedness and homomorphism property', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Structured section headers', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses clear hierarchical section headers (## Setup and Strategy, ## Inductive Construction, ## Verification, ## Formulas) to organize the proof, while the SxS winner uses minimal structural markers.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = some headers present, 3-4 = clear logical sectioning with descriptive headers, 5-6 = comprehensive hierarchical structure that guides reader through proof architecture', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit base case statement', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly labels and fully develops the base case (n=1) with detailed reasoning about the ideal I_1, generator a_1, and the two subcases (a_1=0 and a_1≠0), whereas the SxS winner compresses this into a single terse sentence.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = base case mentioned, 3-4 = explicit labeled base case with clear reasoning, 5-6 = fully elaborated base case with complete case analysis and detailed justification', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Separate linear independence and spanning proofs', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly separates the verification of linear independence and spanning into distinct, clearly labeled subsections with step-by-step derivations, while the SxS winner combines these into brief italicized paragraphs.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = both properties verified, 3-4 = explicit separation with clear structure, 5-6 = fully elaborated separate proofs with detailed algebraic steps and justifications', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Centralized formula summary section', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated '## Formulas' section at the end that enumerates all key equations used in the proof with descriptive labels, a feature completely absent from the SxS winner.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = formulas listed without context, 3-4 = numbered formulas with brief descriptions, 5-6 = comprehensive, labeled enumeration with clear references to proof locations', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Explicit kernel submodule definition', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines N' = N ∩ ker(π_k) as a named submodule with clear notation, while the SxS winner uses the equivalent construction without explicit definition or notation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = kernel intersection used implicitly, 3-4 = explicit definition with clear notation, 5-6 = formal definition with explanation of why it is a submodule', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed algebraic step exposition', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro shows explicit intermediate algebraic steps such as π_k(x - qv_k) = π_k(x) - qπ_k(v_k) = qa_k - qa_k = 0, whereas the SxS winner states conclusions without showing the computation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = some steps shown, 3-4 = key algebraic manipulations explicitly displayed, 5-6 = comprehensive step-by-step derivations with all intermediate equalities', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit rank bound tracking', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly tracks and states the rank bound (rank ≤ k-1, rank ≤ k) throughout the inductive argument, while the SxS winner only mentions this in passing at the end.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = rank mentioned occasionally, 3-4 = explicit rank bounds stated at key steps, 5-6 = systematic tracking of rank with clear inequalities at each inductive stage', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'LaTeX equation rendering quality', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses proper LaTeX display environments with $$...$$ delimiters for multi-line equations and aligned structures, while the SxS winner uses minimal or inline LaTeX formatting', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better LaTeX, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence of proper environments, 5-6 substantially superior rendering with consistent display math', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Structural organization with explicit sections', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes clearly demarcated sections with headers (## Setup and Strategy, ## Inductive Construction, ## Verification, ## Formulas) that organize the proof logically, while the SxS winner presents as a continuous block without section breaks', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor structural improvements, 3-4 clear sectioning that aids navigation, 5-6 comprehensive hierarchical organization with explicit headers', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit verification section with basis construction', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Verification' section that explicitly constructs the basis B = {x, f_2, ..., f_r} and proves both linear independence and spanning, while the SxS winner omits this explicit basis verification step", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor additional verification, 3-4 explicit basis construction with proofs, 5-6 complete rigorous verification with all details', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Formula summary section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 concludes with a numbered 'Formulas' section listing 10 key equations used in the proof, while the SxS winner has no such summary", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 brief mention of formulas, 3-4 organized list of key equations, 5-6 comprehensive numbered reference of all critical formulas', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Explicit coordinate projection definitions', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly defines the coordinate projection maps π_i: F → A with formula π_i(∑ a_j e_j) = a_i, while the SxS winner uses π without explicit definition', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions projections, 3-4 explicit definition with domain/codomain, 5-6 complete formal definition with explicit formula', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Handling of arbitrary rank case', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 focuses on and completes the finite rank case rigorously, while the SxS winner attempts but contains errors in the infinite/well-ordered case (the N_i definition has issues with well-ordering and the proof is incomplete)', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor differences in approach, 3-4 one proof is complete while other has gaps, 5-6 one proof is fully rigorous while other contains significant errors', 'rating': 4}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response B', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 20993, 'output_tokens': 4247, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.09295}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.09295}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_toke