elds]'}], 'rubric_ratings': [{'rubric_name': 'Overall Strategy Statement', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response begins with a clear, explicit strategy statement: 'Use Zorn's lemma to extend the given isomorphism to a maximal subfield of P, then show this subfield must be all of P by extending further at any missing algebraic element; surjectivity follows because the image is a splitting field of Ω̄.' This covers all key aspects (Zorn's lemma, maximality, extension to all of P, surjectivity) in exactly two sentences before any detailed argument. I considered whether this could be more detailed, but the rubric only requires one or two sentences, and this is complete and precise."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response begins with an explicit strategy statement: 'We enlarge the given isomorphism Φ→Φ̄ step-by-step to subfields of the splitting field P by adjoining one root at a time. Zorn’s Lemma gives a maximal such extension (M,ψ); the fact that P is generated by the roots of the polynomials in Ω forces M=P. Because P̄ is generated by the corresponding roots of Ω̄, the maximal map is automatically onto.' This is three sentences but covers all required elements. I considered whether the slightly longer length is a fault, but the rubric allows 'one or two sentences' and this is still concise and complete."}, 'gemini': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response begins with: 'We will use Zorn's lemma on the partially ordered set of intermediate subfield isomorphisms to establish the existence of a maximal extension of the base isomorphism. We will then show by contradiction that this maximal extension must be defined on all of P because P is generated by the roots of Ω, and finally deduce that the map is surjective because its image contains all roots generating P̄.' This covers all elements but is somewhat verbose and run-on. I considered whether the length and slightly awkward phrasing ('baseisomorphism' as one word, 'isgenerated') constitutes a fault; while minor, the strategy is still clearly stated and complete."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response begins with: 'Consider all partial extensions of the given base-field isomorphism to isomorphisms between intermediate subfields of the two splitting fields. Use Zorn’s lemma to obtain a maximal such partial extension, then show maximality forces its domain to contain every root used to generate the splitting field P, hence its domain is all of P; finally show its image must be all of P̄.' This is exactly two sentences covering all key elements (Zorn's lemma, maximality, extension to all of P, surjectivity) with clear, precise language. I considered whether anything is missing, and found no fault."}}, {'rubric_name': "Zorn's Lemma Application", 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Zorn's lemma is explicitly invoked: 'so by Zorn's lemma there is a maximal element (M, τ).' The setup includes the partially ordered set S of pairs (K,τ), the ordering, and verification that chains have upper bounds. I considered whether the verification of chain upper bounds is sufficiently detailed; it states 'Every chain has an upper bound (union of fields, union of maps)' which is correct but somewhat brief. The application is clear and correct."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Zorn's lemma is explicitly invoked: 'Hence, by Zorn’s Lemma, S contains a maximal element (M,ψ).' The setup is thorough: the set S is defined, the ordering is specified, non-emptiness is verified, and chains having upper bounds is proved with explicit construction (union of fields, union of maps with well-definedness check). I considered whether any element is missing and found the application complete and rigorous."}, 'gemini': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Zorn's lemma is explicitly invoked: 'By Zorn's lemma, S contains a maximal element, which we will call (M, σ).' The setup includes the set S, the ordering, and verification that chains have upper bounds with explicit construction. I considered whether the verification is complete; it mentions 'K* is a field and τ* is a well-defined isomorphism' but doesn't explicitly verify the isomorphism property or that the image stays in P̄. The application is present and mostly correct."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Zorn's lemma is explicitly invoked: 'By Zorn’s lemma, S has a maximal element; fix one and denote it by (E_max, Ē_max, θ_max).' The setup is thorough: the set S is defined with triples (E,Ē,θ), the ordering is specified, non-emptiness is verified, and chains having upper bounds is proved with explicit construction including well-definedness of the union map. I considered whether any element is missing and found the application complete and rigorous."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Maximal Extension Verification Location', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof explicitly identifies the verification location with a labeled section '**Verification that M = P:**' and the concluding sentence 'Thus M = P.' The verification is clearly demarcated. I considered whether the explicit label 'Verification that M = P' satisfies the rubric's requirement to 'explicitly identify where in your proof you verify that this maximal extension is defined on all of P'; it does so clearly."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof explicitly identifies the verification location with a labeled section '### 2. The maximal domain is all of P' and explicitly states '*This is the point where we verify that the maximal extension is defined on all of P.*' at the end of that section. This is exceptionally clear identification. I considered whether anything more could be asked, and found this exceeds the requirement."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof explicitly identifies the verification location with a labeled subsection '*Explicit verification that the maximal extension is defined on all of P:*' and the concluding statement 'This contradiction proves that our assumption M ⊊ P was false, verifying that M = P and the maximal extension is indeed defined on all of P.' This is exceptionally clear identification. I considered whether anything more could be asked, and found this exceeds the requirement."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof explicitly identifies the verification location with a labeled section '### 4) Where we force the maximal extension to be defined on all of P' and explicitly states '### Conclusion (this is the explicit point where we verify the maximal extension is defined on all of P):' followed by the conclusion 'hence E_max = P.' This is exceptionally clear identification with explicit self-referential language. I considered whether anything more could be asked, and found this exceeds the requirement."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Simple Extension Polynomial and Root Specification', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "When extending to the simple algebraic extension, the response states: '**Here, when extending to the simple algebraic extension M(α)/M, we consider the polynomial f(x) and map α to the root β of f̄(x).**' However, this is slightly imprecise: it says 'the polynomial f(x)' but actually uses the minimal polynomial m(x) (denoted as f(x) in the response, which is confusing). Looking more carefully: the response defines 'let f(x) ∈ M[x] be its minimal polynomial over M' then says 'we consider the polynomial f(x) and map α to the root β of f̄(x).' So technically the polynomial is specified (f, the minimal polynomial) and the root β is specified. I considered whether the explicit bolded statement satisfies the requirement; it does, though the notation could be clearer."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "When extending to the simple algebraic extension, the response explicitly states: 'Let m(x) ∈ M[x] be the minimal polynomial of α over M (so m|f in M[x]).' Then: 'Apply ψ to the coefficients of m; we obtain the polynomial ψ(m)(x) ∈ ψ(M)[x] ⊆ P̄[x].' Then: 'Consequently ψ(m) has a root β ∈ P̄.' Then: 'Now extend ψ to the simple algebraic extension M(α) by ψ̃(α)=β.' Both the specific polynomial m(x) (minimal polynomial) and the specific root β are explicitly stated. I considered whether anything is missing and found this complete."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "When extending to the simple algebraic extension, the response explicitly states: 'we specifically consider the root r and its minimal polynomial m(x) ∈ M[x] over M.' Then: 'applying the isomorphism σ to the coefficients yields the image polynomial m̄(x) = σ(m(x)), which must divide f̄(x) = σ(f(x))... m̄(x) must have a root r̄ in P̄.' Then: 'we can uniquely extend σ to an isomorphism σ': M(r) → σ(M)(r̄) by mapping the root r to the root r̄.' Both the specific polynomial m(x) and specific roots r and r̄ are explicitly stated. I considered whether anything is missing and found this complete."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "When extending to the simple algebraic extension, the response explicitly states in the Lemma: 'Let α be algebraic over E, and let m_α(x) ∈ E[x] be the minimal polynomial of α over E. Let m̄_α(x) ∈ Ē[x] be the polynomial obtained by applying θ to the coefficients... If β is a root of m̄_α(x)... then θ extends to an isomorphism θ': E(α) → Ē(β) with θ'(α)=β.' Then in the application: 'Let m(x) ∈ E_max[x] be the minimal polynomial of α over E_max... Choose β ∈ P̄ such that m̄(β)=0... We obtain an extension θ': E_max(α) → Ē_max(β)... with θ'(α)=β.' Both the specific polynomial m(x) and specific root β are explicitly stated. I considered whether anything is missing and found this complete."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Surjectivity Explanation', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof concludes with a section '**Surjectivity:**' that explains: 'The image τ(P) is a subfield of P̄ containing Φ̄ and all roots of Ω̄ (since P contains all roots of Ω and τ maps each root of f ∈ Ω to a root of f̄ ∈ Ω̄). As P̄ is the smallest such field, τ(P) = P̄.' This is a brief, explicit explanation of why the map is surjective. I considered whether this could be more detailed; it covers the key points (image contains generators, splitting field is minimal) concisely."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof concludes with a section '### 3. Surjectivity onto P̄' that explains: 'P̄/Φ̄ is a splitting field of Ω̄; therefore it is generated over Φ̄ by the roots of the polynomials f̄ (f ∈ Ω). For each such root ᾱ there is an f ∈ Ω and a root α ∈ P with ψ(α)=ᾱ... Thus every generator of P̄ lies in ψ(P); consequently ψ(P) = P̄.' This is a clear explanation. I considered whether this is 'brief' as requested; it is appropriately concise but perhaps slightly more verbose than necessary. The explanation is correct and explicit."}, 'gemini': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof concludes with a section '*Surjectivity onto P̄:*' that explains: 'The image σ(P) is a subfield of P̄ that contains Φ̄... σ(P) must contain all the roots of every image polynomial f̄ ∈ Ω̄. By definition, the splitting field P̄ is generated over Φ̄ entirely by the roots of the polynomials in Ω̄. Since all of these generating roots already lie in the subfield σ(P), it follows necessarily that σ(P) = P̄.' This is a clear explanation. I considered whether this is 'brief'; it is somewhat verbose but covers the key points correctly."}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof concludes with a section '### 5) Why the resulting map is surjective onto P̄' that explains: 'It remains to show Ē_max = P̄... Thus every f̄ ∈ Ω̄ splits over Ē_max. Equivalently, Ē_max contains all roots of every polynomial in Ω̄. But P̄ is (by definition) generated over Φ̄ by all those roots, so P̄ ⊆ Ē_max. Since also Ē_max ⊆ P̄, we get Ē_max = P̄.' This is a clear, explicit explanation. I considered whether this is 'brief'; it is appropriately detailed and correct, though the section title is longer than 'brief' might suggest. The explanation itself is concise."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Mathematical Correctness', 'opalite': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof is mostly correct but has some issues. First, it states 'let f(x) ∈ M[x] be its minimal polynomial over M' then later 'Let g(x) ∈ Φ[x] be the minimal polynomial of α over Φ' — this is confusing notation since f is used for two different polynomials. More seriously, it claims 'Since P/Φ is normal (splitting fields are normal)' — but P/Φ is only normal if Φ is perfect or if we assume separability; the theorem doesn't assume separability. The argument that f divides g in M[x] is correct, but the normality claim is not generally valid without separability assumptions. The surjectivity argument is correct."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct. It correctly handles the extension step using minimal polynomials, and the Zorn's lemma application is standard. One minor issue: it states 'Because P/Φ is a splitting field of Ω, α is a root of some f ∈ Ω' — this is true but doesn't immediately imply α is algebraic over M; however, since P/Φ is algebraic (as a splitting field), α is algebraic over Φ and hence over M. The proof correctly notes α is algebraic over M. The surjectivity argument correctly uses that P̄ is generated by roots of Ω̄. I considered whether there are any significant errors and found none."}, 'gemini': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof has some issues. It states 'Because P is the splitting field of Ω over Φ, P is generated over Φ (and hence over M) by the roots of the polynomials in Ω' — this is correct. However, it claims 'If M ≠ P, there must exist some polynomial f ∈ Ω that does not split completely in M, meaning there exists a root r ∈ P \\ M of f' — this is correct. But then it says 'we specifically consider the root r and its minimal polynomial m(x) ∈ M[x] over M' — this is fine. The main issue is that it doesn't explicitly verify that the extended map is well-defined or that the image stays in P̄; it just asserts this. The surjectivity argument is correct. I found no major errors but some gaps in rigor."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct and rigorous. It correctly sets up the Zorn's lemma argument with triples (E,Ē,θ) ensuring the codomain is tracked explicitly. The simple extension lemma is stated precisely with minimal polynomial m_α and root β. The application correctly shows that if E_max ≠ P, we can extend further, contradicting maximality. The surjectivity argument correctly shows Ē_max contains all roots of Ω̄ and hence equals P̄. I considered potential issues: the proof correctly handles that m|f implies m̄|f̄, and that splitting of f̄ implies existence of root β for m̄. No errors found."}}], 'criteria': ['structural organization', 'logical progression structure', 'explicit specification requirements', 'mathematical correctness'], 'usage': {'input_tokens': 9362, 'output_tokens': 4061, 'cached_tokens': 0}, 'error': None, 'task_id': 'tv_stem_15390'}] 
