nds uniquely, and applying to relation yields e_j=0. [CORRECT] Extreme cases handled properly. All claims verified against standard group theory. No outdated information. No incorrect conclusions. Score 6 justified with explicit verification of each requirement."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths: Clear three-stage structure with visual separators; consistent mathematical notation; logical flow from definitions to conclusion. Issues identified: 1) Minor: 'abelianisation' (British spelling) is consistent but may vary by audience preference — trivial. 2) Minor: 'Fix one index j' could be 'Fix an index j' — trivial preference. 3) The phrase 'the universal property of the free group' is bolded mid-text, slightly breaking flow but emphasizing key tool. No run-on sentences, no grammar errors, appropriate formal tone for mathematical proof. Before scoring 6: re-read for any improvement — the bold formatting of theorem statements and stage headers is slightly heavy but serves clarity. Score 5 for one minor style element (bold mid-sentence) that could be smoother."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof is medium-long (requires three substantial stages plus extreme cases). Response achieves this. Nothing to cut: Stage 1 (abelian) is minimal; Stage 2 (generation) efficiently uses universal property; Stage 3 (independence) requires the homomorphism construction. Extreme cases add value. Nothing missing: All three requirements of free abelian group (abelian, generated by basis, linearly independent basis) are proven. Before scoring 6: Could Stage 1 be shorter? It's already minimal. Could extreme cases be omitted? No, they address 'any basis X' as claimed. Score 5 — near-perfect, only trivial trimming possible (e.g., 'We prove this in three stages' is slightly redundant with headers)."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist: [X] Prove F/F' is abelian (Stage 1), [X] Prove X_# generates F/F' (Stage 2), [X] Prove X_# is Z-linearly independent (Stage 3), [X] Address extreme cases (empty and infinite X). All required elements present. No unrequested content. Format matches mathematical proof conventions. Before scoring 6: checked for missing steps — Stage 2 uses 'reorder factors' which is slightly implicit but valid in abelian quotient; independence proof complete; extreme cases covered. No additions beyond proof. Score justified."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] Commutator [a,b] = aba^{-1}b^{-1} definition correct. [VERIFIED] F/F' abelian via (ab)F' = (ba)[a,b]F' = (ba)F'. [VERIFIED] Reduced word representation and exponent sum n_x is valid. [VERIFIED] Universal property application with δ_{ij} notation correct. [VERIFIED] Empty and infinite cases handled properly. [UNVERIFIABLE] No citations to standard texts. One minor concern: In Stage 2, 'we may reorder the factors' is stated without explicit justification — in abelian group this is valid, but the step 'In the abelian quotient we may reorder' is correct. No false claims. Score 5 due to lack of explicit citations, though mathematically sound."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] F/F' abelian — proof correct, though Stage 1 uses slightly different commutator manipulation than Response A, both valid. [CORRECT] X_# generates — the exponent sum argument is correct: in abelianization, word reduces to product of (xF')^{n_x}. [CORRECT] X_# linearly independent — homomorphism construction with δ_{ij} is standard and correct. [CORRECT] Extreme cases valid. No errors detected. The Stage 2 approach using exponent sums is actually more direct than Response A's approach. Score 6 with explicit verification."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths: Clean three-stage structure; good use of δ_{ij} notation for clarity; slightly more concise than Response A in Stage 2. Issues: 1) 'Recall: F' is the subgroup...' colon usage slightly inconsistent (Stage 1 vs 'Recall that' in Response A). 2) 'the independence argument (Stage 3) involve only finitely many' — 'involve' should be 'involves' (subject-verb agreement: 'argument...involves'). 3) 'Thus for any basis X (empty, finite, or infinite)' — parentheses slightly awkward. Minor grammar issue in extreme cases section prevents 6. Otherwise clear, appropriate tone, good paragraph breaks. Score 5."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length achieved. Stage 1 is slightly more concise than Response A while equally rigorous. Stage 2's exponent sum approach is more direct than Response A's projection argument. Stage 3 matches Response A in length. Extreme cases efficiently handled. Nothing to cut: each sentence serves the proof. Nothing missing: all requirements covered. Before scoring 6: explicit check — Stage 2's 'we may reorder' is efficient; could add 'since F/F' is abelian' but implied by context. The δ_{ij} notation in Stage 3 is elegant. Score 6 justified: 'Nothing can be cut because each stage is minimal and rigorous; nothing is missing because all three defining properties of free abelian group are proven with extreme cases covered.'"}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.6, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.60'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both responses are mathematically equivalent and equally rigorous, with nearly identical structure (three stages, same universal property argument, same extreme case verification). Response A uses slightly more formal notation (\\widetilde f_j, \\bar f_j vs \\varphi_j, \\overline{\\varphi}_j) and explicitly states 'reduced word' in Stage 2, while Response B more clearly notes that reordering in Stage 2 uses abelianness. Neither has meaningful errors or omissions; the differences are cosmetic."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and clarity across Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Writing Style metrics. The SxS winner provides cleaner notation (using \\varepsilon for exponents, proper set-builder notation for X_\\#), more precise stage separation with visual dividers, and explicitly verifies the homomorphism factorization through F' \\subseteq \\ker\\widetilde{f}_j. The SxS winner also handles the infinite case more elegantly by noting 'words are finite' and that the independence argument treats finite relations. Gemini 3 Pro contains distracting formatting errors (spacing issues like 'agroup', 'thequotient', 'anytwo'), uses less standard notation (\\epsilon instead of \\varepsilon, n_i instead of e_i), and has verbose phrasing ('explicitly invoke the universal property') that reduces concision. While both proofs are truthfully correct, Gemini 3 Pro's Stage 3 presentation is more convoluted—defining \\varphi then \\bar{\\varphi} with redundant explanatory clauses—whereas the SxS winner's parallel structure (\\widetilde{f}_j to \\bar{f}_j) is more elegant. Both handle edge cases adequately, though the SxS winner's 'Verification for extreme cases' section is better organized."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': None, 'comparison_comment': ''}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit universal property invocation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names and states the universal property of free groups before applying it, whereas the SxS winner only implicitly uses it without formal statement.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 implicit vs explicit mention, 3-4 clear statement with application, 5-6 full formal statement with precise notation', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Quotient homomorphism notation consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro consistently uses π for the canonical projection throughout all three stages, while the SxS winner switches between π and bar notation (f̄_j) without clear connection.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor inconsistency, 3-4 noticeable notational drift, 5-6 completely unified notation system', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit kernel argument for quotient factorization', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that F' ⊆ ker(φ) and invokes the universal property of quotients to justify the descent to φ̄, while the SxS winner merely asserts the factorization without this justification.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions kernel containment, 3-4 explicitly justifies factorization, 5-6 full rigorous justification with universal property of quotients', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Multiplicative vs additive notation clarity', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly translates between multiplicative notation in F/F' and additive notation in ℤ when applying the homomorphism, showing the sum ∑ n_i φ̄(x_iF'), while the SxS winner omits this explicit translation step.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 implicit translation, 3-4 explicit translation shown, 5-6 fully explicit with clear notational distinction', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Arbitrary index selection clarity', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states 'To isolate a specific coefficient n_j (where 1 ≤ j ≤ k)' and 'Since j was chosen arbitrarily,' making the logical structure transparent, while the SxS winner uses 'Fix one index j' without explicit arbitrary quantification.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor clarity difference, 3-4 explicit quantification, 5-6 fully rigorous with clear logical flow', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Empty set function definition', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly describes the empty set map f: ∅ → ℤ in the verification section, while the SxS winner merely states the empty set is the basis without this explicit construction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions empty set, 3-4 explicit function definition, 5-6 full formal treatment', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent $...$ inline delimiters throughout, while the SxS winner mixes \\( ... \\) and $...$ delimiters (e.g., \\(F^{\\operatorname{ab}}\\) vs $F'$).", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor inconsistency, 3-4 noticeable mixing, 5-6 completely inconsistent vs unified', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit universal property invocation', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly states and invokes the universal property of free groups with the phrase 'Now explicitly invoke the universal property of free groups' and explains that any function from X to any group extends uniquely to a homomorphism, whereas the SxS winner only implicitly uses this property without naming or explaining it.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if marginally more explicit, 3-4 if clearly better explained, 5-6 if substantially clearer with explicit naming', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear equivalence between coset and element equations', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly shows the equivalence between equation (1) in the quotient and equation (2) in F, writing '(1) is equivalent to (2)' with the explicit element form x_1^{n_1}...x_k^{n_k} ∈ F', while the SxS winner works only with the coset equation without this explicit translation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor clarification, 3-4 if helpful bridge, 5-6 if critical missing step', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit commutator calculation in abelian proof', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly computes φ_i([u,v]) = 0 with the full expansion showing why commutators map to zero, while the SxS winner merely states 'every commutator lies in ker' without showing the calculation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor detail, 3-4 if helpful demonstration, 5-6 if critical pedagogical gap', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Consistent use of numbered stages with visual separators', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses consistent 'Stage 1:', 'Stage 2:', 'Stage 3:' la