2026-03-25 08:24:40,287 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10536', 'prompt': 'Prove the following: \\n\\nIf \\(F\\) is a free group with basis \\(X\\), then \\(F / F^{\\prime}\\) is a free abelian group with basis \\(X_{\\#}=\\left\\{x F^{\\prime}: x \\in X\\right\\}\\).', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The prompt asks to prove that F/F' is a free abelian group with basis X_#. Response A provides a complete proof with all required components: (1) shows X_# generates F/F', (2) proves independence of X_#, (3) establishes uniqueness of expression, and (4) constructs the isomorphism to ⊕ℤ. No explicit format constraints were given, so the mathematical proof format is appropriate. I checked for: missing proof steps (none found), wrong structure (none), added unrequested content (none). All instruction elements satisfied."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major mathematical claims are correct and verifiable: [VERIFIED] universal property of free groups correctly applied; [VERIFIED] [F,F] ≤ ker f_x since ℤ is abelian; [VERIFIED] f_x induces well-defined homomorphism on quotient; [VERIFIED] reduced word representation in free groups; [VERIFIED] commutativity allows reordering in abelianization; [VERIFIED] independence argument via evaluation homomorphisms. One minor issue: the claim 'xN ≠ N because f̄_x(xN) = 1' is correct but could note this requires x ≠ 1 in F, which holds for basis elements. No false claims found. Score capped at 5 due to lack of explicit citation to standard references, though the mathematics is self-contained and verifiable."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] F/F' is abelian — shown via universal property with maps to ℤ; [CORRECT] X_# generates F/F' — explicit construction showing any gN is product of (xN)^{n_x}; [CORRECT] X_# is a basis — independence proven via evaluation homomorphisms f̄_x; [CORRECT] free abelian structure — isomorphism Φ: ⊕ℤ → A explicitly constructed and proven bijective. The conclusion 'A ≅ ⊕_{x∈X} ℤ' with X_# as basis is fully established. No incorrect or missing elements."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: The proof uses professional mathematical writing with clear section headers (Generation, Independence, Uniqueness of expression). LaTeX formatting is clean and readable. Sentence structure is precise and appropriate for advanced mathematics. One minor issue: the phrase 'with n_x = ∑_{i:x_i=x} ε_i, n_x = 0 for all but finitely many x' is slightly compressed; could benefit from 'where n_x = ... and n_x = 0 ...' for clarity. No grammar errors, no jargon without explanation (appropriate for target audience), good paragraph breaks. Score 5 due to this one minor compression issue; otherwise publication-ready."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: The proof is appropriately detailed for a graduate-level algebra result. Each section (Generation, Independence, Uniqueness) contains necessary content. Nothing can be cut because: (1) the universal property setup is essential for the evaluation maps, (2) the explicit reduced word argument is needed for generation, (3) the independence argument requires the δ_{xy} construction, (4) the isomorphism Φ construction is needed for the final conclusion. Nothing is missing because: the proof covers generation, independence, and the explicit isomorphism to ⊕ℤ. One trivial trim possible: 'also xN ≠ N because f̄_x(xN) = 1' could be slightly condensed, but this is minor. Ideal length ±10%."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The prompt asks to prove F/F' is a free abelian group with basis X_#. Response B adds an unrequested 'Verification of Reasoning' section discussing empty X and countably infinite X cases. While mathematically relevant, this was not requested. Checklist: [X] Prove F/F' abelian — done; [X] Show X_# generates — done; [X] Prove linear independence — done; [ ] Stay within requested scope — failed (added verification section). The 'three requested stages' framing is self-imposed, not from prompt. Score capped at 4 due to unrequested content addition."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] commutator subgroup definition and quotient being abelian; [VERIFIED] generation argument via reduced words; [VERIFIED] universal property application for independence; [VERIFIED] edge cases in verification section. However: [QUESTIONABLE] claim 'X_# generates F/F' as a ℤ-module' — while true, this mixes group and module language without clear distinction; the proof hasn't yet established free abelian structure at this point. [UNVERIFIABLE] 'the universal property still legally applies' — informal language, but mathematically correct. The verification section contains correct but unrequested analysis. Score capped at 4 due to questionable timing of 'ℤ-module' claim and unverifiable informal phrasing."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] F/F' abelian — correct via commutator calculation; [CORRECT] X_# generates — correct via reduced words; [CORRECT] linear independence — correct via universal property; [PARTIALLY CORRECT] conclusion 'free abelian group with basis X_#' — the proof shows X_# generates and is independent, but never explicitly constructs the isomorphism to ⊕ℤ or proves the universal property of the resulting group. The verification section adds correct but unrequested material. The core proof is correct but less complete than Response A (missing explicit isomorphism construction)."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Quality audit: Multiple issues found. (1) Grammar/spacing errors: 'agroup' (twice), 'thequotient', 'anytwo', 'thedefinition', 'trivial group, linear', 'setgenerates', 'x_j \\mapsto 1' with broken spacing. (2) Inconsistent tone: 'legally applies', 'perfectly well-defined', 'completely intact' — slightly informal for mathematical proof. (3) Redundancy: 'This demonstrates that... Consequently... Combined with...' — multiple transition phrases for one conclusion. (4) Structure: the self-imposed 'three stages' plus 'verification' creates a somewhat artificial structure. The writing is readable but has identifiable weaknesses that would need editing for publication."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Length analysis: Response B is noticeably longer than necessary. Specific padding identified: (1) The verification section (~200 words) is entirely unrequested; (2) Stage 1 repeats the standard commutator quotient argument with excessive explicit calculation; (3) The universal property explanation in Stage 3 is verbose, restating what is standard; (4) Multiple filler phrases: 'To isolate a specific coefficient... we explicitly invoke', 'Now, we apply...', 'Bydefinition' [sic]. Missing: explicit isomorphism construction to ⊕ℤ that would complete the 'free abelian' claim rigorously. Could cut: entire verification section, ~30% of Stage 1, ~20% of Stage 3. Ideal length exceeded by ~40%."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 3.85, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 3.85'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better due to superior mathematical rigor and concision. Response A correctly proves xN ≠ N via the homomorphism f̄_x(xN)=1 before establishing generation, while Response B omits this non-triviality check entirely. Response A's independence argument is cleaner—applying f̄_{x_j} directly to isolate coefficients—whereas Response B's Stage 3 is unnecessarily verbose with redundant universal property exposition. Response B also contains minor errors: 'agroup' typo, imprecise 'generates as a Z-module' phrasing before independence is proven, and the verification section's 'empty set map' language is sloppy (empty functions don't 'extend'). Response A's structured proof with explicit 'Generation/Independence/Uniqueness' sections and direct isomorphism construction via Φ demonstrates cleaner mathematical writing."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and conciseness in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner elegantly constructs explicit homomorphisms $\\bar{f}_x$ with $\\bar{f}_x(yN)=\\delta_{xy}$ to prove independence directly, then synthesizes generation, independence, and uniqueness into a clean isomorphism $\\Phi: \\bigoplus_{x\\in X}\\mathbb{Z}\\to A$. Gemini 3 Pro's response, while correct in substance, exhibits verbosity and structural inefficiency: it separates the proof into three stages plus verification sections that rehash edge cases unnecessarily, uses imprecise phrasing ('generates...as a $\\mathbb{Z}$-module' appears before linear independence is established), and contains distracting formatting artifacts like 'agroup' and 'thequotient'. The SxS winner's 'Uniqueness of expression' section directly establishes the free abelian property, whereas Gemini 3 Pro's verification section for empty and infinite $X$—while thoughtful—goes beyond the prompt's requirements and disrupts the proof's flow. Both responses correctly apply the universal property, but the SxS winner's construction of the isomorphism $\\Phi$ provides a more complete and elegant characterization of the free abelian structure."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "Both models prove the theorem correctly, but the SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior mathematical elegance and concision. The SxS winner uses the isomorphism Φ: ⊕ℤ → A directly, establishing generation, independence, and uniqueness in a unified structural argument that immediately yields F/F' ≅ ⊕ℤ. GPT 5.2, while thorough, is more verbose with a three-stage breakdown and includes unnecessary edge case verification that doesn't add mathematical value. The SxS winner's construction of θ_x and the induced maps f̄_x is more elegant than GPT 5.2's repeated index notation φ_i. However, GPT 5.2's explicit statement of the universal property and clearer signposting of 'Stage 1: F/F' is abelian' may aid pedagogical clarity. The SxS winner's proof that xN ≠ N via f̄_x(xN) = 1 is more direct than GPT 5.2's implicit handling. Overall, the SxS winner achieves the same correctness with tighter mathematical prose."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit verification of edge cases', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated 'Verification of Reasoning' section that explicitly checks the edge case where the basis X is empty and the case where X is countably infinite.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear structural organization with labeled stages', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro organizes the proof into three explicitly labeled stages (Stage 1: abelian property, Stage 2: generation, Stage 3: linear independence) with descriptive headers, while the SxS winner uses less prominent bold markers.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit statement of universal property invocation', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names and describes the 'universal property of free groups' before applying it, whereas the SxS winner applies the property without explicitly naming or describing it first.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Complete explicit derivation of abelian property', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a full explicit proof that F/F' is abelian by computing the commutator in the quotient, while the SxS winner only implicitly uses this fact without proving it.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit connection to Z-module structure', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that X_# generates F/F' as a Z-module (not just as a group), connecting the group structure to the free abelian group structure, which the SxS winner omits.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit verification of abelian property', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated Stage 1 that explicitly proves F/F' is abelian by showing commutators vanish in the quotient, whereas the SxS winner assumes this without proof.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention, 3-4 = clear proof with minor gaps, 5-6 = complete rigorous proof with clear reasoning', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Structural organization with labeled stages', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses clearly labeled stages (Stage 1, Stage 2, Stage 3) with visual separators (---) to structure the proof, while the SxS winner uses informal bold headings without consistent hierarchical organization.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = some structure, 3-4 = clear labeled sections, 5-6 = exemplary hierarchical organization with visual clarity', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit invocation of universal property', 'description': "GPT 5.2 contains the sentence 'Now explicitly invoke the universal property of free groups' with bold formatting and clear statement of the property, while the SxS winner merely says 'By the universal property' without elaboration.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions property briefly, 3-4 = states property clearly, 5-6 = explicitly highlights and fully articulates the universal property', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Edge case verification section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Quick verification of edge cases' section addressing X empty and X countably infinite, while the SxS winner contains no edge case analysis.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions one edge case, 3-4 = analyzes multiple edge cases adequately, 5-6 = comprehensive edge case analysis with full verification', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Clear statement of linear independence conclusion', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly states 'This is exactly Z-linear independence of X_#' connecting the proof to the definition, while the SxS winner uses the term 'independence' without specifying Z-linear independence.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = implies the connection, 3-4 = states connection clearly, 5-6 = explicitly bridges proof to formal definition', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Equation numbering for key steps', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses numbered equations (1) and (2) to track the equivalence between the quotient condition and membership in F', while the SxS winner presents this equivalence inline without numbering.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = some equations present, 3-4 = strategic numbering of key equations, 5-6 = excellent use of numbering for proof navigation', 'rating': 4}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 19049, 'output_tokens': 3727, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.082822}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.082822}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 19049, 'output_tokens': 3727, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.082822}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.082822}}] 
