 Requirement 1 — Show F/F' is abelian group: [CORRECT] Stage 1 proves this via commutator argument. Requirement 2 — Show F/F' is free abelian: [CORRECT] Stages 2-3 prove it's free abelian by showing free abelian universal property (free on X_#). Requirement 3 — Show basis is specifically X_# = {xF' : x ∈ X}: [CORRECT] Explicitly constructed and verified. Requirement 4 — Handle arbitrary basis X: [CORRECT] Proof structure uses only universal properties, valid for any cardinality. All requirements correctly answered with verifiable reasoning. No incorrect elements, no missing elements."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths: Clear three-stage structure with explicit headers; logical flow from abelian → generated → linearly independent; appropriate mathematical notation; explicit invocation of universal property highlighted. Minor issues identified: (1) 'every pair of elements in F/F' commutes' — slightly awkward phrasing, better as 'F/F' is abelian' or 'cosets commute'; (2) 'Now explicitly invoke' — imperative tone slightly informal for pure proof; (3) 'Quick verification of edge cases' section, while valuable, is slightly conversational. These are trivial style preferences. No run-on sentences, no grammar errors, appropriate formal mathematical tone maintained throughout."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof is medium-to-long (requires establishing three properties and linear independence argument). Response achieves this with: Stage 1 (~80 words), Stage 2 (~50 words), Stage 3 (~200 words), Conclusion (~30 words), Edge cases (~100 words). Total ~460 words. Nothing can be cut because: each stage is necessary (abelian property, generation, independence); the explicit construction of φ_i is essential to the proof; edge cases demonstrate completeness. Nothing is missing because: all three defining properties of free abelian group are proven; basis construction is explicit; universal property is invoked correctly. Minor trimming possible: edge cases could be condensed, but they add value. Score 5 for near-perfect calibration.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Checklist verification: (1) Prove F/F' is free abelian group — [X] accomplished via isomorphism with A = ⊕Z; (2) Prove basis is X_# — [X] shown through explicit basis verification at end; (3) Handle arbitrary basis X — [X] proof works for any set X. Minor issue: The proof constructs an isomorphism with ⊕Z rather than proving the universal property directly; this is equivalent but adds unrequested machinery (the explicit construction of mutual inverse maps). The '∎' symbol at end is slightly non-standard formatting but acceptable. Score capped at 5 due to this additional structural approach that wasn't strictly necessary."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check of claims: [VERIFIED] A = ⊕_{x∈X} Z is free abelian on basis {e_x} — correct. [VERIFIED] θ: F → A exists by universal property — correct. [VERIFIED] θ([u,v]) = 0 since A abelian — correct. [VERIFIED] Induced map θ̄: G → A exists — correct by universal property of quotients. [VERIFIED] xyN = yxN because [x,y] ∈ N — correct. [VERIFIED] ψ: A → G exists by free-abelian property — correct. [QUESTIONABLE] Claim that ψ(∑ n_x e_x) = ∏ x^{n_x}N is 'independent of the order' — this requires proof that elements commute in G, which is shown for x,y ∈ X but not explicitly for arbitrary products; however this follows from G being abelian (shown implicitly). [UNVERIFIABLE] The claim that ψθ̄ = id_G follows from 'X_# generates G' — this is correct but the generation claim itself is not explicitly proven in the text (only stated as 'whence'). This is a gap: the proof asserts X_# generates G without proof, unlike Response A which explicitly proves this. Score capped at 4 due to unverifiable generation claim and slightly compressed reasoning."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Correctness audit: Requirement 1 — Show F/F' is abelian: [PARTIALLY CORRECT] Shown implicitly via xyN = yxN for basis elements, but not explicitly stated as 'G is abelian' — the reader must infer. Requirement 2 — Show F/F' is free abelian: [CORRECT] Proven via isomorphism with ⊕Z. Requirement 3 — Show basis is X_#: [CORRECT] Verified at end. Requirement 4 — Handle arbitrary X: [CORRECT] Works generally. Gap identified: The generation of G by X_# is asserted ('X_# generates G, whence') without proof — this is a required step for the inverse argument to complete. In Response A this is Stage 2; here it's missing. This is a significant gap in the proof structure, capping score at 4."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths: Elegant structure with explicit 'The map...' headers; efficient use of categorical language (mutual inverse maps); appropriate notation; professional mathematical tone. Minor issues: (1) 'Put A = ...' — slightly abrupt transition; (2) 'whence' — slightly archaic but acceptable; (3) The final paragraph combines basis verification with conclusion, slightly dense; (4) '∎' symbol without preceding context. These are trivial. No grammar errors, clear sentence structure, appropriate formality. The proof is well-written and professional."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof via isomorphism approach is concise (~280 words). Response achieves this with: Setup (~40 words), Map F→A (~60 words), Map A→G (~70 words), Inverse verification (~80 words), Basis (~50 words). Total ~300 words. Nothing can be cut because: each construction is minimal; the explicit formula for ψ is necessary; the mutual inverse verification is essential. Nothing is missing because: while generation of G by X_# is asserted rather than proven, this is a correctness issue not a verbosity issue — the length itself is perfectly calibrated for this proof strategy. The isomorphism approach is inherently more compact than the element-wise approach in Response A. This is a model of concise mathematical writing.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.7, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.70'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better due to superior pedagogical clarity and completeness. Response A explicitly develops the proof through three intuitive stages (abelian, generates, linearly independent) with detailed explanations of the universal property invocation and edge case verification. Response B, while mathematically correct and elegant in its isomorphism approach, compresses key steps—particularly the verification that X# generates G and the basis argument—into terse 'similarly' clauses that skip explicit reasoning. Response A's Stage 3 provides a clearer template for how the universal property yields linear independence, whereas Response B's 'basis' paragraph merely asserts uniqueness without showing the reduction process."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both models produce mathematically correct proofs with identical logical structure across all three stages. The SxS winner uses cleaner notation (e.g., [aF',bF'] for commutators in Stage 1) and more elegant exposition, particularly in Stage 3 where it directly computes φ_i on the relation without introducing the extraneous quotient map φ̄ that Gemini 3 Pro uses. Gemini 3 Pro contains minor typographical errors ('agroup', 'thequotient', 'theidentity') and slightly more verbose phrasing ('explicitly invoke the universal property of free groups' appears twice). Both correctly handle edge cases with equivalent reasoning. The SxS winner's conclusion integrates stages more crisply, while Gemini 3 Pro's redundant descent to φ̄ adds unnecessary complexity without error. Neither model makes mathematical mistakes; differences are purely presentational."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response and GPT 5.2 response are identical in every respect—same structure, same mathematical content, same notation, same three-stage proof, same edge case verification, and even identical formatting with LaTeX. Both correctly prove that F/F' is free abelian with basis X_# by establishing abelianness, generation, and Z-linear independence via the universal property. No differences exist in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, or Verbosity; both responses are mathematically rigorous, complete, and identically verbose."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit universal property invocation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the universal property of free groups as a named theorem before applying it, whereas the SxS winner embeds it in running text without formal statement.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if marginally more explicit, 3-4 if clearly stated as named theorem, 5-6 if formally stated with full hypotheses', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Quotient homomorphism notation consistency', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro consistently uses the named homomorphism π: F → F/F' throughout all three stages, while the SxS winner switches between π and implicit quotient notation.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if minor inconsistency, 3-4 if clear preference for named map, 5-6 if rigorous consistency aids proof tracking', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Descent to quotient homomorphism', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly constructs the descended homomorphism φ̄: F/F' → ℤ via the universal property of quotients, whereas the SxS winner only shows F' ⊆ ker(φ) without explicitly naming the induced map.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if mentioned in passing, 3-4 if clearly constructed, 5-6 if rigorously proven well-defined', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 