2026-03-25 08:24:58,895 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: tasks1_response---------------------------[{'index': 0, 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nWe consider communication networks in which the number of sender-receiver-pairs and intermediate parties and the capacities of the channels connecting them are arbitrary. To be concrete, consider $k$ senders $A_1$, $\\cdots$, $A_k$, who want to send $k$ independent messages $m_1$, $\\cdots$, $m_k$ to $k$ respective receivers $B_1$, $\\cdots$, B_k. Available to them is an arbitrary directed network of noiseless channels and intermediate helpers. For each call to the network, each channel in the network can be used once. The number of calls to the network represents our ``cost\'\' to be minimized. In the quantum setting, both messages and the available channels are quantum.\n\n**Notations**\nA state in a $2$-dimensional Hilbert space is called a qubit, and the ability to transmit it is called a qbit. Let $S(\\cdot)$ denote the von Neumann entropy, and $I(A:B) = S(A) + S(B) - S(AB)$ denote the quantum mutual information between two systems.\n\n**Definition 1: Achievable Rate Region**\nIn the asymptotic scenario, we allow large number of calls to the network. Let ${\\cal P}_n$ denote a protocol that uses the network $n$ times along with other allowed resources, and communicates $m_1,m_2$ of sizes $n (r_1{-}\\delta_n),n (r_2{-}\\delta_n)$ qubits with fidelities at least $1{-}\\epsilon_n$ for $\\delta_n, \\epsilon_n \\to 0$. Then, we say that the rate pair $(r_1,r_2)$ is achievable. The achievable rate region is the set of all achievable rate pairs.\n\n**Convexity and Monotonicity of Achievable Rate Regions**\nIf a rate pair $(r_1,r_2)$ is achievable, so is any $(r_1\',r_2\')$ with $r_1\' \\leq r_1$ and $r_2\' \\leq r_2$. Also, the convex hull of a set of achievable rate pairs are also achievable by time sharing of the underlying protocols.\n\n**The Butterfly Network**\nConsider two senders $A_1$ and $A_2$, who want to send two independent messages $m_1$ and $m_2$ to two respective receivers $B_1$ and $B_2$. Available to them is a network of $7$ noiseless directed channels and two helpers $C_1$ and $C_2$ depicted in Fig.1. The channels are: $A_1 \\to C_1$, $A_1 \\to B_2$, $A_2 \\to C_1$, $A_2 \\to B_1$, $C_1 \\to C_2$, $C_2 \\to B_1$, $C_2 \\to B_2$. Each channel has a capacity of 1 qbit per network use.\n\n<center>\n<img src="https://i.imgur.com/k2x21lP.png" alt="Figure 1 The butterfly network. The left diagram represents the task to be achieved, the right diagram represents the available resources." width="400"/>\n</center>\n\n**Quantum Secret Sharing Lemma**\nA quantum secret sharing scheme is an encoding of a quantum state (the secret) in a multiparty system. Each party owns one system called a ``share.\'\' Authorized sets of parties can reconstruct the secret (with high fidelity), while unauthorized sets of parties can learn negligible information about the secret. A share $S_s$ is ``significant\'\' if there exists an unauthorized set $S_u$ such that $\\{ S_s \\} \\bigcup S_u$ is authorized. Let $S$ be the secret, purified by the reference system $R$. It was proved in [15, 16] that for near-exact schemes, the size of any significant share is at least the size of the quantum secret. More precisely,\n$$S(S_s) \\geq S(S) - (\\epsilon\' + \\gamma)/2$$\nwhere $\\epsilon\'$ is a measure of the recovery error and $\\gamma$ is an upper bound on the quantum mutual information $I(S_u:R)$, and they are both negligible when recovery of the secret is near-exact.\n## Theorem\nFor the quantum butterfly network with no free auxiliary resources (the unassisted case), the achievable rate region is the set of all rate pairs $(r_1, r_2)$ satisfying $r_1 \\geq 0$, $r_2 \\geq 0$, and $r_1 + r_2 \\leq 1$.', 'status': 'REJECT', 'result': {'status': 'REJECT', 'rejection_reason': 'Image Handling', 'justification': "The prompt contains an image reference (https://i.imgur.com/k2x21lP.png) that is described as being essential to understanding the problem ('depicted in Fig.1'). The setup refers to a specific network topology that is illustrated in this figure, making the image necessary to complete the proof. This falls under 'interpret or analyze images' rejection category."}, 'accepted': False, 'error': None, 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 1494, 'output_tokens': 105, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.004248}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.004248}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 1494, 'output_tokens': 105, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.004248}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.004248}}] 
