∼1 (bounded), not superdiffusive behavior. B also lacks explicit tightness proof and uses hand-wavy 'roughly scales' language in the large-t analysis where A provides rigorous integral bounds."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with tightness arguments and explicit Kolmogorov condition verification, correctly deriving all three asymptotic regimes (ρ>1, ρ=1, 0<ρ<1) with proper dominated convergence arguments and careful ε-δ analysis for the large-t behavior. In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro's response contains critical errors: it mischaracterizes the ρ<1 regime as 'superdiffusive' when the theorem states msd(t)∼1 (bounded/localized), and its 'Verification' section fabricates a claim that the prompt asked to confirm superdiffusive behavior—this instruction never appeared in the original prompt. Gemini 3 Pro also makes technical errors in the ρ=1 case, incorrectly stating the 'broad intermediate bulk region' dominates when in fact the singularity at x=0 drives the logarithm, and its change-of-variables analysis lacks the rigorous bounds present in the SxS winner. The SxS winner's compact notation (g(λ;t,s)) and explicit Lipschitz continuity argument for covariance convergence demonstrate superior mathematical sophistication."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide complete, correct proofs with comparable technical depth. The SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) offers a more compact presentation with tighter mathematical notation (e.g., using g(λ;t,s) and direct dominated convergence arguments) and achieves brevity without sacrificing rigor. GPT 5.2 provides more verbose exposition with explicit step labeling ('Step 1', 'Step 2', etc.) and additional explanatory text, which aids readability but increases length substantially. Both correctly handle t