n\n**Assumption 3**\nFor $x \\in [0,1]$, define the sequence of coefficient measures,\n$$\n\\mu_N(dx) := \\sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \\delta\\left(x - \\frac{k}{N}\\right) c_{k,N}^2\n$$\nwhere $\\delta(x)$ is the Dirac $\\delta$-distribution. There exists a nonnegative finite Radon measure $\\mu$ on the interval $[0,1]$ such that $\\mu_N \\to \\mu$ weakly.\n\nFor the large-$t$ asymptotic statements, we say that a function $g(t)$ satisfies $g(t) \\sim t^\\nu$ if $\\lim_{t \\to \\infty} g(t) / t^\\nu = C$ for some nonnegative constant $C$.\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 1**\nLet the sets of processes $\\{x_N(t)\\}$ and $\\{z_{k,N}(t)\\}$ be defined as in the setup. Take $z_{k,N}(0) = 0$ for all $k$ and $N$. Suppose that the diffusive spectrum $\\{\\lambda_{k,N}\\}$ converges to a shape function $f$ in the sense of Assumption 1. Furthermore, suppose the coefficients $\\{c_{k,N}\\}$ satisfy Assumption 3 with limiting weight measure $\\mu$.\n\nThen the family $\\{x_N(t)\\}$ converges in distribution as $N \\to \\infty$ to a mean zero Gaussian process $x(t)$ defined by its auto-correlation function\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}[x(t) x(s)] = \\int_0^1 \\frac{e^{-f(x)|t-s|}}{2 f(x)} \\left(1 - e^{-2 f(x) (t \\wedge s)}\\right) \\mu(dx).\n$$\nIf the shape function $f$ furthermore satisfies Assumption 2 with spectral parameter $\\rho > 0$ and the limiting weight measure $\\mu$ is Lebesgue measure, then asymptotically, the limiting mean-squared displacement (MSD) function $\\text{msd}(t) := \\mathbb{E}[x^2(t)]$ satisfies\n$$\n\\text{msd}(t) \\sim t, \\quad t \\to 0\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\text{msd}(t) \\sim \\left\\{ \\begin{array} {cc} t^{1 - \\frac{1}{\\rho}} & \\rho > 1 \\\\ \\ln t & \\rho = 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 < \\rho < 1 \\end{array}\\right. \\quad t \\to \\infty\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under '## Proof' heading. It addresses all parts of the theorem: convergence in distribution and MSD asymptotics. Minor fault considered: the prompt asks for '## Proof' as a heading, but Response A uses '## Proof Strategy' followed by subsections rather than a single unified '## Proof' section. This is a trivial formatting variance—the content is complete and properly structured. All required components (finite-N covariance, weak convergence, asymptotic analysis) are present. No unrequested content added."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Most claims are mathematically sound, but several are unverifiable or stated without sufficient rigor. [UNVERIFIABLE]: The claim 'the limit of the OU kernel must be shown to equal min(t,s)' for the k=0 term is asserted but not actually proven—Response A states this as 'recovered by the limit λ↓0' without showing the calculation. [UNVERIFIABLE]: The dominated convergence argument for handling the singularity at f(x)=0 is invoked implicitly but not rigorously justified. [QUESTIONABLE]: The tightness bound 'E[|x_N(t)-x_N(s)|²] ≤ C|t-s|' is stated without explicit derivation of the constant C's N-independence. The asymptotic analysis for ρ>1 uses an indefinite integral to ∞ without verifying convergence. No false claims detected, but several gaps in rigorous justification."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "[CORRECT]: The covariance formula and limiting Gaussian process are correctly derived. [CORRECT]: Small-time MSD asymptotics ∼ t are correct. [PARTIALLY CORRECT]: Large-time asymptotics for ρ>1 and ρ=1 are correct in form, but the ρ<1 case is hand-waved—Response A states 'the tail contributes a bounded constant' without showing why the integral converges. [MISSING]: No explicit verification that the k=0 Brownian term properly converges to the claimed limit (the min(t,s) appears magically). The theorem states the autocorrelation as E[x(t)x(s)] = ∫... but Response A's equation (2) shows a different form that requires algebraic manipulation to match; this manipulation is not shown. The claim that 'the k=0 term is recovered by the limit λ↓0' is asserted, not demonstrated."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear sectioning and logical flow. Minor fault identified: some notation is introduced without definition (e.g., 't∧s' appears in the theorem statement but Response A uses 'min(t,s)' exclusively, which is clearer but inconsistent). The phrase 'the delicate step is handling the k=0...' appears in strategy but isn't clearly resolved in execution. One minor redundancy: 'Thus the theorem holds. ∎' is slightly informal for a formal proof. Otherwise excellent structure with purposeful subsections."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing critical is missing. Minor trimming possible: the 'Verification' subsection at the end repeats conclusions already stated; the 'Diffusive classification' paragraph adds interpretive commentary not strictly required by the theorem. The proof strategy preamble could be condensed. However, all mathematical steps are appropriately detailed—no section feels padded. The asymptotic analysis for each ρ regime is succinct but complete enough."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.55, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.55'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Follows the instruction to provide a complete proof with '## Proof' heading (using '## Proof Strategy' as opener). All theorem components addressed: convergence in distribution and both MSD asymptotic regimes. Minor fault: the prompt asks for '## Proof' as a clean heading, but Response B uses '## Proof Strategy' followed by subsections. This is trivial variance. The response is more explicit than A about the k=0 term convention, writing it directly into the integral representation. All required elements present; no unrequested content."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Claims are generally well-supported. [VERIFIED]: The explicit convention '(e⁰-e⁰)/(0) = t∧s' for the k=0 term is correctly stated and handled. [VERIFIED]: The bound '(e^{-a|t-s|}-e^{-a(t+s)})/(2a) ≤ t∧s' is a correct elementary inequality. [VERIFIED]: Small-time expansion '1-e^{-2ft} = 2ft + O(f²t²)' is correct. Minor [UNVERIFIABLE]: The claim that '[x_t,1] converges to ∫₀¹(2f(x))⁻¹dx < ∞' for ρ<1 assumes integrability without explicit verification (though it follows from ρ<1 and Assumption 2). The dominated convergence argument is more explicit than A but still compressed. No false claims; tighter rigor than Response A on the critical k=0 term."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "[CORRECT]: Covariance derivation with explicit k=0 convention matches the theorem's claimed form after algebraic manipulation. [CORRECT]: Weak convergence argument properly separates finite-dimensional convergence (via covariance convergence) from tightness (via Kolmogorov). [CORRECT]: Small-time MSD ∼ t is correctly derived with explicit error term O(ft). [CORRECT]: Large-time asymptotics for all three ρ regimes are correctly identified with proper scaling analysis. The split at x_t = (a₀t)^{-1/ρ} is the correct critical scale. Minor [PARTIALLY CORRECT]: For ρ>1, Response B claims both [0,x_t] and [x_t,1] contribute ∼ t^{1-1/ρ}, but the [x_t,1] contribution is actually O(t^{1-1/ρ}) from the tail, not identical scaling—this is slightly imprecise but directionally correct. The final forms match the theorem."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear, professional mathematical prose. Minor fault: inconsistent notation—'t∧s' is used in the theorem and Response B's covariance formula, but 'min(t,s)' appears in the dominated convergence bound discussion. The phrase 'the delicate step is justifying dominated convergence near x=0' is stated but the actual justification is brief. Some sentences are dense: 'Both [0,x_t]...and [x_t,1]...contribute ∼ t^{1-1/ρ}' could be unpacked. Otherwise excellent organization with explicit purpose statements for each section."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Well-calibrated length. Nothing essential is missing. Minor possible trim: the 'Verification' subsection at the end adds interpretive commentary ('subdiffusive', 'superdiffusive') not strictly required. The explicit handling of the k=0 term convention adds clarity without excess. The asymptotic analysis for each ρ case is appropriately detailed with the critical scale x_t explicitly identified. The integral representation with the convention is more compact than A's separate treatment, saving space without losing clarity."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs are correct and complete, but A provides more explicit detail in the large-t asymptotics: it explicitly computes the scaling constant for the ρ>1 case and shows the integral transformation, whereas B merely states 'C_ρ' without derivation. A also includes a clearer verification section with physical interpretation (subdiffusive/superdiffusive classification). B's dominated convergence argument for the k=0 singularity is more elegant, but A's explicit handling of the λ→0 limit is more pedagogically complete."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and completeness across Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness metrics. The SxS winner provides explicit covariance calculations with equation (1), rigorous tightness arguments using Kolmogorov's criterion with concrete bounds, and detailed asymptotic analysis with proper scaling substitutions (e.g., x = t^{-1/ρ}y for ρ>1). In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro contains significant errors: it incorrectly claims 'msd(t)/t ~ 1/t → 0' describes 'superdiffusive behavior' when it actually describes subdiffusive/localized behavior, and it fabricates a contradiction with the prompt that doesn't exist—the prompt's theorem correctly states msd(t) ~ 1 for 0<ρ<1, which is subdiffusive, not superdiffusive. Gemini 3 Pro also lacks explicit tightness proofs and provides less rigorous justification for the interchange of limits in the covariance convergence. The SxS winner's Writing Style is more structured with clear section purposes, while Gemini 3 Pro's verbosity includes unnecessary self-corrections and confused physical interpretations."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) provides a cleaner, more focused proof with superior mathematical elegance and concision, while GPT 5.2 is more verbose with some redundant steps. Both models correctly prove the theorem with rigorous covariance calculations, weak convergence arguments, and asymptotic analysis. Gemini 3 Pro's proof strategy section efficiently outlines the key insights (Cramér-Wold, Kolmogorov tightness, small-x dominance) in under 150 words, and its covariance formula (1) immediately captures the structure without excessive case notation. GPT 5.2's introduction of the kernel φ_{u,v}(λ) with explicit λ=0 case, while technically correct, adds notational overhead; its tightness argument with explicit Itô isometry calculations for z_{k,N}(t)-z_{k,N}(s) is more detailed but unnecessarily lengthy since the uniform bound suffices. Both handle the critical small-x asymptotics correctly: Gemini 3 Pro's scaling substitution x=t^{-1/ρ}y for ρ>1 is presented more cleanly, while GPT 5.2's explicit computation of J_ρ(t) with the u-substitution is equally valid but more verbose. GPT 5.2's 'Verification' section contains a misleading statement calling ρ<1 'superdiffusive' when it actually gives bounded MSD (the model corrects this to 'bounded/localized'), whereas Gemini 3 Pro correctly identifies 'superdiffusive' behavior immediately. The SxS winner's concision represents better mathematical writing style without sacrificing rigor."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit OU solution derivation', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly derives the Itô solution for the OU process and computes the covariance using Itô's isometry, whereas the SxS winner states the result without derivation.", 'scale': "1-6 scale: 1-2 = marginal improvement, 3-4 = clear explicit derivation present, 5-6 = complete step-by-step derivation with Itô's isometry", 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Unified covariance formula with limit', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly notes that taking λ→0 recovers t∧s via L'Hôpital's rule and writes a unified formula including k=0, whereas the SxS winner treats the Brownian term separately.", 'scale': "1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions limit briefly, 3-4 = explicit L'Hôpital justification, 5-6 = unified formula with clear limit treatment", 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Uniform continuity argument for G', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that G(·,t,s) is uniformly continuous and uses this to justify uniform convergence of the integrand, whereas the SxS winner only mentions uniform convergence of λ_{k,N}.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = vague continuity mention, 3-4 = explicit uniform continuity claim, 5-6 = rigorous application to integrand convergence', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Change of variables with explicit scaling', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly performs the change of variables u = 2a₀x^ρt with proportional relationships for x and dx, whereas the SxS winner only states the substitution without showing the differential scaling.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions substitution, 3-4 = partial differential shown, 5-6 = complete change of variables with dx scaling explicit', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Dominant contribution identification', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly identifies the dominant contribution region for each asymptotic regime (scaling window x ~ t^{-1/ρ}, broad intermediate bulk, etc.), whereas the SxS winner describes regions less precisely.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = vague region mention, 3-4 = specific scaling identified, 5-6 = precise dominant contribution with mathematical characterization', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Dominated Convergence Theorem application', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly invokes the Dominated Convergence Theorem for the 0<ρ<1 case with integrability justification, whereas the SxS winner only states convergence to a finite limit.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions convergence, 3-4 = DCT named, 5-6 = DCT with explicit dominating function and integrability check', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Mathematical error identification in prompt', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro correctly identifies that the prompt's claim of 'superdiffusive behavior' for 0<ρ<1 contradicts the theorem and mathematical reality (bounded MSD implies localization, not superdiffusion), whereas the SxS winner silently accepts the prompt's framing.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor clarification, 3-4 = explicit contradiction noted, 5-6 = rigorous correction with mathematical justification', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Verification section completeness', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a structured verification section with three explicit consistency checks (t→0 limit, ρ>1 consistency, 0<ρ<1 consistency), whereas the SxS winner has only two brief verification notes embedded in the text.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = verification mentioned, 3-4 = structured checks present, 5-6 = comprehensive verification with explicit limit calculations', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX formatting quality', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper LaTeX display math with consistent spacing and punctuation, whereas the SxS winner has formatting issues including run-together text in the proof strategy and inconsistent equation numbering.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor formatting, 3-4 = proper display math, 5-6 = professional LaTeX with consistent structure', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit kernel definition with continuous extension', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 defines the kernel function φ_{u,v}(λ) explicitly with a piecewise formula that includes the continuous extension at λ=0, whereas the SxS winner only mentions this limit in passing without formal definition.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentioned briefly, 3-4 = defined but not used systematically, 5-6 = formally defined and used throughout', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Rigorous tightness proof with explicit moment bounds', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a complete tightness proof with explicit computation of E|z_{k,N}(t)-z_{k,N}(s)|^2 using Itô isometry and derives the bound C_T|t-s|, while the SxS winner only asserts the bound without derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = asserts tightness without proof, 3-4 = sketches argument, 5-6 = full derivation with explicit constants', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Handling of k=0 Brownian mode via λ_{0,N}:=0 convention', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly introduces the convention λ_{0,N}:=0 to unify the treatment of the Brownian mode with the OU modes, allowing a single formula for all k, while the SxS winner treats k=0 separately without this unification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = treats separately without comment, 3-4 = mentions unification idea, 5-6 = explicitly implements λ_{0,N}:=0 convention', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Detailed error analysis for covariance convergence', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly bounds the error between φ_{u,v}(λ_{k,N}) and φ_{u,v}(f(k/N)) using uniform continuity on compact sets and shows this error vanishes, while the SxS winner states the replacement is valid with only 'o(1)' justification.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = asserts convergence without error analysis, 3-4 = mentions error terms informally, 5-6 = explicit bound with uniform continuity argument', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit verification section with numbered items', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Verification' subsection with numbered items checking consistency of limits, while the SxS winner has only brief verification remarks embedded in the text.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no structured verification, 3-4 = scattered remarks, 5-6 = dedicated numbered verification section', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Precise dominated convergence justification for small-t limit', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly states the dominating function (bounded by 1) and invokes dominated convergence for the small-t limit of msd(t)/t, while the SxS winner only says 'uniformly' without specifying the dominating function.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = claims uniformity without justification, 3-4 = mentions dominated convergence without details, 5-6 = explicit bound 0≤(1-e^{-y})/y≤1 and full DCT justification', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit scaling analysis for ρ>1 case', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly analyzes why regions x≪t^{-1/ρ}, x∼t^{-1/ρ}, and x≫t^{-1/ρ} give the stated contributions, while the SxS winner only performs the substitution without discussing the region-by-region behavior.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = performs substitution only, 3-4 = mentions regions informally, 5-6 = explicit three-region analysis with scaling arguments', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit formula for constant C_ρ in ρ>1 case', 'description': "GPT 5.2 provides the explicit formula C_ρ = (1/ρ)(2a_0)^{-1/ρ}∫_0^∞(1-e^{-u})u^{1/ρ-2}du, while the SxS winner leaves the constant as unspecified 'C'.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = leaves constant unspecified, 3-4 = mentions constant exists, 5-6 = explicit closed-form expression', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Corrected physical interpretation for 0<ρ<1 case', 'description': "GPT 5.2 correctly identifies that 0<ρ<1 yields bounded/localized behavior (not superdiffusive), correcting the SxS winner's misleading claim of 'superdiffusive' for this case.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = repeats error, 3-4 = ambiguous phrasing, 5-6 = explicit correction with corre