ag this? Let me re-examine... Actually, I think I made an error in my initial analysis. Both preferences appear correct. BUT WAIT - re-reading the GPT comment more carefully: The SxS winner here is the internal model (Response A or B). From ab_preference = 1, B is the SxS winner. So human_ab_gpt_comment compares B (SxS winner) vs GPT. It says B is better. ab_gpt_preference = -2 means SxS winner (B) is preferred. This is consistent. Similarly for Gemini. So this should pass. However, I need to re-check if there's a subtle issue. Actually, re-reading human_ab_gpt_comment again: 'The SxS winner's clean single-integral formula for I_c(t)... is more elegant than GPT 5.2's separated case analysis.' This suggests B > GPT. -2 means B is better. Consistent. But wait - I need to check if the SxS winner is actually B. From weighted scores: A=4.95, B=5.10. B wins. ab_preference=1 confirms B wins. So SxS winner = B. GPT comment says B is better. ab_gpt_preference=-2 (B better). Consistent. Gemini comment says B is better. ab_gemini_preference=-2 (B better). Consistent. So this should be pass. BUT I already flagged as fail. Let me reconsider if there's any hidden issue... Actually, re-reading the Gemini comment: 'The SxS winner response (GPT 5.2)' - WAIT! This says 'The SxS winner response (GPT 5.2)'. This is a critical error! The SxS winner is B (internal), not GPT 5.2! The comment incorrectly identifies the SxS winner as GPT 5.2. This is a major grounding error. The SxS winner is the winning internal response (B), but the comment says 'The SxS winner response (GPT 5.2)'. This is completely wrong. GPT 5.2 is the external model being compared, not the SxS winner. This is a Major severity 1 issue."}, 'severity': 1, 'gpt_preference_matches_comment': {'result': True, 'issue': "ab_gpt_preference = -2 (SxS winner preferred) matches comment sentiment that SxS winner is better than GPT. However, note that comment uses 'SxS winner (internal)' terminology correctly."}, 'gemini_preference_