2026-03-25 08:26:52,551 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_12144', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nOur model consists of central force springs between mass points located on a triangular lattice. We introduce unit vectors in the lattice directions as follows: $\\hat{d}_1$ points in the direction of the $x$-axis and each of the subsequent unit vectors $\\hat{d}_i$ with $2 \\leq i \\leq 6$ rotated by an additional $\\pi/3$ in the counter-clockwise direction. This means that $\\hat{d} _{i+3} = -\\hat{d} _{i}$. Specifically, in Cartesian coordinates $(x,y)$, we have $\\hat{d}_1 = (1,0)$, $\\hat{d}_2 = (1/2, \\sqrt{3}/2)$, and $\\hat{d}_3 = (-1/2, \\sqrt{3}/2)$.\n\nWe assume a steady-state crack moving with a constant speed $v$. We change variables to $\\tau=x-v t$, $\\vec{z}=(\\tau, y)$. The equation describing the motion of the masses in the lattice is Fourier transformed over the continuous variable $\\tau$ (with conjugate variable $q$) and the discrete variable $y=n \\sqrt 3/2$ (with conjugate variable $s$). This yields a linear system for the double Fourier transform of the displacement field, $\\vec{u}^{FF}(q,s)$:\n$$\nM \\vec{u}^{FF} = \\vec{\\sigma}^{FF}\n$$\nwith the 2x2 matrix $M$ given by\n$$\nM = \\left(\n\\begin{array}{cc}\nA_1 & A_3 \\\\\nA_3 & A_2\n\\end{array}\\right)\n$$\nwhere, with $\\vec{r}=(q,s)$:\n\\begin{align*}\nA_1 &= -v^2 q^2 + (1+ i \\eta v q) \\left(4 \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{1}}{2}+ \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{2}}{2} + \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{3}}{2} \\right) , \\\\\nA_2 &= -v^2 q^2 + 3 (1+ i \\eta v q) \\left(\\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{2}}{2} + \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{3}}{2}\\right) , \\\\\nA_3 &= \\sqrt 3 (1+ i \\eta v q) \\left(\\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{2}}{2} - \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{3}}{2} \\right) ,\n\\end{align*}\nand the driving term $\\vec{\\sigma}^{FF} = (\\sigma_x^{FF}, \\sigma_y^{FF})$ is\n$$\n\\vec{\\sigma}^{FF}=-\\frac{N(q)}{2} \\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n(1- e^{i \\frac{q}{2}})(1 + e^{ i \\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}s} ) \\\\\n\\sqrt 3 (1+ e^{i \\frac{q}{2}})(1 - e^{ i \\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}s })\n\\end{array}\\right) .\n$$\nThe elongation of a bond in direction $\\hat{d}_i$ on the crack plane ($y=0$, corresponding to discrete index $n=0$) is denoted $Q_i$. Its Fourier transform with respect to $\\tau$ is $Q_i^F(q, n=0)$, which is obtained by solving for $\\vec{u}^{FF}$ and integrating over $s$:\n$$\nQ_i^F(q, n=0)=\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{4 \\pi} \\int_{-\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\sqrt 3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\sqrt 3}} (e^{-i \\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_i}-1) \\vec{u}^{FF} \\cdot \\hat{d}_i ds .\n$$\nIn particular, we are interested in $Q_2^F(q, n=0)$ and $Q_3^F(q, n=0)$. The vector dot products required are:\n$$\n\\vec{u}^{FF} \\cdot \\hat{d}_2=\\frac{\n\\left |\n\\begin{array}{cc}\n-d_{2x}A_3+d_{2y}A_1 & -d_{2x}A_2+d_{2y}A_3 \\\\\n\\sigma^{FF}_x & \\sigma^{FF}_y\n\\end{array}\n\\right| } { \\det M } .\n$$\n$$\n\\vec{u}^{FF} \\cdot \\hat{d}_3=\\frac{\n\\left |\n\\begin{array}{cc}\nd_{2x}A_3+d_{2y}A_1 & d_{2x}A_2+d_{2y}A_3 \\\\\n\\sigma^{FF}_x & \\sigma^{FF}_y\n\\end{array}\n\\right| } { \\det M } .\n$$\nwhere we have used $d_{3x}=-d_{2x}$ and $d_{3y}=d_{2y}$.\n## Theorem\nThe Fourier transforms of the bond elongations $Q_2$ and $Q_3$ on the crack plane ($y=0$) satisfy the symmetry condition:\n$$\nQ_3^F(q, n=0)=e^{i \\frac{q}{2}} Q_2^F(q, n=0) .\n$$\nThis corresponds to the real-space symmetry $Q_2(\\tau, y=0) = Q_3(\\tau - 1/2, y=0)$, which was assumed in the setup of the driving forces. The theorem shows that the solution is consistent with this assumption.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist verification: [X] Provides complete and correct proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Uses multiple approaches/sections (parity analysis, numerator relations, integrand transformation, completion); [X] Honest about completion (provides full proof with ∎); [X] Addresses the specific theorem about Q_3^F = e^{iq/2} Q_2^F symmetry; [X] Works through the mathematical setup as given. No unrequested content added, no format violations, no missing elements. The proof is cleaned up and properly structured as requested.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All mathematical claims verified: [VERIFIED] Parity properties of A_1, A_2 (even) and A_3 (odd) correctly derived from sin²(-x)=sin²(x); [VERIFIED] Determinant evenness follows from A_1A_2-A_3²; [VERIFIED] Driving force transformation formulas in (sigpar) correctly computed; [VERIFIED] Key identity Δ_2(-s) = -e^{-ia}Δ_3(s) derived through explicit determinant manipulation; [VERIFIED] Final integrand relation (integrand) and integration step are mathematically sound. One minor unverifiable: the claim that N(q)=1 'plays no role' is technically true but slightly casual—N(q) cancels in the ratio, but this is a trivial imprecision. No false claims detected."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] The symmetry condition Q_3^F(q,n=0) = e^{iq/2} Q_2^F(q,n=0) is proven; [CORRECT] Real-space interpretation Q_2(τ)=Q_3(τ-1/2) correctly stated; [CORRECT] All matrix elements A_1, A_2, A_3 used correctly; [CORRECT] The Cramer's rule formulas for u^FF·d_2 and u^FF·d_3 applied correctly; [CORRECT] Integration over s with correct limits [-2π/√3, 2π/√3] performed; [CORRECT] The physical interpretation of consistency with driving force assumption addressed. 100% of requirements satisfied with correct reasoning throughout."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Exceptionally clear mathematical writing with logical section breaks (1-4). Notation is consistent (d_2=(α,β), a=βs). Equation references (exp), (sigpar), (udrel), (integrand) aid navigation. Minor issue identified: 'the formulas given in the statement' is slightly informal; 'as stated in the problem' would be more precise. One trivial redundancy: 'This is precisely the claimed symmetry...which in real space is...' restates the conclusion. Otherwise publication-ready for a mathematical physics audience. Proper use of ∎ for QED."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for a technical proof of this complexity. Nothing can be cut because: each section builds necessarily (parity → numerator relation → integrand → completion); the explicit determinant expansion in section 2 is essential for verification; the substitution a=βs is necessary for clarity. Nothing is missing because: all intermediate steps are shown; the s→-s substitution is explicitly justified; the final integration step connects back to the definition. Only trivial trimming possible: the parenthetical '(the formulas given in the statement)' could be removed without loss."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Checklist analysis: [X] Provides proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Attempts multiple approaches/sections; [X] Addresses the theorem; [X] Uses the mathematical setup. HOWEVER: [ ] Missing explicit '## Proof' heading—instead has '## Setup Summary' and '## Proof' but with '## Verification' and '## Common Mistakes to Avoid' and '## Conclusion' sections that were NOT requested; [ ] Adds unrequested content: 'Common Mistakes to Avoid' section is entirely outside scope; [ ] 'Setup Summary' section repeats given information unnecessarily. The prompt asked for 'a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof'—not a multi-section report with pedagogical extras. Significant additions beyond what was requested."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] Matrix component symmetries correctly identified (A_1, A_2 even; A_3 odd); [VERIFIED] Determinant evenness correct; [VERIFIED] Driving force transformations for σ_x, σ_y essentially correct (though notation is messier than A). [QUESTIONABLE] Step 3 derivation contains a critical error: claims '(d_{2x}A_2 - d_{2y}A_3) = -(-d_{2x}A_2 + d_{2y}A_3)'—this is algebraically false; the correct relation is (d_{2x}A_2 - d_{2y}A_3) vs (-d_{2x}A_2 + d_{2y}A_3) which are negatives only if d_{2x}A_2 = d_{2y}A_3, which is not generally true. The derivation continues with 'Recognizing that...' but this recognition is incorrect. [UNVERIFIABLE] The final result is stated to match but the intermediate algebra is garbled. The conclusion is correct but reasoning contains a gap/error that is papered over."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Final result Q_3^F = e^{iq/2} Q_2^F is stated correctly. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] The proof structure follows the right strategy (s→-s substitution). [INCORRECT] Critical algebraic error in Step 3: the manipulation of the Cramer's rule expression contains a sign error and an unjustified 'recognition' step. Specifically, the factorization claims '-e^{-i√3s/2}[...]' but the bracketed terms do not match the required form for u^FF·d_2. The expression '(-d_{2x}A_3 + d_{2y}A_1)σ_y - (-d_{2x}A_2 + d_{2y}A_3)σ_x' is NOT equal to the determinant for u^FF·d_2—the signs on the A_2, A_3 terms are wrong. [MISSING] Proper verification that the transformed integrand equals e^{iq/2} times the original Q_2 integrand—the 'Verification' section asserts this without showing the complete algebra. The proof arrives at the right conclusion through flawed reasoning."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Quality audit: Multiple issues identified. [1] Inconsistent formatting: 'Oursystem' (missing space), 'moving frame$(\tau=x-v t, y)$' (missing space before math), 'a$2\times 2$ matrix' (inconsistent math spacing), 'bondin directions' (missing space). [2] Run-on sentences: 'ApplyingCramer's rule...' (missing space). [3] Awkward phrasing: 'extracts specific overall phase shifts', 'effectively negating d_{2x}in the Cofactor determinant structure' (capitalization error, missing space). [4] Redundancy: 'Setup Summary' repeats given material. [5] Tone inconsistency: 'Common Mistakes to Avoid' is pedagogical/tutor-like, not proof-appropriate. [6] The 'Verification' section is misnamed—it performs derivation, not verification. The writing obscures the mathematics with formatting errors and verbosity."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Length analysis: Significantly over length due to unrequested sections. Identifiable padding: 'Setup Summary' (~150 words) restates given information; 'Common Mistakes to Avoid' (~100 words) is entirely extraneous; 'Conclusion' (~50 words) restates what the proof already showed. The actual proof content is buried in ~60% of the response. Missing: clean, direct derivation—the 'Verification' section jumps steps ('Factoring out exactly e^{iq/2}') without showing the algebra. Could be cut: entire 'Setup Summary', 'Common Mistakes', 'Conclusion'; 'Key Observations' could be integrated into proof steps. The response is ~2× longer than necessary for a clean proof."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 3.5, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 3.50'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better due to cleaner mathematical structure and more rigorous derivation. Response A establishes the key relation \\eqref{udrel} between dot products directly via determinant manipulation, then elegantly derives the integrand relation \\eqref{integrand} before completing the proof. Response B contains critical errors: in Step 3, the Cramer's rule application incorrectly handles signs (the final claimed relation has wrong sign: should be $-e^{ia}$ not $-e^{-i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}s}$), and the 'Verification' section contains algebraic errors where $(e^{i(q/2+a)}-1)(-e^{-ia}) = -e^{iq/2}+e^{-ia} \\neq e^{iq/2}(e^{-i(q/2+a)}-1)$. Response B's conclusion also misstates the real-space relation (reverses the shift direction). Response A's compact, correct derivation avoids these pitfalls."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response (internal) demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and clarity in proving the symmetry condition. The SxS winner correctly identifies the key parity properties: A₁, A₂ even in s while A₃ is odd, and rigorously derives the determinant relation Δ₂(-s) = -e^{-ia}Δ₃(s) using explicit matrix manipulation, leading to the clean integrand relation in equation (4). In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro's proof contains critical errors: in Step 3, the sign handling in the Cramer's rule expansion is muddled—the claimed result u^{FF}(q,-s)·d̂₃ = -e^{-i√3s/2} u^{FF}(q,s)·d̂₂ is incorrect; the correct relation should involve u^{FF}(q,s)·d̂₂ on the right side, not u^{FF}(q,-s)·d̂₂. Gemini 3 Pro also makes algebraic errors in the 'Verification' section where it incorrectly expands (e^{i(q/2+s√3/2)}-1)(-e^{-i√3s/2}) as -e^{iq/2}+e^{-i√3s/2} rather than -e^{iq/2}+e^{-i√3s/2}, and then magically claims this equals e^{iq/2}(e^{-i(q/2+s√3/2)}-1) without valid justification. The SxS winner's proof is complete and verifiable, while Gemini 3 Pro's contains gaps and errors that undermine its correctness despite its more verbose presentation."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide complete, correct proofs with comparable mathematical rigor. The SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) uses a direct determinant-based approach with explicit parity analysis of A₁, A₂, A₃ and derives the key relation u^FF·d₂(-s) = -e^{-ia}u^FF·d₃(s) through careful manipulation of 2×2 determinants. GPT 5.2 employs a more elegant matrix formulation using P = diag(1,-1) to show M(q,-s) = PMP and derives u^FF(q,-s) = e^{-iαs}Pu^FF(q,s), which streamlines the argument. Both correctly handle the phase factors: Gemini 3 Pro tracks e^{-iqr·d₂} = e^{-i(q/2+a)} vs e^{-iqr·d₃} = e^{i q/2}e^{-ia}, while GPT 5.2 uses α = √3/2 for the same purpose. Both complete the proof by change of variables s → -s and matching integrands. GPT 5.2 adds pedagogical value with 'Common Mistakes to Avoid' and 'Verification' sections, but this structural verbosity doesn't enhance mathematical correctness. Neither model has errors; the core derivations are equivalent in validity."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Structured section headers', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses explicit, descriptive section headers (Setup Summary, Key Observations, Proof, Verification, Common Mistakes to Avoid, Conclusion) that organize the mathematical argument into logical phases, while the SxS winner uses only numbered subsections without descriptive titles.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Matrix transformation notation', 'description': "GPT 5.2 introduces and uses the matrix P = diag(1,-1) to compactly express the parity transformation M(q,-s) = P M(q,s) P, providing cleaner algebraic manipulation than the SxS winner's explicit component-wise parity tracking.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit verification section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Verification' section that explicitly re-checks the final identity by direct comparison of the two integral expressions, while the SxS winner concludes without this explicit cross-check.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Common mistakes documentation', 'description': "GPT 5