2026-03-25 08:27:04,558 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_12144', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nOur model consists of central force springs between mass points located on a triangular lattice. We introduce unit vectors in the lattice directions as follows: $\\hat{d}_1$ points in the direction of the $x$-axis and each of the subsequent unit vectors $\\hat{d}_i$ with $2 \\leq i \\leq 6$ rotated by an additional $\\pi/3$ in the counter-clockwise direction. This means that $\\hat{d} _{i+3} = -\\hat{d} _{i}$. Specifically, in Cartesian coordinates $(x,y)$, we have $\\hat{d}_1 = (1,0)$, $\\hat{d}_2 = (1/2, \\sqrt{3}/2)$, and $\\hat{d}_3 = (-1/2, \\sqrt{3}/2)$.\n\nWe assume a steady-state crack moving with a constant speed $v$. We change variables to $\\tau=x-v t$, $\\vec{z}=(\\tau, y)$. The equation describing the motion of the masses in the lattice is Fourier transformed over the continuous variable $\\tau$ (with conjugate variable $q$) and the discrete variable $y=n \\sqrt 3/2$ (with conjugate variable $s$). This yields a linear system for the double Fourier transform of the displacement field, $\\vec{u}^{FF}(q,s)$:\n$$\nM \\vec{u}^{FF} = \\vec{\\sigma}^{FF}\n$$\nwith the 2x2 matrix $M$ given by\n$$\nM = \\left(\n\\begin{array}{cc}\nA_1 & A_3 \\\\\nA_3 & A_2\n\\end{array}\\right)\n$$\nwhere, with $\\vec{r}=(q,s)$:\n\\begin{align*}\nA_1 &= -v^2 q^2 + (1+ i \\eta v q) \\left(4 \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{1}}{2}+ \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{2}}{2} + \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{3}}{2} \\right) , \\\\\nA_2 &= -v^2 q^2 + 3 (1+ i \\eta v q) \\left(\\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{2}}{2} + \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{3}}{2}\\right) , \\\\\nA_3 &= \\sqrt 3 (1+ i \\eta v q) \\left(\\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{2}}{2} - \\sin^2 \\frac{\\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_{3}}{2} \\right) ,\n\\end{align*}\nand the driving term $\\vec{\\sigma}^{FF} = (\\sigma_x^{FF}, \\sigma_y^{FF})$ is\n$$\n\\vec{\\sigma}^{FF}=-\\frac{N(q)}{2} \\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n(1- e^{i \\frac{q}{2}})(1 + e^{ i \\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}s} ) \\\\\n\\sqrt 3 (1+ e^{i \\frac{q}{2}})(1 - e^{ i \\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}s })\n\\end{array}\\right) .\n$$\nThe elongation of a bond in direction $\\hat{d}_i$ on the crack plane ($y=0$, corresponding to discrete index $n=0$) is denoted $Q_i$. Its Fourier transform with respect to $\\tau$ is $Q_i^F(q, n=0)$, which is obtained by solving for $\\vec{u}^{FF}$ and integrating over $s$:\n$$\nQ_i^F(q, n=0)=\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{4 \\pi} \\int_{-\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\sqrt 3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\sqrt 3}} (e^{-i \\vec{r} \\cdot \\hat{d}_i}-1) \\vec{u}^{FF} \\cdot \\hat{d}_i ds .\n$$\nIn particular, we are interested in $Q_2^F(q, n=0)$ and $Q_3^F(q, n=0)$. The vector dot products required are:\n$$\n\\vec{u}^{FF} \\cdot \\hat{d}_2=\\frac{\n\\left |\n\\begin{array}{cc}\n-d_{2x}A_3+d_{2y}A_1 & -d_{2x}A_2+d_{2y}A_3 \\\\\n\\sigma^{FF}_x & \\sigma^{FF}_y\n\\end{array}\n\\right| } { \\det M } .\n$$\n$$\n\\vec{u}^{FF} \\cdot \\hat{d}_3=\\frac{\n\\left |\n\\begin{array}{cc}\nd_{2x}A_3+d_{2y}A_1 & d_{2x}A_2+d_{2y}A_3 \\\\\n\\sigma^{FF}_x & \\sigma^{FF}_y\n\\end{array}\n\\right| } { \\det M } .\n$$\nwhere we have used $d_{3x}=-d_{2x}$ and $d_{3y}=d_{2y}$.\n## Theorem\nThe Fourier transforms of the bond elongations $Q_2$ and $Q_3$ on the crack plane ($y=0$) satisfy the symmetry condition:\n$$\nQ_3^F(q, n=0)=e^{i \\frac{q}{2}} Q_2^F(q, n=0) .\n$$\nThis corresponds to the real-space symmetry $Q_2(\\tau, y=0) = Q_3(\\tau - 1/2, y=0)$, which was assumed in the setup of the driving forces. The theorem shows that the solution is consistent with this assumption.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading '## Proof'. It includes preliminary sections (Setup Summary, Key Observations) before the main proof, and adds Verification, Common Mistakes, and Conclusion sections. The instruction asks for 'a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof' with honesty if unable to complete. The response does provide the proof under that heading, but adds substantial extra content beyond what was requested. The instruction says 'If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so' — this was not triggered as the proof was completed. The extra sections (Common Mistakes, Conclusion) were not explicitly requested but do not violate constraints. The core instruction to provide a proof under '## Proof' was satisfied. I considered whether the extra sections constitute 'unrequested content' that should cap at 4, but they are ancillary and don't detract from the main deliverable. Score 5 for near-perfect adherence with minor additions."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The mathematical claims appear correct and verifiable. Key claims checked: (1) Parity of matrix entries: A_3(q,-s) = -A_3(q,s) — VERIFIED from definition since sin^2 terms swap with sign change. (2) Transformation of σ^FF under s→-s: the derivation using 1+e^{-iαs} = e^{-iαs}(1+e^{iαs}) is correct. (3) The dot-product identity: u^FF(q,-s)·d̂_3 = -e^{-iαs} u^FF(q,s)·d̂_2 — VERIFIED through explicit calculation. (4) The final algebraic simplification: -(e^{i(q/2+αs)}-1)e^{-iαs} = e^{-iαs} - e^{iq/2} — VERIFIED. No false claims detected. One minor issue: the response states 'the limits are symmetric' when changing variables s→-s, which is true, but doesn't explicitly note that ds → -ds and the limits swap, which cancel — though this is standard and the result is correct. No unverifiable technical claims stated as fact."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'sc