2026-03-25 08:28:29,875 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_11711', 'prompt': 'For this problem we will work in Euclidean \\(A d S\\). Feel free to redo the problem for the Lorenzian case (using the notion of normalizability described in problem two).\\\\\na) Consider the usual bulk action for a (free) scalar field in \\(A d S_{D+1}\\) :\n\n\\[\nS_{\\mathrm{usual}}[\\phi]=-\\frac{1}{2} \\int d^{D} x d z \\sqrt{g}\\left((\\partial \\phi)^{2}+m^{2} \\phi^{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nShow that for \\(m^{2} L^{2}>-\\frac{D^{2}}{4}+1\\),\n\n\\[\nS_{\\text {usual }}\\left[z^{\\Delta_{-}}\\right]=\\infty\n\\]\n\nThat is, the integral over the radial direction in this action diverges near \\(z=0\\) when evaluated on a solution of the form \\(\\phi \\sim z^{\\Delta_{-}}\\), where \\(\\Delta_{-}\\)is the smaller root of \\(\\Delta(\\Delta-D)=L^{2} m^{2}\\); such a solution is non-normalizable. On the other hand, show that \\(z^{\\Delta_{+}}\\)gives a finite value, and hence a solution with these asymptotics is normalizable. With this action, show that the smallest conformal dimension \\(\\Delta=\\Delta_{+}\\)of a scalar operator in the dual theory is \\(\\Delta \\geq \\frac{D}{2}\\).\\\\\nb) Show that the following modified action\n\n\\[\nS_{K W}[\\phi] \\equiv-\\frac{1}{2} \\int d^{D+1} x \\sqrt{g}\\left(\\phi\\left(-\\square+m^{2}\\right) \\phi\\right)=S_{\\text {usual }}[\\phi]-\\int_{\\partial A d S} \\sqrt{\\gamma} \\phi \\partial_{n} \\phi\n\\]\n\nproduces the same bulk equations, but has the following property: in the window of mass values (we could call this the KW window)\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{D^{2}}{4}<L^{2} m^{2}<-\\frac{D^{2}}{4}+1\n\\]\n\nboth roots \\(\\Delta_{ \\pm}\\)give normalizable solutions with respect to the action \\(S_{K W}\\) (i.e. \\(\\left.S_{K W}\\left[z^{\\Delta_{ \\pm}}\\right]<\\infty\\right)\\).\\\\\nWhat is the lowest conformal dimension you can obtain now? (Hint: you can now consider \\(\\Delta_{+}\\)to be the dimension of the source, and \\(\\Delta_{-}\\)to be the dimension of the operator.)', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Completes all parts (a) and (b) with requested calculations. Shows divergence for z^Δ₋, finiteness for z^Δ₊, derives Δ ≥ D/2 bound, verifies S_KW gives same EOM, analyzes KW window, and finds lowest dimension (D-2)/2. Minor issue: prompt mentions 'problem two' for normalizability notion in Lorenzian case as optional, which is noted but not pursued—this is acceptable as it's marked 'feel free to redo' not required. Structure follows logical flow matching problem parts. No unrequested content added."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All technical claims verified: scaling exponents 2Δ-D-1 for S_usual and 2Δ-D+1 for S_KW are correct; convergence conditions Δ > D/2 and Δ > (D-2)/2 are correct; root formulas Δ± = D/2 ± ν with ν = √(D²/4 + m²L²) are correct; KW window bounds -D²/4 < m²L² < -D²/4 + 1 are correct; final answer Δ_min = (D-2)/2 is correct. One unverifiable claim: 'the added boundary term removes that leading radial divergence'—the mechanism described is heuristic rather than rigorously derived, though the conclusion is correct. No false claims identified."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'All mathematical derivations are correct: (a) shows S_usual[z^Δ₋] = ∞ when m²L² > -D²/4 + 1 (i.e., ν > 1), shows S_usual[z^Δ₊] < ∞, and derives Δ ≥ D/2. (b) Shows S_KW produces same bulk equations, demonstrates both roots give finite action in KW window, and finds lowest dimension (D-2)/2. The explicit check that (-□+m²)z^Δ = 0 on-shell for Δ = Δ± is correct. The boundary term scaling analysis, while slightly heuristic, reaches correct conclusion. Numerical examples correctly illustrate the transition.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear mathematical exposition with well-structured sections. Good use of displayed equations and logical flow. Minor issues: occasional redundancy in explanations (e.g., 'Explicitly for...' section repeats what was already established); 'power counting' parenthetical is slightly informal; the phrase 'the added boundary term removes that leading radial divergence' could be more precise. These are trivial style preferences. Overall professional quality suitable for physics exposition."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Generally appropriate length for a detailed physics derivation. However, contains identifiable padding: the 'Two numerical examples' section, while illustrative, goes beyond what the prompt strictly requires and could be trimmed without losing core content. The explicit check 'Using the defining 'bulk' form...' in part (b) is somewhat redundant given the earlier variation argument. The summary table, while useful, is also supplementary. These additions push length slightly above ideal for a direct problem solution."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.85, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.85'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Completes all required parts: (a) shows divergence for z^Δ₋ and finiteness for z^Δ₊ with condition m²L² > -D²/4 + 1, derives Δ ≥ D/2; (b) shows S_KW gives same bulk EOM, analyzes KW window, shows both roots normalizable, finds lowest dimension (D-2)/2. Compact format with clear part labeling. One minor gap: does not explicitly state 'S_KW[z^Δ±] < ∞' with the explicit inequality verification for both roots in the window, though this is implicitly shown. No unrequested content."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All claims verified: the key formula √g[(∂φ)²+m²φ²] = L^{D-1}Δ(2Δ-D)z^{2Δ-D-1} is correct (derived from Δ(Δ-D)=m²L²). The S_usual evaluation giving -Δ/2 V_D L^{D-1} ε^{2Δ-D} is correct. The S_KW scaling argument with ∼ z^{2Δ-D+1} is correct. The condition 0<ν<1 for KW window is correct. Final answer Δ_min = (D-2)/2 is correct. One unverifiable: 'the first non-zero piece is O(z^{Δ+2})'—this is standard for AdS but not explicitly derived here; however, this is a standard result and the conclusion holds."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Mathematically correct throughout. The derivation in (a) elegantly uses the on-shell condition to simplify the integrand. The evaluation with cutoff z=ε correctly shows divergence for Δ₋ when ν>1. The (b) part correctly identifies the changed scaling for S_KW and the new convergence condition. The final answer (D-2)/2 is correct. One very minor point: the prompt asks to 'show that S_KW[z^Δ±] < ∞' explicitly, and while this is demonstrated, the explicit statement for both roots could be more prominent."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Exceptionally clear and concise mathematical physics writing. Efficient use of equations with minimal prose. Logical flow is immediate and transparent. No grammar issues, no redundancy, no jargon without context. The compact presentation loses no mathematical rigor. Publication-ready for a physics journal or problem set solution. Nothing to improve.'}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Perfectly calibrated. Every equation and sentence serves a purpose. No numerical examples (not required), no summary table (not required), no redundant explanations. The derivation is complete yet minimal. Nothing can be cut without losing mathematical content; nothing is missing for the requested solution.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.3, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.30'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both responses correctly solve the problem, but Response A provides more pedagogical structure with clear dimensional analysis, explicit power-counting steps, and concrete numerical examples that illustrate the normalizability transition. Response B is more compact but skips key explanatory steps—particularly the explicit derivation showing how the boundary term in S_KW removes the leading divergence and shifts the normalizability condition from Δ > D/2 to Δ > (D-2)/2. Response A's detailed breakdown of tangential vs radial derivative contributions makes the mechanism transparent, while Response B merely states the result without showing the cancellation explicitly."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner correctly identifies that for S_usual, the condition m²L² > -D²/4 + 1 (i.e., ν > 1) makes Δ_- non-normalizable, and explicitly shows the divergence with ∫₀ dz z^(-2ν-1) = ∞. For S_KW, the SxS winner correctly derives the modified normalizability condition Δ > (D-2)/2 by analyzing how the boundary term removes the leading radial divergence, leaving tangential derivative contributions that scale as z^(2Δ-D+1). The SxS winner correctly concludes the lowest conformal dimension is (D-2)/2. In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro makes critical errors: it incorrectly states the boundary term 'diverges for Δ_-' and that S_KW 'cancels' this divergence, when in fact S_KW = S_usual - boundary term and the cancellation happens algebraically, not by having both diverge separately. More seriously, Gemini 3 Pro incorrectly claims the KW window condition arises from 'subleading corrections' requiring ν < 1 for convergence, when the actual mechanism is that the boundary term modifies the effective scaling requirement from Δ > D/2 to Δ > (D-2)/2, as correctly derived in the SxS winner. Gemini 3 Pro also contains a calculation error in Example 2 where it computes ν = √(-4+0) = 2 for m²L² = 0, when it should be ν = √(4+0) = 2, though it recovers the correct value. The SxS winner's power counting is more rigorous and its physical interpretation of why the KW window exists is correct, while Gemini 3 Pro's explanation conflates leading and subleading divergence analysis."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both responses are virtually identical in content, structure, and mathematical derivation. The SxS winner and GPT 5.2 both correctly apply power-counting analysis in Poincaré coordinates, derive the convergence condition Δ > D/2 for S_usual, identify the KW window -D²/4 < m²L² < -D²/4 + 1, and obtain the lower bound Δ_min = (D-2)/2. Both include identical numerical examples with D=3, m²L²=-2 and D=3, m²L²=0, and feature the same summary table. The responses match even at the level of phrasing, equation formatting, and section organization. No meaningful differences exist in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, or Verbosity. The SxS winner contains a minor header artifact ('## Strategy Overview') that GPT 5.2 also contains, confirming these are the same response."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper display math delimiters ($$...$$) for equations while the SxS winner uses inconsistent inline math for multi-line derivations.', 'scale': '1-6: 1=minor formatting difference, 6=severe readability impact from delimiter misuse', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit subleading divergence analysis', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly analyzes why subleading corrections require the KW window constraint (ν < 1), while the SxS winner only states the condition without deriving the subleading divergence mechanism.', 'scale': '1-6: 1=brief mention, 6=complete derivation of subleading power counting', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Physical interpretation of boundary term cancellation', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that the boundary term 'analytically cancels the leading ε^{-2ν} divergence of S_usual,' while the SxS winner describes the mechanism without this explicit cancellation language.", 'scale': '1-6: 1=implicit understanding, 6=explicit cancellat