rve theorem to conclude at least 2 intersection points. The proof is complete and correct. The key insight—using the intermediate value theorem to find a line through O that bisects both the square and the component D, then showing this line must hit Γ at least twice—is mathematically sound and fully addresses the prompt.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Exceptionally clear mathematical writing. Strengths: logical flow from setup to conclusion, precise notation (ℓ_θ, H_θ, f(θ)), clear case separation, proper use of '∎' to mark end. One minor issue: the phrase 'whence D lies in one of the two closed half–squares' uses 'whence' which is slightly archaic but acceptable in mathematical writing; also 'half–squares' with en-dash is slightly inconsistent formatting. The proof structure is exemplary with clear paragraph breaks between major steps. Professional quality throughout."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for a competition-level geometry proof. Nothing can be cut because: (1) the trivial case O∈Γ must be mentioned, (2) the definition of f(θ) and continuity argument is minimal, (3) the IVT application needs explicit g(θ) definition, (4) the 'ℓ meets Γ' subsection is essential to the argument, (5) the Jordan curve theorem conclusion is the coup de grâce. Nothing is missing because: the proof is self-contained, defines all terms, and reaches the required conclusion. One could possibly trim 'Choosing θ_0 so that ℓ_θ_0 avoids the four vertices' as generic position is usually assumed, but this is minor. Score 5."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Instruction checklist: [X] Prove existence of two points A,B on Γ [X] with line AB through center O [X] given equal area division. However: [ ] The proof contains a logical gap/inconsistency.