re [-1,1]×[-1,1], clearly non-linear. It verifies equal area by computing Area(S₂) = ∫₋₁¹(x² + ⅔)dx = 2, exactly half of total area 4. It then explicitly verifies the general result by: setting up the collinearity condition, deriving x₁x₂ = -⅓, choosing x₁=1, x₂=-⅓, computing A=(1,⅔) and B=(-⅓,-²⁄₉), and verifying slopes match (⅔). No fault found—thorough verification with explicit calculations.'}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response provides a circle of radius r=√(2/π) centered at O, which is non-linear. It verifies equal area (inside has area 2, outside has area 2). However, this example is centrally symmetric, making the verification trivial (any diameter works). The response acknowledges this by saying 'indeed here there are infinitely many such pairs—any diametrically opposite points.' While valid, this doesn't test the general proof's mechanism as strongly as a non-symmetric example would. The response offers to give a 'non-symmetric wavy Jordan curve' example but doesn't actually provide it. This is a minor weakness—the example works but is too symmetric to fully demonstrate the theorem's non-triviality."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Mathematical Correctness', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct. Key steps: (1) defines rotation ρ and Γ'=ρ(Γ), (2) shows if Γ∩Γ'=∅ then Γ'⊂R₁ or R₂, say R₁, (3) then R₁'=ρ(R₁)⊂R₁, but equal areas force R₁'=R₁, hence Γ'=Γ, contradicting disjointness, (4) so Γ∩Γ'≠∅, giving P∈Γ∩Γ', so P∈Γ and ρ(P)∈Γ, with O as midpoint of Pρ(P). The logic is sound: equal areas prevent proper containment, forcing intersection. The handling of P=ρ(P) case (P=O) is also correct. No fault found—rigorous and complete."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof is mostly correct but has a subtle issue. The strategy uses f(θ)=|R∩H_θ| and shows g(θ)=f(θ)-¼|S| has g(θ+π)=-g(θ), giving a zero by IVT. This finds a line L_θ₀ that bisects R. The argument that 'both half-planes contain points of R and of T' is correct, but the conclusion that 'the line must intersect their common boundary Γ on both sides of O' assumes Γ is connected and that the regions on each side of the line are separated by Γ. However, the proof doesn't explicitly rule out that Γ might intersect L_θ₀ at exactly one point (tangent) or at O itself in a degenerate way. The claim 'once in the left half-plane and once in the right half-plane' needs more justification—if Γ is tangent to L_θ₀ at a single point on one side, or passes through O, the conclusion might fail. The proof would benefit from addressing why the intersection gives two distinct points on opposite sides of O."}, 'gemini': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof has several issues. First, step 2 claims 'no two points on Γ can lie in the same or exactly opposite directions from O'—but this is incorrect reasoning. The assumption is 'no line through O intersects Γ in more than one point,' which doesn't prevent two points in the same direction (they would be on the same ray from O, not on a line through O). Actually, if two points are in the same direction from O, they lie on a line through O, contradicting the assumption—so this part is actually okay, but the 'exactly opposite directions' claim is the key. The main error is in step 7: 'S₁ is an open region'—but S₁ is not necessarily open (it's a component of S\\Γ, which is open in S, but S₁ relative to the plane may not be open if Γ approaches the boundary). More seriously, the claim that 'Area(S₁∩H₁)>0' because it's 'non-empty open set' assumes S₁∩H₁ is open, which requires Γ to not accumulate at points of L, which isn't guaranteed. The proof also doesn't handle the case where Γ passes through O. These gaps weaken the rigor."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct. The structure: (1) defines ρ and Γ'=ρ(Γ), (2) shows Γ' also divides into equal areas, (3) assumes Γ∩Γ'=∅, so Γ'⊂U (one component), (4) then V⊂ρ(U) or ρ(V), say W, (5) equal areas force V=W, so U=ρ(V), (6) but then Γ'⊂Γ∪∂