n$$\nE(X_s^2|{{\\mathcal F}}_{\\ge t})=\\frac{s(s+\\tau)}{t(t+\\tau)}X_t^2+\\frac{s(t-s)}{t(t+\\tau)}\\theta X_t+\\frac{s(t-s)}{t+\\tau}.\n$$\nAlternatively, in the case corresponding to $\\tau=\\infty$, the conditional second moment is given by\n$$\nE(X_s^2|{{\\mathcal F}}_{\\ge t})=\\frac{s}{t}X_t^2+\\frac{s(t-s)}{t}\\theta X_t.\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading '## Proof'. It uses the setup's notation and assumptions correctly. Minor formatting variance: uses '\\medskip\\noindent\\textbf{...}' for section headers rather than simple bold or numbered sections, but this is stylistic and doesn't impede readability. All required elements from the setup are incorporated: the process definition (1), linear conditional expectation (2), quadratic conditional variance (3), and linear independence assumption. The proof is complete and honest (no false claims of completion). Checked: [X] Uses ## Proof header, [X] Incorporates all setup assumptions, [X] Derives both cases (τ<∞ and τ=∞), [X] Cleaned up final presentation. The only trivial issue is use of LaTeX formatting macros that are slightly non-standard but fully comprehensible."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The mathematical claims are sophisticated and appear internally consistent, but several key steps involve unverifiable assertions without explicit justification: (1) The claim that uniqueness of m,n,o 'follows from the linear independence of 1,X_t,X_t^2' is stated without proof—while plausible, this requires justification that the conditional expectation's quadratic form has unique coefficients; (2) The solution to the Jensen functional equation 'g(t)=αt+β' is asserted without verification that g is continuous/measurable (needed for affine solutions); (3) The derivation 'f'(x)=θ/(x+τ)^2' involves differentiation without establishing differentiability of f. These are standard gaps in stochastic process literature but remain unverified claims. No false claims detected, but multiple unverifiable technical assertions cap score at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'The response correctly proves the stated proposition. Verification of key claims: [CORRECT] The multiplicative property m(s,u)=m(s,t)m(t,u) is correctly derived from the tower property; [CORRECT] The form m(s,t)=h(s)/h(t) follows from the functional equation; [CORRECT] The Jensen equation solution leads to h(t)=t(αt+β); [CORRECT] The final formulas match the proposition statement exactly for both cases (τ<∞ and τ=∞). The response correctly identifies τ≥0 from non-negativity of conditional variance. One minor gap: the case analysis for n(s,t) when h(t)=t versus h(t)=t(t+τ) could be more explicit about why these are the only cases, but the conclusion is correct. All major requirements satisfied with correct conclusion.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is professional and clear with excellent mathematical exposition. Specific strengths: (1) Clear sectioning with numbered steps and descriptive headers ('Consequences of (3)', 'Determination of m', etc.); (2) Proper use of displayed equations and alignment; (3) Logical flow from assumptions to conclusion; (4) Appropriate level of formality for a research-level mathematics proof. One minor issue: the notation 'E_t^u' is introduced without definition (appears to mean E(·|F_{≥u}) after conditioning on F_{≥t}), which could confuse readers. Otherwise, the style is publication-ready with only this trivial notational ambiguity."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'The length is well-calibrated for a research-level proof. Nothing can be cut because: (1) Each of the 5 sections addresses a distinct necessary component (consequences of assumptions, determining m, determining n, determining o, final form); (2) The functional equation derivations require the displayed steps shown; (3) The case analysis for τ<∞ vs τ=∞ is essential. Nothing is missing because: (1) All three functions m,n,o are fully determined; (2) Initial conditions are verified; (3) Both forms in the proposition are derived. Minor trimming possible: the explicit display of equation labels (4) and (5) could be condensed, but this aids cross-referencing. Ideal length ±10%.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction with '## Proof' header and complete derivation. It incorporates all setup assumptions correctly. Minor variance: uses '{\\bf ...}' for section headers rather than cleaner formatting, and uses '\\label{rev}', '\\label{mult}', etc. for equation references which is standard but creates slight visual clutter. Checked: [X] ## Proof header present, [X] All assumptions (1)-(3) used, [X] Both τ<∞ and τ=∞ cases derived, [X] Complete proof provided. One small issue: the limit 'u→∞' in step 1 is used without explicit justification that this limit exists in L^2, though square-integrability is mentioned—this is minor but technically requires more care."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Mathematically sophisticated with internal consistency, but several unverifiable claims: (1) The limit u→∞ in step 1 to obtain E(X_s|F_{≥t})=s/t X_t is asserted without proper justification—while correct, the convergence requires more than just square-integrability (martingale convergence or similar); (2) The claim that 'c:=h(t)/A(t) is constant' from h(u)A(t)=h(t)A(u) assumes continuity or measurability conditions not explicitly stated; (3) The deduction that f(x)=c-θ/(x+τ) from the functional equation involves assuming differentiability. These are standard gaps in the literature but remain technically unverified. No false claims detected, but unverifiable technical steps cap score at 4."},