2026-03-25 08:29:55,718 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_376', 'prompt': 'Prove the following: \\n\\nLet $v \\in \\mathfrak{g}$. Then $L^{v}(x)=R^{\\operatorname{Ad}_{x} v}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist: [X] Prove the identity L^v(x) = R^{Ad_x v} — fully satisfied with clear proof structure. [X] Use standard Lie group definitions — provided. [X] Work with left/right invariant vector fields — explicitly defined and used. [X] Incorporate adjoint representation — correctly defined and applied. No unrequested content added; no constraints violated. All instruction elements satisfied. Considered fault: whether explicit 'for all x' quantification was needed — the proof concludes with 'for every x ∈ G and v ∈ 𝔤', satisfying this."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Fact-check of claims: [VERIFIED] Definition L^v(x) = (dL_x)_e(v) — standard. [VERIFIED] Definition R^w(x) = (dR_x)_e(w) — standard. [VERIFIED] Ad_x = (dc_x)_e with c_x(g) = xgx^{-1} — standard. [VERIFIED] Identity L_x = R_x ∘ c_x — verified by computation (xgx^{-1})x = xg. [VERIFIED] Chain rule application (dL_x)_e = (dR_x)_{c_x(e)} ∘ (dc_x)_e — correct. [VERIFIED] c_x(e) = e — correct. Minor issue: uses 'c_x' for conjugation while some texts use 'C_x' or 'Int_x', but this is notation, not error. No unverifiable claims. Score capped at 5 due to lack of explicit citation to authoritative source, though mathematics is standard."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] The identity L^v(x) = R^{Ad_x v}(x) is proven. [CORRECT] Step 1: Conjugation identity L_x = R_x ∘ c_x — verified. [CORRECT] Step 2: Differentiation at identity — chain rule applied correctly with correct base point c_x(e) = e. [CORRECT] Step 3: Identification of (dc_x)_e with Ad_x — by definition. [CORRECT] Final evaluation on v — yields desired equality. No partially correct or incorrect elements. The proof is complete and correct.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Structure is excellent with clear Strategy, Problem Analysis, Proof sections. Mathematical notation is consistent and precise. Grammar is correct. Minor issue identified: 'Differentiating this identity at the identity element will give the desired equality' in Strategy section uses future tense while rest is present tense — trivial inconsistency. Another minor issue: 'the first factor simplifies' could be more explicit about what 'first factor' refers to. These are trivial style preferences. Overall professional quality with clear logical flow."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof is medium — requires definitions, key observation, and 3-step proof. Response provides: Strategy (3 items), Problem Analysis (concrete interpretation), Proof (3 numbered steps with clear justification). Assessment: Nothing can be cut because each section serves distinct purpose — Strategy sets up tools, Problem Analysis interprets the goal, Proof executes. Nothing is missing because all definitions are given, the key geometric observation is explicit, and the chain rule application is detailed. Slightly more verbose than minimal but aids clarity; could trim 'In concrete terms we must prove' paragraph which restates obvious interpretation, hence score 5 not 6."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist: [X] Prove the identity L^v(x) = R^{Ad_x v} — fully satisfied. [X] Use standard Lie group definitions — provided with C_x notation for conjugation. [X] Work with left/right invariant vector fields — explicitly defined. [X] Incorporate adjoint representation — correctly defined. No unrequested content; no constraints violated. All instruction elements satisfied. Considered fault: uses C_x instead of c_x for conjugation — this is acceptable notation variation, not a failure.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Fact-check of claims: [VERIFIED] All definitions match standard Lie theory. [VERIFIED] Identity L_x = R_x ∘ C_x — verified by computation. [VERIFIED] Chain rule: d_eL_x = d_{C_x(e)}R_x ∘ d_eC_x — correct. [VERIFIED] C_x(e) = e — correct. [VERIFIED] Ad_x = d_eC_x — by definition. [VERIFIED] Final equality — correct. Minor issue: Step 2 writes 'd_e(R_x ∘ C_x) = d_{C_x(e)}R_x ∘ d_eC_x' which is correct, but the intermediate display 'd_eL_x = d_e(R_x ∘ C_x)' could be misread as equating differentials before chain rule is applied — actually this is equality of maps, so composition is implied. Not a factual error. No unverifiable claims. Score 5 due to lack of explicit citations."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Identity proven. [CORRECT] Step 1: Express L_x via conjugation — verified. [CORRECT] Step 2: Differentiate at identity — chain rule with correct base point. [CORRECT] Step 3: Identify d_eC_x with Ad_x — correct. [CORRECT] Step 4: Apply to v — yields result. Notation is slightly cleaner than A: uses d_e consistently vs (dL_x)_e, and C_x vs c_x. Both are correct. The proof is complete and correct.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Excellent structure with Strategy, Problem Analysis, Proof sections. Notation is clean and consistent (d_eL_x vs (dL_x)_e is slightly more readable). Minor issue: 'since R_x(C_x(y)) = (xyx^{-1})x = xy = L_x(y)' — the 'since' clause is slightly awkward grammatically; better as 'because' or restructured. Another minor issue: 'Thus the left-invariant vector field evaluated at x coincides with the right-invariant vector field evaluated at x on the adjoint-transformed Lie algebra element' — 'on' is slightly awkward preposition choice, 'generated by' would be clearer. These are trivial preferences. Professional quality overall."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length assessment: Response B is more compact than A while maintaining completeness. Strategy: 3 concise bullet points. Problem Analysis: one focused paragraph. Proof: 4 numbered steps, each essential. Assessment: Nothing can be cut — Step 1 gives key identity, Step 2 differentiates, Step 3 identifies Ad_x, Step 4 applies to v. Each step is necessary. Nothing is missing — all definitions appear in Strategy, the geometric insight is explicit, chain rule is detailed. The slightly more compact Problem Analysis (one paragraph vs A's two) improves efficiency without losing clarity. Perfectly calibrated length."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.6, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.60'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both responses are mathematically equivalent and correct, using identical proof strategies (L_x = R_x ∘ C_x, differentiate at identity, apply Ad_x definition). Response A uses slightly more verbose notation ((dL_x)_e vs d_eL_x) and includes a 'Problem Analysis' section with redundant restatement, while Response B is more compact with cleaner notation. Neither contains errors or omissions; the differences are purely presentational."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and clarity in Instruction Following and Truthfulness. The SxS winner correctly identifies the key identity L_x = R_x ∘ c_x (conjugation followed by right translation), which directly yields the result through clean differentiation at e, with proper handling of base points: (dL_x)_e = (dR_x)_e ∘ Ad_x. Gemini 3 Pro contains a critical error in Method 1: it incorrectly states C_x = R_{x^{-1}} ∘ L_x when in fact C_x = L_x ∘ R_{x^{-1}} = R_{x^{-1}} ∘ L_x only holds when these commute, which they don't; more seriously, Gemini's chain rule application has wrong base points—(dR_{x^{-1}})_x ∘ (dL_x)_e is dimensionally wrong since (dL_x)_e: T_eG → T_xG and (dR_{x^{-1}})_x: T_xG → T_eG, giving a map T_eG → T_eG, not matching Ad_x: T_eG → T_eG. Gemini's 'correction' via (dR_x)_e ∘ (dR_{x^{-1}})_x = id is also wrong since (dR_x)_e: T_eG → T_xG and (dR_{x^{-1}})_x: T_xG → T_eG, so their composition is T_eG → T_eG, not T_xG → T_xG as claimed. Method 2 in Gemini is correct but verbose. SxS winner's concise, correct proof with proper differential geometry notation outperforms Gemini's flawed first method and unnecessarily lengthy second method."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide correct, complete proofs of the identity using different but equally valid approaches. The SxS winner uses a direct differential-geometric approach via the chain rule on the identity L_x = R_x ∘ c_x, yielding (dL_x)_e = (dR_x)_e ∘ Ad_x; this is elegant and concise. GPT 5.2 uses an exponential-map characterization, proving x exp(tv) = exp(t Ad_x v) x via the property that automorphisms preserve one-parameter subgroups, then differentiating; this is equally rigorous and perhaps more intuitive for readers familiar with matrix groups. Both correctly handle the key step of relating left and right translations through conjugation. Neither contains errors; both cite definitions accurately and reach the conclusion. The SxS winner's proof is slightly more general (works for any Lie group without requiring exponential map surjectivity), while GPT 5.2's approach is more concrete and accessible. Verbosity is comparable and appropriate for the mathematical depth."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Multiple proof methods provided', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides two distinct, complete proof methods (differential approach and smooth curves approach) while the SxS winner provides only one.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = one extra method mentioned briefly, 3-4 = second method fully worked out with clear distinction, 5-6 = multiple substantially different approaches each complete and illuminating', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit curve-based verification', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a complete alternative proof using smooth curves and the exponential map, with explicit construction of test curves γ(t) and η(t), which the SxS winner lacks entirely.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = curves mentioned in passing, 3-4 = curves used with some explicit construction, 5-6 = fully explicit curve construction with clear derivative calculations and cancellation', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Chain rule application explicitness', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly tracks evaluation points of differentials (e.g., noting (dR_{x^{-1}})_x is evaluated at L_x(e) = x) and shows intermediate chain rule steps, while SxS winner collapses these.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = some intermediate points noted, 3-4 = clear tracking of evaluation points in key steps, 5-6 = meticulous tracking of all evaluation points and compositions', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Geometric interpretation depth', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides richer geometric commentary explaining what each operation represents (pushforward, tangent vectors, one-parameter subgroups) beyond mere algebraic manipulation.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = slightly more commentary, 3-4 = substantive geometric insight added, 5-6 = deep geometric intuition that illuminates why the identity holds structurally', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent $$...$$ display math delimiters throughout, while SxS winner mixes \\[...\\] and $$...$$ and has formatting inconsistencies.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 = minor delimiter preference, 3-4 = noticeable consistency advantage, 5-6 = perfectly consistent professional formatting', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Conjugation decomposition clarity', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly decomposes C_x = R_{x^{-1}} ∘ L_x and tracks t