2026-03-25 08:30:29,114 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_361', 'prompt': 'Prove the following: \\n\\nSuppose \\(q: X \\rightarrow Y\\) is an open quotient map. Then \\(Y\\) is Hausdorff if and only if the set \\(\\mathcal{R}=\\left\\{\\left(x_{1}, x_{2}\\right): q\\left(x_{1}\\right)=q\\left(x_{2}\\right)\\right\\}\\) is closed in \\(X \\times X\\).', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist: [X] Prove 'Y Hausdorff ⇒ R closed' - fully addressed in Section 1; [X] Prove 'R closed ⇒ Y Hausdorff' - fully addressed in Section 2; [X] Explain role of openness hypothesis - addressed in Section 3 with explicit counterexample discussion; [X] Appropriate mathematical formatting - LaTeX used correctly. No unrequested content added. All instruction elements satisfied with clear structure matching the bidirectional proof requirement."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Fact-check of claims: [VERIFIED] 'quotient map implies continuous' - standard definition; [VERIFIED] 'open map sends open sets to open sets' - definition; [VERIFIED] 'Hausdorff spaces have disjoint open neighborhoods for distinct points' - definition; [VERIFIED] 'preimages of open sets under continuous map are open' - standard theorem; [VERIFIED] 'product of open sets is open in product topology' - standard; [VERIFIED] 'complement of R open implies R closed' - set theory. One minor issue: The claim 'there are quotient maps with closed fibre-relation whose quotients are non-Hausdorff' is [UNVERIFIABLE] without specific citation, though the general principle is correct. No false claims found. Score capped at 5 due to unverifiable specific example claim."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Forward direction (Hausdorff ⇒ R closed): Correctly uses continuity of q and Hausdorff property of Y to construct disjoint open neighborhoods in X×X whose product avoids R. [CORRECT] Reverse direction (R closed ⇒ Hausdorff): Correctly uses openness of q to ensure q(U), q(V) are open in Y, with proper justification that (U×V)∩R=∅ implies q(U)∩q(V)=∅. [CORRECT] Analysis of openness necessity: Correctly identifies that openness is only needed for reverse direction, with accurate explanation of failure mode. All mathematical reasoning verified correct.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths - Clear section structure (1,2,3), logical flow, appropriate mathematical tone, good use of displayed equations. Minor issue identified: The phrase 'Where openness of q is used' and similar italicized commentary, while helpful, slightly interrupts the formal proof structure. This is a trivial style preference. No grammar errors, no run-on sentences, appropriate technical register for mathematical proof. Professional quality throughout."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Nothing can be cut because: (1) Section 3's discussion of what fails without openness is essential for complete understanding; (2) The explicit note about where openness is/isn't used clarifies the proof structure; (3) Each step in both directions is necessary for rigor. Nothing is missing because: The proof covers both directions completely, includes the necessity analysis, and provides appropriate level of detail for a topology proof. Ideal length for this mathematical content; only trivial trimming possible (e.g., the italicized 'do not really need' commentary could be slightly condensed)."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist: [X] Prove 'Y Hausdorff ⇒ R closed' - fully addressed in Section 1; [X] Prove 'R closed ⇒ Y Hausdorff' - fully addressed in Section 2; [X] Explain role of openness hypothesis - addressed in Section 3 with explicit counterexample; [X] Appropriate mathematical formatting - LaTeX used correctly. All instruction elements satisfied. Structure matches bidirectional proof requirement with clear numbering."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check of claims: [VERIFIED] All standard topological claims (continuity, openness, Hausdorff definition, product topology). [VERIFIED] Logical structure of both directions. [QUESTIONABLE] The counterexample given: 'identity map id: R_usual → R_indiscrete is a quotient map with closed R (the diagonal), yet codomain not Hausdorff.' This is [FALSE/QUESTIONABLE]. The identity map from R_usual to R_indiscrete is NOT a quotient map. A quotient map requires: (1) surjective (yes), (2) continuous (yes), (3) U open in codomain iff preimage open in domain. For R_indiscrete, only ∅ and R are open. Preimage of R is R (open in usual), preimage of ∅ is ∅ (open). So yes, it satisfies the quotient definition. BUT: The fiber relation R = {(x,x): x∈R} is NOT closed in R×R with usual topology? Actually the diagonal IS closed in R_usual × R_usual. However, the issue: is this actually a quotient map? The topology on R_indiscrete is {∅, R}. The map id: R_usual → R_indiscrete is continuous. Is it a quotient map? Check: V open in R_indiscrete iff id^{-1}(V) open in R_usual. id^{-1}(∅)=∅ (open), id^{-1}(R)=R (open). Yes, this holds. So the example is actually [VERIFIED] correct. However, the claim that this shows 'what fails without openness' is slightly misleading because this quotient map is not 'not open'—it's actually open (since image of any open set in R_usual is... no wait, image of (0,1) is (0,1) which is not open in R_indiscrete). So it's NOT open. The example is valid. Score 4 due to initial concern about example validity requiring careful verification."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Forward direction: Proper use of continuity and Hausdorff property. [CORRECT] Reverse direction: Proper use of openness to get open sets in Y. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] The counterexample in Section 3: While the identity map id: R_usual → R_indiscrete is indeed a quotient map that is not open, with R closed (diagonal), and codomain not Hausdorff, the presentation slightly conflates issues. The fiber relation being 'the diagonal' is correct. However, the explanation 'the step that collapses' is slightly less clear than Response A's explanation. The mathematics is correct but the explanatory clarity in Section 3 is marginally weaker."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths - Clean structure, professional mathematical tone, good equation formatting. Minor issue: The phrase 'Where openness of q matters?' with question mark is slightly informal for a formal proof. The transition 'We claim O_1 ∩ O_2 = ∅' followed by proof by contradiction is standard but the 'We claim' construction is slightly less direct than necessary. These are trivial preferences. No significant grammar or clarity issues."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Nothing can be cut because: Both directions are proven with necessary detail, Section 3 provides essential counterexample analysis. Nothing is missing because: Complete proof with all necessary steps. The counterexample in Section 3, while valid, could be slightly more explicitly analyzed (the explanation of 'what collapses' is slightly brief). Ideal length with only trivial possible trimming."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both responses are mathematically correct and structurally equivalent, with nearly identical organization, notation, and reasoning. Response A uses slightly more formal LaTeX formatting (\\( \\) vs plain text) and explicitly labels the fiber-relation with a displayed equation, while Response B uses 'O₁, O₂' versus 'q(U₁), q(U₂)' and includes a concrete counterexample (id: ℝ_usual → ℝ_indiscrete) in section 3 where Response A only gives a vague description. These differences are stylistic and minor; neither response contains errors or significant pedagogical advantages over the other."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Truthfulness and Writing Style. The SxS winner correctly notes that openness of q is not needed for the forward direction, with a clear aside explaining this nuance, and provides a rigorous section 3 analyzing exactly what fails without openness. Gemini 3 Pro's response contains a critical error in its 'faster way' note: it claims R = (q × q)^{-1}(Δ_Y), but this is wrong—the correct statement is that R = (q × q)^{-1}(Δ_Y) only when q is injective; actually R = {(x1,x2) : q(x1)=q(x2)} is the pullback of the diagonal under q×q, which is correct, but the parenthetical claim that this shows openness is irrelevant is misleading since the argument actually requires q×q to be a quotient map, not just continuous. More seriously, Gemini 3 Pro's formatting has numerous spacing errors ('existdisjoint', 'areopen', 'X \timesX', 'isclosed', 'ReverseDirection') and uses excessive bolding that disrupts readability, while the SxS winner maintains clean LaTeX formatting throughout. Both responses correctly identify where openness is essential in the reverse direction, but the SxS winner's explicit equation (2) showing q(U1) ∩ q(U2) = ∅ before invoking openness is clearer than Gemini 3 Pro's prose-heavy verification list."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "Both models prove the theorem correctly with similar logical structure, but the SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior mathematical exposition through more precise notation and deeper pedagogical insight. Gemini 3 Pro explicitly notes that openness is unnecessary for the forward direction with a dedicated subsection, whereas GPT 5.2 only briefly mentions this in a parenthetical. Gemini 3 Pro's Section 3 'What fails without the openness hypothesis?' provides a concrete counterexample sketch ('collapse a closed, non-open subset of a Hausdorff space to a point') that GPT 5.2 lacks—GPT 5.2's analogous section merely restates the general issue without this specific instantiation. Both correctly use the openness hypothesis in the reverse direction, but Gemini 3 Pro's equation (2) explicitly showing q(U₁) ∩ q(U₂) = ∅ before invoking openness creates clearer logical flow than GPT 5.2's Step 1/2/3 breakdown which separates these observations."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Alternative proof via diagonal argument', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes an explicit alternative proof using the diagonal characterization of Hausdorff spaces (Y Hausdorff iff Δ_Y closed in Y×Y) and the continuity of q×q', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 mentions it briefly, 3-4 presents it clearly, 5-6 presents it with full rigor as a distinct proof method', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit saturation discussion', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines and discusses the saturation q^{-1}(q(U)) as the key obstruction when q is not open, naming this concept precisely', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 vague mention, 3-4 names saturation, 5-6 fully explains why saturation of open need not be open', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Structured proof with explicit verification checklist', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses a numbered checklist format (contain points, disjoint, open) to explicitly verify the three required properties of separating neighborhoods', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 has some structure, 3-4 uses clear numbering, 5-6 uses explicit verification checklist with all three properties', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent LaTeX delimiters ($...$) throughout, while the SxS winner mixes \\( ... \\) and $...$ with occasional display math', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 m