p verification with explicit justification throughout. For example, 'Use the chain rule for D⁻¹: d/dt(D⁻¹) = -D⁻² D_t' explicitly states the rule being used, then 'Now compute D_t' with explicit differentiation. Each algebraic step includes its justification. I considered whether the independent check section repeats work unnecessarily, but this is actually additional verification, and all steps remain well-justified."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Boundary Case a=0 Testing', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Section '### 3. Boundary case a=0 (consistency check)' explicitly identifies, tests, and confirms the a=0 case. It states: 'If a=0, the ODE reduces to u_t=0 → the solution must be constant, u(t)=x.' Then explicitly shows: 'Our formula gives u(t)=x/(1-0·x(t-τ))=x/(1)=x'. The consistency with the known solution is explicitly confirmed. I considered whether the physical interpretation could be stronger, but the rubric only requires identification, testing, and confirmation of consistency."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Section '#### (c) Boundary case a=0' explicitly identifies the case, shows the ODE becomes u_t=0 requiring constant solution, and confirms 'The constant solution u≡x is recovered, confirming consistency.' The formula substitution is shown explicitly. I found no fault - the case is fully identified, tested, and confirmed consistent with the known solution."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Section '**Boundary Case a = 0**' explicitly identifies the case, shows both the ODE behavior (u_t=0 → constant solution) and formula substitution (yields x), and explicitly states 'This confirms perfect consistency between the known behavior of the ODE at a=0 and our derived formula.' I found no fault - the identification, testing, and confirmation are all explicit and complete."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Section '## 5) Boundary case check: a=0' explicitly identifies the case, derives that the known solution is u(t)≡x, and shows the formula gives 'x/(1-0·x