. The counting argument properly establishes the contradiction via f(s)>2n for 1≤s≤n-1, with explicit calculation showing f(s)-2n = n(s-1)+(s+1)-s² > 0. The s=0 case is handled with a parity argument (at least two odd components giving 2(n+1)>2n). The logic is sound and the contradiction is properly derived. I considered whether the f(s)-2n calculation could be shown more explicitly, but the given form is verifiable and correct.'}, 'gemini': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct. Dirac's Theorem is correctly applied: δ(G)≥n=(2n)/2 guarantees a Hamiltonian cycle. The extraction of a perfect matching by taking alternating edges from an even-length Hamiltonian cycle is correct. The base case n=1 is handled properly. The counterexample K_{n-1,n+1} correctly has minimum degree n-1 and no perfect matching. I considered whether the simple graph assumption needs more justification, but the explicit note about multigraphs shows awareness of this issue."}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct. Dirac's Theorem is correctly stated and applied. The verification of hypotheses is accurate, including the handling of n=1 as a trivial case. The construction of the perfect matching from the Hamiltonian cycle is correct. The counterexample K_{n-1,n+1} is correctly analyzed with proper degree calculations and matching limitation argument. I considered whether the claim that 'the conclusion can fail as well' might overstate (the proof failing doesn't mean the conclusion must fail), but the explicit counterexample shows the conclusion does fail, so this is accurate."}}], 'criteria': ['theorem and hypothesis verification', 'methodological comparison', 'logical progression structure', 'verification and counterexample testing', 'mathematical correctness'], 'usage': {'input_tokens': 8547, 'output_tokens': 3415, 'cached_tokens': 0}, 'error': None, 'task_id': 'tv_stem_3045'}] 
