f breaks down' section merely states hypothesis failure without demonstrating why the conclusion itself becomes false."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior Instruction Following and Truthfulness by directly proving the result with Tutte's theorem, the minimal sufficient tool, while GPT 5.2 uses the overpowered Dirac's theorem which obscures where the degree bound is actually needed. The SxS winner provides a rigorous Tutte-based proof with explicit counting argument showing exactly how δ(G)≥n forces o(G-S)≤|S|, including a sharp counterexample where the bound fails; GPT 5.2's Dirac approach, while valid, cannot localize where the proof breaks when δ(G)=n-1—its 'breaks at Step 1' analysis is vacuous since any Hamiltonicity theorem would fail, whereas the SxS winner precisely identifies that inequality (3) becomes non-strict allowing singleton odd components. The SxS winner's Writing Style is more mathematically mature with cleaner lemma structure, while GPT 5.2's verbose 'Step 1/Step 2' format and redundant 'Where the hypothesis is used' callouts create unnecessary Verbosity without added clarity."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit hypothesis verification', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly verifies all three hypotheses of Dirac's Theorem (simple graph, N≥3, δ≥N/2) before applying it, whereas the SxS winner merely mentions Dirac's theorem in a table without actually using it.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions hypotheses briefly, 3-4 = checks some hypotheses, 5-6 = rigorously verifies all three hypotheses with edge case handling', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Base case handling', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly handles the n=1 base case (2 vertices) separately before applying Dirac's Theorem for n≥2, while the SxS winner completely omits this edge case.", 'scale': "1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions base case exists, 3-4 = handles base case with some justification, 5-6 = ri