miltonian structure', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX formatting consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent LaTeX delimiters ($...$ and $$...$$) with proper spacing, while the SxS winner mixes \\mathbf{k} and \\bm{k} notation and uses non-standard \\medskip\\noindent\\textbf{} formatting that deviates from clean LaTeX conventions.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor formatting issues, 3-4 = consistent professional formatting, 5-6 = exemplary formatting with proper environments and no inconsistencies', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit IVT invocation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names and invokes the Intermediate Value Theorem as a formal mathematical tool in step 4 of the forward proof, while the SxS winner uses the equivalent sign-change argument without naming the underlying theorem.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = implicit use of continuity, 3-4 = explicit theorem naming with correct application, 5-6 = formal theorem statement with careful justification of hypotheses', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit strategy statement', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Strategy' section at the beginning that explicitly outlines the proof approach using the inversion polarization scalar η(k) before diving into technical details.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = marginally better, 3-4 = noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 = substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Alternative verification section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 provides an 'Independent concrete check' subsection that verifies the forward implication using the orbital-weight picture as an alternative confirmation, not present in the SxS winner.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = marginally better, 3-4 = noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 = substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Necessity of non-triviality condition', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated section explaining precisely why the theorem fails when orbital parities are equal (ξ₁=