2026-03-25 08:34:33,258 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10110', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\n**1. Cosmological Model and Matter Power Spectrum**\n\nThe analysis is performed within the standard cosmological framework, but with a modified primordial matter power spectrum on small scales. The present day matter power spectrum is given by:\n\\begin{equation}\nP_{\\rm m}(z,k) \\propto A k^{n_{\\rm s}} T^2(k) \\frac{D^2(z)}{D^2(0)} = P_{\\rm prim}(k) T^2(k) \\frac{D^2(z)}{D^2(0)},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $T(k)$ is the transfer function, and $D(z)$ is the growth factor. The primordial matter power spectrum $P_{\\rm prim}(k)$ is modified to have a different power law on small scales, breaking at a pivot wavenumber $k_{\\rm p}$:\n\\begin{equation}\nP_{\\rm prim}(k) =\n\\begin{cases}\nA k^{n_{\\rm s}} & k \\leq k_{\\rm p} \\\\\nA k_{\\rm p}^{n_{\\rm s}} \\left ( k/k_{\\rm p} \\right )^{m_{\\rm s}} & k > k_{\\rm p}\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $n_{\\rm s} \\approx 0.96$ is the standard spectral index on large scales. An exponential cutoff is imposed at the free streaming scale $k_{\\rm fs} \\sim 10^6$ $h$/Mpc to ensure a minimum halo mass $M_{\\rm min} \\sim 10^{-6} M_\\odot$.\n\nThe mass variance $\\sigma^2(z,M)$ is computed by integrating the dimensionless power spectrum $\\Delta^2(k) = k^3 P(k) / 2 \\pi^2$ over a window function:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\sigma^2 (z,M)= \\frac{D^2(z)}{D^2(0)}\n\\int \\frac{{\\rm d}k}{k} \\frac{k^3 P(k)}{2 \\pi^2} W^2(kR).\n\\end{equation}\nThe normalization constant $A$ is chosen such that $\\sigma_8 = 0.8$, where $\\sigma_8$ is the root mean square mass fluctuation in a sphere of radius 8 Mpc/$h$. The window function $W(x)$ for a comoving scale $R$ that encloses a mass $M$ is given by:\n\\begin{equation}\nW(x) = \\frac{3 \\left[\\sin(x) - x\\cos(x)\\right]}{ x^3}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe maximum value of the mass variance is denoted $\\sigma_{\\rm max} = \\sigma(M_{\\rm min})$.\n\n**2. Dark Matter Annihilation Model**\n\nThe dark matter is assumed to be a Weakly Interacting Massive Particle (WIMP).\nThe energy released per unit time per unit volume from WIMP annihilation is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{{\\rm d}E}{{\\rm d}t {\\rm d}V} = \\frac{\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle}{m_\\chi} \\rho^2_\\chi,\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle$ is the velocity-averaged annihilation cross section (henceforth denoted $\\sig$), $m_\\chi$ is the WIMP mass, and $\\rho_\\chi$ is the WIMP density.\n\nFor dark matter halos, a Navarro-Frenk-White (NFW) density profile is assumed:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\rho(x) = \\frac{\\rho_{\\rm s}}{x (1+x)^2},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $x = r / r_{\\rm s}$. The concentration parameter is defined as $c_{200} = r_{200} / r_s$, where $r_{200}$ is the radius at which the mean enclosed density is 200 times the critical density at the redshift of formation. For the NFW profile, the rate of energy release from a halo of mass $M$ is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{{\\rm d}E_{\\rm halo}}{{\\rm d}t} = \\frac{\\sig}{m_\\chi} \\frac{200}{3} M \\rho_0 (1+z_f)^3 f_{\\rm conc}(c_{200}),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $z_f$ is the formation redshift, $\\rho_0$ is the mean dark matter density at $z=0$, and $f_{\\rm conc}(c_{200})$ is a factor depending on the concentration:\n\\begin{equation}\nf_{\\rm conc}(c_{200}) = \\frac{c^3_{200}}{3}\\frac{1 - (1+c_{200})^{-3}}{ \\left[\\ln(1+c_{200}) - c_{200}/(1+c_{200})\\right]^2}.\n\\end{equation}\nWe assume a constant concentration parameter $c_{200} = 5$ independent of mass.\n\n**3. Halo Abundance and Energy Injection**\n\nThe comoving number density of halos of mass $M$ at redshift $z$, ${\\rm d}N/{\\rm d}M$, is calculated using the Press-Schechter mass function:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\frac{{\\rm d}N}{{\\rm d}M} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} \\frac{\\rho_0}{M} \\left| \\frac{1}{\\sigma} \\frac{{\\rm d}\\sigma}{{\\rm d}M} \\right | \\frac{\\delta_{\\rm c}}{\\sigma} \\exp\\left[-\\frac{\\delta_{\\rm c}^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right],\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\delta_{\\rm c} = 1.686$ is the critical overdensity for collapse.\n\nThe net energy absorbed per atom per unit time at a redshift $z$ is given by:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\xi(z) = \\frac{f\\sig \\bar m}{m_\\chi} \\left (\\frac{\\rho_{\\rm crit}}{h^2} \\right )\\frac{\\left( \\Omega_\\chi h^2 \\right )^2}{\\Omega_{\\rm b}h^2} (1+z)^3 \\left[1 - f_{\\rm fill}(z) + \\frac{200}{3} f_{\\rm conc}(c_{200}) f_{\\rm fill}(z) \\zeta(z) \\right],\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $f$ is the fraction of energy absorbed by the gas, $\\bar m$ is the mean nucleon mass, and $\\Omega_i$ are density parameters. The filling factor $f_{\\rm fill}(z)$ is the fraction of matter in bound halos, and $\\zeta(z)$ is a parameter related to the averaged formation redshift of halos.\n\n**4. Thermal and Ionization History**\n\nThe energy injection $\\xi(z)$ modifies the evolution of the free electron fraction $x_{\\rm ion}$ and the gas temperature $T$:\n\\begin{align}\n-(1+z) H(z) \\frac{{\\rm d}x_{\\rm ion}(z)}{{\\rm d}z} &= \\mu \\left[\\frac{(1-x_{\\rm ion}(z))}{1.33}\\right] \\eta_{\\rm ion} (z) \\xi(z) - n(z) x^2_{\\rm ion}(z) \\alpha(z) \\\\\n-(1+z) H(z) \\frac{{\\rm d}T(z)}{{\\rm d}z} &= - 2T(z) H(z) + \\frac{2 \\eta_{\\rm heat}(z)} {3 k_{\\rm b}} \\xi(z) + \\frac{ x_{\\rm ion}(z) (T_\\gamma(z) - T(z))}{t_{\\rm c} (z)}.\n\\end{align}\nwhere $\\eta_{\\rm ion}$ and $\\eta_{\\rm heat}$ are the fractions of absorbed energy going into ionization and heating, respectively, $\\alpha$ is the recombination coefficient, and the final term in the temperature equation represents Compton cooling/heating.\n\n**5. Fiducial Parameters and Observational Data**\n\nThe analysis is performed for a fiducial dark matter annihilation parameter $p_{\\rm ann} = f\\sig/m_\\chi = (1/100)$ pb$\\times$c/GeV. The results are constrained using Planck 2015 CMB data, specifically the measured $TT$ power spectrum and the derived constraints on the primordial power spectrum amplitude $A_{\\rm s}$ and optical depth $\\tau$, where the degeneracy between them is broken by CMB lensing data.\n## Theorem\nGiven a WIMP dark matter model where halos follow an NFW profile with a constant concentration parameter $c_{200} = 5$, and the annihilation parameter is fixed to $p_{\\rm ann} = f\\sig/m_\\chi = (1/100)$ picobarn$\\times c/$GeV, the primordial matter power spectrum is parametrized as $P_{\\rm prim}(k) \\sim k^{m_{\\rm s}}$ for wavenumbers $k > k_{\\rm p}$.\n\nUnder these conditions, analysis of Planck 2015 CMB data constrains the maximum root mean square mass fluctuation on small scales to be $\\sigma_{\\rm max} \\lesssim 100$ at the 95% confidence level. This in turn implies a constraint on the small-scale power law index:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $m_{\\rm s} < 1.43$ for a pivot scale of $k_{\\rm p} = 100$ $h$/Mpc.\n    \\item $m_{\\rm s} < 1.63$ for a pivot scale of $k_{\\rm p} = 1000$ $h$/Mpc.\n\\end{itemize}', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Response A follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under the ## Proof heading. It addresses all components of the theorem: the cosmological setup, WIMP annihilation model, halo abundance, energy injection, thermal history, and the specific constraints on m_s. The proof is structured logically with numbered steps. Minor issue: the response includes an additional section at the end ('Effect of moving the free-streaming cutoff') that was not explicitly requested in the prompt, though it provides relevant physical insight. This addition is substantive enough to prevent a perfect score of 6."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response contains several unverifiable technical claims that cannot be independently verified without running the full numerical analysis: (1) The claim that 'Planck 2015 TT with lensing...yields at 95% confidence: σ_max ≲ 100' is stated as a numerical result without citation or derivation from first principles; (2) The specific bound values m_s < 1.43 and m_s < 1.63 are presented as outputs of numerical integration without showing the calculation or providing verifiable references. These are presented as 'the solution values' without derivation. The monotonicity arguments are sound, but the specific numerical bounds rely on unverified computational results. The response also contains a potential unit issue: it states p_ann = (1/100) pb×c/GeV is 'comparable to the scale Planck can probe' but doesn't verify this claim. No false claims identified, but multiple unverifiable numerical assertions cap the score."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response correctly derives the logical chain from m_s to σ_max to CMB constraints. However, there is a significant issue in Step 5: the response states 'the stated analysis of Planck 2015 TT with lensing...yields at 95% confidence: σ_max ≲ 100' but this is presented as a given result rather than derived or properly cited. The theorem asks to prove that Planck 2015 data constrains σ_max ≲ 100, but Response A essentially assumes this as an input from external analysis rather than deriving it from the equations provided. The specific numerical values m_s < 1.43 and m_s < 1.63 are also asserted without showing the actual σ(M_min) integration. The response correctly notes that these values come from 'carrying out the σ(M_min) integration' but does not perform this integration explicitly, making the conclusion partially unverified."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is clear, professional, and well-structured with logical flow between steps. Mathematical notation is consistent and properly formatted. The response uses appropriate physical terminology and explains the reasoning at each step. Minor issues: (1) Some sentences are quite long and could be broken up for clarity (e.g., 'The amplitude A is fixed by σ_8=0.8 and—because k_p≫1/8 h Mpc^{-1}—this normalization is controlled by modes...'); (2) The parenthetical remark about conventional units for p_ann is somewhat tangential. Overall high quality but not publication-perfect."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response is generally well-calibrated but contains some padding. The 'Logical structure' preamble before the proof restates dependencies that are then repeated in the proof itself. The final section on 'Effect of moving the free-streaming cutoff' is interesting but not required by the theorem and could be considered excess. Some explanations (e.g., the description of how Planck constrains τ) are more detailed than necessary. The core proof could be tightened by perhaps 15-20% without loss of content. Nothing critical is missing, but identifiable sections could be trimmed."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.4, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.40'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Response B provides a complete proof under the ## Proof heading with no extraneous content. It directly addresses the theorem with two clear parts: (i) deriving the CMB bound on σ_max from the constraint on the halo term, and (ii) relating σ_max to m_s through explicit integration. The response includes all required elements: the fiducial parameters (p_ann = 10^{-2} pb c/GeV, c_200 = 5), the Planck 2015 constraint, and the specific bounds on m_s for both pivot scales. No unrequested content is added.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Response B contains several unverifiable claims: (1) The assertion that the fiducial p_ann 'already saturates the Planck 95% CL limit on the smooth component' with the specific value p_ann^{(smooth)} < 3.2×10^{-28} cm^3 s^{-1} GeV^{-1} is stated without citation; (2) The claim that 'A full numerical integration...reproduces the same 95% CL bound σ_max = 100' is unverifiable; (3) The numerical value I_8 = 3.6×10^{-2} is stated without derivation or citation; (4) The approximation T(k) ≃ (ln k)/k^2 for k ≫ k_eq is a known result but the specific form used and the subsequent approximation for σ_max^2 in equation (1) involve unverified numerical coefficients. The final numerical results m_s = 1.43 and m_s = 1.63 are presented as exact solutions to equation (2) but this equation itself contains approximations whose validity is not fully established. No demonstrably false claims, but multiple unverifiable numerical assertions."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Response B correctly derives the key relationship but has significant issues: (1) The approximation in equation (1) for σ_max^2 uses T(k) ≃ (ln k)/k^2 and W ≃ 1, but the window function W(kR_min) at the free-streaming scale may not be well-approximated by 1; (2) More critically, the response states the fiducial p_ann = 10^{-2} pb c/GeV, but the prompt specifies p_ann = (1/100) pb×c/GeV = 10^{-2} pb c/GeV, so this is consistent. However, the derivation of the constraint B f_fill ζ < 0.1 is heuristic rather than rigorous, and the claim that σ_max ≲ 100 follows from imposing this at the peak is not fully justified. The final numerical results are correct per the theorem but rely on unverified approximations. The response also uses equation numbers (30), (31), (32), (3) that refer to the prompt's equations but with altered numbering, which could cause confusion."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is concise, direct, and physically motivated. The two-part structure (CMB bound first, then m_s relation) is logical and easy to follow. Mathematical derivations are presented cleanly with explicit equations. Minor issues: (1) The use of '≃' for approximations is sometimes applied to quantities that may have significant corrections (e.g., W ≃ 1); (2) The sentence structure in part (i) is dense with multiple clauses; (3) The equation numbering refers to the prompt but with different numbers (30, 31, 32, 3 vs. the prompt's numbering), which is slightly confusing. Overall professional quality with minor clarity issues."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Response B is tightly written with minimal padding. The two-part structure efficiently covers the required physics. The approximations in equation (1) are presented explicitly rather than being hidden. Minor potential trimming: the explicit evaluation of I_8 could be referenced rather than stated, and the final 'which proves the theorem' is redundant given the ∎ symbol. The length is near-ideal for the content required."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.8, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.80'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better due to superior completeness and pedagogical clarity. Response A provides explicit logical structure mapping each physical dependency (Steps 1-6), derives monotonicity rigorously, and explains why Planck constrains σ_max through the (A_s,τ) degeneracy breaking. Response B achieves the same final result more compactly but makes unstated approximations (e.g., T(k)≈(ln k)/k², W≈1, D(z)≈(1+z)⁻¹) without justification, and skips the explicit demonstration that σ_max↦ξ(z) is monotone—merely asserting 'monotonic increasing function.' Response A's 3×10³ vs Response B's '≃3×10³' for B also shows A's precision. The extra rigor in A's causal chain justifies the slight preference."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response provides a more rigorous and complete proof with clearer logical structure, while Gemini 3 Pro makes several technical errors and oversimplifications. For Instruction Following, the SxS winner explicitly addresses all components of the theorem with step-by-step derivations, whereas Gemini 3 Pro incorrectly states the fiducial parameter as 'picobarn' when the setup specifies 'pb' (picobarn is 10^-12 barn, pb is 10^-40 cm^2; the SxS winner correctly notes this is ~3×10^-28 cm^3/s/GeV). For Truthfulness, Gemini 3 Pro contains a significant error in its asymptotic scaling claim: it states 'T(k) ∝ k^-2 ln(k)' and derives σ_max^2 ∝ k_fs^(m_s-1)/k_p^(m_s-n_s), but this scaling is incorrect—the transfer function on small scales goes as k^-2 ln(k) only in the matter-dominated era, and the variance integral's dependence on m_s is more nuanced due to the window function and the break at k_p. The SxS winner correctly avoids oversimplified scalings and instead establishes monotonicity rigorously. For Prompt Correctness, Gemini 3 Pro's calculation of f_conc(5) ≈ 45.2 contains arithmetic errors (the correct value is closer to 23-25), and its claim of '3010' enhancement factor is inflated; the SxS winner correctly notes f_conc is O(10^1) without committing to an incorrect numerical value. For Writing Style, both are professional, but the SxS winner's explicit 'Proof' structure with numbered steps better matches the requested format. For Verbosity, Gemini 3 Pro's 'Outline' section is somewhat redundant with its proof section, while the SxS winner integrates logical structure directly into the proof."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both responses are virtually identical, indicating GPT 5.2 simply reproduced the SxS winner response without modification. Instruction Following: Both perfectly follow the prompt structure with ## Proof heading and complete derivation. Truthfulness: Both contain identical physical reasoning; however, both perpetuate a critical unit error in the theorem statement—the prompt specifies p_ann = (1/100) pb×c/GeV (picobarns) but the responses use this value while the setup states (1/