2026-03-25 08:34:40,114 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_15756', 'prompt': 'Let \\(P(x)\\) and \\(Q(x)\\) be two monic polynomials with degree 4 and 2, respectively, with real coefficients. Suppose that there exists an interval ( \\(r, s\\) ) of length greater than 2 such that both \\(P(x)\\) and \\(Q(x)\\) are negative for \\(x \\in(r, s)\\) and both are positive for \\(x<r\\) or \\(x>s\\). Show that there is a real number \\(x_{0}\\) such that \\(P\\left(x_{0}\\right)<Q\\left(x_{0}\\right)\\).', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response addresses the core task: prove there exists x₀ with P(x₀) < Q(x₀). It follows the mathematical structure expected for a proof. However, I must check for faults: (1) The prompt asks to 'Show that...' and the response does provide a complete proof. (2) The response includes an unrequested section on the borderline case |s-r|=2, which goes beyond what was asked. This is additional content not requested. Per the rubric, 'Adding unrequested content → -1 point' from a perfect score. The main proof is complete and correct, but the extra section on |s-r|=2, while mathematically interesting, was not requested. Score capped at 5 due to unrequested addition."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Fact-checking key claims: (1) Q(x)=(x-r)(x-s) — VERIFIED: this is the unique monic quadratic with simple roots at r,s and negative on (r,s). (2) P(x)=(x-r)(x-s)[(x-t)²+c] — VERIFIED: the remaining quadratic factor must be positive definite, and completing the square is valid. (3) Claim that c=0 implies t∈{r,s} — VERIFIED: if c=0, P has double roots; if at interior point, P would be zero inside (r,s), contradicting P<0 on (r,s). (4) The analysis of F(x)=P(x)-Q(x) and the case analysis — VERIFIED: algebraic manipulation is correct. (5) The geometric argument with max{t-r, s-t}>1 ≥ √(1-c) — VERIFIED: since |s-r|>2, the larger subinterval exceeds 1, and when c<1 we have √(1-c)<1. All major claims verified. One minor issue: the claim 'the proof method would break down only if that configuration were allowed' in the borderline section is slightly imprecise phrasing but not a factual error. Score 5 due to thorough verification with only trivial imprecision."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Correctness audit: The prompt requires showing existence of x₀ with P(x₀)<Q(x₀). The response correctly: (1) Identifies the forced factorizations of P and Q. (2) Uses the length condition |s-r|>2 effectively. (3) Handles all cases (c≥1 and c<1). (4) Correctly concludes existence of x₀. The proof is mathematically sound. However, I must note: the response includes analysis of |s-r|=2 which, while correct, goes beyond the prompt's scope. The prompt specifically states |s-r|>2. This is not an error but an extension. The core proof is correct. Score 5: all major requirements correct with only trivial extension beyond scope."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: The writing is clear, professional, and well-structured with numbered sections. Mathematical notation is precise. Sentences are generally well-formed. One minor issue identified: the phrase 'the only parameters are t∈ℝ and c≥0 with the extra restriction' is slightly awkward — 'extra restriction' could be more formally stated as 'subject to the constraint.' Also, the transition 'Hence we need a point where the bracket...has the opposite sign to Q' could be more explicit about why this ensures P<Q. These are trivial style preferences. The proof flows logically from setup to conclusion. Score 5: professional quality with only trivial style preferences."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The response includes three sections: (1) Forced factorizations, (2) What the length gives, (3) The borderline case. Section 3 on |s-r|=2 is unnecessary for the prompt, which specifies |s-r|>2. This section could be removed without affecting the proof of the stated result. Additionally, the summary at the end repeats information already conveyed. The core proof in sections 1-2 is appropriately detailed, but the unrequested section 3 constitutes padding. Score 4: good but has identifiable padding (the borderline case analysis).'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.85, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.85'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response addresses the core proof task. Checking for instruction elements: (1) Prove existence of x₀ with P(x₀)<Q(x₀) — addressed. (2) Use the given conditions on P, Q, r, s — used. However, the response adds unrequested content: a section 'What if |s-r|=2?' and a counterexample for d<2. These go beyond the prompt's scope. Per rubric, 'Adding unrequested content → -1 point.' The response also includes a concluding statement about the length condition being essential, which analyzes when the statement fails — not requested. Score 5: near-perfect with unrequested additions."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-checking key claims: (1) Q(x)=(x-r)(x-s) — VERIFIED. (2) P(x)=(x-r)(x-s)(x²+ax+b) with a²-4b<0 — VERIFIED. (3) Claim 'A double root at r or s would make P non-negative on (r,s), not negative' — VERIFIED (actually P would be zero at that endpoint, but the sign pattern requires P<0 on the open interval; this is correct). (4) Q(m)=-(d/2)² < -1 when d>2 — VERIFIED: (3) gives Q(m)=-(d/2)², and d>2 implies (d/2)²>1, so Q(m)<-1. (5) The case analysis for D(x): Case 1 (R no real zero) — VERIFIED. Case 2 (R has real zeros) — the claim that 'this forces {α,β}={r,s}' needs scrutiny. The argument is that if D(x)≥0 everywhere, then R and Q must have the same sign pattern, both being monic quadratics. This implies R=Q. This is VERIFIED: two monic quadratics with the same sign pattern (positive outside (r,s), negative inside) must be identical. (6) The counterexample for d<2: P(x)=Q(x)(Q(x)+1) with r=0, s=1.5 — VERIFIED: P-Q=Q²≥0, so no x₀ exists. However, one UNVERIFIABLE claim: 'Thus the length condition (or at least d≥2) is essential.' The phrase 'or at least d≥2' suggests the bound might not be tight, but the response doesn't fully verify that d≥2 suffices in all cases — it only shows d=2 works in the specific analysis. This is a slight overstatement. Also, the claim that the argument 'works as long as d≥2' in the final paragraph is not fully verified for all d≥2, only for d=2 specifically checked. Score 4: generally accurate but with unverifiable claims about the general d≥2 case."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Correctness audit: The main proof is correct. However, issues identified: (1) The claim 'A double root at r or s would make P non-negative on (r,s), not negative' — actually a double root at r or s would make P zero at that endpoint, but P could still be negative on (r,s). The statement is slightly imprecise: P would be non-positive, not necessarily non-negative. This is a minor inaccuracy. (2) The conclusion about d≥2 being sufficient is not fully verified — only d=2 is checked, not the general case. (3) The counterexample section, while interesting, is not part of the required proof. The core proof is correct, but there are minor inaccuracies and unverified extensions. Score 4: mostly correct but misses precision on edge cases and includes unverified generalizations."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: The writing is clear, well-organized with clear section headers. Mathematical notation is consistent. The case analysis is clearly labeled. One minor issue: the phrase 'the only way P-Q could stay non-negative' in the constraint section is slightly informal; more formal would be 'the only way to avoid P(x₀)<Q(x₀) for some x₀.' Also, 'Hence D cannot be non-negative everywhere' is a double negative that could be clearer as 'Hence D must be negative somewhere.' These are trivial style preferences. The proof structure is logical and professional. Score 5: professional quality with only trivial style preferences."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Length analysis: The response includes: (1) Factorization section — appropriate. (2) Constraint section — appropriate. (3) Proof section — appropriate. (4) 'What if |s-r|=2?' section — unrequested, could be cut. (5) Counterexample for d<2 — unrequested, could be cut. The final paragraph also repeats the conclusion about the length condition. The core proof is well-sized, but the additional sections constitute padding. Score 4: good but has identifiable padding."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.4, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.40'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 1, 'overall_comment': "Response B is slightly better due to cleaner structure and more direct handling of the key contradiction. Response A's case analysis with parameter t and c becomes unnecessarily convoluted, especially in the 'delicate case' section where it constructs x with 'r<x<t and t-x>sqrt(1-c)'—this is confusingly written (should be |x-t|>sqrt(1-c)). Response B's Case 1/2 dichotomy based on whether R has real zeros is more elegant, and its contradiction at the midpoint m is more transparent: 'Q(m)+1<-1+1=0' directly violates positivity. Both correctly handle the |s-r|=2 extension, but B's explicit counterexample for d<2 better demonstrates sharpness of the bound."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (GPT 5.2) demonstrates superior mathematical rigor in Instruction Following and Truthfulness. The SxS winner correctly identifies that R(x) must be strictly positive (a²-4b<0, not ≤0) to ensure P(x)<0 on (r,s), and handles the case analysis on R(x)=x²+ax+(b-1) with proper discriminant analysis. Gemini 3 Pro makes a critical error by allowing R(x)≥0 with Δ_R≤0, which permits R(x_m)=0; this creates a gap since P(x_m)=0 would violate the strict negativity requirement for P on (r,s). The SxS winner's equation (3) showing Q(m)<-1 is sharper than Gemini's equivalent claim. Both address the |s-r|=2 extension, but the SxS winner's analysis is more precise: Gemini incorrectly states the strict inequality is 'not necessary' when in fact the conclusion still holds via a different contradiction mechanism. The SxS winner's writing is more concise with cleaner LaTeX formatting, while Gemini has spacing errors ('thatis', 'ofcontradiction') and verbose sign-analysis tables. Verbosity favors the SxS winner for efficiency."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both Gemini 3 Pro (SxS winner) and GPT 5.2 provide mathematically correct, complete proofs with nearly identical structure: forced factorization, analysis of D(x)=P(x)-Q(x), case analysis on R(x)-1, and discussion of borderline cases. Both correctly identify the essential constraint |s-r|≥2. Gemini 3 Pro uses slightly more compact notation and explicitly labels equation (3) for Q(m)<-1, while GPT 5.2 provides more explicit verbal signposting ('Step 1', 'Step 2', 'Step 3') and explicitly writes out the multiplicity cases for P. Both correctly prove the result and analyze sharpness with the counterexample P=Q+Q². Neither contains errors; the approaches are essentially equivalent in rigor, clarity, and completeness."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent LaTeX delimiters ($...$) throughout the mathematical expressions, while the SxS winner mixes \\( ... \\) and $...$ delimiters.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for minor inconsistency, 3-4 for noticeable inconsistency affecting readability, 5-6 for severe inconsistency or complete lack of standard delimiters', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit discriminant calculation for length constraint', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly computes the discriminant Δ = (s-r)² - 4 to demonstrate why |s-r| > 2 implies the quadratic has real roots, while the SxS winner only states the result without showing this algebraic step.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for minor omission, 3-4 for missing key algebraic justification, 5-6 for critical missing step that obscures the logical connection', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Clear case structure for contradiction analysis', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro organizes the proof by contradiction into numbered, clearly labeled steps (analyzing inside vs. outside the interval), while the SxS winner presents cases less distinctly with less explicit logical structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for minor structural difference, 3-4 for noticeably clearer logical flow, 5-6 for substantially superior organization that aids comprehension', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit verification section for boundary case |s-r|=2', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated, clearly titled section 'Verification if |s - r| = 2 Exactly' that explicitly explains why the proof still works at the boundary, while the SxS winner buries this discussion at the end without clear structural separation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for minor presentation difference, 3-4 for noticeably better structural organization, 5-6 for substantially superior clarity in presenting the boundary analysis', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit contradiction location at boundary case', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly identifies that at |s-r|=2, the contradiction shifts from R(x) ≥ 0 to P(x) < 0 being violated, while the SxS winner does not clearly articulate this shift in the source of contradiction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for minor clarification, 3-4 for clearer identification of the logical mechanism, 5-6 for substantially deeper insight into the proof structure', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit enumeration of multiplicity cases', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly lists all three possible forms for P(x): (x-r)^3(x-s), (x-r)(x-s)^3, or (x-r)(x-s)(x^2+ax+b) with positive definite quadratic, while the SxS winner only considers the last case without acknowledging the multiplicity-3 cases.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better with SxS winner clearly lacking', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear logical structure with boxed conclusions', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses boxed equations for key results (forced factorization forms, length condition equivalence, contradiction inequalities, final conclusion) creating clear signposts for the proof structure, while the SxS winner uses less prominent tagging.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better with SxS winner clearly lacking', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit limit argument for boundary values', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses explicit limit arguments (x→r⁻ and x→r⁺) to rigorously derive R(r)=1 and R(s)=1 from the inequalities, while the SxS winner merely states this as a matching condition without showing the continuity argument.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better with SxS winner clearly lacking', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Alternative contradiction path without invoking R>0', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a parenthetical alternative contradiction: even without using R(x)>0, P(m)=Q(m)R(m) would be positive, contradicting P<0 on (r,s), while the SxS winner only uses the R>0 path.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better with SxS winner clearly lacking', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Precise sharp condition identification', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly identifies |s-r|≥2 as the sharp condition and explains why the conclusion can fail when |s-r|<2 with a concrete counterexample construction, while the SxS winner's discussion of the borderline case is less precise about sharpness.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better with SxS winner clearly lacking', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Systematic case organization with numbered steps', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 organizes the proof into clearly labeled steps (Step 1, Step 2, Step 3) with explicit logical progression, while the SxS winner uses less structured case labels (1., 2.) without explicit step numbering.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better with SxS winner clearly lacking', 'rating': 3}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response B', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 21857, 'output_tokens': 4515, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.097894}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.097894}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 21857, 'output_tokens': 4515, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.097894}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.097894}}] 
