s correct since (r,s) has length >2 and [a-1,a+1] has length 2, but the 'hence there is x₀∈(r,s) with |x₀-a|>1' step assumes without proof that such x₀ exists with R(x₀)>1. Actually R(x)=(x-a)²+b, so R(x₀)>(x₀-a)²>1 requires |x₀-a|>1 AND b≥0. The logic is correct but the 'hence' transition is slightly fast. No false claims, but one unverifiable compression in the geometric argument."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Zero identification at r,s; [CORRECT] Q factorization; [CORRECT] P factorization with R monic quadratic; [CORRECT] R(x)>0 outside {r,s}; [CORRECT] R has form (x-a)²+b with b≥0; [CORRECT] Existence of x₀ with |x₀-a|>1 in (r,s) when length>2; [CORRECT] Final inequality P(x₀)<Q(x₀). The proof is correct and elegant. Minor issue: The response doesn't explicitly rule out that R could have form (x-r)(x-s) with double root structure, i.e., R(x)=(x-r)(x-s)+c form is not fully justified — the 'hence R(x)=(x-a)²+b' is a correct classification but the 'hence' could use more justification. The discriminant analysis is correct but compressed. Overall proof is correct but slightly less explicit than A."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clear and concise, but with identifiable weaknesses. Issues found: (1) Abrupt transitions — 'Consequently Δ_R ≤ 0' appears without defining Δ_R; (2) The key geometric step 'Thus there is x₀∈(r,s) with |x₀-a|>1' is stated without explicit justification (why must such x₀ exist?); (3) The proof ends abruptly after the inequality without summarizing; (4) The notation 'Δ_R' for discriminant is standard but should be defined for completeness. The writing is professional but less polished than A — more compressed, requiring reader to fill gaps. Score 4 for good but identifiable structure/weaknesses."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Perfectly calibrated for a concise proof. Nothing can be cut because: (1) Each sentence serves a necessary logical functio