0 should be allowed; the response says 'k≥0' which is correct. The justification is present but slightly less detailed than optimal regarding why R cannot have real roots (the discriminant argument is correct but brief)."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response provides extensive explicit justification for both factorizations. For Q: detailed argument using continuity ('Since Q is continuous, Q(x)>0 for x<r but Q(x)<0 for x>r close to r, so Q(r)=0'), degree and monic properties. For P: 'r,s are real zeros where the sign changes, so each has odd multiplicity in P. Also P cannot have any other real zeros, because it is strictly positive on (-∞,r)∪(s,∞) and strictly negative on (r,s). Therefore (x-r)(x-s) divides P, and the remaining factor must be a monic quadratic R with R(x)>0 for all real x'. The response explicitly considers multiplicity patterns and proves the three possible forms. I considered whether any gaps exist—the justification is remarkably thorough, covering sign changes, continuity, degree constraints, and explicit enumeration of cases."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Length Constraint on Parameterization Identified', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response explicitly identifies the constraint in a dedicated subsection 'Constraint from the length |s-r|>2'. It defines d=s-r>2 and m=(r+s)/2, then computes Q(m)=-(d/2)^2<-1. The response states: 'Thus the vertex of Q lies more than one unit below the x-axis; this will rule out the only way P-Q could stay non-negative.' This clearly articulates that |s-r|>2 imposes Q(m)<-1, which is the key constraint used in the proof. I considered whether the connection to the parameterization could be more explicit—the response uses this to show that R=Q would lead to contradiction, but the identification of the constraint itself is clear and correct."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response identifies the length constraint in section '2. What the length |s-r|>2 gives'. It uses the constraint to show that whe