zes P(x)-Q(x) as the core proof method in a dedicated section 'Proof that P(x_0)<Q(x_0) for some x_0'. It defines D(x)=P(x)-Q(x)=Q(x)[(x^2+ax+b)-1]=Q(x)·R(x) where R(x)=x^2+ax+(b-1). The response explicitly considers what happens inside and outside (r,s): 'On (r,s) we have Q(x)<0' and in Case 1, 'R(x)≥0 for all x...so D(x)=Q(x)R(x)<0 there'. For outside, Case 2 considers when R has real zeros and shows this leads to contradiction. The analysis is thorough, explicit, and correctly handles both regions. I considered whether the outside case analysis could be more explicit—the response focuses on the contradiction argument rather than direct analysis, but the behavior is implicitly covered."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response analyzes P(x)-Q(x) as the core proof method, defining F(x)=P(x)-Q(x)=Q(x)[(x-t)^2+c-1] in equation (4). It explicitly states: 'Inside (r,s) we have Q(x)<0; outside, Q(x)>0. Hence we need a point where the bracket (x-t)^2+c-1 has the opposite sign to Q.' The response then analyzes cases: when c≥1 (bracket ≥0, so F(x)<0 on (r,s)), and when c<1 (bracket negative on interval I of length 2√(1-c)≤2). It explicitly handles t∉(r,s) and t∈(r,s) separately, using the length constraint. The inside/outside analysis is explicit and thorough."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response analyzes P(x)-Q(x) as the core proof method in the section 'Proof by Analyzing P(x) - Q(x)'. It defines D(x)=P(x)-Q(x)=(x-r)(x-s)[R(x)-1] and explicitly analyzes: '1. Inside the interval (r,s): The term (x-r)(x-s) is strictly negative. For the product D(x) to be ≥0, we must have R(x)-1≤0. 2. Outside the interval [r,s]: The term (x-r)(x-s) is strictly positive. For D(x) to be ≥0, we must have R(x)-1≥0.' This is explicit separate consideration of inside and outside behavior. The response then uses this to force R(x)-1=(x-r)(x-s) and derive contradiction. The analysis is clear, explicit, and correctly structured."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response analyzes P(x)-Q(x) as the core proof method, defining P(x)-Q(x)=Q(x)(R(x)-1). It has an explicit subsection 'Step 1: Translate P≥Q into inequalities for R inside and outside (r,s)' which states: 'For x<r or x>s, we have Q(x)>0...R(x)≥1' and 'For x∈(r,s), we have Q(x)<0...R(x)≤1'. This is explicit separate analysis of inside and outside behavior with clear inequalities. The response continues with Step 2 (forcing R(r)=R(s)=1) and Step 3 (contradiction at midpoint). The analysis is exceptionally thorough, explicit, and correctly handles both regions."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Boundary Case |s - r| = 2 Verification', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response has a dedicated subsection 'What if |s-r|=2?' after completing the main argument. It explicitly verifies: 'If d=2, then Q(m)=-1. The same argument shows that the only way to avoid a negative value of D would be R=Q, which would give x^2+ax+b=Q+1 and hence x^2+ax+b|_m=0. This still contradicts the strict positivity of the quadratic factor (the hypothesis demands P<0 on (r,s), not merely ≤0). Hence the conclusion remains true for d=2.' This is a complete verification that the conclusion still holds when |s-r|=2 exactly, with explicit reasoning about why the contradiction persists."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response has a dedicated subsection '3. The borderline case |s-r|=2' after completing the main argument. It explicitly verifies: 'If s-r=2 the same argument works, except that the strict inequality max{t-r,s-t}>1 could become an equality only when t-r=s-t=1, √(1-c)=1 (i.e. c=0). But (3) forbids c=0 with t interior...Hence the equality case cannot occur, and the proof above still yields a point with P<Q.' This is a complete verification with explicit reasoning about why the degenerate case is excluded by the sign conditions."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response has a dedicated subsection 'Verification if |s-r|=2 Exactly' after completing the main argument. It explicitly verifies: 'If |s-r