mini 3 Pro's 'Leibniz rule' justification is hand-wavy. The SxS winner also correctly identifies that the Berezin transform integrates only over B, making its counterexample construction transparent, whereas Gemini 3 Pro obscures this key point."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': 'Both responses are mathematically identical in content, structure, and notation, scoring equally on Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, and Verbosity. The SxS winner and GPT 5.2 both correctly: (1) define the Bergman kernel K(z,w) = n!/(π^n(1-⟨z,w⟩)^{n+1}) for the unit ball, (2) construct the Berezin kernel K(z,w) = |K(z,w)|²/K(z,z), (3) prove the normalization property ∫_B K(z,w)dV(w) = 1 using the reproducing kernel identity, (4) establish interior continuity via dominated convergence with the uniform bound C_r, (5) prove the approximate identity/mass concentration property showing μ_z(B\\U) → 0 as z → P by bounding K(z,w) ≤ C_E(1-|z|²)^{n+1} on compact sets away from P, (6) complete the boundary limit proof by splitting the integral and using continuity of f at P, and (7) provide the same concrete counterexample with f ≡ 0 on B, f(P_0) = 1, f(P) = 0 for P ≠ P_0 to demonstrate necessity of C(B̄) hypothesis. Both use identical section headers (Part I/II), identical step numbering, identical LaTeX formatting, and identical concluding boxed statement. No discernible difference in mathematical rigor, clarity, or completeness exists between the two responses.'}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit kernel formula with correct normalization constant', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the Bergman kernel formula $K(z,w) = \\frac{n!}{\\pi^n} \\frac{1}{(1-\\langle z, w \\rangle)^{n+1}}$ with the correct normalization constant $\\frac{n!}{\\pi^n}$ at the beginning of the proof, whereas the SxS winner buries this in Step 1 without emphasizing the constant's role in subsequent calculations.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: