 section with explicit limit calculation, while the SxS winner has a less prominent 'Verification of the q→1 limit' section.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = verification mentioned briefly, 3-4 = clear verification with some analysis, 5-6 = thorough verification with explicit limit computation and contextual connection to classical theory', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Detailed explanation of where assumptions are used', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro provides an explicit subsection 'The Implicit Use of Full Support and Smoothness' with detailed explanation of why the argument fails without these assumptions, including specific step references and failure modes.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of assumptions, 3-4 = clear identification of where assumptions are used, 5-6 = comprehensive analysis with explicit failure mode explanation and step-by-step breakdown', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Use of visual separators', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses horizontal rules (*** ) to visually separate major sections (Strategy Outline, Proof, Verification, Assumptions), enhancing readability.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minimal visual separation, 3-4 = some visual organization, 5-6 = clear visual hierarchy with consistent separators', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit theorem restatement with formatting', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro does not restate the theorem with a formal heading, while the SxS winner includes '**Theorem 2 (R\\'enyi-LSI decay).**' with proper bold formatting and parenthetical descriptor.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no restatement, 3-4 = informal restatement, 5-6 = formal restatement with clear heading and complete statement', 'rating': 2}, {'name': 'Concise outline before detailed proof', 'description': "The SxS winner includes a distinct 'Outline of the proof' section before 'Detailed proof', while Gemini 3 Pro only has the strategy outline which serves a different purpose.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no outline, 3-4 = brief outline, 5-6 = comprehensive outline summarizing all key steps before details', 'rating': 2}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit escort measure definition', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly defines the escort probability measure μ_t with density h_t = f_t^q/Z_t and uses it throughout the proof, whereas the SxS winner only implicitly uses this construction through the substitution f_t = (ρ_t/ν)^{q/2} without naming or formalizing the escort measure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = implicit use vs explicit mention; 3-4 = explicit definition with some usage; 5-6 = explicit definition with systematic use throughout proof', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Fisher information of escort measure', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly computes and uses the relative Fisher information I(μ_t||ν) of the escort measure, showing the exact dissipation identity d/dt R_q = -(1/q)I(μ_t||ν), while the SxS winner works with Dirichlet forms without this probabilistic interpretation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no Fisher information mentioned; 3-4 = Fisher information mentioned but not connected to escort; 5-6 = explicit Fisher information of escort with clean dissipation identity', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clean separation of steps with explicit headings', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses numbered steps (Step 1 through Step 8) with descriptive headings that clearly separate conceptual phases, while the SxS winner uses a less structured format with numbered items that mix notation, computation, and application without clear thematic boundaries.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minimal structure; 3-4 = some organization; 5-6 = clear numbered steps with descriptive headings enabling easy navigation', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit LSI application justification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated Step 6 that explicitly justifies why LSI can be applied to μ_t (checking that μ_t ≪ ν with smooth positive density), while the SxS winner applies LSI to f_t without discussing why the conditions are satisfied.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = LSI applied without justification; 3-4 = brief mention of applicability; 5-6 = explicit verification step with clear conditions checked', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Convexity argument with explicit φ(s) function', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly introduces φ(s) = log ∫ f^s dν as the log-moment generating function, computes φ'(q), and uses convexity to bound KL(μ||ν) below by R_q, while the SxS winner uses the same convexity property but with less explicit notation and derivation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = convexity used implicitly; 3-4 = convexity mentioned with some notation; 5-6 = explicit φ(s) construction with derivative computation and clear inequality chain', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Complete q→1 limit verification with expansion', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a full section with Taylor expansion showing explicitly how R_q → KL and 2α/q → 2α, while the SxS winner gives a more abbreviated verification without the explicit expansion calculation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = limit mentioned without calculation; 3-4 = brief limit check; 5-6 = explicit Taylor expansion with o(q-1) remainder showing full calculation', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit generator L in divergence form', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly identifies the generator L = (1/ν)∇·(ν∇·) in divergence form and uses this notation, while the SxS winner works directly with the Fokker-Planck equation without naming or using the generator formalism.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no generator mentioned; 3-4 = generator implicit in calculations; 5-6 = explicit L definition with symmetric generator notation used', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Full support and smoothness justification with specific step references', 'description': "GPT 5.2's final section explicitly cites Step 3 (integration by parts) and Steps 5-6 (∇log f_t manipulations) as where full support and smoothness are used, with detailed explanation of why positivity matters for the Fisher information, while the SxS winner gives a mor