2026-03-25 08:36:43,337 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_9533', 'prompt': '(AoPS, css-miu) Let $a, b, c, d$ be nonnegative real numbers such that $a+b+c+d=4$. Prove that\n\n$$\n4(a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d)+12 a b c d \\geq 9(a b c+a b d+a c d+b c d)\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the mathematical proof format expected for an AoPS-style inequality problem. It defines P, uses the substitution a=1+x etc. with constraint x+y+z+w=0, and proceeds through case analysis. Minor formatting: uses \\medskip and \\noindent which are fine, but the LaTeX alignment uses 'aligned' without explicit \\begin and \\end in the displayed format shown. The proof is complete with equality cases stated. No major instruction violations found. Considered fault: whether the substitution and expansion were fully justified—the expansion to (1) is claimed 'straightforward' but not shown, which is acceptable for competition math but slightly terse."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The mathematical claims appear correct but several are unverified without computation: (1) the expansion to P=∑x_i²(1+x_i)+12x₁x₂x₃x₄ is stated as 'straightforward' but not verified; the convexity claim for y²(1+y) is correct; the minimum at y=z=w=t/3 uses Jensen/convexity correctly; the final algebraic simplifications to (4/9)t²(1-t)(t+3) and (4/9)t²(3-t)(t+1) are not verified. The equality cases are stated correctly. No false claims detected, but multiple unverified computational steps that would need checking cap this at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response correctly proves the stated inequality. The approach via substitution a=1+x and case analysis on sign patterns is valid. The inequality is proven with equality cases matching the claimed values: (1,1,1,1), (0,4/3,4/3,4/3), and (4,0,0,0). The logic covers all cases (one negative or three negatives in the x_i; two negatives is impossible with product negative). The conclusion correctly restates the result. Minor: the case of two negative x_i with product positive is mentioned implicitly but not fully addressed—though it's covered by the x₁x₂x₃x₄≥0 case."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear mathematical writing with good structure: definition of P, substitution, key equation (1), then case analysis with visual separation. Notation is consistent. Minor issues: 'x=(-1,1/3,1/3,1/3)' uses x for the tuple which conflicts with x₁ notation; 'x=(3,-1,-1,-1)' similarly. The phrase 'Hence assume' is slightly abrupt. Otherwise professional quality suitable for competition mathematics."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Appropriate length for a competition proof. The expansion details are omitted (claimed 'straightforward'), which is acceptable. The case analysis is efficiently handled. Nothing appears padded. Could the 'One negative' and 'Three negatives' sections be more explicit about why these are the only cases? Briefly yes, but the constraint x+y+z+w=0 with product negative makes this clear to the reader. Near-ideal length."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Perfect adherence. Defines F clearly, introduces the key substitution with x=a+b, y=c+d=4-x, p=ab, q=cd, derives the expression (1), analyzes the optimization problem correctly by showing linearity in p and q leads to corner cases, checks all four cases (i)-(iv) explicitly, and states equality conditions. The structure follows a clear optimization approach. No missing elements, no unrequested content, format is appropriate.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Key claims verified: the expressions for ∑ab, ∑abc, abcd in terms of p,q,x,y are correct. The linearity argument in p and q is correct—slope 4-9y+12q is indeed increasing in q. The corner case analysis is exhaustive. The computations: (i) F=4xy≥0 correct; (ii) F=x(8-3x)²/4 verified by substitution; (iii) symmetric; (iv) F=(xy-4)(3xy-16)/4 = (4-xy)(16-3xy)/4≥0 correct since xy≤4. The inequality xy≤(x+y)²/4=4 is AM-GM. Equality conditions correctly derived. One minor unverified: 'increasing in q' justifies minimum at endpoints, but the logic 'hence minimum in p at p=0 or p=x²/4' could be more explicit about checking both q extremes—though this is handled by the subsequent sentence. Score 5 due to thorough verifiability."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Completely correct proof. The method reduces the 4-variable inequality to checking four cases via a clever two-variable reduction. All algebraic manipulations are correct. The inequality is proven for all admissible a,b,c,d with correct equality cases: (1,1,1,1) from case (iv), and permutations of (4/3,4/3,4/3,0) from cases (ii)/(iii). The logic that checking corners suffices is sound due to the bilinear structure. No errors, no gaps.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Exceptionally clear and efficient. The notation x,y,p,q is introduced cleanly. The optimization argument is explained with just enough detail. Cases (i)-(iv) are clearly delineated. The final paragraph summarizes equality cases well. No grammar issues, no awkward phrasing, appropriate level of formality. The phrase 'The same argument for q shows' efficiently handles symmetry. Publication-ready for competition mathematics."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Perfectly calibrated. The reduction to two variables is explained, the corner case argument is given, four cases are checked with minimal but sufficient detail, and equality cases are stated. Nothing can be cut: the linearity justification is needed, all four cases must be checked, the final conclusion restates the result. Nothing is missing: the constraint 0≤p≤x²/4 is stated, the range of x is implicit in y=4-x, and the non-negativity conditions are used. Ideal length.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.75'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 2, 'overall_comment': "Response B is moderately better due to superior structural elegance and computational efficiency. Response A uses the substitution $a=1+x$ etc. leading to a messy case analysis with one vs. three negative variables, requiring careful convexity arguments and Taylor-like expansions. Response B's key insight is the clever reduction to two variables via $(x,y)$ and $(p,q)$, then observing linearity in $p,q$ reduces to checking just four corner cases—each yielding clean polynomial inequalities. B's approach is more generalizable, avoids case proliferation, and the final verification is immediate (e.g., $(8-3x)^2$ and $(4-xy)(16-3xy)$ are transparently non-negative). A's method, while correct, buries the equality conditions in opaque calculations whereas B explicitly identifies all equality cases with clear geometric meaning."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in mathematical elegance and conciseness, using a clean substitution (x=a+b, y=c+d, p=ab, q=cd) that reduces the problem to checking four corner cases with explicit non-negative expressions. The Gemini 3 Pro response, while correct, is verbose and contains distracting errors: it claims 'all strictly positive variables must be equal' in minimal configurations, but then includes (2,2,0,0) where positive variables are equal and (4,0,0,0) with only one positive variable—neither fits the claimed pattern; it also has formatting artifacts like 'mustattain' and 'strictlypositive'. The SxS winner's case (iv) elegantly shows F = (4-xy)(16-3xy)/4 ≥ 0 using xy ≤ 4, while Gemini's linear mixing argument, though valid, is unnecessarily circuitous and less direct for this symmetric inequality."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner (internal) provides a cleaner, more elegant solution using symmetric variable reduction (x=a+b, y=c+d, p=ab, q=cd) that reduces the problem to checking just four corner cases with explicit polynomial inequalities. GPT 5.2's approach, while correct, is more verbose and computationally intensive—requiring case analysis with AM-GM inequalities, substitution with c=d, and a 2-parameter family check that involves more algebraic manipulation. The SxS winner's key insight of treating (1) as linear in p with slope analysis to establish corner minimality is more mathematically sophisticated and concise. GPT 5.2's final equality check for (0,4/3,4/3,4/3) contains a minor presentation issue: it states 'q=3·(16/9)=16/3' when actually q = ab+ac+ad+bc+bd+cd = 0 + 0 + 0 + (4/3)(4/3) + (4/3)(4/3) + (4/3)(4/3) = 3·(16/9) = 16/3, which is correct but the notation '3·(16/9)' is slightly confusing; more importantly, the SxS winner explicitly identifies all equality cases including the permutation structure (4/3,4/3,4/3,0) more clearly. Both solutions are truthfully correct, but the SxS winner demonstrates superior mathematical elegance and brevity."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Method naming and attribution', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly names the technique used ('Linear Mixing Method' / 'uvw method') at the beginning of the solution, providing mathematical context and attribution that the SxS winner omits.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better naming, 3-4 clear method identification with context, 5-6 explicit attribution with alternative name and clear framing', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Logical structure with explicit step headers', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro organizes the proof into clearly labeled sections ('Step 1: Linearity in the Product of Two Variables', 'Step 2: Deducing the Contradiction', 'Step 3: Evaluating the Candidate Minima') that guide the reader through the logical progression, while the SxS winner uses minimal structural markers.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 some sectioning, 3-4 clear step-by-step organization, 5-6 comprehensive structural framework with descriptive headers', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit contradiction argument structure', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro frames the core optimization argument as a formal proof by contradiction with explicit hypothesis ('Suppose, for the sake of contradiction...'), stated assumptions, and clear derivation of the contradiction, whereas the SxS winner presents the same mathematical logic without this explicit logical scaffolding.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 implicit contradiction logic, 3-4 explicit contradiction framing, 5-6 rigorous proof-by-contradiction structure with clear hypothesis and conclusion', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Complete enumeration of boundary cases', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly lists and evaluates all four candidate configurations (4 equal, 3 equal, 2 equal, 1 positive) with complete calculations, while the SxS winner only implicitly covers some cases through the corner analysis and does not explicitly evaluate (2,2,0,0) or (4,0,0,0).', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions additional cases, 3-4 lists most cases, 5-6 complete enumeration with explicit evaluation of all boundary configurations', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Post-proof analysis section', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated 'Responses to Prompt Inquiries' section after the main proof that addresses specific analytical questions (verifying equality cases, boundary analysis), providing pedagogical value that the SxS winner lacks.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 brief mention, 3-4 structured post-proof discussion, 5-6 comprehensive supplementary analysis with multiple subsections', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX display equation formatting', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper display math environments with $$ delimiters for major equations and aligned multi-line derivations, while the SxS winner uses inline formatting with manual spacing commands (\\medskip, \\noindent) and lacks proper display structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 some display math, 3-4 consistent display formatting, 5-6 proper LaTeX environments with clean visual hierarchy', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Variable definition clarity', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines all auxiliary variables (S, P, Q, S', A, B) with clear mathematical meaning before use, while the SxS winner introduces x, y, p, q with minimal context and uses them immediately without explaining their geometric significance.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 some definitions, 3-4 clear variable introduction, 5-6 comprehensive definition of all auxiliary quantities with explanatory context', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Structural section headers', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses clear, numbered section headers (## 1), ## 2), etc.) to organize the proof into logical phases: reduction, case d=0, case c=d, and conclusion.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minimal use, 3-4 clear numbered sections, 5-6 comprehensive hierarchical organization with descriptive headers', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit reduction justification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly proves and states that for fixed a,b, the expression F is affine in t=cd, with clear derivation showing the coefficient of t and concluding endpoint attainment.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions linearity without proof, 3-4 shows derivation with minor gaps, 5-6 complete derivation with explicit coefficient identification and interval justification', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Case analysis completeness', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 systematically handles both required cases (one variable zero and two variables equal) with explicit subcase analysis for ab endpoints in the c=d case.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions cases without full treatment, 3-4 treats main cases, 5-6 exhaustive with nested subcase analysis and explicit handling of ab=0 vs ab=S²/4', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Equality condition verification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated section explicitly computing and verifying equality cases for both (1,1,1,1) and the nontrivial permutations of (4/3,4/3,4/3,0).', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 mentions equality cases in passing, 3-4 states equality conditions, 5-6 explicit numerical verification with full calculations shown', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Boundary case rigor discussion', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes a final section acknowledging that AM-GM steps and continuity arguments require explicit justification for zero variables, identifying the faces of the simplex needing attention.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 no acknowledgment, 3-4 brief mention, 5-6 explicit discussion of continuity extension and simplex boundary structure', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX display equation formatting', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses proper LaTeX display environments with \\[...\\] and aligned environments for multi-line derivations, with clear visual separation between steps.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 inline math only, 3-4 some display math, 5-6 consistent use of display environments and alignment for readability', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Intermediate algebraic simplification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 shows explicit intermediate steps when simplifying the bracketed term 8xS-9x²S+4x² to 2x(4-3x)², including the factorization.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 jumps to final form, 3-4 shows some steps, 5-6 complete expansion and factorization with key intermediate form 32x-48x²+18x³ shown', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Variable substitution clarity', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 introduces auxiliary variables (m, t, S, x, y) with explicit definitions and domains, maintaining clear tracking throughout the derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 reuses variables ambiguously, 3-4 some auxiliary variables defined, 5-6 systematic introduction with explicit definitions and domain constraints', 'rating': 5}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Respo