hes P≥0 with rigorous case analysis; [CORRECT] Equality cases fully characterized. The substitution method (centering at 1) is valid and clever. The two cases (one negative, three negatives) cover all possibilities when product is negative. Calculations verified: for one negative, P = (4/9)t^2(1-t)(t+3)≥0 for t∈[0,1]; for three negatives, P = (4/9)t^2(3-t)(t+1)≥0 for t∈[0,3]. Both correct. No incorrect elements found.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Exceptionally clear mathematical writing with proper LaTeX formatting. Structure: introduction with substitution, equation (1), then two clearly labeled cases. Minor issue identified: 'x=(-1,1/3,1/3,1/3)' uses 'x' to denote the tuple (x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) without explicit definition—slightly informal but clear from context. No run-on sentences; technical terms used correctly; logical connectors appropriate. The 'medskip' and 'noindent' formatting shows professional competition-math style. Only one trivial style preference: could explicitly define the tuple notation. Otherwise publication-ready for mathematical audience."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for competition mathematics proof. Nothing to cut: substitution expansion is summarized (not fully expanded, which would be verbose); case analysis is minimal necessary; calculations shown explicitly for verification. Nothing missing: all logical steps present, equality cases verified, boundary behavior covered. The proof is concise yet complete—typical of AoPS/olympiad solutions. Only trivial trimming possible: the phrase 'with equality only at...' repeats structure, but this is standard for clarity. Score 5 justified: near-perfect calibration."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Checklist: [X] Prove the inequality—attempted; [X] Use appropriate mathematical methods; [X] Identify equality cases; [ ] NO explicit instruction to add 'Responses to Prompt Inquiries' section—this was UNREQUESTED content added at the end. The prompt asks only to prove the inequality, not to answer follow-up questions about 'nontrivial cases' or 'boundary cases.' This addition violates the 'no unrequested content' rule. Additionally: [PARTIAL] The case (2,2,0,0) is incorrectly included as a candidate minimum when the linear mixing argument actually forces variables to be equal OR zero, but (2,2,0,0) has two equal positive and two zero, which doesn't match 'all strictly positive variables equal'—the logic is slightly garbled. Score capped at 4 due to unrequested content addition."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] Linear mixing / uvw method is valid for symmetric inequalities; [VERIFIED] f(P) is indeed linear in P=ab; [VERIFIED] Evaluations at (1,1,1,1), (4/3,4/3,4/3,0), (4,0,0,0) are correct. [QUESTIONABLE] Claim 'all strictly positive variables must be equal'—the linear mixing argument actually shows that at extremum, either variables are equal OR at least one is zero. The case (2,2,0,0) is correctly evaluated (f=16>0) but the reasoning that leads to checking it is slightly misstated; the method actually generates (t,t,0,0) with 2t=4 so t=2, but this is not a minimum. [FALSE] The statement 'Equality famously holds for...a=b=c=4/3, d=0' is misleading—equality holds there, but the claim 'famously' is unverifiable puffery, and more critically, the response incorrectly suggests this is the only nontrivial case when (0,4/3,4/3,4/3) and permutations are equally valid. [UNVERIFIABLE] The claim about Lagrange multipliers requiring separate boundary checking—while generally true, the specific assertion about 'gradients proportional only captures interior local extrema' is oversimplified. Score capped at 3 due to questionable claims and unverifiable assertions in the added section."}, 'prompt_correctn