2026-03-25 08:40:38,581 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: tasks1_response---------------------------[{'index': 0, 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nIn this paper, every group is finite.\n\n**Definition 1: Minimal degree of a nontrivial representation**\nFor a nontrivial finite group $G$, we denote by $\\mathbf{D}_0(G)$ the minimal degree of a nontrivial irreducible complex representation of $G$, that is,\n$$ \\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\> := \\> \\min \\left\\{ \\dim(\\pi) \\mid \\pi \\in \\mathrm{Irr}(G) \\setminus \\{1\\} \\right\\} . $$\nwhere $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ is the set of irreducible complex characters of $G$, and $\\{1\\}$ denotes the trivial representation.\n\n**Definition 2: $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom group**\nLet $\\varepsilon>0$. A finite group $G$ is {\\itshape $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom} if every nontrivial irreducible complex representation of $G$ has degree at least $|G|^\\varepsilon$. That is, $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\geq |G|^\\varepsilon$.\n\n**Property 1: Perfectness of quasirandom groups**\nIf a finite group $G$ with $|G|>1$ is $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom for some $\\varepsilon>0$, then it has no nontrivial representations of degree 1. This implies that its abelianization $G/[G,G]$ is trivial, so $G$ is a perfect group (i.e., $G$ equals its commutator subgroup, $G=[G,G]$).\n\n**Definition 3: Quasirandom degree**\nLet $G$ be a nontrivial finite group. We introduce the {\\itshape (exponential) quasirandom degree} of $G$ by\n$$ \\a(G) \\> := \\> \\log_{|G|} \\mathbf{D}_0(G) . $$\nBy definition, $G$ is $\\a(G)$-quasirandom.\n\n**Lemma 1**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Then\n$$ \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N) \\> \\geq \\> \\mathbf{D}_0(G) . $$\n**Proof of Lemma 1**\nEach nontrivial irreducible representation of $G/N$ provides a nontrivial representation of $G$ by composition with the quotient map $G \\to G/N$. The inequality follows.\n\n**Property 2: Monotonicity of quasirandom degree for quotients**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Then $\\a(G/N) \\geq \\a(G)$.\n**Derivation of Property 2**\nFrom Lemma 1, we have $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)$. From the definition of $\\a(G)$, we have\n$$ \\a(G) = \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G)}{\\log|G|} \\leq \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G|} . $$\nSince $N$ is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup, $|G| > |G/N|$, so $\\log|G| > \\log|G/N|$. Therefore,\n$$ \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G|} < \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G/N|} = \\a(G/N). $$\nThus, $\\a(G) < \\a(G/N)$.\n\n**Equation 1: Bound on the size of a normal subgroup**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a proper normal subgroup. Then\n$$|N| \\> \\leq \\> \\frac{\\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)}}{|G/N|} .$$\n**Derivation of Equation 1**\nBy the definition of quasirandom degree, $|G| = \\mathbf{D}_0(G)^{1/\\a(G)}$. By Lemma 1, $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)$, so we have\n$$ |G| \\> \\leq \\> \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)} . $$\nSubstituting $|G|=|G/N||N|$, we obtain $|G/N||N| \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)}$, which rearranges to the desired inequality.\n\n**Definition 4: Perfect central extension and Schur Multiplier**\nA finite group $G$ is a {\\itshape perfect central extension} of a group $Q$ by a group $N$ if $G$ is perfect, $N$ is a subgroup of the center of $G$ ($N \\subseteq \\mathbf{Z}(G)$), and $G/N \\cong Q$. The possible groups $N$ for which such an extension of $Q$ exists are related to the {\\itshape Schur multiplier} of $Q$. A key property is that if $G$ is a perfect central extension of $Q$ by $N$, then $N$ must be a homomorphic image of the Schur multiplier of $Q$. In particular, the prime divisors of $|N|$ must also be prime divisors of the order of the Schur multiplier of $Q$.\n\n**Factual Prerequisites**\nThe following facts are assumed:\n1.  Among the finite simple groups, the sporadic simple groups $J_1$ and $O'N$ are the only ones whose quasirandom degree is at least $\\frac{1}{3}$.\n2.  For $J_1$: $\\mathbf{D}_0(J_1) = 56$ and $|J_1| = 175560$.\n3.  For $O'N$: $\\mathbf{D}_0(O'N) = 10944$ and $|O'N| = 460815505920$.\n4.  The Schur multiplier of the simple group $O'N$ has cardinality 3.\n## Theorem\n**Corollary 2**\nThe only $\\frac{1}{3}$-quasirandom groups are the sporadic simple groups $J_1$ and $O'N$.", 'status': 'ACCEPT', 'result': {'status': 'ACCEPT', 'rejection_reason': None, 'justification': "The prompt provides a complete mathematical setup with definitions, lemmas, properties, and factual prerequisites. It asks for a proof of Corollary 2 about 1/3-quasirandom groups based on the provided technical framework. This is a well-structured mathematical reasoning task with all necessary context included. No rejection criteria apply: it's in English, safe, coherent, contains no PII, requires no images, realtime data, or external references."}, 'accepted': True, 'error': None, 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 1815, 'output_tokens': 119, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.005058}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.005058}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 1815, 'output_tokens': 119, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.005058}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.005058}}] 
