2026-03-25 08:42:29,395 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_13105', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nIn this paper, every group is finite.\n\n**Definition 1: Minimal degree of a nontrivial representation**\nFor a nontrivial finite group $G$, we denote by $\\mathbf{D}_0(G)$ the minimal degree of a nontrivial irreducible complex representation of $G$, that is,\n$$ \\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\> := \\> \\min \\left\\{ \\dim(\\pi) \\mid \\pi \\in \\mathrm{Irr}(G) \\setminus \\{1\\} \\right\\} . $$\nwhere $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ is the set of irreducible complex characters of $G$, and $\\{1\\}$ denotes the trivial representation.\n\n**Definition 2: $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom group**\nLet $\\varepsilon>0$. A finite group $G$ is {\\itshape $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom} if every nontrivial irreducible complex representation of $G$ has degree at least $|G|^\\varepsilon$. That is, $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\geq |G|^\\varepsilon$.\n\n**Property 1: Perfectness of quasirandom groups**\nIf a finite group $G$ with $|G|>1$ is $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom for some $\\varepsilon>0$, then it has no nontrivial representations of degree 1. This implies that its abelianization $G/[G,G]$ is trivial, so $G$ is a perfect group (i.e., $G$ equals its commutator subgroup, $G=[G,G]$).\n\n**Definition 3: Quasirandom degree**\nLet $G$ be a nontrivial finite group. We introduce the {\\itshape (exponential) quasirandom degree} of $G$ by\n$$ \\a(G) \\> := \\> \\log_{|G|} \\mathbf{D}_0(G) . $$\nBy definition, $G$ is $\\a(G)$-quasirandom.\n\n**Lemma 1**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Then\n$$ \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N) \\> \\geq \\> \\mathbf{D}_0(G) . $$\n**Proof of Lemma 1**\nEach nontrivial irreducible representation of $G/N$ provides a nontrivial representation of $G$ by composition with the quotient map $G \\to G/N$. The inequality follows.\n\n**Property 2: Monotonicity of quasirandom degree for quotients**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Then $\\a(G/N) \\geq \\a(G)$.\n**Derivation of Property 2**\nFrom Lemma 1, we have $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)$. From the definition of $\\a(G)$, we have\n$$ \\a(G) = \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G)}{\\log|G|} \\leq \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G|} . $$\nSince $N$ is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup, $|G| > |G/N|$, so $\\log|G| > \\log|G/N|$. Therefore,\n$$ \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G|} < \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G/N|} = \\a(G/N). $$\nThus, $\\a(G) < \\a(G/N)$.\n\n**Equation 1: Bound on the size of a normal subgroup**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a proper normal subgroup. Then\n$$|N| \\> \\leq \\> \\frac{\\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)}}{|G/N|} .$$\n**Derivation of Equation 1**\nBy the definition of quasirandom degree, $|G| = \\mathbf{D}_0(G)^{1/\\a(G)}$. By Lemma 1, $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)$, so we have\n$$ |G| \\> \\leq \\> \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)} . $$\nSubstituting $|G|=|G/N||N|$, we obtain $|G/N||N| \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)}$, which rearranges to the desired inequality.\n\n**Definition 4: Perfect central extension and Schur Multiplier**\nA finite group $G$ is a {\\itshape perfect central extension} of a group $Q$ by a group $N$ if $G$ is perfect, $N$ is a subgroup of the center of $G$ ($N \\subseteq \\mathbf{Z}(G)$), and $G/N \\cong Q$. The possible groups $N$ for which such an extension of $Q$ exists are related to the {\\itshape Schur multiplier} of $Q$. A key property is that if $G$ is a perfect central extension of $Q$ by $N$, then $N$ must be a homomorphic image of the Schur multiplier of $Q$. In particular, the prime divisors of $|N|$ must also be prime divisors of the order of the Schur multiplier of $Q$.\n\n**Factual Prerequisites**\nThe following facts are assumed:\n1.  Among the finite simple groups, the sporadic simple groups $J_1$ and $O'N$ are the only ones whose quasirandom degree is at least $\\frac{1}{3}$.\n2.  For $J_1$: $\\mathbf{D}_0(J_1) = 56$ and $|J_1| = 175560$.\n3.  For $O'N$: $\\mathbf{D}_0(O'N) = 10944$ and $|O'N| = 460815505920$.\n4.  The Schur multiplier of the simple group $O'N$ has cardinality 3.\n## Theorem\n**Corollary 2**\nThe only $\\frac{1}{3}$-quasirandom groups are the sporadic simple groups $J_1$ and $O'N$.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under '## Proof' heading. Includes '## Prerequisites' and '## Key Takeaways' sections which were not explicitly requested but do not violate instructions. The proof structure is correct with proper mathematical formatting. Minor issue: The prompt asks to 'try multiple approaches if you are stuck'—there is no evidence of multiple approaches being attempted, though this is arguably unnecessary since the proof succeeds directly. The instruction to be honest if unable to complete is satisfied (not needed). Score capped at 5 due to unrequested 'Key Takeaways' section and lack of explicit demonstration of multiple approaches."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Majority of claims are correct, but contains a critical error in the calculation for O'N: Response A states '|N|≤10944³/460815505920≈2.84, so |N|≤2'. However, 10944³ = 1310998017024, and 1310998017024/460815505920 ≈ 2.845, so |N|≤2 is technically correct. But the Schur multiplier of O'N has order 3, and Response A claims this 'forces N=1 again' because |N|≤2. This is correct logic—since |N| must divide 3 and |N|≤2, we get |N|=1. However, Response A has a subtle issue: it says 'forcing N=1 again' but doesn't explicitly state that |N| must divide 3 (it only says the Schur multiplier has order 3). The logic is sound but slightly compressed. More seriously: for J₁, the Schur multiplier is actually trivial (order 1), not mentioned—Response A correctly gets N=1 but doesn't cite this. The calculation 56³/175560 = 175616/175560 ≈ 1.0003, so |N|<2, hence |N|≤1, so N=1. This is correct. However, Response A states '<2, so N=1' which is slightly imprecise (should be |N|<2 so |N|≤1). The claim 'so N=1' is correct but the reasoning '<2, so N=1' skips the integer step. Also, Response A uses '1/α(G)≤3' which is correct since α(G)≥1/3. Overall: minor imprecisions in reasoning presentation, but no false claims. Score 4 due to slightly compressed justification that could mislead."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The proof is essentially correct but has a gap in handling the J₁ case. For J₁, Response A computes |N|≤56³/175560<2 and concludes N=1. However, to apply Definition 4 (perfect central extension), one needs to know that the Schur multiplier of J₁ is trivial. Response A does not state this explicitly, though it is a standard fact. The proof works because if |N|<2 then |N|=1 regardless, but this obscures the structure. More importantly, Response A does not explicitly verify that J₁ and O'N are indeed 1/3-quasirandom at the end—it shows α>1/3 for both, which is correct (log(56)/log(175560)≈0.3346>1/3, log(10944)/log(460815505920)≈0.3344>1/3). The proof structure is correct but the handling of the J₁ Schur multiplier is implicit. Score 4 due to this gap."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear mathematical writing with good structure. Prerequisites are well-organized. The proof flows logically. Minor issues: '1/α(G)≤3' could be written more clearly as 'since α(G)≥1/3, we have 1/α(G)≤3'. The phrase 'so N=1' after '<2' is slightly abrupt. The use of 'Key Takeaways' is somewhat redundant with the proof itself. Overall professional quality with only trivial style preferences to adjust."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length is appropriate for the complexity of the proof. The Prerequisites section is comprehensive but could be slightly condensed (some items are restatements of definitions already given in the prompt). The proof itself is concise. Key Takeaways section adds some length but provides value. Nothing essential is missing, and only trivial trimming would be possible.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.55, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.55'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Follows instructions with '## Proof' heading and proper structure. Includes '## Prerequisites' and '## Key Takeaways' similar to Response A. The proof is complete. Same minor issue as Response A regarding 'try multiple approaches'—no evidence of multiple approaches. The proof structure is correct. Score 5 for same reasons as Response A."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Contains a critical error in the J₁ case. Response B states: 'If Q≅J₁, its Schur multiplier is trivial, so M=1 and G≅J₁.' This is factually correct (Schur multiplier of J₁ is indeed trivial). However, the logic is flawed: Response B has already assumed G is not simple and chosen a maximal normal subgroup M. If Q≅J₁ and M=1, then G≅J₁, contradicting the assumption that G is not simple. Response B does not explicitly address this contradiction; it simply concludes G≅J₁. The correct conclusion should be that this case is impossible (since we assumed G not simple), or that G must be simple. The proof structure 'If G is simple... Suppose G is not simple...' followed by concluding 'Thus any 1/3-quasirandom group is J₁ or O'N' works, but the J₁ case under 'not simple' leads to G≅J₁ which contradicts 'not simple'. This is a logical gap. Additionally, for O'N, Response B states '|M| divides 3' which is correct, then applies Equation 1 to get |M|<3, hence |M|=1. This is correct. However, Response B uses 'α(G)≥1/3' in Equation 1, giving exponent 3, but doesn't explicitly justify that equality might not hold. The bigger issue is the J₁ case logic: if Schur multiplier is trivial, then M=1, so G≅Q≅J₁, but this contradicts G not simple. The proof should explicitly note this eliminates the non-simple case for J₁. As written, it's confusing. Score 3 due to this logical gap."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "The proof has a structural issue. Response B splits into 'If G is simple' and 'Suppose G is not simple'. In the non-simple case, it considers Q=G/M simple. For Q≅J₁, it concludes M=1 so G≅J₁. But this means G is simple, contradicting the assumption. Response B doesn't explicitly resolve this contradiction—it just states 'so G≅J₁' as if this completes the non-simple case. The correct resolution is that Q≅J₁ is impossible in the non-simple case, so only Q≅O'N needs consideration. But Response B doesn't say this. The proof still arrives at the correct conclusion because the O'N case also gives M=1, so G≅O'N (simple), contradicting non-simple assumption. So both cases under 'not simple' lead to simple groups, meaning no non-simple 1/3-quasirandom groups exist. However, Response B's presentation obscures this logic. The final conclusion 'Thus any 1/3-quasirandom group is J₁ or O'N' is correct, but the reasoning is flawed. Score 3 due to this logical confusion."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear and professional writing. The structure with 'If G is simple' vs 'Suppose G is not simple' is a standard and effective proof technique. Minor issue: the logical flow in the J₁ case under 'not simple' is confusing as noted in Truthfulness. Otherwise well-written with appropriate mathematical notation."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Appropriate length. The Prerequisites section is similar to Response A. The proof is slightly more concise than Response A in handling the cases. Key Takeaways section adds value. Nothing essential is missing.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.1, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.10'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both proofs are correct and complete, with identical logical structure: perfectness → quotient analysis → simple quotient identification → Schur multiplier bound → kernel elimination. Response A has a minor calculation error (claims |N|≤2 for O'N when bound is <3, giving |N|≤2) while Response B correctly states |M| divides 3 then uses bound <3. Response B's 'maximal normal subgroup' framing is slightly cleaner than A's 'assume N exists' approach, but both reach valid conclusions. Neither has significant advantages in rigor or clarity."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Writing Style. The SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with all necessary definitions explicitly stated upfront, clear logical flow, and proper mathematical notation throughout. It correctly handles both cases: for J₁, it shows |N| < 2 forcing N = 1; for O'N, it uses the Schur multiplier constraint (order 3) to rule out |N| ≤ 2, which is more elegant than the external model's approach. Gemini 3 Pro's response has significant formatting issues (missing spaces after periods, run-together words like 'isquasirandom' and 'SchurMultiplier'), contains a logical gap in Case 2 where it assumes |N| = 2 without considering |N| = 1, and unnecessarily introduces 'maximal normal subgroup' when any proper normal subgroup suffices. The SxS winner's direct verification that both J₁ and O'N satisfy α > 1/3 at the end provides cleaner closure, while Gemini 3 Pro omits this verification and has slightly garbled 'Key Takeaways' formatting."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) provides a cleaner, more direct proof structure that immediately establishes simplicity without unnecessary case distinctions, while GPT 5.2 introduces a suboptimal 'maximal proper normal subgroup' approach that complicates the logic. The SxS winner correctly notes that any proper quotient has strictly larger quasirandom degree (Property 2 gives strict inequality), immediately forcing G/N to be simple with α > 1/3, hence G/N ≅ J₁ or O'N by the factual list—this is more elegant than GPT 5.2's maximal subgroup argument. GPT 5.2 makes a subtle error in its O'N case: it computes |N| ≤ 2 but then incorrectly states 'If |N|=2...' when the bound is actually |N| ≤ 2.84..., so |N| could be 1 or 2; however, the Schur multiplier of O'N has order 3, so N=1 is forced, making the extra case analysis unnecessary. The SxS winner correctly computes |N| ≤ 2.84 and immediately concludes |N| ≤ 2, then uses the Schur multiplier constraint to force N=1 without the redundant case distinction. Both handle the J₁ bound correctly (56³/175560 ≈ 1.0003 < 2). The SxS winner's proof is more concise and avoids GPT 5.2's slightly misleading 'maximal' framing and unnecessary case analysis for O'N."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit maximal normal subgroup assumption', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly assumes N is a maximal nontrivial proper normal subgroup to ensure G/N is simple, while the SxS winner omits this justification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed case analysis structure', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses explicit 'Case 1' and 'Case 2' headers with clear logical separation, while the SxS winner presents cases in continuous prose.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit automorphism group argument for centrality', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly derives N ⊆ Z(G) via the automorphism group of N being trivial when |N|=2, while the SxS winner asserts this without justif