2026-03-25 08:42:29,524 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_13105', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nIn this paper, every group is finite.\n\n**Definition 1: Minimal degree of a nontrivial representation**\nFor a nontrivial finite group $G$, we denote by $\\mathbf{D}_0(G)$ the minimal degree of a nontrivial irreducible complex representation of $G$, that is,\n$$ \\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\> := \\> \\min \\left\\{ \\dim(\\pi) \\mid \\pi \\in \\mathrm{Irr}(G) \\setminus \\{1\\} \\right\\} . $$\nwhere $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ is the set of irreducible complex characters of $G$, and $\\{1\\}$ denotes the trivial representation.\n\n**Definition 2: $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom group**\nLet $\\varepsilon>0$. A finite group $G$ is {\\itshape $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom} if every nontrivial irreducible complex representation of $G$ has degree at least $|G|^\\varepsilon$. That is, $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\geq |G|^\\varepsilon$.\n\n**Property 1: Perfectness of quasirandom groups**\nIf a finite group $G$ with $|G|>1$ is $\\varepsilon$-quasirandom for some $\\varepsilon>0$, then it has no nontrivial representations of degree 1. This implies that its abelianization $G/[G,G]$ is trivial, so $G$ is a perfect group (i.e., $G$ equals its commutator subgroup, $G=[G,G]$).\n\n**Definition 3: Quasirandom degree**\nLet $G$ be a nontrivial finite group. We introduce the {\\itshape (exponential) quasirandom degree} of $G$ by\n$$ \\a(G) \\> := \\> \\log_{|G|} \\mathbf{D}_0(G) . $$\nBy definition, $G$ is $\\a(G)$-quasirandom.\n\n**Lemma 1**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Then\n$$ \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N) \\> \\geq \\> \\mathbf{D}_0(G) . $$\n**Proof of Lemma 1**\nEach nontrivial irreducible representation of $G/N$ provides a nontrivial representation of $G$ by composition with the quotient map $G \\to G/N$. The inequality follows.\n\n**Property 2: Monotonicity of quasirandom degree for quotients**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a nontrivial proper normal subgroup. Then $\\a(G/N) \\geq \\a(G)$.\n**Derivation of Property 2**\nFrom Lemma 1, we have $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)$. From the definition of $\\a(G)$, we have\n$$ \\a(G) = \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G)}{\\log|G|} \\leq \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G|} . $$\nSince $N$ is a nontrivial proper normal subgroup, $|G| > |G/N|$, so $\\log|G| > \\log|G/N|$. Therefore,\n$$ \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G|} < \\frac{\\log \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)}{\\log|G/N|} = \\a(G/N). $$\nThus, $\\a(G) < \\a(G/N)$.\n\n**Equation 1: Bound on the size of a normal subgroup**\nLet $N \\lhd G$ be a proper normal subgroup. Then\n$$|N| \\> \\leq \\> \\frac{\\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)}}{|G/N|} .$$\n**Derivation of Equation 1**\nBy the definition of quasirandom degree, $|G| = \\mathbf{D}_0(G)^{1/\\a(G)}$. By Lemma 1, $\\mathbf{D}_0(G) \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)$, so we have\n$$ |G| \\> \\leq \\> \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)} . $$\nSubstituting $|G|=|G/N||N|$, we obtain $|G/N||N| \\leq \\mathbf{D}_0(G/N)^{1/\\a(G)}$, which rearranges to the desired inequality.\n\n**Definition 4: Perfect central extension and Schur Multiplier**\nA finite group $G$ is a {\\itshape perfect central extension} of a group $Q$ by a group $N$ if $G$ is perfect, $N$ is a subgroup of the center of $G$ ($N \\subseteq \\mathbf{Z}(G)$), and $G/N \\cong Q$. The possible groups $N$ for which such an extension of $Q$ exists are related to the {\\itshape Schur multiplier} of $Q$. A key property is that if $G$ is a perfect central extension of $Q$ by $N$, then $N$ must be a homomorphic image of the Schur multiplier of $Q$. In particular, the prime divisors of $|N|$ must also be prime divisors of the order of the Schur multiplier of $Q$.\n\n**Factual Prerequisites**\nThe following facts are assumed:\n1.  Among the finite simple groups, the sporadic simple groups $J_1$ and $O'N$ are the only ones whose quasirandom degree is at least $\\frac{1}{3}$.\n2.  For $J_1$: $\\mathbf{D}_0(J_1) = 56$ and $|J_1| = 175560$.\n3.  For $O'N$: $\\mathbf{D}_0(O'N) = 10944$ and $|O'N| = 460815505920$.\n4.  The Schur multiplier of the simple group $O'N$ has cardinality 3.\n## Theorem\n**Corollary 2**\nThe only $\\frac{1}{3}$-quasirandom groups are the sporadic simple groups $J_1$ and $O'N$.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better. Both responses correctly prove the theorem, but A provides a cleaner, more rigorous structure with explicit minimality argument and sharper analysis. Response B contains formatting errors ('Prerequisites- **Definition 2:**' with missing spaces), redundant 'Key Takeaways' section not requested, and imprecise phrasing ('|N| ≤ 1' contradicts 'N is nontrivial' rather than directly concluding |N|=1). Response A's Step 4 correctly computes |N|<2 for J₁ case yielding |N|=1, while B's '≤ 1' is slightly muddled. A also explicitly verifies the conclusion by checking the extensions don't yield 1/3-quasirandom groups, which B omits."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) is better on Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with proper structure (numbered steps, clear logical flow) and correctly handles all cases including the verification that J₁ and O'N themselves satisfy the 1/3-quasirandom condition (final paragraph). Gemini 3 Pro's response has several issues: (1) it incorrectly states 'a(G/N) > a(G)' when Property 2 only guarantees a(G/N) ≥ a(G), and this strict inequality is never established; (2) it uses 'maximal normal subgroup' without justification when 'minimal normal subgroup' is the natural choice for the proof structure; (3) it completely omits the final verification step showing that 3·O'N is not 1/3-quasirandom, leaving the proof incomplete; (4) the formatting is poor with run-together text like 'Prerequisites- **Definition 2**'. The SxS winner's explicit calculation showing |N| ≤ 2 for O'N case and subsequent Schur multiplier argument is cleaner than Gemini's approximate '2.84' bound."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is slightly better in Instruction Following and Truthfulness. The SxS winner correctly uses a minimal counterexample argument (steps 1-3), which is the standard approach for this type of classification theorem, and properly handles the case analysis with explicit verification that 56³/|J₁| < 2 and 10944³/|O'N| < 3. The SxS winner also correctly notes that the unique non-trivial perfect central extension 3·O'N fails to be 1/3-quasirandom, which GPT 5.2 omits. GPT 5.2 uses a maximal normal subgroup approach which is less natural for this proof, and makes a subtle error in Case 2: it claims |N| ≤ 2 but then says |N| ≠ 2 due to Schur multiplier constraints, when actually |N| could be 3 (the full Schur multiplier), requiring the additional check that 3·O'N isn't 1/3-quasirandom. GPT 5.2's final verification only checks that J₁ and O'N satisfy the condition, not that no extensions do."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit prerequisite enumeration', 'description': "GPT 5.2 begins with a clearly delineated 'Prerequisites' section that explicitly lists all definitions, properties, lemmas, equations, and factual prerequisites used in the proof, making the logical dependencies transparent before the proof begins.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minimal listing, 3-4 = organized enumeration with minor gaps, 5-6 = comprehensive, well-structured prerequisite section that aids proof comprehension', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Maximal normal subgroup strategy', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses the cleaner approach of selecting a maximal proper normal subgroup M to immediately obtain a simple quotient S = G/M, avoiding the SxS winner's two-step argument through minimal normal subgroups and minimality contradiction.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = equivalent strategies, 3-4 = moderately cleaner approach, 5-6 = substantially more elegant and direct proof structure', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit numerical verification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly computes 56³ = 175616 and 10944³ = 1,310,775,312,384, showing the actual arithmetic rather than only stating the final inequality bounds.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no explicit computation, 3-4 = partial explicit computation, 5-6 = full explicit computation with intermediate values shown', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Schur multiplier contradiction clarity', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly states that |N| = 2 must divide 3 (the order of Mult(O'N)), calling this a 'contradiction,' whereas the SxS winner combines this logic more opaquely with the |N| ≤ 2 bound.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = equivalent clarity, 3-4 = moderately clearer logical structure, 5-6 = substantially clearer explicit contradiction identification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Structured case analysis formatting', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses explicit 'Case 1' and 'Case 2' subsections with clear visual separation, whereas the SxS winner uses numbered steps that blend the case analysis into a continuous narrative.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = equivalent structure, 3-4 = moderately better visual organization, 5-6 = substantially clearer case separation aiding readability', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Key Takeaways summary section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a 'Key Takeaways' section that summarizes the proof's main logical steps in bullet form, providing a high-level map of the argument that the SxS winner lacks.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no summary or minimal restatement, 3-4 = useful but basic summary, 5-6 = comprehensive strategic overview that enhances understanding', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX fraction formatting', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 consistently uses \\tfrac13 for inline fractions (\\tfrac13-quasirandom, \\alpha(G) \\ge \\tfrac13) improving readability, while the SxS winner uses 1/3 which is less typographically polished for mathematical writing.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = equivalent formatting, 3-4 = minor improvement, 5-6 = consistently superior typographic choices throughout', 'rating': 3}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 25803, 'output_tokens': 4956, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.111078}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.111078}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 25803, 'output_tokens': 4956, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.111078}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.111078}}] 
