ups,' which requires showing both directions: (1) any 1/3-quasirandom group is J₁ or O'N, and (2) J₁ and O'N are 1/3-quasirandom. The proof only does (1). This is a significant gap. Score 3."}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof is mathematically correct and complete. It establishes: (1) G is perfect by Property 1. (2) Takes maximal normal M, gets simple quotient S=G/M. (3) By Property 2, α(S)≥α(G)≥1/3, so S is J₁ or O'N by classification. (4) Case J₁: Equation 1 gives |N|≤56³/175560<2, so N=1, G≅J₁. (5) Case O'N: |N|≤10944³/460815505920<3, so |N|≤2. (6) If |N|=2, then N≅C₂≤Z(G), making G a perfect central extension, but |N| must divide |Mult(O'N)|=3, contradiction. So N=1, G≅O'N. (7) Explicitly verifies J₁ and O'N are 1/3-quasirandom. The calculation 56³=175616>175560=|J₁| is correct (actually 175616>175560, so 56³>|J₁|, meaning α(J₁)>1/3). Similarly 10944³>|O'N|. The proof correctly notes this at the end. I considered a potential fault: in Case 2, the proof says '|N|≤2' and then excludes |N|=2, but doesn't explicitly exclude |N|=1 as leading to simplicity (which would contradict 'not simple'). However, the proof structure takes M as maximal proper normal, so if N=1, then M=1 and G=S is simple, which is the desired conclusion. The proof handles this by showing N=1 in both cases. The final verification that both groups are 1/3-quasirandom is present. The mathematics is sound."}}], 'criteria': ['formatting specifications', 'quantitative length constraints', 'mathematical correctness'], 'usage': {'input_tokens': 11439, 'output_tokens': 4255, 'cached_tokens': 0}, 'error': None, 'task_id': 'tv_stem_13105'}] 
