uation 29: `\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1} |c(k)|^2 = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{j=-N/2}^{N/2-1} |a_j|^2.`\n2.  **Poisson Statistics Assumption:** For a light curve whose variations are due solely to Poisson statistics, the expected fractional rms variation squared is given by:\n    `s^2 = \\frac{1}{\\overline{c}} = \\frac{N}{C_{tot}}`\n## Theorem\nFor a light curve with variations due to Poisson statistics only, the average power `\\overline{P}` of its power spectrum, under the Leahy normalization, is 2.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All instructions followed perfectly. [X] Complete and correct proof provided. [X] Multiple approaches considered (variance in Fourier space, Parseval's theorem, Leahy normalization). [X] Cleaned up proof under ## Proof heading. [X] Honest disclosure if unable to complete (not needed as proof succeeded). [X] Used all provided definitions and axioms correctly. No unrequested content added. Format matches requested structure with clear sectioning."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All claims verified: [VERIFIED] Parseval's theorem application correctly transforms variance to Fourier space. [VERIFIED] DC component extraction (a_0 = C_tot) matches Definition 5. [VERIFIED] Symmetry |a_j|=|a_{-j}| correctly applied to combine positive/negative frequency pairs. [VERIFIED] Leahy normalization substitution |a_j|^2 = C_tot*P_j/2 applied correctly for j≠0 including Nyquist. [VERIFIED] Algebraic manipulation leading to σ² = (C_tot/N²)*S verified step-by-step. [VERIFIED] Final substitution s²=N/C_tot yields P̄=2. [VERIFIED] Dimensional analysis check is correct. No unverifiable claims; all steps follow from established definitions."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': '100% [CORRECT]. The theorem is proven correctly: [CORRECT] Variance expressed in terms of Fourier amplitudes using Parseval. [CORRECT] Conversion to Leahy-normalized powers using Definition 7. [CORRECT] Relationship between S a