2026-03-25 08:43:14,286 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_16005', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $R$ be a noetherian ring. Let $\\mod R$ be the category of finitely generated $R$-modules. We use the following definitions and lemmas:\n\n**Definitions:**\n- A subcategory $\\X$ of $\\mod R$ is **closed under extensions** if for any short exact sequence $0 \\to L \\to M \\to N \\to 0$ in $\\mod R$, if $L, N \\in \\X$, then $M \\in \\X$.\n- A subcategory $\\X$ of $\\mod R$ is a **Serre subcategory** if it is closed under subobjects, quotients, and extensions.\n- A subcategory $\\X$ of $\\mod R$ is said to be **$\\otimes$-ideal** if $M\\otimes_R X\\in\\X$ for all $M\\in\\mod R$ and $X\\in\\X$.\n- A subcategory $\\X$ is **closed under direct summands** if for any module $M \\oplus N \\in \\X$, both $M$ and $N$ are in $\\X$.\n- For an $R$-module $M$, $\\supp M = \\{\\p \\in \\spec R \\mid M_\\p \\ne 0\\}$, where $\\spec R$ is the set of prime ideals of $R$.\n- For a subcategory $\\X$, $\\supp \\X = \\bigcup_{M \\in \\X} \\supp M$.\n- For a subset $\\Phi \\subseteq \\spec R$, $\\supp^{-1}\\Phi = \\{M \\in \\mod R \\mid \\supp M \\subseteq \\Phi\\}$.\n- For a prime ideal $\\p$, $\\V(\\p) = \\{\\q \\in \\spec R \\mid \\q \\supseteq \\p\\}$.\n- For an $R$-module $X$, $\\Min X$ is the set of minimal prime ideals in $\\supp X$.\n- For an $R$-module $X$, $\\ass X$ is the set of associated prime ideals of $X$.\n- $\\kappa(\\p)$ denotes the residue field $R_\\p/\\p R_\\p$.\n\n**Lemma A (Lemma 1(1) in the paper)**\nLet $\\X$ be a subcategory of $\\mod R$ closed under extensions.\n- Put $\\Phi=\\{\\p\\in\\spec R\\mid R/\\p\\in\\X\\}$. One then has $\\supp^{-1}\\Phi\\subseteq\\X$.\n- If $\\supp\\X\\subseteq\\Phi$, then $\\X$ is Serre.\n\n**Proof of Lemma A:**\nLet $M\\in\\supp^{-1}\\Phi$. Take a filtration $0=M_0\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq M_n=M$ of submodules of $M$ such that for each $i$ one has $M_i/M_{i-1}\\cong R/\\p_i$ with $\\p_i\\in\\supp M$. Then $\\p_i\\in\\supp M\\subseteq\\Phi$ and $R/\\p_i\\in\\X$. As $\\X$ is closed under extensions, we get $M\\in\\X$. If $\\supp\\X\\subseteq\\Phi$, then $\\X=\\supp^{-1}\\Phi$, and it is Serre.\n\n**Lemma B (Lemma 1(2) in the paper)**\nIf $\\X\\ne\\supp^{-1}(\\supp\\X)$, then there exists $\\p\\in\\supp\\X$ with $R/\\p\\notin\\X$ and $\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$.\n\n**Proof of Lemma B:**\nThere is an $R$-module $M$ with $M\\notin\\X$ and $M\\in\\Y:=\\supp^{-1}(\\supp\\X)$. Note that $\\Y$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mod R$. If $M$ is generated by $n$ elements, then there is an exact sequence $0\\to M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$ of $R$-modules such that $M'$ is cyclic, $M''$ is generated by $n-1$ elements, and $M',M''$ are in $\\Y$. By induction on $n$ we may assume that $M$ is cyclic. Hence the set $\\I$ of ideals $I$ of $R$ with $R/I\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\supp\\X)$ and $R/I\\notin\\X$ is nonempty. As $R$ is noetherian, there exists a maximal element $\\p$ of $\\I$ with respect to the inclusion relation. Then $R/\\p\\notin\\X$ and $\\V(\\p)=\\supp R/\\p\\subseteq\\supp\\X$. Take a filtration $0=M_0\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq M_n=R/\\p$ of submodules of the $R$-module $R/\\p$ such that for each $i$ one has $M_i/M_{i-1}\\cong R/\\p_i$ with $\\p_i\\in\\spec R$. Then each $\\p_i$ contains $\\p$. Assume that the ideal $\\p$ of $R$ is not prime. Then each $\\p_i$ must strictly contain $\\p$, and the maximality of $\\p$ implies $R/\\p_i\\in\\X$ for all $i$. As $\\X$ is closed under extensions, we have $R/\\p\\in\\X$. This contradiction shows that $\\p$ is a prime ideal of $R$. Let $N\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})$ and $\\q\\in\\supp N$. Then $\\p\\subsetneq\\q$ and $\\supp R/\\q\\subseteq\\supp R/\\p\\subseteq\\supp\\X$. The maximality of $\\p$ implies $R/\\q\\in\\X$. By Lemma A we get $N\\in\\X$. Hence $\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$.\n\n**Lemma C (Lemma 1(3) in the paper)**\nLet $\\p$ be a prime ideal of $R$ such that $\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$. Suppose that $\\X$ is closed under direct summands and contains a module $X$ satisfying $\\Min X=\\{\\p\\}$ and $\\p X_\\p=0$. Then $R/\\p$ belongs to $\\X$.\n\n**Proof of Lemma C:**\nWe have $\\p\\in\\ass X\\subseteq\\supp X=\\V(\\p)$. Taking the irredundant primary decomposition of the submodule $0$ of the $R$-module $X$, we find submodules $M,N$ of $X$ with $0=M\\cap N$ such that $\\ass X/M=\\{\\p\\}$ and every associated prime ideal of $X/N$ strictly contains $\\p$. There is an exact sequence $\\sigma:0\\to X\\to X/M\\oplus X/N\\to X/(M+N)\\to0$. Note that $\\supp X/(M+N)\\subseteq\\supp X/N\\subseteq\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\}$. We get $X/(M+N)\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$ and $(X/M)_\\p\\cong X_\\p\\cong\\kappa(\\p)^{\\oplus m}$ for some $m>0$ as $\\p X_\\p=0$. Since $\\X$ is closed under direct summands and extensions, the exact sequence $\\sigma$ shows $X/M\\in\\X$. There is an exact sequence $\\tau:0\\to K\\to X/M\\to(R/\\p)^{\\oplus m}\\to C\\to0$ with $K_\\p=C_\\p=0$. As $\\ass K\\subseteq\\ass X/M=\\{\\p\\}$, we have $K=0$. We also have $C\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$. The exact sequence $\\tau$ implies $R/\\p\\in\\X$.\n## Theorem\n**Proposition 2**\nLet $\\X$ be a $\\otimes$-ideal subcategory of $\\mod R$ closed under direct summands and extensions. Then $\\X$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mod R$.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist: [X] Provides complete and correct proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Uses multiple approaches/stages as suggested; [X] Honest about completion (successfully proves); [X] Follows all structural requirements from prompt. No unrequested content added. The response perfectly adheres to every instruction element: the ## Proof heading is present, the proof is complete and correct, and the 'think carefully, trying multiple approaches' is satisfied by the three-stage structure. No formatting violations."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All factual claims verified: [VERIFIED] Lemma A application: correctly states that supp^{-1}Phi ⊆ X when X is closed under extensions; [VERIFIED] Lemma B application: correctly identifies that if X ≠ supp^{-1}(supp X), then exists p ∈ supp X with R/p ∉ X and supp^{-1}(V(p)\\{p}) ⊆ X; [VERIFIED] Lemma C application: correctly states hypotheses (closed under direct summands, contains X with Min X = {p}, pX_p = 0, and supp^{-1}(V(p)\\{p}) ⊆ X) implies R/p ∈ X; [VERIFIED] Nakayama's lemma application: M_p ≠ 0 implies M_p/pM_p ≠ 0 is correct; [VERIFIED] Tensor product properties: M ⊗_R R/p ≅ M/pM is correct; [VERIFIED] Support containment: supp(M/pM) ⊆ V(p) is correct. No unverifiable claims. No outdated information. All technical claims are standard commutative algebra facts that are independently verifiable."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Proves that X is Serre subcategory; [CORRECT] Uses all given hypotheses (tensor-ideal, closed under direct summands, closed under extensions); [CORRECT] Correctly applies Lemma A, Lemma B, Lemma C; [CORRECT] The logical structure is valid: Stage 1 reduces to showing R/p ∈ X for all p ∈ supp X; Stage 2 proves this by contradiction using Lemmas B and C with the tensor-ideal property; Stage 3 concludes via Lemma A; [CORRECT] The construction N = M ⊗_R R/p satisfies the hypotheses of Lemma C: Min N = {p} (verified by supp N ⊆ V(p) and p ∈ supp N), pN_p = 0 (since N is an R/p-module), and N ∈ X (by tensor-ideal property); [CORRECT] The contradiction is valid: Lemma C gives R/p ∈ X, contradicting Lemma B's choice of p. Every requirement answered correctly and verifiably."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clarity: excellent, with clear stage headings and logical flow; Structure: well-organized with explicit Stage 1/2/3 structure; Tone: appropriate mathematical proof style; Grammar: correct. One minor issue identified: The phrase 'the three stages requested' in the opening is slightly informal ('requested' rather than 'outlined' or indicated), and the final 'No extra hypotheses needed' section, while valuable, is slightly redundant with the conclusion. These are trivial style preferences. Otherwise publication-ready quality with excellent use of displayed equations and logical markers."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Ideal length for this proof is medium-long (substantial argument requiring multiple lemmas). Response is near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut because: each stage serves a distinct logical purpose (reduction, core argument, conclusion); the verification of Lemma C hypotheses is necessary for rigor; the final 'No extra hypotheses needed' section provides useful clarification. Nothing is missing because: all lemma applications are fully justified; the contradiction is fully developed; the conclusion explicitly states that X is Serre. Minor trimming possible: the final section could be condensed slightly, and some transitional phrases ('Thus the whole problem is to prove') could be tightened. Overall ideal length ±15%."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.7, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.70'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Complete checklist: [X] Provides complete and correct proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Uses multiple approaches/stages (three explicit stages); [X] Honest about completion (successfully proves); [X] Follows all structural requirements. The response incl