2026-03-25 08:43:15,985 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_16005', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $R$ be a noetherian ring. Let $\\mod R$ be the category of finitely generated $R$-modules. We use the following definitions and lemmas:\n\n**Definitions:**\n- A subcategory $\\X$ of $\\mod R$ is **closed under extensions** if for any short exact sequence $0 \\to L \\to M \\to N \\to 0$ in $\\mod R$, if $L, N \\in \\X$, then $M \\in \\X$.\n- A subcategory $\\X$ of $\\mod R$ is a **Serre subcategory** if it is closed under subobjects, quotients, and extensions.\n- A subcategory $\\X$ of $\\mod R$ is said to be **$\\otimes$-ideal** if $M\\otimes_R X\\in\\X$ for all $M\\in\\mod R$ and $X\\in\\X$.\n- A subcategory $\\X$ is **closed under direct summands** if for any module $M \\oplus N \\in \\X$, both $M$ and $N$ are in $\\X$.\n- For an $R$-module $M$, $\\supp M = \\{\\p \\in \\spec R \\mid M_\\p \\ne 0\\}$, where $\\spec R$ is the set of prime ideals of $R$.\n- For a subcategory $\\X$, $\\supp \\X = \\bigcup_{M \\in \\X} \\supp M$.\n- For a subset $\\Phi \\subseteq \\spec R$, $\\supp^{-1}\\Phi = \\{M \\in \\mod R \\mid \\supp M \\subseteq \\Phi\\}$.\n- For a prime ideal $\\p$, $\\V(\\p) = \\{\\q \\in \\spec R \\mid \\q \\supseteq \\p\\}$.\n- For an $R$-module $X$, $\\Min X$ is the set of minimal prime ideals in $\\supp X$.\n- For an $R$-module $X$, $\\ass X$ is the set of associated prime ideals of $X$.\n- $\\kappa(\\p)$ denotes the residue field $R_\\p/\\p R_\\p$.\n\n**Lemma A (Lemma 1(1) in the paper)**\nLet $\\X$ be a subcategory of $\\mod R$ closed under extensions.\n- Put $\\Phi=\\{\\p\\in\\spec R\\mid R/\\p\\in\\X\\}$. One then has $\\supp^{-1}\\Phi\\subseteq\\X$.\n- If $\\supp\\X\\subseteq\\Phi$, then $\\X$ is Serre.\n\n**Proof of Lemma A:**\nLet $M\\in\\supp^{-1}\\Phi$. Take a filtration $0=M_0\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq M_n=M$ of submodules of $M$ such that for each $i$ one has $M_i/M_{i-1}\\cong R/\\p_i$ with $\\p_i\\in\\supp M$. Then $\\p_i\\in\\supp M\\subseteq\\Phi$ and $R/\\p_i\\in\\X$. As $\\X$ is closed under extensions, we get $M\\in\\X$. If $\\supp\\X\\subseteq\\Phi$, then $\\X=\\supp^{-1}\\Phi$, and it is Serre.\n\n**Lemma B (Lemma 1(2) in the paper)**\nIf $\\X\\ne\\supp^{-1}(\\supp\\X)$, then there exists $\\p\\in\\supp\\X$ with $R/\\p\\notin\\X$ and $\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$.\n\n**Proof of Lemma B:**\nThere is an $R$-module $M$ with $M\\notin\\X$ and $M\\in\\Y:=\\supp^{-1}(\\supp\\X)$. Note that $\\Y$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mod R$. If $M$ is generated by $n$ elements, then there is an exact sequence $0\\to M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$ of $R$-modules such that $M'$ is cyclic, $M''$ is generated by $n-1$ elements, and $M',M''$ are in $\\Y$. By induction on $n$ we may assume that $M$ is cyclic. Hence the set $\\I$ of ideals $I$ of $R$ with $R/I\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\supp\\X)$ and $R/I\\notin\\X$ is nonempty. As $R$ is noetherian, there exists a maximal element $\\p$ of $\\I$ with respect to the inclusion relation. Then $R/\\p\\notin\\X$ and $\\V(\\p)=\\supp R/\\p\\subseteq\\supp\\X$. Take a filtration $0=M_0\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq M_n=R/\\p$ of submodules of the $R$-module $R/\\p$ such that for each $i$ one has $M_i/M_{i-1}\\cong R/\\p_i$ with $\\p_i\\in\\spec R$. Then each $\\p_i$ contains $\\p$. Assume that the ideal $\\p$ of $R$ is not prime. Then each $\\p_i$ must strictly contain $\\p$, and the maximality of $\\p$ implies $R/\\p_i\\in\\X$ for all $i$. As $\\X$ is closed under extensions, we have $R/\\p\\in\\X$. This contradiction shows that $\\p$ is a prime ideal of $R$. Let $N\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})$ and $\\q\\in\\supp N$. Then $\\p\\subsetneq\\q$ and $\\supp R/\\q\\subseteq\\supp R/\\p\\subseteq\\supp\\X$. The maximality of $\\p$ implies $R/\\q\\in\\X$. By Lemma A we get $N\\in\\X$. Hence $\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$.\n\n**Lemma C (Lemma 1(3) in the paper)**\nLet $\\p$ be a prime ideal of $R$ such that $\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$. Suppose that $\\X$ is closed under direct summands and contains a module $X$ satisfying $\\Min X=\\{\\p\\}$ and $\\p X_\\p=0$. Then $R/\\p$ belongs to $\\X$.\n\n**Proof of Lemma C:**\nWe have $\\p\\in\\ass X\\subseteq\\supp X=\\V(\\p)$. Taking the irredundant primary decomposition of the submodule $0$ of the $R$-module $X$, we find submodules $M,N$ of $X$ with $0=M\\cap N$ such that $\\ass X/M=\\{\\p\\}$ and every associated prime ideal of $X/N$ strictly contains $\\p$. There is an exact sequence $\\sigma:0\\to X\\to X/M\\oplus X/N\\to X/(M+N)\\to0$. Note that $\\supp X/(M+N)\\subseteq\\supp X/N\\subseteq\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\}$. We get $X/(M+N)\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$ and $(X/M)_\\p\\cong X_\\p\\cong\\kappa(\\p)^{\\oplus m}$ for some $m>0$ as $\\p X_\\p=0$. Since $\\X$ is closed under direct summands and extensions, the exact sequence $\\sigma$ shows $X/M\\in\\X$. There is an exact sequence $\\tau:0\\to K\\to X/M\\to(R/\\p)^{\\oplus m}\\to C\\to0$ with $K_\\p=C_\\p=0$. As $\\ass K\\subseteq\\ass X/M=\\{\\p\\}$, we have $K=0$. We also have $C\\in\\supp^{-1}(\\V(\\p)\\setminus\\{\\p\\})\\subseteq\\X$. The exact sequence $\\tau$ implies $R/\\p\\in\\X$.\n## Theorem\n**Proposition 2**\nLet $\\X$ be a $\\otimes$-ideal subcategory of $\\mod R$ closed under direct summands and extensions. Then $\\X$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mod R$.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs are correct and complete, but Response A provides clearer structural organization with explicit stage labels, more detailed verification of Lemma C's hypotheses (especially the Min X = {p} condition), and a cleaner separation of the contradiction argument. Response B's Step 2 contains a minor redundancy (re-proving Serre property after already invoking Lemma A(2)) and its justification for Y_p ≠ 0 cites Nakayama without the necessary finite generation assumption on M_p being explicit, whereas Response A's localization argument is more self-contained."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and clarity in handling the tensor product construction and localization arguments. Specifically, the SxS winner correctly computes $X_\\mathfrak{p} \\cong M_\\mathfrak{p}/\\mathfrak{p}M_\\mathfrak{p}$ as an $R_\\mathfrak{p}$-module isomorphism, while Gemini 3 Pro makes a subtle error by writing '$\\mathfrak{p}R_\\mathfrak{p}M_\\mathfrak{p}$' which conflates the ideal action; the SxS winner also explicitly verifies that $\\mathfrak{p}$ is the unique minimal element of $\\supp X$ by noting $\\supp X = \\supp M \\cap \\mathrm{V}(\\mathfrak{p})$, whereas Gemini 3 Pro's argument for $\\Min X = \\{\\mathfrak{p}\\}$ is less precise. Additionally, the SxS winner's Stage 3 correctly invokes Lemma A(2) to conclude Serreness, while Gemini 3 Pro unnecessarily reproves that $\\supp^{-1}(\\Psi)$ is Serre rather than citing the established lemma. Both responses correctly apply the contradiction strategy using Lemmas B and C, but the SxS winner maintains cleaner logical flow with explicit 'Final check' verification that no extra hypotheses were used."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': 'Both responses are mathematically identical in content, structure, and correctness. Both follow the same three-stage proof structure (Lemma A application, contradiction via Lemmas B and C, and final Serre conclusion), use identical notation, and contain the same mathematical reasoning including the key construction of X = (R/p) ⊗_R M using the ⊗-ideal property. The SxS winner and GPT 5.2 both correctly verify the conditions for Lemma C (Min X = {p} and pX_p = 0), derive the contradiction showing X = supp^{-1}(supp X), and conclude via Lemma A(2). Both include the same final check on assumptions regarding noetherianity. There are no differences in truthfulness, instruction following, or writing style—both are complete, rigorous, and well-structured proofs.'}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'LaTeX formatting consistency', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses proper LaTeX delimiters ($...$) consistently throughout mathematical expressions, while the SxS winner mixes LaTeX with raw text (e.g., 'mod R' without delimiters, 'p' instead of '\\p')", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor formatting issues, 3-4 noticeable inconsistency, 5-6 substantially better LaTeX rendering', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit verification of Lemma C hypotheses', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly lists all three hypotheses of Lemma C as numbered conditions before applying it, while the SxS winner buries them in prose', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 slightly more explicit, 3-4 clearly more structured, 5-6 substantially clearer hypothesis verification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': "Nakayama's Lemma citation for non-vanishing", 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly invokes Nakayama's Lemma to prove $M_\\p / \\p R_\\p M_\\p \\neq 0$, 