, 'rubric_ratings': [{'rubric_name': 'Definition Statements Present', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Both definitions appear explicitly before the main proof. The square-free definition: 'An integer n>1 is square-free if no prime square divides it: p²∤n for every prime p.' The Carmichael number definition: 'A composite integer n is a Carmichael number if a^(n-1)≡1 (mod n) for every a with gcd(a,n)=1.' Both appear in the 'Definitions' section before 'Proof by contradiction'. I considered whether the definitions could be more formally separated, but they are clearly labeled and precede all proof content."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Both definitions appear explicitly before the main proof. Square-free: 'n is square-free iff no prime square divides it, i.e. p²∤n for every prime p.' Carmichael: 'A composite integer n>1 is Carmichael iff ∀a∈ℤ, gcd(a,n)=1⟹a^(n-1)≡1 (mod n).' Both appear in section '1. Definitions and a standard tool' before section '2. Proof by contradiction'. The definitions are clearly labeled and properly positioned."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Both definitions appear explicitly before the main proof. Carmichael number: 'A composite integer n>1 is a Carmichael number if, for every integer a that is coprime to n (i.e., gcd(a,n)=1), the following congruence holds: a^(n-1)≡1 (mod n).' Square-free: 'An integer n is square-free if it is divisible by no perfect square other than 1. Equivalently, in the prime factorization of n, the exponent of every prime factor is exactly 1.' Both appear in 'Definitions and Standard Results' before 'Proof by Contradiction'. I considered whether the square-free definition could be more concise, but it is complete and accurate."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Both definitions appear explicitly before the main proof. Carmichael number: 'A (composite) integer n is a Carmichael number if for every integer a with gcd(a,n)=1, a^(n-1)≡1 (mod n).' Square-free: 'An integer n is square-free if no prime square d