2026-03-25 08:45:38,378 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_6650', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThroughout, we represent a probability distribution $\\rho$ on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ via its probability density function with respect to the Lebesgue measure. We typically assume $\\rho$ has full support and is a smooth density. Let $\\nu$ be a target probability distribution.\n\n**R'enyi Divergence**\n\nFor $q > 1$, the R'enyi divergence of order $q$ between $\\rho$ and $\\nu$ is defined as:\n$$R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\frac{1}{q-1} \\log F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$$\nwhere\n$$F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^n} \\frac{\\rho(x)^q}{\\nu(x)^{q-1}} dx = \\mathbb{E}_{\\nu}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\rho(x)}{\\nu(x)}\\right)^q\\right]$$\n\nWe also define the quantity $G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$ for $q>1$ as:\n$$G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\mathbb{E}_\\nu\\left[ \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right)^{q-2} \\left\\|\\nabla \\frac{\\rho}{\\nu} \\right\\|^2 \\right]$$\n\n**Langevin Dynamics**\n\nThe evolution of the probability density $\\rho_t$ along the Langevin dynamics is described by the Fokker-Planck equation:\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\rho_t}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot \\left(\\rho_t \\nabla \\log \\frac{\\rho_t}{\\nu} \\right)$$\n\n**Log-Sobolev Inequality (LSI)**\n\nA probability distribution $\\nu$ is said to satisfy the Log-Sobolev Inequality (LSI) with constant $\\alpha > 0$ if for any probability density $\\rho$ and for $q \\ge 1$, the following inequality holds:\n$$\n\\frac{q^2}{2\\alpha} G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) \\ge q \\mathbb{E}_\\nu\\left[ \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right)^q \\log \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right) \\right] - F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) \\log F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)\n$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 2**\n\nSuppose $\\nu$ satisfies LSI with constant $\\alpha > 0$. Let $q > 1$. Along the Langevin dynamics, for all $t \\ge 0$,\n$$R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho_t) \\le e^{-\\frac{2\\alpha}{q}t} R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho_0)$$", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading [X]; (2) Multiple approaches considered implicitly through careful derivation [X]; (3) Honest completion with no admission of inability [X]; (4) Cleaned up final presentation [X]. No unrequested content added. The proof follows the requested structure exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All mathematical claims verified: (1) Fokker-Planck equation manipulation correct [VERIFIED]; (2) Integration by parts and chain rule applications correct [VERIFIED]; (3) LSI application matches given definition [VERIFIED]; (4) Convexity argument for φ(s) is standard and correct [VERIFIED]; (5) Grönwall application correct [VERIFIED]. One minor issue: The statement 'F(1)=1' appears to be a typo for F(0)=1 or refers to φ(1)=0; this is trivial and doesn't affect validity. All substantive claims are correct."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All prompt requirements correctly addressed: (1) Theorem 2 statement proven exactly as stated [CORRECT]; (2) Exponential decay rate e^{-2αt/q} derived correctly [CORRECT]; (3) LSI assumption used correctly with constant α [CORRECT]; (4) Langevin dynamics correctly applied [CORRECT]; (5) All definitions (Rényi divergence, G_{q,ν}, F_{q,ν}) used consistently with prompt [CORRECT]. The proof is complete and correct.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear structure. Minor issues: (1) The notation 'F(1)=1' is slightly confusing (should be F(0)=1 or clarify φ(1)=0 context); (2) The transition between steps 2-3 could use slightly more explicit connection text. Otherwise excellent: logical flow, appropriate technical precision, clean LaTeX formatting, good use of displayed equations. Single minor clarity issue prevents 6."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Nothing can be cut because: each section serves essential purpose—setup defines quantities, step 1 computes derivatives, step 2 applies LSI, step 3 relates quantities via convexity, step 4 solves ODE. Nothing is missing because: all logical steps are present, no gaps in reasoning, boundary conditions handled implicitly, convergence arguments sufficient. Slightly brief on justification of boundary term vanishing in integration by parts, but acceptable given standard assumptions.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Instructions partially followed with issues: (1) Proof provided but NOT under ## Proof heading—instead uses ### Strategy Outline and ### Step-by-Step Proof [MISSING required format]; (2) Multiple approaches mentioned in strategy but not actually pursued—only one linear path taken [PARTIAL]; (3) Unrequested content added: extensive 'Verification for q→1' section and 'Implicit Use of Full Support' section not required by prompt [ADDITION -1]; (4) Final result correct but structure deviates from requested ## Proof format. The checklist shows: format requirement [ ], multiple approaches [~], no unrequested content [ ], honest completion [X]. Significant format deviation and added content cap score at 4."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Multiple verifiability issues: (1) LSI formulation in Step 1 claims 'Ent_ν(g²) ≤ (2/α)∫|∇g|²dν' but this is backwards—correct form is (2/α)∫|∇g|²dν ≥ Ent_ν(g²), or equivalently Ent_ν(g²) ≤ (2/α)∫|∇g|²dν is wrong constant direction; actually checking: standard LSI is Ent_ν(f) ≤ (1/2α)∫|∇log f|²f dν, and for g², Ent_ν(g²) ≤ (2/α)∫|∇g|²dν is correct [VERIFIED after careful check]. (2) Step 6 has inequality direction error: claims '∫|∇g|²dν ≥ (α/2)∫f_t^q log(f_t^q/F(t))dν' but this is backwards—LSI gives upper bound on entropy, not lower bound on Dirichlet energy. The correct application should give ∫|∇g|²dν ≥ (α/2)Ent_ν(g²) only if using the functional form correctly; actually the response uses LSI as lower bound for Dirichlet energy which is wrong direction. [QUESTIONABLE/ERROR]. (3) Step 7 has confused logic: claims 'If q<1...' but theorem assumes q>1, and the case analysis is unnecessary and confusing. The final conclusion '−KL ≤ −R_q' is correct but derivation is convoluted. Multiple unverifiable/confusing claims cap at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Significant correctness issues: (1) The LSI application in Step 6 is incorrect—the inequality direction is wrong. Response states ∫|∇g|²dν ≥ (α/2)∫f_t^q log(f_t^q/F(t))dν, but LSI provides Ent_ν(g²) ≤ (2/α)∫|∇g|²dν, which rearranges to ∫|∇g|²dν ≥ (α/2)Ent_ν(g²). This is actually correct upon re-reading! Wait: Ent_ν(g²) = ∫g²log(g²/∫g²)dν = ∫f_t^q log(f_t^q/F(t))dν. So the inequality is correct. [CORRECT after verification]. (2) However, the Step 6 derivation then substitutes this to get dR_q/dt ≤ −(2α/q)KL, which follows correctly. (3) Major issue: The convexity argument in Step 7 is garbled. The response defines h(q) = log F_q, then claims h''(q) = Var_{μ_q}(log f_t) ≥ 0, which is correct. But then uses 'first-order condition' h(x) ≥ h(y) + h'(y)(x−y) with x=1, y=q to get h(q) ≤ (q−1)h'(q). This is correct for convex functions. However, the KL expression is KL(μ_q‖ν) = qh'(q) − h(q), and the inequality h(q) ≤ (q−1)h'(q) rearranges to h(q)/(q−1) ≤ h'(q), not directly to KL ≥ R_q. The response's algebra 'qh(q) ≤ (q−1)[KL + h(q)]' is correct, yielding h(q) ≤ (q−1)KL, so R_q = h(q)/(q-1) ≤ KL. This is correct for q>1. [CORRECT]. (4) However, the response then confusingly discusses q<1 case which is irrelevant. The final result is correct but the path is unnecessarily convoluted with confusing case analysis. [PARTIALLY CORRECT]. Score 3 due to unnecessary complexity and potential confusion, though final result correct."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Multiple significant issues: (1) Typos throughout: 'Toprove', 'changeof', 'Let g= f_t^{q/2}.' with space issues, 'Apply the LSI to the function $g= f_t^{q/2}$.' repetition; (2) Inconsistent formatting: some LaTeX inline, some display, mixing $...$ with $$...$$ inconsistently; (3) Run-on paragraphs in strategy outline; (4) Passive voice overuse; (5) Redundant explanations ('This is exactly...' appears multiple times); (6) The final sections (Verification, Implicit Use) are unnecessary editorial commentary not requested. The writing is readable but has 4+ minor issues and 1-2 major organizational issues."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 3, 'reason': 'Significant excess content: (1) Strategy Outline paragraph is unnecessary—could be removed entirely; (2) Step 1 includes excessive background on LSI that repeats prompt definitions; (3) Verification for q→1 section is unrequested and adds ~150 words; (4) Implicit Use of Full Support section is unrequested commentary adding ~100 words. Missing: more explicit justification for why boundary terms vanish (merely assumed). Could cut: Strategy Outline, Verification section, Implicit Use section, excessive LSI background in Step 1. Length is ~30-40% above ideal due to padding.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 3.5, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 3.50'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better. Both proofs are essentially correct, but Response A is significantly more concise and elegant, completing the proof in 4 clean steps versus Response B's 8 verbose steps. Response A's convexity argument in Step 3 (using φ(s) = log ∫w^s dν) is more direct than Response B's convoluted derivation in Step