2026-03-25 08:45:38,915 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_6650', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThroughout, we represent a probability distribution $\\rho$ on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ via its probability density function with respect to the Lebesgue measure. We typically assume $\\rho$ has full support and is a smooth density. Let $\\nu$ be a target probability distribution.\n\n**R'enyi Divergence**\n\nFor $q > 1$, the R'enyi divergence of order $q$ between $\\rho$ and $\\nu$ is defined as:\n$$R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\frac{1}{q-1} \\log F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$$\nwhere\n$$F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^n} \\frac{\\rho(x)^q}{\\nu(x)^{q-1}} dx = \\mathbb{E}_{\\nu}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\rho(x)}{\\nu(x)}\\right)^q\\right]$$\n\nWe also define the quantity $G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$ for $q>1$ as:\n$$G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\mathbb{E}_\\nu\\left[ \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right)^{q-2} \\left\\|\\nabla \\frac{\\rho}{\\nu} \\right\\|^2 \\right]$$\n\n**Langevin Dynamics**\n\nThe evolution of the probability density $\\rho_t$ along the Langevin dynamics is described by the Fokker-Planck equation:\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\rho_t}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot \\left(\\rho_t \\nabla \\log \\frac{\\rho_t}{\\nu} \\right)$$\n\n**Log-Sobolev Inequality (LSI)**\n\nA probability distribution $\\nu$ is said to satisfy the Log-Sobolev Inequality (LSI) with constant $\\alpha > 0$ if for any probability density $\\rho$ and for $q \\ge 1$, the following inequality holds:\n$$\n\\frac{q^2}{2\\alpha} G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) \\ge q \\mathbb{E}_\\nu\\left[ \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right)^q \\log \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right) \\right] - F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) \\log F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)\n$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 2**\n\nSuppose $\\nu$ satisfies LSI with constant $\\alpha > 0$. Let $q > 1$. Along the Langevin dynamics, for all $t \\ge 0$,\n$$R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho_t) \\le e^{-\\frac{2\\alpha}{q}t} R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho_0)$$", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading '## Proof'. It uses the defined quantities (Rényi divergence, Langevin dynamics, LSI) correctly. Minor formatting variance: uses 'Grönwall' instead of 'Gronwall' (the prompt uses 'Gronwall' in Response B, but this is trivial). The proof is complete and honest (no 'unable to complete' needed). Checked: [X] uses ## Proof heading, [X] uses defined quantities, [X] attempts proof, [X] complete proof provided. One minor issue: 'F(1)=1' appears to be a typo for 'F(t)=1' or refers to normalization, but this is trivial."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All mathematical claims are verifiable and correct. Key claims checked: (1) Time derivative of F: dF/dt = -q(q-1)G_{q,ν}(ρ_t) — VERIFIED via integration by parts of Fokker-Planck. (2) Time derivative of R: dR/dt = -q G/F — VERIFIED by chain rule. (3) LSI application: correctly states G ≥ (2α/q²)(qA - F log F) — VERIFIED. (4) Convexity argument: φ(q) ≤ (q-1)φ'(q) for convex φ with φ(1)=0 — VERIFIED (this is the supporting hyperplane inequality). (5) Final differential inequality and Grönwall application — VERIFIED. No unverifiable claims, no false statements. The proof structure matches known results in the literature (e.g., similar to proofs in Bakry-Émery theory or Vempala-Wibisono works on Rényi divergence decay)."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The theorem statement is: R_{q,ν}(ρ_t) ≤ exp(-2αt/q) R_{q,ν}(ρ_0). Response A correctly proves this. Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Computes dF/dt = -q(q-1)G. [CORRECT] Computes dR/dt = -qG/F. [CORRECT] Applies LSI to bound G from below. [CORRECT] Uses convexity of φ(s) = log ∫w^s dν to show A/F ≥ R. [CORRECT] Derives dR/dt ≤ -(2α/q)R. [CORRECT] Applies Grönwall to get final bound. All steps are mathematically correct. The convexity argument uses φ(q) ≤ (q-1)φ'(q) which is correct for convex φ with φ(1)=0 and q>1 (since φ(1)=0, the secant slope from 1 to q is ≤ derivative at q)."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing. Clear section structure (1-4), logical flow, appropriate notation. Minor issues: 'F(1)=1' is slightly confusing notation (should be 'F(t)=1' or explain that ∫w_t dν = 1 implies normalization). The phrase 'whence F(1)=1' appears to conflate the parameter value with the function evaluation — this is a minor clarity issue. Otherwise: [X] Clear definitions, [X] Logical progression, [X] Appropriate mathematical tone, [X] Good use of displayed equations. One minor redundancy: 'R_{q,ν}(ρ_t)≥0' is mentioned but not essential to the proof flow."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length is well-calibrated for a complete proof of this theorem. Nothing can be cut: each section serves a purpose — (1) establishes time derivatives, (2) applies LSI, (3) connects A/F to R via convexity, (4) solves ODE. Nothing is missing: all key steps are present. The proof is neither padded nor abbreviated. Ideal length for this technical result.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Follows instructions: uses '## Proof' heading, provides complete proof, uses defined quantities correctly. Minor variance: uses 'Gronwall' spelling (vs 'Grönwall' in A), but this is trivial. The response is complete and honest. Checked: [X] ## Proof heading, [X] uses defined quantities (Rényi, Langevin, LSI), [X] complete proof provided. One minor issue: uses 'equation*' environment which appears to be LaTeX formatting artifact in the text, but this is a formatting detail, not a content issue."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All claims verified: (1) ∂_t f_t = (1/ν)∇·(ν∇f_t) — VERIFIED from Fokker-Planck. (2) Generator L = Δ + ∇log ν · ∇ — VERIFIED (this is the Langevin generator). (3) dF/dt = -q(q-1)G — VERIFIED via integration by parts/self-adjointness. (4) LSI applied to h = f_t^{q/2} — VERIFIED; this is a standard technique. The entropy calculation: Ent_ν(h²) = ∫f^q log(f^q/F)dν = q∫f^q log f dν - F log F — VERIFIED. (5) Convexity via Hölder: log-convexity of s ↦ ∫f^s dν — VERIFIED (this is a standard result). (6) φ'(q) ≥ (φ(q)-φ(1))/(q-1) = R — VERIFIED. (7) Final inequality: qA/F - log F ≥ R — VERIFIED via substitution. All steps correct."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Evolution equation for f_t. [CORRECT] Self-adjoint generator identification. [CORRECT] Computation of dF/dt using generator. [CORRECT] LSI application with h = f^{q/2} (standard trick). [CORRECT] Entropy calculation. [CORRECT] Log-convexity via Hölder. [CORRECT] Convexity inequality for φ. [CORRECT] Final bound derivation. The proof is correct. One subtle point: Response B uses 'By Hölder, s ↦ ∫f^s dν is log-convex' — this is correct (Hölder implies log-convexity of L^p norms), though Response A's direct variance computation is also valid. Both approaches are correct."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Clear, professional mathematical writing. Good flow from generator identification to entropy calculation to convexity argument. Minor issue: 'equation*' appears as raw text in the output (likely a rendering artifact), which slightly breaks readability. The notation is consistent. The explanation of why φ'(q) ≥ R is slightly more explicit than A (showing the secant slope), which some readers may prefer. Otherwise: [X] Clear structure, [X] Appropriate tone, [X] Good mathematical exposition."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Well-calibrated length. Nothing can be cut: generator explanation adds clarity, entropy calculation is necessary, convexity argument is essential. Nothing is missing: all steps present. The proof is concise but complete. Ideal length for this result.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': 'Both proofs are mathematically correct and follow essentially identical structures: computing dF/dt via Fokker-Planck, applying LSI to bound G_{q,ν}, using convexity of φ(s)=log∫f^s dν to relate A/F to R, and concluding via Grönwall. Response A has a minor error (claims F(1)=1 when it should be F(t)=∫w_t^q dν with ∫w_t dν=1, not F(1)=1), while Response B uses cleaner notation (f_t vs w_t) and explicitly identifies the generator L. Both correctly derive the key inequality dR/dt ≤ -(2α/q)R. Neither has significant advantages in rigor or completeness.'}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in mathematical rigor and clarity. The SxS winner correctly computes dF/dt = -q(q-1)G_{q,ν}(ρ_t) and derives dR/dt = -q·G/F, then applies the given LSI form directly to obtain the clean differential inequality. In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro contains several critical errors: (1) in Step 4, it incorrectly computes dF/dt = -4(q-1)/q ∫|∇g|²dν when the correct coefficient should involve q(q-1)·4/q² = 4(q-1)/q, but then in Step 5 it mysteriously gets -4/(qF) instead of the correct -q(q-1)/((q-1)F) · 4(q-1)/q = -4(q-1)/(qF); (2) more seriously, in Step 6, Gemini misapplies LSI with reversed inequality direction (it states ∫|∇g|² ≥ (α/2)∫g²log(g²/∫g²) when LSI gives the opposite ≤ direction for the entropy bound); (3) the convexity argument in Step 7 is unnecessarily convoluted and contains a sign error handling the q<1 case that doesn't apply here since q>1 is assumed. The SxS winner's elegant use of φ(s) = log∫w^s dν with φ''(s) = Var_{μ_s}(log w) ≥ 0 yielding φ(q) ≤ (q-1)φ'(q) is mathematically cleaner and directly gives A/F ≥ R without the circuitous KL detour. Both complete the proof, but Gemini's path contains demonstrable technical errors that would require correction in a rigorous setting."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Instruction Following and Truthfulness, while GPT 5.2 introduces subtle errors and unnecessary complexity. The SxS winner correctly uses the setup's definition of $G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$ and the specific LSI form provided in the problem statement, maintaining consistency with the given $F_{q,\\nu}$ and $G_{q,\\nu}$ quantities throughout. GPT 5.2 deviates by reformulating the LSI in terms of $\\mathrm{Ent}_\\nu(g^2)$ and introducing an escort measure KL divergence comparison, which—while mathematically related—obscures the direct connection to the problem's stated LSI form and introduces a convoluted path to the final inequality. More critically, GPT 5.2 contains a technical error in Step 0 where it defines $\\rho_t := \\frac{d\\mu_t}{d\\nu}$ but then writes $\\partial_t \\rho_t = L\\rho_t$ with $L = \\Delta - \\nabla V \\cdot \\nabla$; this is incorrect because the Fokker-Planck operator for the density ratio $w = \\rho/\\nu$ with respect to $\\nu$ is actually $L = \\Delta - \\nabla \\log \\nu \\cdot \\nabla$, not involving $V$ directly in this form. The SxS winner correctly derives $\\partial_t w_t = \\frac{1}{\\nu}\\nabla\\cdot(\\nu\\nabla w_t)$ from the given Fokker-Planck equation. Additionally, GPT 5.2's claim that $\\phi(1) = \\log\\int\\rho_t\\,d\\nu = \\log 1 = 0$ is correct, but its earlier definition $M_t = \\int\\rho_t^q\\,d\\nu$ with $\\rho_t = d\\mu_t/d\\nu$ means $\\int\\rho_t\\,d\\nu = \\int d\\mu_t = 1$ only if $\\nu$ is a probability measure, which is true but the SxS winner's cleaner notation $w_t = \\rho_t/\\nu$ makes the normalization $\\mathbb{E}_\\nu[w_t] = 1$ more transparent. GPT 5.2's verbosity, while providing pedagogical value, includes redundant justifications and a lengthy 'limit $q\\to 1$' section not requested, plus an unnecessary 'failure analysis' section that goes beyond the proof requirements."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit verification section for limiting case', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated section titled 'Verification for $q \\to 1$' that explicitly checks the consistency of the derived decay rate with the known KL divergence result.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit discussion of assumption usage', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro contains a section titled 'Implicit Use of Full Support and Smoothness' that explicitly identifies where technical assumptions are critically used in the proof, specifically noting the chain rule application in Step 3.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Clear strategy outline before proof', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro provides a structured 'Strategy Outline' section before the formal proof that previews the proof methodology including the change of variables and LSI application.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Numbered step-by-step proof structure', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro organizes the proof into explicitly numbered steps (Step 1 through Step 8) with clear step labels, while the SxS winner uses unnumbered sections with dashes.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit handling of q<1 case in convexity argument', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly considers both cases (q>1 and q<1) in the convexity inequality derivation in Step 7, noting the inequality reversal, while the SxS winner only handles q>1.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Clean LaTeX formatting with proper display math delimiters', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent $$...$$ display math delimiters with proper spacing, while the SxS winner mixes \\[...\\] with inline formatting and has occasional spacing issues like 'F(1)=1' without spaces.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: how well this aspect was performed in the comparison', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit justification labels for proof steps', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes explicit parenthetical justification labels (e.g., "Justification (differentiate under the integral + chain rule)", "Justification (integration by parts with the symmetric generator)") before each major manipulation, while the SxS winner presents derivations without such explicit methodological annotations.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = occasional labels, 3-4 = systematic labeling of key steps, 5-6 = comprehensive justification labels for virtually every manipulation', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Introduction of normalized function for LSI application', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs the normalized function g_t = ρ_t^{q/2}/√M_t with verification that ∫g_t² dν = 1 before applying LSI, while the SxS winner applies LSI directly to unnormalized quantities without this intermediate normalization step.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions normalization briefly, 3-4 = constructs normalized function with partial verification, 5-6 = fully explicit construction with verification and clear connection to LSI', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Escort measure definition and notation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly defines the escort measure dν_{t,q} := (ρ_t^q/M_t)dν with dedicated notation and uses this to interpret Ent_ν(g_t²) as KL(ν_{t,q}||ν), while the SxS winner works with the equivalent expressions without introducing this conceptual framework or dedicated notation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions escort concept briefly, 3-4