2026-03-25 08:45:39,122 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_6650', 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nThroughout, we represent a probability distribution $\\rho$ on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ via its probability density function with respect to the Lebesgue measure. We typically assume $\\rho$ has full support and is a smooth density. Let $\\nu$ be a target probability distribution.\n\n**R'enyi Divergence**\n\nFor $q > 1$, the R'enyi divergence of order $q$ between $\\rho$ and $\\nu$ is defined as:\n$$R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\frac{1}{q-1} \\log F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$$\nwhere\n$$F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^n} \\frac{\\rho(x)^q}{\\nu(x)^{q-1}} dx = \\mathbb{E}_{\\nu}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\rho(x)}{\\nu(x)}\\right)^q\\right]$$\n\nWe also define the quantity $G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)$ for $q>1$ as:\n$$G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) = \\mathbb{E}_\\nu\\left[ \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right)^{q-2} \\left\\|\\nabla \\frac{\\rho}{\\nu} \\right\\|^2 \\right]$$\n\n**Langevin Dynamics**\n\nThe evolution of the probability density $\\rho_t$ along the Langevin dynamics is described by the Fokker-Planck equation:\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\rho_t}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot \\left(\\rho_t \\nabla \\log \\frac{\\rho_t}{\\nu} \\right)$$\n\n**Log-Sobolev Inequality (LSI)**\n\nA probability distribution $\\nu$ is said to satisfy the Log-Sobolev Inequality (LSI) with constant $\\alpha > 0$ if for any probability density $\\rho$ and for $q \\ge 1$, the following inequality holds:\n$$\n\\frac{q^2}{2\\alpha} G_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) \\ge q \\mathbb{E}_\\nu\\left[ \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right)^q \\log \\left(\\frac{\\rho}{\\nu}\\right) \\right] - F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho) \\log F_{q,\\nu}(\\rho)\n$$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 2**\n\nSuppose $\\nu$ satisfies LSI with constant $\\alpha > 0$. Let $q > 1$. Along the Langevin dynamics, for all $t \\ge 0$,\n$$R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho_t) \\le e^{-\\frac{2\\alpha}{q}t} R_{q,\\nu}(\\rho_0)$$", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All instructions satisfied: [X] Complete and correct proof provided under ## Proof heading (actually labeled '2. Proof' with clear structure). [X] Multiple approaches considered - the strategy paragraph explains the approach before execution. [X] Cleaned up proof with numbered steps. [X] Honest disclosure of where assumptions are used (Section 4). [X] Consistency check at q→1 provided (Section 3). The response goes beyond minimum requirements by including helpful sections on strategy, consistency check, and assumption usage. No unrequested content; format is appropriate for mathematical proof."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] The computation of Ṁ in (2.3) using integration by parts is correct. [VERIFIED] The substitution h_t^{q-2}|∇h_t|² = (4/q²)|∇f_t|² with f_t = h_t^{q/2} is correct algebra. [VERIFIED] The LSI application in (2.7) matches the standard form Ent_ν(g²) ≤ (2/α)∫|∇g|²dν. [VERIFIED] The entropy computation in (2.8) yielding (q-1)R_q is correct. [VERIFIED] The final differential inequality Ṙ_q ≤ -(2α/q)R_q follows correctly. One minor issue: The statement 'the boundary term vanishes because ρ_t has full support and is smooth' is slightly imprecise - full support alone doesn't guarantee decay at infinity, though this is standard in such proofs. No false claims detected."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All requirements [CORRECT]: [CORRECT] Theorem statement correctly reproduced and proven. [CORRECT] Uses the provided LSI definition with constant α. [CORRECT] Uses the provided Fokker-Planck equation for Langevin dynamics. [CORRECT] Defines Rényi divergence exactly as given. [CORRECT] Uses G_{q,ν}(ρ) implicitly through the Dirichlet form. [CORRECT] Establishes exponential decay with rate 2α/q. [CORRECT] The proof correctly handles the q>1 case. The response correctly interprets all mathematical objects from the prompt and derives the claimed inequality without error.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with only minor issues. Strengths: Clear section structure (Strategy, Proof, Consistency check, Assumptions), numbered equations with tags, logical flow between steps. Minor issue identified: Some sentences are slightly dense (e.g., 'The derivative becomes a Dirichlet form of the normalized density...'), but this is standard for advanced mathematical writing. The tone is appropriately formal. No grammar errors detected. One trivial preference: 'Grönwall' vs 'Gronwall' spelling is acceptable. Overall publication-ready with one minor stylistic density issue."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut because: Each section serves distinct purpose - strategy provides intuition, proof provides rigor, consistency check verifies correctness, assumption section addresses prompt's full support condition. The proof steps (2.1)-(2.9) are all necessary for logical completeness. Nothing is missing because: All key algebraic steps are shown, the LSI application is explicit, the final Grönwall step is included. Minor trimming possible: The strategy paragraph slightly overlaps with proof content, but this is intentional for reader guidance. Length is appropriate for a research-level mathematical proof."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All core instructions satisfied: [X] Complete proof under appropriate heading. [X] Multiple approaches considered in outline. [X] Cleaned up step-by-step proof provided. [X] Honest disclosure of assumption usage. [X] Verification at q→1 included. Minor issue: The outline paragraph contains a claim about 'convexity of t↦log∫(ρ_t/ν)^t dν' that is not actually used in the proof - the proof uses a direct computation (step 5) rather than convexity. This is unrequested content (the convexity argument) that doesn't appear in the final proof, suggesting a slight deviation between outline and execution. No other significant omissions."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Major claims verified but with one significant issue: [VERIFIED] Steps 1-3 for computing Ṁ_q and Ṙ_{q,ν} are correct. [VERIFIED] LSI application in step 4 is correct. [VERIFIED] Final differential inequality and Grönwall application correct. [QUESTIONABLE] Step 5 claim: 'A direct computation shows Ent_ν((ρ_t/ν)^q) ≥ M_q R_{q,ν}(ρ_t)'. This inequality is correct (can be verified by expanding definitions), but the proof provided is incomplete. The response mentions convexity of φ(s) = log∫(ρ_t/ν)^s dν and claims φ'(q) ≥ (φ(q)-φ(1))/(q-1) = R_{q,ν}, but this is the wrong direction for the inequality needed. Actually φ'(q) = (1/M_q)∫(ρ_t/ν)^q log(ρ_t/ν)dν, and the entropy is q∫(ρ_t/ν)^q log(ρ_t/ν)dν - M_q log M_q. The inequality Ent_ν ≥ M_q R_{q,ν} requires showing qφ'(q) - φ(q) ≥ φ(q)/(q-1) - φ(1)/(q-1), which needs more careful analysis. The 'direct computation' is not shown, and the convexity argument is misleadingly applied. This caps score at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Requirements mostly correct with gap: [CORRECT] Theorem statement and proof structure. [CORRECT] Fokker-Planck equation usage. [CORRECT] LSI application. [CORRECT] Final exponential decay. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] The key inequality Ent_ν((ρ_t/ν)^q) ≥ M_q R_{q,ν}(ρ_t) in step 5. The response claims this follows from 'direct computation' and mentions convexity, but doesn't actually provide the computation. The inequality is true (equivalent to the LSI form given in the prompt), but the proof omits the verification. The prompt's LSI is: (q²/2α)G_{q,ν}(ρ) ≥ qE[(ρ/ν)^q log(ρ/ν)] - F_{q,ν} log F_{q,ν}. The right side is exactly Ent_ν((ρ/ν)^q) when properly normalized. The response should have connected to G_{q,ν} explicitly but doesn't, using only the standard LSI form. This is a correct but less direct approach."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional quality with minor issues. Strengths: Clear outline, numbered steps, appropriate mathematical notation. Minor issues identified: (1) Step 2 has a slightly awkward phrase 'A short algebraic simplification gives' without showing the simplification, which is a minor clarity gap. (2) The outline mentions convexity which isn't used in the proof, creating a slight disconnect. (3) Step 5's 'A direct computation shows' without showing the computation is a minor weakness. Otherwise well-structured and readable."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut because: Each step is necessary for the logical flow. The outline provides intuition, each proof step builds on previous. The verification and assumption sections address prompt requirements. Nothing is missing because: Core proof is complete, though step 5's 'direct computation' could have been explicit. The response is appropriately detailed for the mathematical level. Minor note: The convexity mention in outline is unused, suggesting slight over-planning, but this doesn't significantly affect length."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.55, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.55'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better due to cleaner notation and more direct derivation. Response A uses consistent notation (h_t = ρ_t/ν, f_t = h_t^{q/2}, u_t normalized) that makes the LSI application transparent, while Response B's step 5 introduces unnecessary convexity argument with φ(s) that obscures the direct computation. Response A's equation (2.8) directly shows Ent_ν(u_t²) = (q-1)R_q without invoking convexity, which is more elegant and rigorous. Both correctly identify where full support/smoothness are used, but Response A's integration by parts in (2.3) is more explicit about the measure (dν vs dx/ν)."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner (internal) demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and clarity compared to Gemini 3 Pro. The SxS winner correctly identifies that the LSI can be applied directly to the normalized function u_t, yielding a clean derivation where the relative entropy of u_t^2 equals (q-1)R_q (equations 2.7-2.9), avoiding unnecessary complexity. In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro introduces a convoluted convexity argument in Step 7 that contains critical errors: it incorrectly derives h(q) ≤ (q-1)KL(μ_q||ν) when in fact R_q = h(q)/(q-1) ≥ KL(μ_q||ν) for q>1 (the inequality direction is wrong), and then attempts to salvage this with a confusing case analysis that misapplies the sign reversal. Gemini 3 Pro also contains formatting artifacts ('Step2', 'Step 4: Changeof Variables') and imprecise language ('convexity-based inequality that lower-bounds this relative entropy by the Rényi divergence itself' is backwards). The SxS winner's consistency check in Section 3 is more elegant than Gemini's verification, and its Section 4 provides a clearer explanation of why full support matters for the integration by parts. Both correctly obtain the final result, but Gemini's path contains significant mathematical detours and logical gaps that the SxS winner avoids entirely."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide correct proofs, but the SxS winner (internal) achieves tighter concision without sacrificing rigor. The SxS winner directly establishes the key inequality (2.8) that Ent_ν(u_t²) = (q-1)R_{q,ν}(ρ_t) through clean algebraic manipulation, avoiding the detour through escort measures and convexity arguments that GPT 5.2 employs. GPT 5.2's Step 4 introduces unnecessary machinery: it defines φ(q) as log-moment-generating function, proves convexity, computes φ'(q), and derives KL(ν_{t,q}||ν) ≥ R_{q,ν}(ρ_t) via secant slope comparison—when this inequality is actually immediate from the SxS winner's direct computation in (2.8). GPT 5.2's verbosity (longer by ~40%) adds cognitive load without mathematical benefit; its 'escort measure' framing, while standard in information geometry literature, is extraneous for this proof. Both correctly identify the q→1 limit and full support requirements, though GPT 5.2's discussion of '∞·0 indeterminate expression' in Step 4 is slightly overwrought compared to the SxS winner's precise boundary-term analysis. The SxS winner's (2.3)→(2.4) transition using f_t = h_t^{q/2} is more elegant than GPT 5.2's equivalent (1)→(2). Instruction following is equivalent; truthfulness and prompt correctness are equivalent; writing style favors the SxS winner for mathematical elegance."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 37237, 'output_tokens': 4639, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.130142}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.130142}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 37237, 'output_tokens': 4639, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.130142}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.130142}}] 
