ysis in Section 2 is heuristic rather than derived, and the phase condition 'c₀² = -2c₁c₋₁' appears without justification. The SxS winner's symmetry argument in Section 3 correctly identifies the p-wave representation, while Gemini 3 Pro's topological reasoning conflates angular momentum states with spatial dimensions. Both address sample size independence, but SxS winner's argument about exponentially small boundary effects is more physically precise than Gemini 3 Pro's 'hyper-localized' claim."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide correct proofs with the SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) being more concise and pedagogically structured, while GPT 5.2 is more verbose with redundant sections. Gemini 3 Pro establishes the key identity D² = -∇² + A² - 2μ·L/r³ compactly in equation (3) and efficiently derives the threefold degeneracy through angular momentum coupling. GPT 5.2 repeats similar derivations in sections 2.1-2.2 with excessive wordiness ('This identity is the clean reason...'), then adds redundant section 6 restating the symmetry argument already covered in section 3. Both correctly compute the Landau coefficients (K>L for Gemini, a=3b for GPT) and prove 3-component stability via Hessian analysis, but GPT 5.2's explicit calculation of angular integrals (a=9/20π, b=3/20π) versus Gemini's abstract K,L notation offers no additional insight. GPT 5.2's section 8 on sample-size independence largely duplicates Gemini's section 5. The SxS winner's 'Verification and common mistake' section is more focused than GPT 5.2's equivalent. Overall, GPT 5.2's ~2.5x length reflects elaboration without substantive additional rigor, making Gemini 3 Pro's tighter exposition preferable for expert readers, though both proofs are mathematically sound."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit Hessian eigenvalue calculation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly calculates a specific Hessian eigenvalue (A_0 - A_1 = μ - γμ²) to demonst