 extension, then $R$ is a direct summand of $S$ as an $R$-module.", 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading '## Proof'. It uses all three given theorems (Kunz's, Krull's, Nakayama's) as required. Minor formatting issues: uses \\medskip\\noindent which is LaTeX formatting not explicitly requested, and uses non-standard notation 'Frob I' instead of $I^{[p^n]}$ or $F^n(I)$ as might be expected. The proof structure is clean and complete. The response does not explicitly state 'If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so' — but this is moot since the proof is completed. Score capped at 5 due to minor notational choices and formatting not matching standard mathematical conventions exactly."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The mathematical claims appear correct and verifiable. Key claims: (1) $I$ is an ideal and $R \\subset S$ splits iff $1 \\in I$ — [VERIFIED], standard fact. (2) $S$ torsion-free over domain $R$ embeds in fraction field — [VERIFIED]. (3) Existence of $d \\neq 0$ with $d\\Tr_{L/K}(S) \\subseteq R$ — [VERIFIED], standard trace argument. (4) The claim $I = (\\text{Frob } I)R$ — this is the critical technical step; the proof provided uses the basis extension and projection construction which is valid. (5) Iteration gives $I \\subseteq (\\text{Frob}^n I)R \\subseteq \\mathfrak{n}_n R$ — [VERIFIED] by construction. (6) Krull's theorem gives $\\bigcap_n \\mathfrak{n}_n R = 0$ — [VERIFIED], matches given theorem. The proof is technically sound. One minor issue: the notation $\\mathfrak{n}_n = F^n \\mathfrak{m}$ is used but the prompt says $F^n \\mathfrak{m}$ is the maximal ideal of $F^n R$, which is correct. No false claims detected. Score 5 due to complete verifiability of all major claims."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof correctly establishes that $R$ is a direct summand of $