on, establishing the key claim I=(Frob I)R through explicit basis manipulation and Nakayama's Lemma, and concluding via Krull's theorem. The SxS winner correctly handles the Frobenius pullback implicitly through the claim I=(Frob I)R without unnecessary matrix formalism. Gemini 3 Pro introduces extraneous complexity with matrix presentations and Frobenius pullback functors that obscure the core argument; contains critical errors including the false claim that '1 generates the fraction field of S over K' (Step 1), confusing notation (m^[p^n] for m^(p^n)), and an incorrect statement that 'F^n R ⊗_{F^n R} S simply evaluates to S' when the intended meaning is R ⊗_{F^n R} S. The SxS winner's proof is more faithful to the classical Hochster-Roberts argument structure, while Gemini 3 Pro's verification section for non-domain S, though present, cannot compensate for the convoluted main proof with its technical inaccuracies."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response provides a more elegant and mathematically rigorous proof with superior Instruction Following and Truthfulness. The SxS winner correctly identifies the core strategy: defining the ideal of traces I, proving I≠0 via field trace, then using the key claim that I=(Frob I)R to derive a contradiction via Krull's theorem when 1∉I. This is the standard approach found in Hochster's original work. In contrast, GPT 5.2's response contains significant errors: Step 1 incorrectly constructs φ using a coordinate projection that need not land in R (τ(S)⊂K but not necessarily in R), and more critically, Step 5's construction of λ contains a fatal flaw—the map g is not well-defined as an R-linear map because F_*^nS is not naturally an R-module structure that makes g R-linear (the R-action on F_*^nS is via r·F_*^ns = F_*^n(r^{1/p^n}s), requiring p^n-th roots). GPT 5.2's 'verification' section also fails to address that the Frobenius pushforward functor F_*^n is not R-linear in the way claimed, making the entire Step 5 construction invalid. The SxS winner avoids these pitfalls by working directly with Hom modules and never incorrectly manipulating Frobenius pushforwards."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit Frobenius pullback construction', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly constructs the Frobenius pullback functor $F^{n*} = R \\otimes_{F^n R} -$ and applies it to the module presentation, while the SxS winner omits this concrete categorical framework.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of pullback, 3-4 = partial construction with some details, 5-6 = complete explicit construction with exact sequence preservation', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Matrix presentation of module structure', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides an explicit finite presentation $R^a \\xrightarrow{A} R^m \\xrightarrow{C} S \\to 0$ with matrix $A$ and explains how $R$-linear maps correspond to kernel vectors, while the SxS winner uses only abstract module language.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = abstract description, 3-4 = partial matrix notation without full development, 5-6 = complete matrix presentation with explicit correspondence to maps', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Verification section for non-domain case', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated 'Verification when $S$ is not assumed to be a domain' section that rigorously modifies Step 1 using flatness of $K$ and common denominator arguments, while the SxS winner assumes $S$ is a domain without addressing the general case.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief acknowledgment, 3-4 = partial argument, 5-6 = complete rigorous treatment with flatness and denominator arguments', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Explicit trace ideal element construction', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly constructs $c \\in I \\setminus \\mathfrak{m}^{[p^n]}$ as a concrete element with associated map $\\phi$, while the SxS winner uses minimal $n$ argument without explicit element selection.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 =