i 3 Pro uses proper LaTeX display environments with aligned equations and clear delimiters, while the SxS winner has some inline math that is harder to read and less structured equation displays.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor formatting difference, 3-4 noticeably better readability, 5-6 substantially superior presentation', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit parameter dependence tracking', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly tracks how all approximation parameters (δ, τ, α, η, M_δ) depend on each other and shows the limiting argument where δ→0 controls the final estimates, whereas the SxS winner omits explicit parameter choices and limiting arguments.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=parameters mentioned informally, 3=some explicit dependencies shown, 6=complete explicit tracking with limiting argument', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Bihari-Osgood stability estimate', 'description': "GPT 5.2 derives and applies the explicit Bihari inequality bound (9) with the power-type stability exponent e^{-Lτ}, while the SxS winner only invokes 'standard Osgood theory' without any explicit stability estimate for trajectories.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=Osgood condition mentioned, 3=qualitative stability mentioned, 6=explicit Bihari inequality with derived bound', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Mollification error quantification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly computes the mollification error bound (2) as ≲ Lδ log(1/δ) and the derivative bound (3) as ≲ L log(1/δ), while the SxS winner only asserts existence of a mollifier without quantitative error control.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=mollification mentioned, 3=qualitative error control, 6=explicit quantitative bounds with constants', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Characteristic flow construction with inverse function theorem', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs the characteristic flow map Y^δ, proves it is a C^1 diffeomorphism via the implicit function theorem, and derives explicit Lipschitz bounds (7) on the inverse, while the SxS winner jumps directly to claiming Φ is C^1 without explicit flow construction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=flow mentioned, 3=some construction details, 6=complete explicit construction with derivative bounds', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Partition of unity derivative control', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly introduces the partition of unity {ψ_j}, computes the bound B on derivative sums (10), and carefully tracks how the derivative terms cancel using ∑ψ_j' = 0 in equation (22), while the SxS winner has no partition of unity or derivative control mechanism.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=global approximation mentioned, 3=some localization, 6=explicit partition of unity with derivative cancellation', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Final parameter selection algorithm', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides an explicit algorithm in Step 10 for choosing parameters (fix τ, then α, then δ) to satisfy the inequalities (23)-(24), while the SxS winner has no explicit parameter selection or verification that ε-approximation is achievable.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=claim that approximation works, 3=informal parameter discussion, 6=explicit sequential algorithm with inequalities', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Tangential derivative operator identification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly identifies the tangential derivative operator X = ∂_x + F∂_y in (18) and uses it systematically to compute d/dx along leaves, while the SxS winner computes derivatives ad hoc without identifying this operator structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=derivatives computed, 3=some operator awareness, 6=explicit vector field identification and systematic use', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Grönwall estimate with explicit exponential bound', 'description': "GPT 5.2 applies Grönwall's inequality to obtain the explicit exponential bound e^{M_δ τ} in (7), while the SxS winner asserts u_a(x) > 0 without showing how the bound depends on the Lipschitz constant.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=positivity asserted, 3=qualitative bound, 6=exp