ters $$ for equations and aligned environments, while the SxS winner has formatting issues (missing backslashes before text in math mode, inconsistent spacing).', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Structural section headers', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses clear markdown headers (###) with descriptive titles for each major proof component, while the SxS winner uses less informative numbered sections.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit constraint on digit values', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly notes that digits cannot be 0 (as this would force product to 0, requiring all digits to be 0, impossible for a leading digit), which the SxS winner omits.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit necessary condition derivation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 derives a concrete necessary condition: if P(n) is true, then the interval (n, n+8log_2(9n)] must contain a 7-smooth number, with clear algebraic steps showing M = n + Δ and Δ ≤ 8k ≤ 8log_2(9n).', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = condition mentioned without derivation; 3-4 = derived with some gaps; 5-6 = rigorously derived with all intermediate steps explicit', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Smooth number density argument', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses the 7-smooth number counting function H(X) = O((log X)^4) to prove that P(n) can only hold for O((log N)^5) values up to N, establishing infinitude of counterexamples through growth rate comparison.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions smooth numbers vaguely; 3-4 = uses smooth numbers with incomplete counting; 5-6 = complete counting argument with explicit bound H(9N)·8log_2(9N)', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Arithmetic progression extraction', 'description': 'GP