out enumeration.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = partial enumeration, 3-4 = full enumeration with some detail, 5-6 = complete explicit enumeration of all 9 states with full expansions', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Concrete verification example', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes a fully worked numerical example verifying the protocol for a specific case (king measures A_1 with outcome k=2), computing explicit overlaps and confirming the decoding rule, while the SxS winner only asserts correctness without demonstration.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of verification, 3-4 = partial calculation shown, 5-6 = complete step-by-step verification with explicit state expansions and overlap calculations', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Necessity argument for 9 basis states', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 provides a rigorous counting argument proving that 9 measurement outcomes are necessary (not merely sufficient), using incidence counting and dimensional constraints, while the SxS winner does not address optimality or necessity.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief claim of optimality, 3-4 = informal argument, 5-6 = rigorous combinatorial proof with explicit inequality (4N ≥ 36)', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Explicit post-measurement state formulas', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly writes out all 12 post-measurement states |Ψ_{m,k}⟩ in equation (3), including the conjugation relations for each basis, while the SxS winner derives these implicitly without explicit listing.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = states mentioned indirectly, 3-4 = some states written explicitly, 5-6 = complete explicit enumeration of all 12 states with conjugation rules', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Decoding rule summary table', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 presents the decoding conditions in a clear tabular format (equation 6) showing for each m the condition on (u,v) that yields nonzero overlap, while the SxS winner embeds this information in running text and final piecewise formula.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = conditions 