2026-03-25 08:51:25,706 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: tasks1_response---------------------------[{'index': 0, 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nAn entangling gate between two trapped atomic ion qubits is implemented following the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol. The gate is driven by optical fields tuned near the red and blue sidebands of a vibrational mode. The two optical field pairs address the red ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,r}$) and blue ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,b}$) vibrational sidebands.\n\nThe gate is implemented in a specific "optical phase insensitive geometry" defined as follows:\n1.  The red sideband transition is driven by absorbing a photon from the $k_{A}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{B,r}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the red sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{r} = k_{A} - k_{B,r}$.\n2.  The blue sideband transition is driven by the opposite process: absorbing a photon from the $k_{B,b}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{A}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the blue sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{b} = k_{B,b} - k_{A}$.\n\nThe gate phase, $\\phi_{G}$, is given by the sum of the phases associated with the red and blue sideband transitions, $\\phi_{rsb}$ and $\\phi_{bsb}$, respectively:\n$$ \\phi_{G}=-(\\phi_{rsb}+\\phi_{bsb}) $$\nDrifts of the optical path length from the source to the ions, $\\delta x$, along the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beam paths change the optical phases of these fields at the ion position. This results in a phase shift $\\delta\\phi$ where $\\delta\\phi=k_{B,r}\\delta x \\approx k_{B,b}\\delta x$.\n## Theorem\nIn the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol implemented with the optical phase insensitive geometry, the gate phase $\\phi_G$ is approximately independent of common-mode optical path length fluctuations $\\delta x$ affecting the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beams.', 'status': 'ACCEPT', 'result': {'status': 'ACCEPT', 'rejection_reason': None, 'justification': 'The prompt provides a complete physics problem about quantum computing with trapped ion qubits. All necessary context, setup, and theorem are included in the prompt itself. This is a mathematical proof request with no missing reference text, no image handling, no safety concerns, and no other rejection criteria apply.'}, 'accepted': True, 'error': None, 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 963, 'output_tokens': 87, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.00297}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.00297}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 963, 'output_tokens': 87, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.00297}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.00297}}] 
