2026-03-25 08:52:55,710 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_11265', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nAn entangling gate between two trapped atomic ion qubits is implemented following the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol. The gate is driven by optical fields tuned near the red and blue sidebands of a vibrational mode. The two optical field pairs address the red ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,r}$) and blue ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,b}$) vibrational sidebands.\n\nThe gate is implemented in a specific "optical phase insensitive geometry" defined as follows:\n1.  The red sideband transition is driven by absorbing a photon from the $k_{A}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{B,r}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the red sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{r} = k_{A} - k_{B,r}$.\n2.  The blue sideband transition is driven by the opposite process: absorbing a photon from the $k_{B,b}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{A}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the blue sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{b} = k_{B,b} - k_{A}$.\n\nThe gate phase, $\\phi_{G}$, is given by the sum of the phases associated with the red and blue sideband transitions, $\\phi_{rsb}$ and $\\phi_{bsb}$, respectively:\n$$ \\phi_{G}=-(\\phi_{rsb}+\\phi_{bsb}) $$\nDrifts of the optical path length from the source to the ions, $\\delta x$, along the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beam paths change the optical phases of these fields at the ion position. This results in a phase shift $\\delta\\phi$ where $\\delta\\phi=k_{B,r}\\delta x \\approx k_{B,b}\\delta x$.\n## Theorem\nIn the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol implemented with the optical phase insensitive geometry, the gate phase $\\phi_G$ is approximately independent of common-mode optical path length fluctuations $\\delta x$ affecting the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beams.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instructions satisfied: (1) Provided complete and correct proof under ## Proof heading [X], (2) Used multiple approaches/strategy section before formal proof [X], (3) Honest about limitations (verification section discusses imperfect cancellation) [X], (4) Cleaned up final proof with clear structure [X]. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirements exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Raman process phase = absorbed - emitted phase [VERIFIED] Δk_r = k_A - k_{B,r} and Δk_b = k_{B,b} - k_A match setup [VERIFIED] φ_G = -(φ_rsb + φ_bsb) [VERIFIED] δφ = kδx propagation phase [VERIFIED] Cancellation of θ_A terms [VERIFIED] Residual sensitivity (k_{B,r}-k_{B,b})δx. One minor issue: 'k_{B,r}δx ≈ k_{B,b}δx in magnitude' - the setup says δφ = k_{B,r}δx ≈ k_{B,b}δx, which implies equality of the products, not just magnitude. This is technically accurate but slightly imprecise wording. No false claims found."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All requirements [CORRECT]: (1) Proves gate phase φ_G is approximately independent of common-mode δx [CORRECT] - explicitly shows δφ_G = (k_{B,r}-k_{B,b})δx ≈ 0 [CORRECT] (2) Uses correct optical phase insensitive geometry definitions [CORRECT] - properly tracks absorption/emission roles [CORRECT] (3) Accounts for both sidebands correctly [CORRECT] (4) Shows why cancellation occurs (θ_A cancellation + k_B common-mode cancellation) [CORRECT]. No incorrect or missing elements.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional quality with clear structure. Minor issue: 'the phase of the product of field amplitudes participating in absorption and stimulated emission' is slightly awkward phrasing - could be clearer. Otherwise excellent: logical flow from strategy to step-by-step proof to verification, proper LaTeX formatting, clear variable definitions. One minor style issue prevents 6."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Could trivially trim: the 'Verification' section repeats the residual sensitivity formula already derived in Step 3. The boxed final result is slightly redundant with the preceding sentence. Nothing significant missing; all key steps (phase definitions, cancellation mechanism, final result) present with appropriate detail. Strategy section adds value by previewing the geometric insight."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Instructions checklist: (1) Proof under ## Proof heading [X], (2) Multiple approaches/strategy section [ ] - MISSING: jumps directly into proof without strategic overview or 'if stuck' exploration, (3) Honest about limitations [X] - mentions residual shift, (4) Cleaned up proof [X]. Missing the 'try multiple approaches if stuck' and strategy preamble reduces score from 6. No unrequested content."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Raman phase φ_i - φ_j [VERIFIED] Sideband phase expressions with Δk·x terms [VERIFIED] φ_G = -(φ_rsb + φ_bsb) [VERIFIED] Δk_r + Δk_b = k_{B,b} - k_{B,r} [VERIFIED] Common-mode cancellation. One issue: claims 'the negligible term (k_{B,b}-k_{B,r})·x' - this is actually the residual sensitivity, not necessarily 'negligible' without justification. The relative frequency ratio claim |k_{B,b}-k_{B,r}|/|k| ~ (ω_bs-ω_rs)/ω_opt ≪ 1 is [UNVERIFIABLE] without specific numbers - while physically reasonable, stated as definite fact. Also 'to leading order it vanishes' is slightly imprecise - it's suppressed, not vanishing."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'s