2026-03-25 08:52:58,427 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_11265', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nAn entangling gate between two trapped atomic ion qubits is implemented following the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol. The gate is driven by optical fields tuned near the red and blue sidebands of a vibrational mode. The two optical field pairs address the red ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,r}$) and blue ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,b}$) vibrational sidebands.\n\nThe gate is implemented in a specific "optical phase insensitive geometry" defined as follows:\n1.  The red sideband transition is driven by absorbing a photon from the $k_{A}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{B,r}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the red sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{r} = k_{A} - k_{B,r}$.\n2.  The blue sideband transition is driven by the opposite process: absorbing a photon from the $k_{B,b}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{A}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the blue sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{b} = k_{B,b} - k_{A}$.\n\nThe gate phase, $\\phi_{G}$, is given by the sum of the phases associated with the red and blue sideband transitions, $\\phi_{rsb}$ and $\\phi_{bsb}$, respectively:\n$$ \\phi_{G}=-(\\phi_{rsb}+\\phi_{bsb}) $$\nDrifts of the optical path length from the source to the ions, $\\delta x$, along the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beam paths change the optical phases of these fields at the ion position. This results in a phase shift $\\delta\\phi$ where $\\delta\\phi=k_{B,r}\\delta x \\approx k_{B,b}\\delta x$.\n## Theorem\nIn the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol implemented with the optical phase insensitive geometry, the gate phase $\\phi_G$ is approximately independent of common-mode optical path length fluctuations $\\delta x$ affecting the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beams.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Strategy section provided before proof, (2) ## Proof heading used with cleaned-up proof, (3) Multiple approaches considered (strategy outlined, verification section included), (4) Honest assessment given (acknowledges approximation/ideal case). No unrequested content added. Format matches requirements exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Equation (3) derivation φ_G = φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b} is algebraically correct. [VERIFIED] Equation (6) showing (k_{B,r}-k_{B,b})δx dependence is correct. [VERIFIED] Claim that Δk_r + Δk_b = k_{B,b} - k_{B,r} is correct. [UNVERIFIABLE] The physical claim that 'the two B-beams are derived from the same laser comb tooth' in step 5—this is stated as given in setup but not explicitly verified as universal truth; however, this is consistent with standard Mølmer-Sørensen implementations. One minor issue: step 6 mentions 'motional contribution' cancellation but this is slightly conflated—the gate phase φ_G is purely optical, while motional phases affect the gate fidelity through different mechanisms. This conflation is minor but present. Score capped at 5 due to this unverified physical interpretation in step 6."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All requirements addressed correctly: [CORRECT] Proof that φ_G is independent of δx when k_{B,r}≈k_{B,b}. [CORRECT] Proper handling of sign conventions for absorption/emission. [CORRECT] Explicit use of given geometry with Δk_r and Δk_b. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] Step 6 adds discussion of 'motional contribution' which, while physically relevant to gate implementation, slightly conflates optical gate phase with motional phase—the prompt asks specifically about gate phase φ_G. This is a minor extension beyond strict prompt requirements. The core theorem is proven correctly with proper acknowledgment of approximation."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional scientific writing with clear structure. Minor issues: (1) 'The effective wave-vectors...are also opposite' in strategy is slightly imprecise—they sum to small value rather than being opposite; (2) Step 6's 'reinforcing the insensitivity' is slightly vague about mechanism. Otherwise excellent: equations numbered, logical flow, appropriate technical register. One trivial style preference: '∎' symbol not used (but not required)."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Strategy section efficiently sets up approach. Proof steps 1-6 are well-paced. Verification section appropriately shows imperfect cancellation. Minor trim possible: step 6's motional discussion could be shortened as it's supplementary to the core proof. Nothing essential missing; no significant padding."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.25, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.25'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Strategy provided before proof, (2) ## Proof heading used with formal proof, (3) Multiple approaches considered (strategy outlined, verification included), (4) Honest assessment given (acknowledges approximation). No unrequested content. Format matches requirements exactly. Uses ∎ symbol appropriately.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Equation (4) showing φ_{rsb}+φ_{bsb} = φ_{B,b}-φ_{B,r} is algebraically correct. [VERIFIED] Equation (5) showing (k_{B,b}-k_{B,r})δx dependence is correct. [VERIFIED] Sign conventions in (2) and (3) match setup. [UNVERIFIABLE] Equation (1) states general phase formula φ_i = φ_i^{(0)} + k_i x—this is standard but presented without explicit justification that x represents path length; however, this is consistent with physics convention. The response is slightly more careful than A in distinguishing that δφ_G = -(k_{B,b}-k_{B,r})δx (negative sign explicit), which is more precise. No false claims; all technical statements are accurate.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All requirements addressed correctly: [CORRECT] Proof that φ_G is approximately independent of δx. [CORRECT] Proper use of given geometry with explicit Δk_r, Δk_b references in strategy. [CORRECT] Clean derivation showing exact cancellation of φ_A and approximate cancellation of B-beam phases. [CORRECT] Explicit handling of 'common-mode' aspect. The response stays strictly focused on the optical gate phase φ_G without conflating with motional effects, maintaining tighter alignment with prompt scope than Response A."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Publication-ready quality. Exceptional clarity: strategy explicitly identifies 'geometric sign reversal' as key insight. Equation numbering is consistent. Tone is appropriately formal. No grammar issues. Structure is impeccable: strategy → numbered proof steps → verification. The explicit '∎' end marker is a nice professional touch. No issues found after thorough review."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Perfectly calibrated length. Strategy is concise but complete. Six proof steps cover all necessary logic without redundancy. Verification section is appropriately brief—just the residual formula and limiting case. Nothing can be cut: each step is essential (1: setup, 2-3: sideband phases, 4: sum, 5: fluctuation effect, 6: conclusion). Nothing is missing: all logical connections explicit, no gaps requiring reader inference.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.75'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both responses provide correct, complete proofs with identical mathematical structure. Response A adds a section on effective wave vectors and motional phase cancellation (step 6), which is physically relevant but not strictly required by the theorem statement. Response B's verification section has a sign error in the final equation (should be +(k_{B,r}-k_{B,b}) not -(k_{B,b}-k_{B,r}) to match its own Eq. 5), though this is inconsequential. Both are equivalently clear and rigorous; neither demonstrates meaningful superiority."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner demonstrates superior Instruction Following and Truthfulness through rigorous step-by-step derivation with explicit equation numbering (1)-(8) and clear logical flow from optical phases to final cancellation; it correctly identifies that the gate phase becomes φ_G = φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b} and rigorously tracks how the fluctuating term (k_{B,r} - k_{B,b})δx vanishes. Gemini 3 Pro contains critical errors: in Step 2 it incorrectly simplifies to φ_G = -(φ_{B,b} - φ_{B,r}) = φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b} with a sign error in the intermediate expression (should be -φ_{B,b} + φ_{B,r}), and more seriously uses imprecise language like 'variance of our formula' instead of 'variation' or 'differential', while its Verification section incorrectly states the difference 'approaches zero' then 'reduces strictly to zero'—contradicting the 'approximately independent' requirement in the theorem; the SxS winner correctly maintains 'approximately' throughout and explicitly derives the residual δφ_G = (k_{B,r} - k_{B,b})δx for imperfect cancellation."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': 'Both models produce correct, complete proofs with identical mathematical structure and physical reasoning. SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) and GPT 5.2 both derive that φ_G = φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b}, showing cancellation of the k_A phase and common-mode insensitivity when k_{B,r} ≈ k_{B,b}. Both explicitly compute δφ_G = (k_{B,r} - k_{B,b})δx and include verification sections discussing imperfect cancellation. Neither contains errors; both use proper sign conventions for absorption/emission, correctly interpret the geometric phase-insensitive condition, and reach the same boxed final expression. The responses are equivalent in instruction following, truthfulness, prompt correctness, and writing style—both are clear, rigorous, and appropriately verbose for a physics proof.'}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent LaTeX delimiters ($...$ for inline, $$...$$ for display) throughout the proof, while the SxS winner mixes LaTeX with raw text in some expressions.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor inconsistency, 3-4 noticeable improvement, 5-6 substantially better formatting', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit physical interpretation of phase signs', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly explains the physical reasoning for why absorption adds phase and emission subtracts phase in the Raman transitions, grounding the sign conventions in physical intuition.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 brief mention, 3-4 clear explanation, 5-6 thorough physical justification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Structured step numbering in proof', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses clearly numbered steps (1, 2, 3, 4) with descriptive titles for each major phase of the derivation, making the logical flow more navigable than the SxS winner's mixed heading styles.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor organization, 3-4 clear improvement, 5-6 substantially clearer structure', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit variance/derivative notation for phase shift', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly introduces the notation $\\delta\\phi_G$ and frames the calculation as taking the 'variance' (differential) of the gate phase formula, making the mathematical operation transparent.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 implicit handling, 3-4 explicit notation, 5-6 ri