2026-03-25 08:52:59,005 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_11265', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nAn entangling gate between two trapped atomic ion qubits is implemented following the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol. The gate is driven by optical fields tuned near the red and blue sidebands of a vibrational mode. The two optical field pairs address the red ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,r}$) and blue ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,b}$) vibrational sidebands.\n\nThe gate is implemented in a specific "optical phase insensitive geometry" defined as follows:\n1.  The red sideband transition is driven by absorbing a photon from the $k_{A}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{B,r}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the red sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{r} = k_{A} - k_{B,r}$.\n2.  The blue sideband transition is driven by the opposite process: absorbing a photon from the $k_{B,b}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{A}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the blue sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{b} = k_{B,b} - k_{A}$.\n\nThe gate phase, $\\phi_{G}$, is given by the sum of the phases associated with the red and blue sideband transitions, $\\phi_{rsb}$ and $\\phi_{bsb}$, respectively:\n$$ \\phi_{G}=-(\\phi_{rsb}+\\phi_{bsb}) $$\nDrifts of the optical path length from the source to the ions, $\\delta x$, along the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beam paths change the optical phases of these fields at the ion position. This results in a phase shift $\\delta\\phi$ where $\\delta\\phi=k_{B,r}\\delta x \\approx k_{B,b}\\delta x$.\n## Theorem\nIn the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol implemented with the optical phase insensitive geometry, the gate phase $\\phi_G$ is approximately independent of common-mode optical path length fluctuations $\\delta x$ affecting the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beams.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist verification: [X] Provides proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Addresses the specific setup with Mølmer-Sørensen protocol; [X] Uses the optical phase insensitive geometry definition; [X] Derives independence of gate phase from common-mode path fluctuations; [X] Includes honest assessment of residual error. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirement exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] The phase assignments φ_rsb = φ_A - φ_B,r and φ_bsb = φ_B,b - φ_A match the geometry definition; [VERIFIED] The cancellation φ_rsb' + φ_bsb' = φ_rsb + φ_bsb is mathematically correct; [VERIFIED] The residual error estimate ~(Δω/c)δx ≈ 10⁻⁷ rad for δx ~ μm is physically reasonable (for ω_m ~ 2π×1-10 MHz, Δω = 2ω_m ~ 10 MHz, c ~ 3×10⁸ m/s, giving ~3×10⁻⁸ m⁻¹ × 10⁻⁶ m ~ 10⁻⁷). One minor issue: the claim 'spatial term is negligible' assumes specific geometry without explicit justification, but this is standard in MS gate treatments. No false claims found."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Red sideband phase derivation matches geometry (absorb k_A, emit k_B,r); [CORRECT] Blue sideband phase derivation matches geometry (absorb k_B,b, emit k_A); [CORRECT] Gate phase formula φ_G = -(φ_rsb + φ_bsb) applied correctly; [CORRECT] Common-mode drift cancellation demonstrated explicitly; [CORRECT] Residual error from k_B,b ≠ k_B,r included. All requirements of the theorem are satisfied with correct physics.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Structure is clear with labeled sections (i)-(iii). Mathematical notation is consistent and professional. One minor issue: the phrase 'well below experimental error' is slightly informal for a formal proof, and the estimate '10⁻⁷ rad' lacks explicit parameter values used. Otherwise excellent: equations well-aligned, logical flow from setup to conclusion, appropriate technical register for physics audience."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Length analysis: Nothing can be cut because each section serves distinct purpose—(i) establishes phase conventions, (ii) derives base result, (iii) proves insensitivity. The residual error estimate adds value. Nothing is missing because the proof covers geometry definition, phase assignment, drift analysis, and error estimation. Could be slightly more explicit about why k_B,b ≈ k_B,r (only stated as 'differ only by 2ω_m'), but this is minor."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist verification: [X] Proof under ## Proof heading; [X] Uses specified geometry; [X] Derives gate phase insensitivity; [X] Addresses common-mode fluctuations. The response adds a boxed conclusion which, while not explicitly requested, serves as clean summary without being unrequested content. All core instructions satisfied.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] Phase assignments match geometry; [VERIFIED] Cancellation of δφ terms in φ_G is correct; [VERIFIED] Residual shift Δφ_G = -(k_B,b - k_B,r)δx is correct; [VERIFIED] Suppression factor estimate |k_B,b - k_B,r|/|k| ~ (ω_bs - ω_rs)/ω_opt ≪ 1 is physically correct. One minor issue: the statement 'to leading order it vanishes' is slightly vague—'leading order in what?'—but standard in physics context. No false claims."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] All phase derivations match geometry; [CORRECT] Gate phase construction correct; [CORRECT] Drift transformation of φ_B,r and φ_B,b correct; [CORRECT] Final cancellation demonstrated; [CORRECT] Residual error identified. The proof is complete and physically accurate. The boxed final statement is a correct summary.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Clear logical structure with explicit phase definitions. Mathematical presentation is clean with aligned equations. One minor issue: the phrase 'to leading order it vanishes' could be more precise (specify expansion parameter). The use of '≈0' in the final box is slightly imprecise notation—should be '~0' or '≈ 0' with understanding it's small. Otherwise professional and clear."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Nothing can be cut—each step from phase definition to drift analysis to conclusion is necessary. Nothing is missing—the proof is self-contained. The boxed conclusion adds clarity without excess. Slightly more compact than Response A but equally complete. The explicit statement of Δk_r + Δk_b = k_B,b - k_B,r is valuable and not redundant.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 0, 'overall_comment': "Both proofs are correct and complete, reaching the same conclusion through essentially identical reasoning. Both correctly identify that the gate phase depends only on the relative phase difference between the two B beams (φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b}), which causes common-mode fluctuations to cancel. Both include the residual term (k_{B,b}-k_{B,r})δx and note its suppression. Response A uses slightly more formal Hamiltonian notation with Rabi frequencies, while Response B uses cleaner phase arithmetic; neither approach is superior. The boxed final statement in B is a minor presentational nicety but doesn't affect correctness."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner demonstrates superior Instruction Following and Truthfulness by explicitly deriving the phase cancellation mechanism from first principles with proper Hamiltonian terms, correctly identifying that φ_G = -(φ_rsb + φ_bsb) leads to cancellation because δφ enters with opposite signs. Gemini 3 Pro commits a critical error in Step 4 by incorrectly stating 'taking the variance of our formula' when it should be substituting perturbed phases into the gate phase definition; more seriously, Gemini 3 Pro writes δφ_G = δφ_{B,r} - δφ_{B,b} which inverts the sign structure—the correct derivation shows φ_G' = (φ_{B,r}+δφ) - (φ_{B,b}+δφ) = φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b} = φ_G, not a difference of perturbations. SxS winner correctly shows φ_rsb' = φ_rsb - δφ and φ_bsb' = φ_bsb + δφ, making the sum unchanged. Both models reach the correct conclusion but Gemini 3 Pro's derivation contains logical gaps and sign errors that would confuse a careful reader, while SxS winner's explicit Raman Hamiltonian terms and careful tracking of phase contributions provide verifiable correctness. SxS winner also quantifies the residual error ~10^-7 rad, which Gemini 3 Pro omits."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide complete, correct proofs with equivalent physical reasoning. Gemini 3 Pro (SxS winner) structures the proof in three clear steps (i)-(iii), explicitly writes out the Raman Hamiltonian terms with spatial dependence, and provides a concrete numerical estimate of residual sensitivity (~10⁻⁷ rad for μm-scale fluctuations). GPT 5.2 (external model) uses a more narrative 'Strategy' preamble, defines optical phases θ_j explicitly, and presents the cancellation through cleaner algebraic steps with a boxed final result for imperfect cancellation. Both correctly identify the key mechanism: k_A phases cancel due to reversed absorption/emission roles, leaving only relative B-phase dependence, and both handle the common-mode approximation k_{B,r} ≈ k_{B,b} appropriately. Neither contains errors; both satisfy the prompt requirements for a complete proof under the ## Proof heading."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit verification section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated '## Verification' section at the end of the proof that explicitly derives the residual sensitivity formula and confirms the limiting case.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if section exists but is minimal, 3-4 if section clearly derives residual formula, 5-6 if section also confirms limiting case and provides boxed final result', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Strategic overview before formal proof', 'description': "GPT 5.2 provides a '**Strategy**' preamble before the formal proof that explains the geometric cancellation mechanism in conceptual terms.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if brief mention, 3-4 if clear conceptual preview, 5-6 if preview enables reader to follow the formal proof', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit absorption/emission sign convention', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly states and applies the rule that sideband phase equals 'absorbed field phase minus emitted field phase' as a definitional principle.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if mentioned in passing, 3-4 if clearly stated as rule, 5-6 if rule is explicitly applied to derive both sideband phases', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX boxed final result', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses \\boxed{} to highlight the key residual sensitivity formula in the verification section.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if present but minor, 3-4 if used for important result, 5-6 if boxing draws attention to the critical quantitative result', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Numbered step structure in proof', 'description': "GPT 5.2 organizes the formal proof into explicitly numbered steps ('Step 1', 'Step 2', 'Step 3') with clear descriptive labels.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if steps are loosely indicated, 3-4 if clearly numbered, 5-6 if numbering with labels aids logical progression tracking', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit cancellation of k_A phase', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly notes and explains that the k_A optical phase cancels exactly due to reversed absorption/emission roles.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if cancellation is implicit, 3-4 if mentioned, 5-6 if explicitly highlighted as key geometric feature', 'rating': 5}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 19991, 'output_tokens': 3369, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.08041}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.08041}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 19991, 'output_tokens': 3369, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.08041}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.08041}}] 
