2026-03-25 08:53:00,067 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_14826', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nWe introduce a set of functions, called Q-functions, which depend on a spectral parameter `u`. The Q-functions for the two copies of the $\\mathrm{#1}{psu}(1,1|2)$ algebra are distinguished by dotted and undotted indices.\n\n**Notation for Q-functions and shifts:**\nWe use a simplified notation where $Q$ functions analytic in the Upper Half Plane (UHPA) are denoted by $Q$, and functions analytic in the Lower Half Plane (LHPA) are denoted by $\\bar Q$. For any function $g(u)$, we denote shifts in the spectral parameter as $g^{\\pm}(u) \\equiv g(u \\pm \\frac{i}{2})$.\n\n**QQ-relations:**\nThe Q-functions satisfy a set of functional relations. A key one, for the LHPA functions, is the following finite difference equation:\n$$\n\\bar Q_{a|k}^+ - \\bar Q_{a|k}^- = P_a \\bar Q_k\n$$\nwhere $P_a$ and $\\bar Q_k$ are other Q-functions.\n\n**Hodge-dual functions:**\nFrom the Q-system, one can derive relations for Hodge-dual functions. A useful relation is:\n$$\nQ^l = Q^{a|l+} P_a\n$$\nwhere $Q^{a|l}$ is related to $Q_{a|l}$.\n\n**Analytic structure and continuation:**\nThe Q-functions are analytic functions of the spectral parameter $u$ with branch cuts. We are interested in the branch cut $(-2h, 2h)$ on the real axis.\nWe introduce analytic continuation paths $\\gamma$ and its inverse $\\gamma^{-1} \\equiv \\bar{\\gamma}$. The path $\\gamma$ crosses the branch cut $(-2h, +2h)$ from above, while $\\bar{\\gamma}$ crosses it from below. We denote the analytic continuation of any function $f(u)$ along these paths as $f^{\\gamma}$ or $f^{\\bar{\\gamma}}$. These operations are inverse to each other, so $(f^{\\gamma})^{\\bar{\\gamma}} = f$ and $(f^{\\bar{\\gamma}})^{\\gamma} = f$.\n\nWe assume the following analytic properties for the functions involved:\n-   $\\bar{Q}_{a|k}^-$ is analytic in the lower half-plane and is invariant under the analytic continuation along $\\bar\\gamma$, i.e., $(\\bar{Q}_{a|k}^-)^{\\bar\\gamma} = \\bar{Q}_{a|k}^-$.\n-   $Q^{a|l+}$ is analytic in the upper half-plane and thus has no discontinuity across the real axis cut when approached from above.\n\n**Gluing conditions and periodic matrices:**\nThe Q-functions from the two different Q-systems (dotted and undotted) are related by "gluing conditions" across the branch cut. There exist constant matrices $G$ that implement this gluing. One such relation is:\n$$\nQ_{\\dot k}^{\\gamma} = G_{\\dot k}^{ k} \\bar{Q}_{ k}\n$$\nThere exist $i$-periodic matrices $\\Omega$ which relate the UHPA ($Q$) and LHPA ($\\bar Q$) bases of solutions. We define the matrix $\\omega$ from $\\Omega$ and the gluing matrix $G$:\n$$\n\\omega_{\\dot k}^{l} = G_{\\dot k}^{ k} \\Omega_{ k}^{l}\n$$\nThe matrix $\\Omega_k^{l}$ can be expressed in terms of Q-functions. One such expression from Appendix A.2 of the paper is $\\Omega_k^{l} = \\bar{Q}_{a|k}^+ Q^{a|l+}$.\n## Theorem\nThe $i$-periodic matrix $\\omega_{\\dot k}^{l}$ has a branch cut on the real axis. Its discontinuity, obtained by analytic continuation along the path $\\bar{\\gamma}$, is given by the following relation:\n$$\n(\\omega_{\\dot k}^{l})^{\\bar\\gamma}-\\omega_{\\dot k}^{l} =\n{Q}_{\\dot k} Q^{l\\bar\\gamma} -\n{Q}^\\gamma_{\\dot k} Q^l.\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "MISSING: The prompt explicitly defines ω using G and Ω (ω_{dot k}^{l} = G_{dot k}^{k} Ω_{k}^{l}) and gives Ω_k^{l} = bar{Q}_{a|k}^+ Q^{a|l+}. Response A ignores this definition entirely and instead introduces a non-standard 'Wronskian-type' definition with a 1/2 factor in equation (5): ω_{dot k}{}^{l}(u) ≡ 1/2(Q^{a|l-} bar{Q}_{a|dot k}^{+} - Q^{a|l+} bar{Q}_{a|dot k}^{-}). This is a significant deviation from the prompt's setup. The prompt also specifies the QQ-relation for LHPA functions (bar{Q}_{a|k}^+ - bar{Q}_{a|k}^- = P_a bar{Q}_k), but Response A uses a different form with Q_{a|dot k} and P-functions. The prompt asks to prove a specific formula with bar{gamma} continuation, but Response A uses gamma continuation throughout and only mentions bar{gamma} in consistency checks. The final result is equivalent (up to sign conventions) but the derivation path violates explicit instructions. Checked: [X] provides proof under ## Proof heading; [X] attempts multiple approaches; [ ] uses given definition of ω (FAIL - uses different definition); [ ] uses bar{gamma} as specified (FAIL - uses gamma); [X] arrives at correct formula structure. Score capped at 4 due to missing required elements."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "QUESTIONABLE claims: (1) Response A asserts 'Q^{a|l+} - Q^{a|l-} = -P^a Q^l' in equation (2) without derivation or citation. This is not given in the prompt and cannot be verified. (2) The claim 'Q^l = Q^{a|l-} P_a' in equation (11) contradicts the prompt's given relation 'Q^l = Q^{a|l+} P_a'. These are inconsistent (shift mismatch). (3) The definition of ω in equation (5) is presented as standard but is non-standard and not justified. (4) The claim that P-functions 'have no long cut' is stated as fact without verification. These unverifiable technical claims, especially the sign/shift inconsistencies with the prompt's given formulas, warrant a score of 3. The mathematics is internally consistent but contains claims that cannot be verified against standard references and contradicts explicit prompt formulas."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "INCORRECT: The prompt explicitly states the theorem with bar{gamma} continuation: (ω_{dot k}^{l})^{bar{gamma}} - ω_{dot k}^{l} = ... Res