2026-03-25 08:53:04,224 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_11265', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nAn entangling gate between two trapped atomic ion qubits is implemented following the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol. The gate is driven by optical fields tuned near the red and blue sidebands of a vibrational mode. The two optical field pairs address the red ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,r}$) and blue ($k_{A}$, $k_{B,b}$) vibrational sidebands.\n\nThe gate is implemented in a specific "optical phase insensitive geometry" defined as follows:\n1.  The red sideband transition is driven by absorbing a photon from the $k_{A}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{B,r}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the red sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{r} = k_{A} - k_{B,r}$.\n2.  The blue sideband transition is driven by the opposite process: absorbing a photon from the $k_{B,b}$ comb tooth and emitting a photon into the $k_{A}$ comb tooth. This results in an effective wave vector for the blue sideband transition of $\\Delta{k}_{b} = k_{B,b} - k_{A}$.\n\nThe gate phase, $\\phi_{G}$, is given by the sum of the phases associated with the red and blue sideband transitions, $\\phi_{rsb}$ and $\\phi_{bsb}$, respectively:\n$$ \\phi_{G}=-(\\phi_{rsb}+\\phi_{bsb}) $$\nDrifts of the optical path length from the source to the ions, $\\delta x$, along the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beam paths change the optical phases of these fields at the ion position. This results in a phase shift $\\delta\\phi$ where $\\delta\\phi=k_{B,r}\\delta x \\approx k_{B,b}\\delta x$.\n## Theorem\nIn the Mølmer-Sørensen protocol implemented with the optical phase insensitive geometry, the gate phase $\\phi_G$ is approximately independent of common-mode optical path length fluctuations $\\delta x$ affecting the $k_{B,r}$ and $k_{B,b}$ beams.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instructions: provides a proof under '## Proof' heading, attempts a complete derivation, and includes cleanup. However, I must check for faults: (1) The prompt asks for '## Proof' as the exact heading—Response A uses '## Proof' ✓; (2) asks for 'cleaned up proof'—the proof is reasonably clean but includes some informal notation like 'h.c.' and '∎' which are standard; (3) asks to 'be honest and say so' if unable—this was not needed. Minor fault: The response includes a numerical estimate '10^{-7} rad' that was not requested in the prompt, which is unrequested content. This is a small addition but not a major violation. The proof structure is clear and complete. Score capped at 5 due to unrequested numerical estimate."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-checking claims: (1) The phase definitions φ_rsb = φ_A - φ_{B,r} and φ_bsb = φ_{B,b} - φ_A are correct per the setup [VERIFIED]. (2) The cancellation φ_rsb + φ_bsb = φ_{B,b} - φ_{B,r} is algebraically correct [VERIFIED]. (3) The claim that k_{B,b} ≃ k_{B,r} because they differ by 2ω_m ≪ ω_opt is physically correct for optical frequencies [VERIFIED]. (4) The numerical estimate '10^{-7} rad for δx ∼ μm' is UNVERIFIABLE without knowing specific experimental parameters (ω_m, optical wavelength). The claim that this is 'well below experimental error' is also UNVERIFIABLE as stated. (5) The residual phase formula φ_G' = φ_G + (k_{B,r} - k_{B,b})δx is correct [VERIFIED]. Due to unverifiable numerical claim, score capped at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Correctness audit: The theorem states φ_G is approximately independent of common-mode path fluctuations δx. Response A correctly: (1) Identifies the two-photon Raman processes [CORRECT]; (2) Derives the gate phase expression [CORRECT]; (3) Shows explicit cancellation of δφ terms in the sum φ_rsb' + φ_bsb' [CORRECT]; (4) Acknowledges the residual term from k-difference [CORRECT]. The core requirement—proving approximate independence—is satisfied. Minor issue: The response uses vector notation (k⃗, r⃗) where the prompt uses scalars, but this is a notational choice that doesn't affect correctness. The final conclusion matches the theorem. Score 5."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: (1) Structure is clear with labeled sections (i), (ii), (iii); (2) Mathematical notation is consistent and professional; (3) Grammar and spelling are correct; (4) Tone is appropriate for a physics proof. Minor issues: (1) 'h.c.' abbreviation used without expansion (Hermitian conjugate), which may not be obvious to all readers; (2) The symbol '∎' at the end is non-standard in some contexts; (3) The phrase 'well below experimental error' is slightly informal. These are trivial style preferences. Score 5."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The proof is concise and well-calibrated. Nothing can be cut because: (i) the two-photon phase identification is essential setup; (ii) the gate phase derivation is the core algebraic step; (iii) the path fluctuation analysis directly addresses the theorem. Nothing is missing because: the cancellation mechanism is fully explained, the residual term is acknowledged, and the conclusion is stated. The numerical estimate at the end, while unrequested, adds physical intuition without excessive length. Score 5.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.75, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.75'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Checking instructions: (1) The prompt asks for '## Proof' heading—Response B uses '### Proof' instead, which is a format deviation [FAULT]; (2) The prompt asks for 'cleaned up proof'—Response B includes a '### Strategy' section before the proof and a '### Verification' section after, adding unrequested structure [FAULT]; (3) The prompt asks to 'be honest and say so' if unable—this was not needed. The proof itself is complete, but the format deviates: wrong heading level (### vs ##), extra sections added. These are non-trivial deviations from explicit formatting instructions. Score capped at 4."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-checking claims: (1) φ_rsb = φ_A - φ_{B,r} and φ_bsb = φ_{B,b} - φ_A are correct [VERIFIED]. (2) The algebraic cancellation to φ_G = φ_{B,r} - φ_{B,b} is correct [VERIFIED]. (3) The claim that δφ_G = δφ_{B,r} - δφ_{B,b} is correct [VERIFIED]. (4) The statement 'the wave numbers for the red and blue sidebands of the same atomic transition are nearly identical' is slightly imprecise—it's the optical wave numbers k_{B,r} and k_{B,b} that are nearly identical, not atomic transition wave numbers [QUESTIONABLE phrasing]. (5) The final claim that (k_{B,r} - k_{B,b})δx → 0 'strictly' is overstated—it's approximately zero, not strictly zero [QUESTIONABLE]. Due to questionable claims, score capped at 4."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Correctness audit: (1) Step 1 correctly identifies sideband phases [CORRECT]. (2) Step 2 calculates gate phase correctly [CORRECT]. (3) Step 3 describes path fluctuations correctly [CORRECT]. (4) Step 4 derives δφ_G ≈ 0 correctly [CORRECT]. However, there is a significant issue: In Step 4, the response states 'taking the variance of our formula from Step 2'—this appears to be a typo/misuse; 'variance' should be 'variation' or 'differential.' This creates momentary confusion about whether statistical variance is intended [PARTIALLY CORRECT/UNCLEAR]. The Verification section correctly derives the residual term. The core proof is correct but the 'variance' wording is a flaw. Score 4."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Quality audit: Issues identified: (1) Multiple spacing errors throughout ('thepath', 'absorptionand', 'Forthe', 'Bfields', 'formulafrom', 'phase\nthe')—at least 6-7 instances of missing spaces [FAULT]; (2) Inconsistent formatting in math (some inline, some display, inconsistent use of $$); (3) The 'variance' typo noted above; (4) The '### Strategy' and '### Verification' sections create a somewhat fragmented structure. The tone is appropriate but the execution has noticeable mechanical errors. Score 4."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Length analysis: The response has identifiable padding. The '### Strategy' section largely repeats what will be shown in the proof and could be condensed or integrated. The '### Verification' section, while useful, goes beyond what the prompt requested (a proof, not verification). The proof itself in Steps 1-4 is appropriately concise. Sections that could be cut: the Strategy preamble (repetitive), the Verification section (unrequested). Missing: nothing essential. The length is reasonable but the structure adds unnecessary sections. Score 4."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both responses correctly prove the theorem, but A provides more rigorous physics context with explicit Hamiltonian terms showing the Raman transitions, while B's 'Strategy' section contains distracting typos ('thepath', 'absorptionand') and its 'Verification' section redundantly restarts the argument rather than adding value. A's quantitative estimate of residual phase (~10⁻⁷ rad) demonstrates deeper physical insight than B's hand-waving 'approaches zero.'"}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': None, 'comparison_comment': ''}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) provides a more rigorous and complete proof with explicit Hamiltonian terms showing the physical origin of the phases, while GPT 5.2 relies on heuristic 'absorption minus emission' rules without deriving from first principles. Gemini 3 Pro correctly tracks both spatial and temporal phase components (Δk·r terms) and explicitly computes the residual error (~10^-7 rad for μm-scale fluctuations), giving concrete physical magnitude; GPT 5.2 only states the residual formula without numerical estimation. Both models correctly identify the cancellation mechanism and arrive at valid conclusions, but Gemini 3 Pro's proof is more physically grounded and complete with explicit field operators and quantitative error analysis."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit strategy section before proof', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated '### Strategy' section that outlines the proof approach before presenting the formal proof, explaining the physical intuition of phase cancellation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of approach, 3-4 = clear strategy outline, 5-6 = comprehensive physical intuition with explicit cancellation mechanism explained', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Numbered proof steps with clear headers', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses numbered steps (1-4) with bold descriptive headers ('Identify the effective sideband phases', 'Calculate the overall gate phase', etc.) to structure the proof logically.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = some structure, 3-4 = clear numbered steps, 5-6 = explicit step headers that describe the mathematical/physical goal of each step', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit verification section', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated '### Verification' section after the proof that analyzes the residual error term and confirms the physical approximation.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention, 3-4 = explicit verification section, 5-6 = comprehensive verification with explicit error analysis and physical justification', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Physical interpretation of phase sign conventions', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly explains why absorption adds phase and emission subtracts phase, connecting the wave vector definitions to the physical process.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = states sign conventions without explanation, 3-4 = brief physical justification, 5-6 = explicit connection between absorption/emission physics and phase signs', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear separation of unaffected A-beam phase', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that δφ_A = 0 in Step 3, making the unaffected nature of the A-beam phase explicit in the derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = implicit in derivation, 3-4 = mentioned in passing, 5-6 = explicitly stated as a numbered step with clear justification', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Proper proof termination symbol', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses the formal black square symbol (□) to terminate the proof, while the SxS winner uses an informal '∎' or text termination.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = no termination symbol, 3-4 = informal symbol, 5-6 = standard formal mathematical proof termination symbol', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit residual error analysis', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro derives the exact residual error formula δφ_G = (k_{B,r} - k_{B,b})δx and analyzes when it vanishes, while the SxS winner only briefly mentions this as a side note.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions residual error, 3-4 = derives formula, 5-6 = comprehensive analysis with limit behavior and physical confirmation', 'rating': 5}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit strategy section before proof', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Strategy' section before the formal proof that explains the geometric cancellation mechanism at a conceptual level.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention, 3-4 = clear conceptual preview, 5-6 = comprehensive strategic overview that frames the proof', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clear absorption/emission role labeling', 'desc