2026-03-25 08:55:00,535 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10141', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nGiven two collections of objects $S$, $T$, which might or might not be identical, and a binary alphabet $\\Gamma = \\{\\pm 1\\}$, an asymmetric clustering is specified by a pair of mappings $f:S\\rightarrow\\Gamma$ and $g:T\\rightarrow\\Gamma$. The cluster incidence function $\\kappa_{f,g}:S\\times T \\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\}$ associated with this clustering is defined as $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)=1$ if $f(x)=g(y)$ and $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)=-1$ otherwise. For the binary alphabet $\\Gamma = \\{\\pm 1\\}$, this is equivalent to $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y) = f(x)g(y)$.\n\nWe denote the set of all valid asymmetric cluster incidence functions over $S,T$ with a binary alphabet as $ M_{(S,T),2} = \\left\\{ \\kappa_{f,g} \\middle| f:S\\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\}, g:T\\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\} \\right\\}$. For notational simplicity, we will use $M_2$ to denote $M_{(S,T),2}$.\n\nThe tightest possible convex relaxation of $M_2$ is its convex hull $conv M_2$. To measure tightness of a convex set $B \\supseteq M_2$, for each function (or matrix) $Z: S \\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, we define its binary cluster ratio:\n$$\n\\rho_2(Z) = \\min \\{r | Z \\in r \\ conv M_2\\} = \\min \\{ r | Z/r \\in conv M_2 \\}.\n$$\nThis can be written as the following optimization problem:\n$$\n\\rho_2(Z) = \\min_{\\mu} \\sum_{f\\in M_{S,2}}\\sum_{g\\in M_{T,2}}\\mu(f,g)\n$$\nsubject to:\n$$\nZ(x,y) = \\sum_{f\\in M_{S,2}}\\sum_{g\\in M_{T,2}}\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)\\mu(f,g), \\quad \\forall x \\in S, y \\in T\n$$\n$$\n\\mu(f,g) \\ge 0, \\quad \\forall f, g\n$$\nwhere $M_{S,2}$ is the set of functions from $S$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$ and $M_{T,2}$ is the set of functions from $T$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$.\n\nThe max-norm of a function (or matrix) $Z: S \\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is defined as:\n$$\n\\|Z\\|_{\\max}=\\min_{Z=UV^{\\top}}\\max(\\|U\\|^2_{2,\\infty},\\|V\\|^2_{2,\\infty})\n$$\nwhere $U$ and $V$ can be thought of as mappings from $S$ and $T$ respectively into a Hilbert space, such that $Z(x,y)=\\langle U(x),V(y)\\rangle$. The term $\\|U\\|_{2,\\infty}$ is the maximum $\\ell_2$ norm of the vectors $U(x)$ over all $x \\in S$, i.e. $\\|U\\|_{2,\\infty}=\\sup_x \\|U(x)\\|_2$.\n## Theorem\nFor any two sets $S$ and $T$ of objects and any function $\\text{sim}:S\\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, we have that $\\|sim\\|_{\\max} \\leq \\rho_2(sim)$ and the inequality is tight.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading [X], (2) Both directions of inequality proved [X], (3) Tightness demonstrated with explicit example [X], (4) Mathematical notation properly formatted [X], (5) No extraneous content added [X]. The response follows the requested structure exactly: proof strategy first, then cleaned-up proof with numbered sections. No unrequested elements present.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified. The construction of U(x) and V(y) from the LP solution is correct and standard in max-norm literature. The tightness example uses Z = I_2 (2x2 identity), which indeed has ρ_2(Z) = 1 and ||Z||_max = 1. One minor consideration: the claim that 'no smaller scaling works' for the identity matrix relies on the fact that any convex combination of ±1 matrices has entries in [-1,1], which is correct but could be more explicitly justified. The factorization U=V=I_2 for the max-norm is valid. No false claims detected; all technical statements align with established results in convex geometry and matrix factorization."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Every requirement answered correctly: (1) The inequality ||sim||_max ≤ ρ_2(sim) is proved via explicit factorization construction [CORRECT], (2) The factorization U(x)_(f,g) = √μ(f,g)·f(x) is correctly derived and verified [CORRECT], (3) The norm calculation ||U(x)||_2^2 = r is correct [CORRECT], (4) Tightness is established with a concrete matrix where equality holds [CORRECT], (5) The specific values ρ_2(Z)=1 and ||Z||_max=1 are correctly computed for the 2x2 identity [CORRECT]. All mathematical claims check out against the definitions provided in the setup.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear structure. Minor issue identified: the phrase 'The construction uses explicitly that each κ_{f,g} is rank-one' is slightly awkward (better: 'The construction explicitly uses...'). Otherwise excellent: logical flow from strategy to execution, clear equation numbering, appropriate use of display math, and consistent notation. The tightness section is particularly well-motivated. The proof is accessible to readers familiar with convex analysis and matrix factorization."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Near-perfect length. The proof strategy paragraph efficiently previews the approach. Section 1 develops the factorization with appropriate detail—no steps are skipped, yet no excessive elaboration. Section 2 provides a concrete tightness example with sufficient verification. One could trim the explicit matrix displays in the tightness section slightly, but they serve pedagogical clarity. Nothing essential is missing; the proof is self-contained given the setup.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Proof under ## Proof heading [X]