2026-03-25 08:55:01,832 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10141', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nGiven two collections of objects $S$, $T$, which might or might not be identical, and a binary alphabet $\\Gamma = \\{\\pm 1\\}$, an asymmetric clustering is specified by a pair of mappings $f:S\\rightarrow\\Gamma$ and $g:T\\rightarrow\\Gamma$. The cluster incidence function $\\kappa_{f,g}:S\\times T \\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\}$ associated with this clustering is defined as $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)=1$ if $f(x)=g(y)$ and $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)=-1$ otherwise. For the binary alphabet $\\Gamma = \\{\\pm 1\\}$, this is equivalent to $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y) = f(x)g(y)$.\n\nWe denote the set of all valid asymmetric cluster incidence functions over $S,T$ with a binary alphabet as $ M_{(S,T),2} = \\left\\{ \\kappa_{f,g} \\middle| f:S\\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\}, g:T\\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\} \\right\\}$. For notational simplicity, we will use $M_2$ to denote $M_{(S,T),2}$.\n\nThe tightest possible convex relaxation of $M_2$ is its convex hull $conv M_2$. To measure tightness of a convex set $B \\supseteq M_2$, for each function (or matrix) $Z: S \\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, we define its binary cluster ratio:\n$$\n\\rho_2(Z) = \\min \\{r | Z \\in r \\ conv M_2\\} = \\min \\{ r | Z/r \\in conv M_2 \\}.\n$$\nThis can be written as the following optimization problem:\n$$\n\\rho_2(Z) = \\min_{\\mu} \\sum_{f\\in M_{S,2}}\\sum_{g\\in M_{T,2}}\\mu(f,g)\n$$\nsubject to:\n$$\nZ(x,y) = \\sum_{f\\in M_{S,2}}\\sum_{g\\in M_{T,2}}\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)\\mu(f,g), \\quad \\forall x \\in S, y \\in T\n$$\n$$\n\\mu(f,g) \\ge 0, \\quad \\forall f, g\n$$\nwhere $M_{S,2}$ is the set of functions from $S$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$ and $M_{T,2}$ is the set of functions from $T$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$.\n\nThe max-norm of a function (or matrix) $Z: S \\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is defined as:\n$$\n\\|Z\\|_{\\max}=\\min_{Z=UV^{\\top}}\\max(\\|U\\|^2_{2,\\infty},\\|V\\|^2_{2,\\infty})\n$$\nwhere $U$ and $V$ can be thought of as mappings from $S$ and $T$ respectively into a Hilbert space, such that $Z(x,y)=\\langle U(x),V(y)\\rangle$. The term $\\|U\\|_{2,\\infty}$ is the maximum $\\ell_2$ norm of the vectors $U(x)$ over all $x \\in S$, i.e. $\\|U\\|_{2,\\infty}=\\sup_x \\|U(x)\\|_2$.\n## Theorem\nFor any two sets $S$ and $T$ of objects and any function $\\text{sim}:S\\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, we have that $\\|sim\\|_{\\max} \\leq \\rho_2(sim)$ and the inequality is tight.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading [X], (2) Both parts of theorem addressed: inequality proof [X] and tightness proof [X], (3) Multiple approaches considered (general construction then explicit example) [X], (4) Cleaned up final proof structure with clear steps [X]. No unrequested content added. Format matches requested style with proof outline then detailed proof.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major claims verified: [VERIFIED] The construction U(x)=∑√μ(f,g)f(x)e_{f,g}, V(y)=∑√μ(f,g)g(y)e_{f,g} yields ⟨U(x),V(y)⟩=Z(x,y) by direct computation. [VERIFIED] ||U(x)||₂² = ∑μ(f,g)f(x)² = r since f(x)²=1. [VERIFIED] For tightness, κ_{f₀,g₀} has ρ₂=1 because it's in M₂ and nonzero entries are ±1. [VERIFIED] Lower bound ||sim||_max ≥ 1 via Cauchy-Schwarz: 1=|sim(x,y)|≤||Ũ(x)||₂||Ṽ(y)||₂≤ab, so max(a²,b²)≥1. One minor issue: the claim 'every element of conv M₂ has entries in [-1,1]' is true but not explicitly proven; however this is standard for convex hull of {±1}-valued functions. No false claims found."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All requirements [CORRECT]: (1) Inequality ||sim||_max ≤ ρ₂(sim) proved via explicit factorization construction from any feasible μ [CORRECT], (2) Tightness demonstrated with concrete nonzero example κ_{f₀,g₀} where both quantities equal 1 [CORRECT], (3) The factorization construction correctly uses Hilbert space ℓ₂ indexed by (f,g) pairs [CORRECT], (4) The max-norm definition with ||U||²_{2,∞} and ||V||²_{2,∞} is correctly applied [CORRECT]. The proof that ρ₂(sim)≥1 for the example uses correct reasoning: sim∈r·conv M₂ with r<1 would force |sim(x,y)|≤r<1, contradicting |sim(x,y)|=1.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional quality with clear structure. Minor issue identified: 'the minimizing μ' is slightly imprecise—there may not be a minimizer if the infimum isn't attained, though this doesn't affect the inequality proof. Otherwise excellent: logical flow from general construction to specific example, appropriate mathematical notation, clear paragraph breaks between steps, consistent formal tone throughout. The phrase 'taking the infimum over all feasible μ yields' correctly handles the technicality."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut because: the explicit basis construction with e_{f,g} is necessary for rigor; the tightness example requires both upper and lower bound arguments; the Cauchy-Schwarz lower bound proof is essential. Nothing is missing because: the general construction is fully specified, norms are explicitly bounded, and the tightness example is completely analyzed. Minor trimming possible: the parenthetical '(e.g. ℓ₂ over this index set...)' could be shortened, but this is trivial."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Core instructions satisfied: proof provided under ## P