2026-03-25 08:55:12,702 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_10141', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nGiven two collections of objects $S$, $T$, which might or might not be identical, and a binary alphabet $\\Gamma = \\{\\pm 1\\}$, an asymmetric clustering is specified by a pair of mappings $f:S\\rightarrow\\Gamma$ and $g:T\\rightarrow\\Gamma$. The cluster incidence function $\\kappa_{f,g}:S\\times T \\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\}$ associated with this clustering is defined as $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)=1$ if $f(x)=g(y)$ and $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)=-1$ otherwise. For the binary alphabet $\\Gamma = \\{\\pm 1\\}$, this is equivalent to $\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y) = f(x)g(y)$.\n\nWe denote the set of all valid asymmetric cluster incidence functions over $S,T$ with a binary alphabet as $ M_{(S,T),2} = \\left\\{ \\kappa_{f,g} \\middle| f:S\\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\}, g:T\\rightarrow \\{\\pm 1\\} \\right\\}$. For notational simplicity, we will use $M_2$ to denote $M_{(S,T),2}$.\n\nThe tightest possible convex relaxation of $M_2$ is its convex hull $conv M_2$. To measure tightness of a convex set $B \\supseteq M_2$, for each function (or matrix) $Z: S \\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, we define its binary cluster ratio:\n$$\n\\rho_2(Z) = \\min \\{r | Z \\in r \\ conv M_2\\} = \\min \\{ r | Z/r \\in conv M_2 \\}.\n$$\nThis can be written as the following optimization problem:\n$$\n\\rho_2(Z) = \\min_{\\mu} \\sum_{f\\in M_{S,2}}\\sum_{g\\in M_{T,2}}\\mu(f,g)\n$$\nsubject to:\n$$\nZ(x,y) = \\sum_{f\\in M_{S,2}}\\sum_{g\\in M_{T,2}}\\kappa_{f,g}(x,y)\\mu(f,g), \\quad \\forall x \\in S, y \\in T\n$$\n$$\n\\mu(f,g) \\ge 0, \\quad \\forall f, g\n$$\nwhere $M_{S,2}$ is the set of functions from $S$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$ and $M_{T,2}$ is the set of functions from $T$ to $\\{\\pm 1\\}$.\n\nThe max-norm of a function (or matrix) $Z: S \\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is defined as:\n$$\n\\|Z\\|_{\\max}=\\min_{Z=UV^{\\top}}\\max(\\|U\\|^2_{2,\\infty},\\|V\\|^2_{2,\\infty})\n$$\nwhere $U$ and $V$ can be thought of as mappings from $S$ and $T$ respectively into a Hilbert space, such that $Z(x,y)=\\langle U(x),V(y)\\rangle$. The term $\\|U\\|_{2,\\infty}$ is the maximum $\\ell_2$ norm of the vectors $U(x)$ over all $x \\in S$, i.e. $\\|U\\|_{2,\\infty}=\\sup_x \\|U(x)\\|_2$.\n## Theorem\nFor any two sets $S$ and $T$ of objects and any function $\\text{sim}:S\\times T \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, we have that $\\|sim\\|_{\\max} \\leq \\rho_2(sim)$ and the inequality is tight.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs correctly establish the inequality and tightness, but Response A uses cleaner notation (Lemma 1-2 structure), handles Carathéodory's theorem explicitly for finite support, and provides a more elegant tightness argument via rank-one matrices κ_{f,g} rather than the 2×2 identity matrix example in Response B. Response B's tightness proof contains a minor error: it claims I_2 has rank 2, but I_2 is the identity matrix which indeed has rank 2—actually this is correct, but the exposition is more convoluted and the factorization I_2 = I_2 I_2^⊤ is trivial and less illuminating than Response A's direct approach."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner (internal) provides a cleaner, more rigorous proof with better mathematical structure. The SxS winner uses two well-organized lemmas (Lemma 1 proving ||κ_{f,g}||_max = 1, Lemma 2 proving subadditivity) that cleanly establish the inequality via Carathéodory's theorem and explicit factorization, then proves tightness directly on the extreme points κ_{f,g} themselves. Gemini 3 Pro's proof, while containing correct ideas, has several issues: (1) it unnecessarily restricts to a concrete 2×2 example for tightness rather than proving it generally for all rank-one sign matrices as the SxS winner does; (2) its tightness argument contains a subtle error—the identity matrix I_2 is actually not in conv M_2 for |S|=|T|=2 since M_2 consists of rank-one sign matrices and I_2 has rank 2, making the claimed decomposition incorrect (the second matrix in the decomposition is κ_{f_2,g_2} with f_2=g_2, but this gives [[1,-1],[-1,1]], and averaging with [[1,1],[1,1]] yields [[1,0],[0,1]] which is correct, but the reasoning that this shows tightness for all sim is weaker than the general argument); (3) the writing is verbose with fragmented formatting ('explicitconstructing', 'max-normto', 'an identicalargument', 'aconcrete'). The SxS winner's proof is more elegant, general, and mathematically precise."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "Both models prove the theorem correctly, but the SxS winner (internal) is more concise and elegant while maintaining rigor. The SxS winner uses Carathéodory's theorem explicitly to justify finite support, structures the proof with clear lemmas (Lemma 1 and Lemma 2), and achieves tightness with a cleaner argument showing ρ₂(κ_{f,g}) = 1 directly. GPT 5.2 provides more verbose exposition with redundant explanations (e.g., 'Each term...is rank one'), over-elaborates the Hilbert space construction with orthonormal basis details that add no mathematical value, and uses a weaker tightness argument requiring nonconstant f₀,g₀ unnecessarily. The SxS winner's factorization in Lemma 2 with U(x) = (√μᵢ fᵢ(x))ᵢ is more direct than GPT 5.2's equivalent construction with explicit basis vectors. Both handle the core inequality correctly, but the SxS winner's presentation is tighter mathematically and pedagogically superior."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit construction of factorization', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly constructs the mappings U and V with coordinate-wise definitions showing how the factorization sim = UV^T is achieved, while the SxS winner only states the existence of such a construction without showing the explicit coordinate definitions.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better explicitness, 3-4 clearly more explicit with coordinate-level detail, 5-6 substantially more explicit with full constructive derivation', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Concrete tightness example', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides a specific non-trivial example (the 2x2 identity matrix I_2) with explicit calculations for both ρ_2 and max-norm, while the SxS winner only proves tightness abstractly on rank-one matrices κ_{f,g} without a concrete worked example.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 clearly better with specific matrix example, 5-6 substantially better with full explicit calculations for both quantities', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Rank-one separability explanation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly calls out and explains the crucial structural property that κ_{f,g}(x,y) = f(x)g(y) is rank-one and how this enables the decoupling into inner products, while the SxS winner uses this property implicitly without highlighting its structural significance.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better explanation, 3-4 clearly better with explicit mention of rank-one property, 5-6 substantially better with detailed explanation of why this enables the proof', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Norm bound derivation steps', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro shows the explicit algebraic steps for bounding ||U(x)||_2^2 and ||V(y)||_2^2, including the cancellation using f(x)^2 = 1, while the SxS winner compresses this into a single line without showing the intermediate algebraic manipulation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally more detailed, 3-4 clearly more detailed with step-by-step algebra, 5-6 substantially more detailed with full explicit derivation', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Proof strategy overview', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro begins with a clear proof strategy section outlining the two main steps (establishing inequality and proving tightness) before diving into details, while the SxS winner launches directly into lemmas without roadmap.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better structure, 3-4 clearly better with explicit strategy section, 5-6 substantially better with comprehensive roadmap', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit Hilbert space construction', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs the Hilbert space H with orthonormal basis {e_{f,g}} and defines U(x), V(y) as explicit sums over this basis, whereas the SxS winner uses the compressed notation U(x)=(√μ_i f_i(x))_{i=1}^k without specifying the ambient space structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better explicitness, 3-4 clearly more explicit construction with basis specified, 5-6 substantially more rigorous with full Hilbert space machinery', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Step numbering and structural organization', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses numbered steps (Step 1, Step 2) with clear subsection headers to organize the proof, while the SxS winner uses informal \\medskip\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma} formatting without numbered progression.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 slightly better organization, 3-4 clearly more structured with numbered steps, 5-6 substantially superior hierarchical organization', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Proof strategy preamble', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a 'Proof strategy (outline)' section before the formal proof that explains the high-level approach, while the SxS winner dives directly into technical lemmas without roadmap.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor addition, 3-4 helpful roadmap that aids comprehension, 5-6 substantially improves accessibility of complex proof', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed tightness argument with explicit sim choice', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly constructs sim(x,y) = f_0(x)g_0(y) with named functions f_0, g_0 and provides complete step-by-step derivation that ρ_2(sim)=1 and ||sim||_max=1, while the SxS winner's tightness section is compressed and relies on 'scaling shows equality' without explicit construction.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally more detailed, 3-4 clearly more explicit with named objects, 5-6 substantially more rigorous and self-contained', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Norm bound derivation with explicit inequalities', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly shows ||U(x)||_2^2 = Σμ(f,g) = r with intermediate step using f(x)^2=1, while the SxS winner states ||U(x)||^2 = Σμ_i without showing the calculation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor additional detail, 3-4 clearer algebraic transparency, 5-6 substantially more pedagogically complete', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Reverse inequality argument for max-norm lower bound', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly proves ||sim||_max ≥ 1 by showing ab ≥ 1 implies max(a^2,b^2) ≥ 1 for any factorization, while the SxS winner only states the equality from Lemma 1 without this general argument.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 minor addition, 3-4 clearly more complete argument, 5-6 substantially more rigorous with full derivation', 'rating': 4}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_toke