ly asserts this bound without proof ('Knowing ln 2 ≈ 0.693147, we can conservatively over-approximate it as ln 2 < 25/36'). The SxS winner also correctly handles the case analysis: it proves that if min{8q_2, 12q_1} = 12q_1, then R(η)/η² ≤ p_1q_1/4 ≤ 1/4 < 36/25, creating a contradiction that forces the minimum to be 8q_2. Gemini 3 Pro's case analysis contains a critical error in Case 1: it claims A(1) = p_1q_1/4 and concludes 'A(η_ch) < 0.25', but this incorrectly assumes p_1 ≤ 1 and q_1 ≤ 1 are tight bounds when the actual constraint is p_1q_1 ≤ 1, making the supremum potentially larger; more seriously, Gemini 3 Pro fails to establish that this case leads to a contradiction with the repeaterless bound requirement. The SxS winner also maintains cleaner notation (using η consistently vs Gemini's mixing of η_ch and η) and provides a more structured proof with clear section headers. Both models correctly identify the two bounds and their combination via min, but the SxS winner's explicit verification of the numerical constant and more careful asymptotic analysis demonstrate superior Truthfulness and Prompt Correctness."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': 'Both responses are mathematically identical in content, structure, and even LaTeX formatting. Both correctly prove the theorem by establishing: (1) the probability constraint q₂ ≤ 1−q₁ from normalization, (2) the lower bound B(η) ≥ (36/25)η² via the trapezoidal rule with n=8 subintervals yielding ln 2 < 25/36, and (3) the high-loss comparison yielding q₂ ≤ (25/96)p₁q₁². Both use the same strategy of comparing η² coefficients and endpoint analysis of D(η). The SxS winner and GPT 5.2 produce verbatim-equivalent proofs with identical numerical verification (200107/288288 < 25/36 via 7,203,852 < 7,207,200). No meaningful distinction exists in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, or Verbosity.'}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Case analysis for denomina