2026-03-25 08:56:49,095 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_7062', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $(X_t)_{t\\geq 0}$ be a separable square-integrable stochastic process, normalized so that for all $t,s\\geq 0$\n\\begin{equation}\nE(X_t)=0,E(X_tX_s)=\\min\\{t,s\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\end{equation}\nWe are interested in the processes with linear conditional expectations and quadratic conditional variances. More specifically, we assume the following.\n\nFor all $0\\leq s<t<u$,\n\\begin{equation}\nE(X_t|{\\mathcal F}_{\\leq s}\\vee {\\mathcal F}_{\\geq u})=aX_s+bX_u, \\tag{2}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a=a(s,t,u)=\\frac{u-t}{u-s},b=b(s,t,u)=\\frac{t-s}{u-s}$ are the deterministic functions of $s,t,u$, and ${\\mathcal F}_{\\leq s}\\vee {\\mathcal F}_{\\geq u}$ denotes the $\\sigma$-field generated by $\\{X_r: r \\leq s \\text{ or } r \\geq u\\}$. A consequence of this assumption is the relation\n$$E(X_s|{{\\mathcal F}}_{\\ge t}) = \\frac{s}{t}X_t, \\quad \\text{for } s<t.$$\n\nBy assumption, for any $0<s<t$ the conditional second moment is quadratic in $X_t$, i.e.,\n\\begin{equation}\nE(X_s^2|{\\mathcal F}_{\\ge t})=m(s,t)X_t^2+n(s,t)X_t+o(s,t), \\tag{3}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $m$, $n$, $o$ are some functions.\n\nWe also assume that for any $t>0$, the random variables $1, X_t, X_t^2$ are linearly independent.\n## Theorem\n**Proposition: The structure of reversed conditional second moments.**\nLet $(X_t)_{t\\geq0}$ be a stochastic process satisfying the conditions in the Setup. Then there are constants $0\\leq \\tau<\\infty$ and $-\\infty<\\theta<\\infty$ such that for any $0\\le s<t$, the conditional second moment is given by\n$$\nE(X_s^2|{{\\mathcal F}}_{\\ge t})=\\frac{s(s+\\tau)}{t(t+\\tau)}X_t^2+\\frac{s(t-s)}{t(t+\\tau)}\\theta X_t+\\frac{s(t-s)}{t+\\tau}.\n$$\nAlternatively, in the case corresponding to $\\tau=\\infty$, the conditional second moment is given by\n$$\nE(X_s^2|{{\\mathcal F}}_{\\ge t})=\\frac{s}{t}X_t^2+\\frac{s(t-s)}{t}\\theta X_t.\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete proof provided under ## Proof heading, (2) Multiple approaches used (tower property, two-sided regression, coefficient matching), (3) Honest completion - no admission of inability needed as proof succeeds. Checklist: [X] Proof heading present, [X] Complete derivation, [X] Multiple approaches attempted, [X] Cleaned up final proof. No unrequested content added. Format matches expectation with clear section structure.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major mathematical claims verified. Key claims: [VERIFIED] Multiplicativity m(s,u)=m(s,t)m(t,u) derived correctly from tower property; [VERIFIED] Affine solution f(t)=ct+d to Jensen's equation for measurable functions; [VERIFIED] Normalization o(s,t)=s-t·m(s,t) from E(X_s^2)=s; [VERIFIED] Final formulas match proposition statement. One minor issue: claim 'any measurable solution of (J) is affine' - this is correct (Cauchy's functional equation with measurability), but could cite that regularity (measurability/boundedness) is needed. No false claims detected."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All prompt requirements answered correctly: [CORRECT] Derives conditional second moment formula; [CORRECT] Handles both τ<∞ and τ=∞ cases; [CORRECT] Uses all given assumptions (1), (2), (3), linear independence; [CORRECT] Derives explicit forms for m(s,t), n(s,t), o(s,t); [CORRECT] Shows consistency with E(X_s^2)=s. The proof correctly identifies the two-parameter family (τ, θ) and derives the specific functional forms claimed in the theorem.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Professional mathematical writing with clear structure. Strengths: logical flow with numbered sections, clear equation labeling, proper use of LaTeX formatting, helpful boxed equations for key results. Minor issue: occasional redundancy in explanations (e.g., 'Standardly, this implies' could be more direct). The phrase 'For instance, fix T>t and set...' is slightly informal. Otherwise publication-ready with excellent clarity in the mathematical argument."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut: each section serves essential purpose - (1) tower property derivation, (2) two-sided regression, (3) solving for m, (4) solving for o, (5) solving for n, (6) assembly. Nothing is missing: all steps in the logical chain are present, including the crucial coefficient matching via linear independence. The detailed derivation of the functional equation for f(u) in step 3 is necessary for rigor. Only trivial trimming possible (e.g., 'Standardly' phrase)."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All instruction elements satisfied: [X] Proof heading present, [X] Complete proof provided, [X] Multiple approaches used (tower property, double computation of mixed moment), [X] Cleaned up final proof. Minor issue: uses \\label and \\eqref commands that reference undefined labels (\\eqref{rev}, \\eqref{mult}, etc.) - these appear to be LaTeX residue that would fail to compile properly. This is a formatting imperfection that doesn't affect content but violates 'cleaned up' requirement. No admission of inability needed."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All major mathematical claims verified. Key claims: [VERIFIED] Derivation of reversed linear regression E(X_s|F≥t)=(s/t)X_t; [VERIFIED] Multiplicativity relations (mult); [VERIFIED] Second system of equations (eq2) from coefficient matching; [VERIFIED] Solution forms with h(t), k(t) functions; [VERIFIED] Final identification of τ and θ parameters. One issue: the claim 'using square-integrability' for u→∞ limit to get (rev) is technically correct but glosses over details; the setup already states this as 'a consequence of (2)' so this is acceptable."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All prompt requirements answered correctly: [CORRECT] Derives both cases τ<∞ and τ=∞; [CORRECT] Explicit formulas for m, n, o match proposition; [CORRECT] Uses all assumptions including linear independence for coefficient matching; [CORRECT] Shows non-negativity constraint τ≥0 arises from o≥0 requirement. The proof is complete and correct, with the clever use of h(t) and k(t) ansätze efficiently reducing the functional equations.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Good but has identifiable issues. Strengths: concise, logical flow, effective use of equation numbering. Weaknesses: (1) LaTeX \\label/\\eqref commands without definitions create compilation errors; (2) dense notation with minimal explanatory text makes some steps harder to follow (e.g., 'Comparing coefficients of s gives' could elaborate); (3) abrupt transitions between steps; (4) 'whence' and 'hence' overuse creates slightly archaic tone. The writing is professional but less polished than Response A."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Near-perfect length. Nothing can be cut: the 6-step structure is efficient with each step essential. The ansatz o(s,t)=s(t-s)h(t) and n(s,t)=s(t-s)k(t) is elegant and well-motivated. Nothing is missing: all functional equations are solved, boundary conditions checked, both cases derived. The compactness is a strength - no padding, direct path to solution. Only trivial trimming possible (some 'hence'/'thus' connectors)."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.05, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.