mbedded in derivation rather than a dedicated explanation. The physical why—why configurational entropy contributes to heat capacity discontinuity—is not deeply explained. Fault: lacks extended physical interpretation section; the explanation is more descriptive than explanatory of the underlying physical mechanism."}, 'gemini': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response includes a dedicated 'Physical Explanation' paragraph in Step 1: 'According to Assumption 2, entropy is split into fast vibrational modes and slow configurational modes... As the liquid cools... these slow configurational modes fall out of equilibrium and become structurally arrested... The constraints on these degrees of freedom alter their ability to absorb and store heat.' This provides clear physical reasoning beyond algebraic manipulation. Fault considered: could connect more explicitly to the specific term in the final formula, but the physical explanation is thorough."}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response includes a dedicated 'Physical explanation' paragraph: 'The entropy S corresponds to fast, equilibrated modes... The configurational entropy I describes slow structural modes... Because those slow modes are not equilibrated with the bath in the glass, the energetic cost of changing I is controlled by T_e, hence I contributes to C_p and to its discontinuity.' This clearly explains the physical mechanism. Fault considered: could explicitly mention structural arrest, but the explanation is complete and substantive."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Classical Ehrenfest Limit Verification', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response includes a dedicated 'Classical limit' section: 'If the system remains in equilibrium, T_e=T everywhere, so ∂_T T_e|_p=1. The extra term vanishes and Eq. (9) reduces to Π=1, the classical Ehrenfest result, as required.' This is explicit verification with clear demonstration. Fault considered: could show more intermediate steps, but the verification is complete."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 1, 'reason': 'The response does not contain any verification that the result reduces to the classical Ehrenfest case when T_e=T throughout. There is no section or statement checking the Π=1 limit. Fault: completely missing this required element.'}, 'gemini': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response includes a dedicated Step 5: 'If the system stays in strict equilibrium throughout... (∂T_e/∂T)_p=1 identically. Substituting this into Equation 9 makes the second term strictly vanish, properly recovering Π=1, which is the classical Ehrenfest relation signature for pure second-order phase transitions.' Explicit demonstration with clear reasoning. Fault considered: none significant."}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response includes a dedicated section 'Check the equilibrium (classical Ehrenfest) limit': 'If the system remains in equilibrium throughout, then T_e≡T everywhere, so (∂T_e/∂T)|_p=1. Then the correction term in Eq. 9 vanishes and Π=1, recovering the classical Ehrenfest result.' Also notes recovery of second Ehrenfest relation. Explicit and thorough. Fault considered: none significant."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Additional Assumptions Assessment', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response begins with 'I begin with a brief assessment of hidden assumptions. The derivation below uses only the seven listed assumptions, but it implicitly relies on two points... (i) ... (ii) ... Both follow from... so no new independent postulates are required.' Clear assessment before the proof. Fault considered: could be more explicit about whether these are truly 'additional' or derived, but the assessment is present and substantive."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response includes 'Remarks on additional assumptions' at the end, noting: 'The step (8) assumes that the configurational entropy depends on pressure only through the effective temperature... This is not stated explicitly... The use of the equilibrium second Ehrenfest relation (5) for the liquid side relies on...' This is present but at the end rather than before the cleaned-up proof. Fault: placement after rather than before; also could more clearly distinguish derived from additional assumptions."}, 'gemini': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response includes 'Assessment of Additional Assumptions' at the end, listing: '1. Equilibrium property of the Liquid phase... 2. Continuity of Enthalpy...' This is present with clear statements. Fault: placement at the end rather than before the cleaned-up proof as specified; also the enthalpy continuity arguably follows from Assumption 5 rather than being truly additional."}, 'gpt': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response begins with '### Brief assessment of extra assumptions used' before the ## Proof heading, explicitly stating: 'Besides the seven listed assumptions, one standard equilibrium fact is implicitly needed... (A1) In the equilibrated liquid phase...' Clear assessment in correct position. Fault considered: could discuss whether smoothness/differentiability assumptions are fully covered by Assumption 1, but the assessment is thorough and well-placed."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Proof Heading Compliance', 'opalite': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The cleaned-up proof appears under the exact heading '## Proof' as specified. Fault considered: none; perfect compliance."}, 'ophelia': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The cleaned-up proof appears under the exact heading '## Proof' as specified. Fault considered: none; perfect compliance."}, 'gemini': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The cleaned-up proof appears under the exact heading '## Proof' as specified. Fault considered: none; perfect compliance."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The cleaned-up proof appears under the exact heading '## Proof' as specified. Fault considered: none; perfect compliance."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Mathematical Correctness of Prigogine-Defay Derivation', 'opalite': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The derivation has several issues. In Section 2, the claim 'Continuity of U and V... gives