2026-03-25 09:01:51,374 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: tasks1_response---------------------------[{'index': 0, 'prompt': "You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $\\mathbb{F}=\\overline{\\mathbb{F}}_{q^2}$ be the algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}.$ Let $\\zeta_3$ be a primitive cube root of unity. Let $p$ be the characteristic of the field. We assume $q \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$, which implies $\\zeta_3^q = \\zeta_3^2$ and $\\zeta_3^{2q} = \\zeta_3$. Let $m=(q+1)/3$.\n\nThe paper considers points $P_{(a,b)}$ on a curve $\\mathcal{X}_3$ lying in $\\mathbb{F} \\times \\mathbb{F}$. For these points, a quantity $\\alpha(P_{(a,b)})$ is defined as $\\alpha(P_{(a,b)}) = \\frac{a^m}{1+a^m}$. The coordinates of the points on the curve are related by $b^{q+1}=-a^{2m}-a^m$. A point $P_{(a,b)}$ is $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-rational if $a,b \\in \\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$. The set of all points on the curve is denoted by $\\mathcal{X}_3(\\overline{\\mathbb{F}}_{q^2})$. There is a specific subset of points denoted $\\mathcal{O}$.\n\nFor any $i \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, polynomials $\\mathcal{P}_i(s)$ are defined. For any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{F} \\setminus \\{0,1,-\\zeta_3,-\\zeta_3^2\\}$, one has $\\mathcal{P}_{i+1}(\\alpha)=0$ if and only if $((\\alpha+\\zeta_3)/(\\alpha+\\zeta_3^2))^{3i+3}=1$. Since any nonzero element of $\\mathbb{F}$ has a finite multiplicative order, for any such $\\alpha$ there exists an $i>0$ such that $\\mathcal{P}_{i+1}(\\alpha)=0$. Also, $\\mathcal{P}_1(s)=1$.\n\n**Definition 33** Let $\\alpha \\in \\overline{\\mathbb{F}}_{q^2} \\setminus \\{0,1,-\\zeta_3,-\\zeta_3^2\\}$. Then we define the $\\mathcal{P}$-order of $\\alpha$ as the smallest positive integer $i$ such that $\\mathcal{P}_{i+1}(\\alpha)=0$.\n\nIf $\\alpha:=\\alpha(P_{(a,b)})$ has a $\\mathcal{P}$-order for some positive integer $i$, then $\\alpha \\not \\in \\{0,1,-\\zeta_3,-\\zeta_3^2\\}$ and $P_{(a,b)} \\not \\in \\mathcal O$.\nThe relation $\\alpha^2-\\alpha+1 = 0$ is equivalent to $a^{2m}+a^m+1=0$.\n$\\varphi(\\cdot)$ denotes Euler's totient function.\n## Theorem\n**Lemma 34**\nLet $i$ be a positive integer. The number of $P_{(a,b)} \\in \\mathcal{X}_3(\\overline{\\mathbb{F}}_{q^2}) \\setminus \\mathcal{O}$ such that $\\alpha(P_{(a,b)})$ has $\\mathcal{P}$-order $i$ is equal to $(q+1)^2 \\varphi(i+1)$ if $\\gcd(i+1,p)=1$ and $0$ otherwise. Here $\\varphi(\\cdot)$ denotes Euler's totient function. Moreover, $P_{(a,b)} \\in \\mathcal{X}_3(\\overline{\\mathbb{F}}_{q^2}) \\setminus \\mathcal{O}$ is $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-rational if and only if $\\alpha(P_{(a,b)})^2-\\alpha(P_{(a,b)})+1=0$ or its $\\mathcal{P}$-order $i$ satisfies that $i+1$ divides $m$.", 'status': 'ACCEPT', 'result': {'status': 'ACCEPT', 'rejection_reason': None, 'justification': 'This is a well-formed mathematical proof request with complete setup and reference text. The prompt provides all necessary definitions, notation, and the theorem to be proven. It involves finite field theory, algebraic curves, and number theory - all legitimate mathematical content. No rejection reasons apply.'}, 'accepted': True, 'error': None, 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 1404, 'output_tokens': 83, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.003804}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.003804}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 1404, 'output_tokens': 83, 'cached_tokens': 0, 