oof of Theorem 6 using a minimality argument on μ(x), while GPT 5.2 fails to complete the proof. Instruction Following: SxS winner follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under ## Proof; GPT 5.2 explicitly states 'I cannot complete a full proof' and provides only a partial result. Truthfulness: SxS winner's proof is mathematically correct—the key inequality sin(π/7) < 1/2 is valid (sin(π/7) ≈ 0.434), and the minimality argument properly constructs a contradiction; GPT 5.2's lemma is correct but the claim that 'I do not see how to guarantee the existence of such a point' reflects a failure to execute the standard tower/minimality argument that the SxS winner successfully deploys. Prompt Correctness: SxS winner correctly interprets the full-dimensional condition to ensure off-axis points exist in every 2R-cluster; GPT 5.2 misses that this, combined with the discreteness of distances in a Delone set, guarantees the minimum μ₀ is attained. Writing Style: Both are clear, but SxS winner provides a complete structured proof while GPT 5.2 provides an incomplete analysis with an honest admission of failure. The SxS winner's construction in Step 3 showing y₀⁽¹⁾ is not on the axis of y₀ is the critical insight GPT 5.2 lacks."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit geometric construction of regular polygon', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly constructs the regular n-gon by defining consecutive vertices y_0, y_1, y_2 using the rotation g and its inverse, whereas the SxS winner only mentions y_0 and y_0^(1) without the symmetric construction', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Clear separation of local vs global infimum argument', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines rho_0 as the infimum over all points and uses the factor q = 2sin(pi/7) to rigorously establish the inequality chain for the contradiction, whereas the SxS winner uses a less formal 'tower' argument wi