roof, while the SxS winner uses informal bold text and minimal structural markers.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minimal structure, 3-4 = some headers, 5-6 = rigorous hierarchical organization with numbered steps throughout', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit handling of p|n case with field theory justification', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly explains why no element of order divisible by p exists in the algebraic closure (citing that q^{2r}-1 is coprime to p), while the SxS winner only states the contradiction without explaining the underlying field theory.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions case, 3-4 = some explanation, 5-6 = complete justification with explicit field theory reasoning', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit verification of Möbius transformation bijectivity', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly solves for α in terms of t and verifies injectivity, while the SxS winner asserts the bijection without showing the inverse formula or checking the excluded point condition.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = asserts bijection, 3-4 = partial verification, 5-6 = complete explicit derivation with formula and domain check', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit derivation of t formula in terms of x', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly derives t = (1-ζ₃x)/(ζ₃-x) from α = x/(1+x), while the SxS winner uses this formula in the rationality section without showing its derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = uses formula without derivation, 3-4 = brief mention of derivation, 5-6 = explicit step-by-step algebraic derivation', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit verification of x^q = x via algebraic check', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly verifies x^q = x by cross-multiplying and showing both sides equal, while the SxS winner asserts this without the explicit algebraic verification in the converse direction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = asserts equality, 3-4 = brief justification, 5-6 = explicit algebraic verification with cross-multiplication shown', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Clea