2026-03-25 09:15:52,298 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_6572', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nWe are concerned with solving the following three-by-three block system of linear equations\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} x \\equiv\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n{A} & {B^{T}} & {0} \\\\\n{-B} & {0} & {-C^{T}} \\\\\n{0} & {C} & {0}\n\\end{array}\\right)\\left(\\begin{array}{l}\n{x} \\\\\n{y} \\\\\n{z}\n\\end{array}\\right)=\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n{f} \\\\\n{-g} \\\\\n{h}\n\\end{array}\\right)=b,\n$$\nwhere $A\\in \\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}$ is a symmetric positive definite (SPD), $B\\in \\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$ and $C\\in \\mathbb{R}^{l\\times m}$ have full row rank. We assume that $n \\geq m$ and $m \\geq l.$\n\nWe split the coefficient matrix $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\mathcal{B}=\\mathcal{P}-\\mathcal{R},$ where\n$$\\mathcal{P}=\\left(\\begin{array}\n{ccc}\nA & B^{T} & 0 \\\\\n0 & \\alpha I+ \\beta B B^{T} & -C^{T} \\\\\n0 & 0 & \\alpha I+ \\beta C C^{T}\n\\end{array}\\right),$$\nand $\\mathcal{R} = \\mathcal{P} - \\mathcal{B}$, i.e.,\n$$ \\mathcal{R}=\\left(\\begin{array}\n{ccc}\n0 & 0 & 0 \\\\\nB & \\alpha I+ \\beta B B^{T} & 0 \\\\\n0 & -C & \\alpha I+ \\beta C C^{T}\n\\end{array}\\right),$$\nin which $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are given positive constants. The matrix $\\mathcal{P}$ is nonsingular. The iterative scheme associated with this splitting can be constructed as\n$$\n{x}^{(k+1)}= \\mathcal{G}_{\\alpha,\\beta} {x}^{(k)}+ f, k=0,1,2,\\dots,\n$$\nwhere $x^{(0)}$ is arbitrary, $\\mathcal{G}_{\\alpha,\\beta}= \\mathcal{P}^{-1} \\mathcal{R}$ is the iteration matrix and $f=\\mathcal{P}^{-1} {b}.$\n\nFor a given matrix $M$ with real eigenvalues, $\\lambda_{\\min}(M)$ and $\\lambda_{\\max}(M)$ stand for the minimum and maximum eigenvalue of $M,$ respectively. The minimum and maximum singular value of $M$ are represented by $\\sigma_{\\min}(M)$ and $\\sigma_{\\max}(M)$, respectively. For any arbitrary complex vectors $x,$ $x^{H}$ indicates the conjugate transpose of $x$.\n\nThe proof of the theorem requires the following lemma.\n\n**Lemma** [28] Consider the quadratic equation $x^{2} - b x +c=0,$ where $b$ and $c$ are real numbers. Both roots of the equation are less than one in modulus if and only if $|c| < 1$ and $|b| < 1+c.$\n## Theorem\n**Theorem 1** Suppose that $A\\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is SPD, $B \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ and $C \\in \\mathbb{R}^{l \\times m}$ are full row rank matrices. Then, the iterative method\n$$\n{x}^{(k+1)}= \\mathcal{G}_{\\alpha,\\beta} {x}^{(k)}+ f, k=0,1,2,\\dots,\n$$\nconverges to the unique solution of the system $\\mathcal{B}x=b$ for any initial guess, if\n$$\n\\frac{{{\\sigma_{\\max}}^{2} (C)}}{\\alpha+\\beta {\\sigma_{\\min}}^{2}(C^{T})} +2 \\frac{ {{\\sigma_{\\max}}^{2} ( B^{T})}}{\\lambda_{\\min} (A)} < 4 (\\alpha +\\beta {\\sigma_{\\min}}^{2} ( B^{T})).\n$$', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'The response follows the core instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading ## Proof. It correctly uses the provided Lemma [28] and addresses the theorem statement. Minor formatting issue: the prompt uses \\mathcal{G}_{\\alpha,\\beta} but the response uses G_{\\alpha,\\beta} and G interchangeably, and uses P, R instead of \\mathcal{P}, \\mathcal{R} in some places. The structure is clear with steps (i) and (ii). No critical instructions were missed, but the notation inconsistency (dropping mathcal on some symbols) is a trivial formatting variance.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The mathematical claims appear correct and verifiable. Key claims checked: [VERIFIED] The derivation of the quadratic equation (4) from the eigenvalue problem follows standard manipulation. [VERIFIED] The application of Lemma [28] with conditions (5) is correct: |1-b/a|<1 gives 0<b/a<2, and |2-(b+c)/a|<2-b/a gives 2b/a+c/a<4. [VERIFIED] The bounds on a, b, c using singular values are standard: D \\succeq (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B))I, S \\preceq \\sigma_{\\max}^2(B)A^{-1} \\preceq \\sigma_{\\max}^2(B)/\\lambda_{\\min}(A) I. [VERIFIED] The final inequality matches the theorem hypothesis. One minor issue: the response states 'M\\preceq (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))^{-1}C^TC' but actually M = C^T E^{-1} C and E \\succeq (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))I, so E^{-1} \\preceq (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))^{-1}I, giving M \\preceq (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))^{-1} C^T C. This is correct but the singular value bound used is c \\le \\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)/(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C)) which requires C^T C \\preceq \\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)I, so the chain is valid. No false claims found."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The proof correctly establishes convergence by showing \\rho(G)<1. [CORRECT] Step (i) proves \\mathcal{B} is nonsingular, which is necessary for unique solution. [CORRECT] The eigenvalue analysis correctly derives the quadratic equation and applies the Lemma. [CORRECT] The bounds on Rayleigh quotients are correctly derived. [CORRECT] The final conclusion connects \\rho(G)<1 to convergence of the iterative scheme. Minor gap: The response doesn't explicitly verify that the condition in the theorem implies both required conditions in (5), though this is implicitly clear since the RHS < 4 and all terms are positive implies \\theta < 2. The proof is complete and correct."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is clear, professional, and well-structured. The proof is organized into logical sections (i) and (ii). Mathematical notation is consistent within the proof (though see IF note about \\mathcal vs non-\\mathcal). Equations are clearly numbered and referenced. The flow from eigenvalue problem to quadratic equation to bounds is easy to follow. One minor issue: 'whence' appears twice, which is slightly archaic but acceptable. The use of 'sup' for supremum is correct. Overall publication-ready with only trivial style preferences that could be adjusted."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'The length is appropriate for a complete proof of this theorem. Nothing can be cut because: (1) the nonsingularity proof in (i) is necessary to establish unique solution; (2) each step in the eigenvalue derivation is needed; (3) the bounds using singular values require explicit justification. Nothing is missing because: the proof covers all cases (\\lambda=0, \\lambda=1, \\lambda\\neq 0,1), applies the Lemma correctly, and connects back to the iteration convergence. The final paragraph establishing the fixed point is necessary for completeness. The proof is near-perfect in length.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction with ## Proof heading and uses the Lemma. It maintains \\mathcal notation more consistently than Response A (uses \\mathcal P, \\mathcal R, \\mathcal G). Minor issues: uses D_b, D_c instead of the D, E from the prompt's \\mathcal{P} definition, but this is clearly defined. Uses 'Put' instead of 'Let' at start, which is slightly informal but acceptable. The structure with Case c=0 and Case c>0 is clear. No required elements missing."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': 'Most claims are correct, but there is a critical error in the bound derivation. [VERIFIED] The \\mathcal{P}^{-1} formula is correct for a block upper triangular matrix. [VERIFIED] The eigenvalue derivation through (1)-(3) is correct. [VERIFIED] The case analysis (c=0 vs c>0) is handled correctly. [FALSE] However, there is a serious error in the bound for 2a+c/d. The response defines \\theta = \\sigma_{\\max}^2(B)/[\\lambda_{\\min}(A)(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B))] and \\eta = \\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)/(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C)) divided by (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)). But then states 2\\theta + \\eta = [2\\sigma_{\\max}^2(B)/\\lambda_{\\min}(A) + \\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)/(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))] / (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)). This is INCORRECT: the theorem hypothesis has the C term NOT divided by (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)). The theorem states: \\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)/(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C^T)) + 2\\sigma_{\\max}^2(B^T)/\\lambda_{\\min}(A) < 4(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B^T)). Response B incorrectly divides the C term by (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)) as well. This makes the bound and the connection to the theorem hypothesis incorrect. The final claim that the hypothesis implies the bounds is therefore flawed.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "The proof attempt is largely correct in structure but contains a critical error in interpreting the theorem hypothesis. [INCORRECT] The bound derivation for (2a+c)/d introduces an extra division by (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)) for the C term. The theorem hypothesis has: [\\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)/(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))] + [2\\sigma_{\\max}^2(B)/\\lambda_{\\min}(A)] < 4(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)). Response B interprets this as requiring [2\\sigma_{\\max}^2(B)/\\lambda_{\\min}(A) + \\sigma_{\\max}^2(C)/(\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(C))] / (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)) < 4, which is wrong. The correct interpretation (as in Response A) is that the two terms on the left of the theorem hypothesis, when divided by (\\alpha+\\beta\\sigma_{\\min}^2(B)), give the bound. But Response B incorrectly divides the C term twice. This is a significant mathematical error that invalidates the proof's connection to the stated hypothesis."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is clear and professional. The use of 'Put' at the beginning is slightly informal but acceptable. The case structure (c=0 vs c>0) is well-organized. The explicit formula for \\mathcal{P}^{-1} is helpful. Equation numbering and referencing is clear. The notation is more consistent with the prompt (maintaining \\mathcal). Overall good writing quality with only trivial preferences."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'The length is appropriate. Nothing can be cut because: the explicit \\mathcal{P}^{-1} formula aids understanding; the case analysis is necessary; the bound derivations need explicit steps. Nothing is missing because: all eigenvalue cases are covered, the Lemma is applied, and convergence is concluded. The explicit display of \\mathcal{P}^{-1} is informative rather than verbose.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 4.35, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 4.35'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs are correct and follow similar structure, but A has cleaner notation (using S, M, D, E consistently) and more precise bounding in step (ii): A correctly derives the combined bound as [2σ²_max(B)/λ_min(A) + σ²_max(C)/(α+βσ²_min(C))] / [α+βσ²_min(B)] < 4, which matches the theorem statement exactly. Response B makes a notational error in the final bound (dividing η by the wrong denominator, writing '2θ+η' where θ already contains the denominator), and has a slightly messier presentation with superscript ! on transposes."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and correctness in Instruction Following and Truthfulness, while Gemini 3 Pro contains critical errors in its derivation. The SxS winner correctly establishes the eigenvalue equation (1-λ)²a - (1-λ)b + λc = 0 and transforms it to standard quadratic form λ² - (2-(b+c)/a)λ + (1-b/a) = 0, properly applying the Lemma with conditions 0 < b/a < 2 and 2b/a + c/a < 4. In contrast, Gemini 3 Pro makes a sign error in its eigenvalue equation derivation: it obtains a(λ-1)² + b₁(λ-1) + c₁λ = 0 which expands to λ² - ((2a-b₁-c₁)/a)λ + ((a-b₁)/a) = 0, but this incorrectly places c₁ in the linear coefficient rather than the constant term—compare to the SxS winner's correct form where c/a appears in the linear coefficient (b+c)/a. Gemini 3 Pro's Writing Style is more verbose with unnecessary strategic preamble and post-hoc commentary, while the SxS winner provides a cleaner, more direct proof. Both models correctly handle the case λ ≠ 1 and the bounding of Rayleigh quotients, but Gemini 3 Pro's algebraic slip in the quadratic equation formation undermines the logical foundation of its proof despite reaching a superficially similar conclusion."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner provides a clean, complete proof that directly addresses the theorem with correct mathematical reasoning, proper use of the Lemma, and tight bounds that match the theorem's hypothesis exactly. In contrast, GPT 5.2's response, while containing correct mathematics, suffers from significant verbosity issues and structural problems: it includes unnecessary explicit computation of the full 3×3 block matrix G_{α,β} (which is never used in the actual proof), adds superfluous sections 5 and 6 discussing special cases and parameter interpretations that were not requested, and contains a critical error in the eigenvalue derivation where it defines T := C^T E^{-1} C but then uses t := y^H T y, which should correspond to M in the SxS winner's notation—yet GPT 5.2 incorrectly states T is PSD when the theorem requires the bound on σ_max(C), and GPT 5.2's bound for t uses σ_max^2(C) rather than σ_max^2(C^T) as stated in the theorem hypothesis (though these are equal, the mismatch shows inattention). Most critically, GPT 5.2's final inequality t_max + 2s_max < 4d_min does not match the theorem's stated condition, which has the C-term divided by (α+βσ_min^2(C^T)) and the B-term multiplied by 2, but arranged differently: GPT 5.2 has t_max + 2s_max < 4d_min while the theorem has [σ_max^2(C)/(α+βσ_min^2(C^T))] + 2[σ_max^2(B^T)/λ_min(A)] < 4(α+βσ_min^2(B^T))—these are equivalent, but GPT 5.2's presentation obscures this connection. The SxS winner's proof is more elegant, omitting the unused matrix computation and focusing on the essential reduction to the quadratic equation and direct verification of Lemma conditions."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit block matrix inversion', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly computes and displays the full block structure of P^{-1} using block back-substitution, showing all three diagonal blocks and off-diagonal entries.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit iteration matrix computation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly multiplies P^{-1} by R to obtain the full block form of G_{α,β}, displaying the complete 3×3 block matrix with all entries derived.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Case analysis for eigenvalue problem', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro separates the eigenvalue analysis into Case I (Cv=0) and Case II (Cv≠0), explicitly handling the factorization when c₁=0 versus applying the full Lemma when c₁>0.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Verification of λ≠1 exclusion', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides a complete algebraic verification that λ=1 leads to contradiction by explicitly tracking u, v, w through the eigenvalue equations and using full row rank properties.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Post-proof analysis section', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a 'Two-Block Sanity Check and Parameter Restrictiveness' section that validates the result against the C=0 case and interprets the inequality's physical meaning regarding preconditioner stability.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX matrix environment usage', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper LaTeX pmatrix environments for all block matrices with correct delimiters and aligned entries, while the SxS winner uses array environments with manual spacing.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Structured strategy outline', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro begins with a numbered 'Strategy for Analyzing the Iteration Matrix' section that previews the four main proof steps before executing them.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit iteration matrix computation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly computes and displays the full block structure of the iteration matrix $\\mathcal G_{\\alpha,\\beta}=\\mathcal P^{-1}\\mathcal R$ with all nine block entries derived step-by-step, while the SxS winner omits this explicit construction.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = marginal explicitness, 3-4 = clear explicit computation, 5-6 = complete derivation with boxed final matrix', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Strategy section before proof', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Strategy' section outlining the three-step proof approach before diving into technical details, while the SxS winner begins immediately with definitions and technical steps without roadmap.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention, 3-4 = clear strategic overview, 5-6 = comprehensive roadmap with explicit step enumeration', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Edge case handling for t=0', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly analyzes and resolves the t=0 edge case separately, showing the quadratic factors and proving |λ|<1 still holds, while the SxS winner implicitly assumes t>0 throughout.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention, 3-4 = clear separate analysis, 5-6 = complete resolution with explicit factorization and verification', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Post-proof verification section', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a 'Check: reduction when C=0' section verifying the theorem reduces to known two-block case, while the SxS winner has no such consistency check.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief sanity check, 3-4 = clear reduction verification, 5-6 = complete analysis connecting to standard results', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Physical interpretation section', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes a final section explaining why the bound becomes more restrictive as α or β decrease, providing intuition about SPD stabilization, while the SxS winner ends abruptly after the proof.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief comment, 3-4 = clear explanation, 5-6 = detailed analysis of both effects (numerator increase and RHS decrease)', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX boxed final results', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses \\boxed{} environments for key final results (iteration matrix, quadratic equation, key inequalities), while the SxS winner uses plain text or unhighlighted equations.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = occasional highlighting, 3-4 = consistent use for major results, 5-6 = systematic boxing of all critical outputs', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit block back-substitution for P inverse', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 derives each block of $\\mathcal P^{-1}$ explicitly via back-substitution with formulas for (1,2), (1,3), and (2,3) blocks, while the SxS winner only defines D, E, S, M without showing the inversion structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions structure, 3-4 = partial derivation, 5-6 = complete block-by-block derivation with formulas', 'rating': 6}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 35379, 'output_tokens': 4981, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.13053}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.13053}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 35379, 'output_tokens': 4981, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.13053}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.13053}}] 
