rrectness; comparable on Writing Style; worse on Verbosity. The SxS winner correctly identifies this as Painlevé's Removability Criterion and provides a rigorous proof constructing the extension via Cauchy integrals with proper handling of the limit definition (equation 4), explicit use of Morera's theorem for analyticity verification, and a correct explanation of where boundedness is essential (controlling the constants c_j in Part 1). Gemini 3 Pro contains critical errors: it incorrectly claims 'the sum of the integrals over γ_j evaluates exactly to 0' when only the limit as ε→0 is shown, misstates that '∫_{γ_j} 1/(ζ-z) dζ = 0 since z is strictly outside all γ_j' (this is false—z is inside Γ but the γ_j curves surround K, so z could be outside or inside individual γ_j; the winding number argument is garbled), and incorrectly concludes 'F(z) is analytic on the entirety of the interior of Γ' without properly addressing points in K. The SxS winner's Morera argument correctly handles triangles intersecting K, while Gemini 3 Pro's approach conflates the Cauchy integral formula with a flawed 'masking' argument. Gemini 3 Pro's counterexample with f(z)=1/z is illustrative but its explanation of why boundedness matters is less precise than the SxS winner's technical analysis. The SxS winner is more verbose but with necessary mathematical detail; Gemini 3 Pro's concision comes at the cost of logical gaps."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior mathematical rigor and structural clarity, while GPT 5.2 contains critical errors in definitions and logical gaps. Gemini 3 Pro correctly identifies this as Painlevé's Removability Criterion and provides a clean two-part proof with explicit Cauchy-Schwarz application in estimate (3), proper handling of the limit construction in (4), and correct Morera argument with precise error control. GPT 5.2 incorrectly redefines 'losc' as 'least oscillation' (inf ove