2026-03-25 09:33:33,268 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_3253', 'prompt': 'Let $a, b, c$ be real numbers and let $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ be a quadratic function with integer coefficients such that\n\n$$\n|f(k)|<a k^{2}+b k+c+1,\n$$\n\nfor all integers $k$. Prove that $b^{2}-4 a c<9 a^{2}$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist: [X] Prove b²-4ac < 9a² for quadratic f with integer coefficients. [X] Use condition |f(k)| < ak²+bk+c+1 for all integers k. [X] Handle all cases (a ≥ 1/2 and 0 < a < 1/2). [X] Proper mathematical formatting with LaTeX. No unrequested content, no format violations. The proof is self-contained and addresses exactly what was asked. Faults considered: missing edge case a=0 — but this is implicitly handled since a>0 is derived from g(k)>-1 for large |k|. No significant omissions found.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Fact-check of key claims: [VERIFIED] For quadratic px²+qx+r, second differences equal 2p. [VERIFIED] If |m + b/(2a)| ≤ 1/2, then g(m) = -Δ/(4a) + at² where t = m + b/(2a). [VERIFIED] Linear integer polynomial cannot have 3 integral zeros. [VERIFIED] If a ≥ 1/2, then a²+4a ≤ 9a² (since 4a ≤ 8a² when a ≥ 1/2). Minor issue: The claim 'for a < 1/2 we have p=0' — this follows from -a < p ≤ a with p integer, so |p| < 1/2 implies p=0. This is correct but slightly terse. No false claims detected; all mathematical reasoning is sound."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Establishes a > 0 from growth conditions. [CORRECT] Shows -a < p ≤ a for leading coefficient. [CORRECT] Case a ≥ 1/2: uses vertex shift to get b²-4ac < a²+4a ≤ 9a². [CORRECT] Case 0 < a < 1/2: shows p=0 (f linear), uses discriminant condition to find 3 consecutive integers in [α,β], forces f to have 3 zeros, contradiction. [CORRECT] Conclusion covers all cases. The inequality b²-4ac < 9a² is proven with correct reasoning throughout.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Structure is clear with numbered sections. Mathematical notation is precise and consistent. One minor issue: 'right–hand side' uses an en-dash which is slightly non-standard (should be hyphen or space). The logical flow is excellent: setup → case analysis → conclusion. No run-on sentences, no jargon issues, appropriate formal mathematical tone. Paragraph breaks well-placed. Could be slightly more explicit about why g(k) > -1 implies a > 0 (the 'large |k|' argument is brief but clear to intended audience)."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The proof is concise and efficient. Nothing can be cut because: (1) the setup with h(x) is essential for the bound, (2) the case distinction is necessary, (3) the vertex shift calculation is minimal. Nothing is missing because: the key insight (using three consecutive integers when discriminant is large) is fully developed, and the bound a²+4a ≤ 9a² is verified. The response achieves the proof in minimal space without sacrificing rigor. Ideal length for a competition mathematics problem.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Checklist analysis: [X] Prove b²-4ac < 9a². [X] Use condition on |f(k)|. [X] Consider quadratic f with integer coefficients. [PARTIAL] Handle all cases — the response adds an unnecessary section 4 on a=0 that wasn't required (the problem states a,b,c are real numbers with the inequality holding for all k, which implicitly requires a>0; this addition is unrequested). [X] Proper formatting. The 'Note' at the end discussing 'integer-valued' polynomials is also unrequested commentary. These additions cap the score at 4."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Fact-check reveals serious issues: [FALSE] Claim 'a ≥ |A| ≥ 1' — this is wrong. The argument shows a ≥ |A|, but A is a non-zero integer so |A| ≥ 1, hence a ≥ 1. However, this contradicts the problem which allows 0 < a < 1. The response later uses this to claim 4a ≤ 4a², but this fails when 0 < a < 1. [QUESTIONABLE] The conclusion 'b²-4ac < 5a² < 9a²' only holds when a ≥ 1. For 0 < a < 1, the bound a²+4a could exceed 9a² (e.g., a=0.1 gives 0.01+0.4=0.41 > 0.09). The response completely misses the case 0 < a < 1/2 that Response A handles. [VERIFIED] The discriminant invariance under shift is correct. [VERIFIED] The finite difference argument gives a ≥ |A|. The false claim about a ≥ 1 and missing small-a case severely damages truthfulness."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] Setup and strategy outline. [CORRECT] Finite difference analysis showing a ≥ |A|. [INCORRECT] Conclusion that a ≥ 1 — this incorrectly restricts to a ≥ 1 when 0 < a < 1 is possible. [INCORRECT] Final bound b²-4ac < 5a² < 9a² fails for small a. The response does not prove the required inequality for all valid (a,b,c). The case 0 < a < 1/2 is completely missing, making the proof incomplete. The boundary case a=0 discussion is irrelevant to the actual proof. The core mathematical argument has a fatal gap.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Quality audit: The writing is generally clear with good structure (numbered sections). Issues: 'givenquadratic' and 'theminimum' and 'to neatly bound' — missing spaces (3 instances). 'concave down parabola would dive' — informal phrasing. 'vacuously unfulfillable' — awkward construction. The strategy section is overly verbose with enumerated points that don't match the actual proof structure. The 'Note' at the end is distracting and not clearly marked as optional commentary. Overall readable but with noticeable polish issues."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Length analysis: Significant padding identified. Section 1 'Strategy' is overly long with 3 enumerated points that don't directly correspond to the proof steps. The 'Note' at the end is unnecessary speculation. Section 4 on a=0 is irrelevant — the problem conditions implicitly exclude this. However, the core proof in sections 2-3 is missing critical content (the 0 < a < 1 case). Specific cuts: 'Strategy' could be 1 sentence; 'Note' should be deleted; Section 4 should be deleted. Missing: proper handling of small a. The response is simultaneously too long in places and too short where it matters."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 3.4, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 3.40'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better. Both solutions correctly prove the result, but Response A handles the critical case 0 < a < 1/2 (where f becomes linear) with a rigorous three-roots argument, while Response B incorrectly concludes a ≥ 1 from |A| ≥ 1, missing that A could be 0 when a < 1/2. Response A's case analysis is complete and correct; Response B's 'boundary case a = 0' section is actually irrelevant since a > 0 was already established, and the note about integer-valued polynomials reveals confusion about the actual tightness of the bound."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner provides a cleaner, more rigorous proof with better case handling; Gemini 3 Pro has significant logical gaps and weaker bounds. The SxS winner correctly handles the subtle case where 0 < a < 1/2 (where f becomes linear, p=0) using a root-spacing argument with three consecutive integers, which is essential for the tight bound 9a². Gemini 3 Pro incorrectly concludes 'a ≥ 1' from |A| ≥ 1, but this fails when A=0 (f linear), and their bound b²-4ac < 5a² is weaker than needed; they also miss the critical small-a case entirely, making their proof incomplete. The SxS winner's inequality (3) giving b²-4ac < a²+4a is sharp, and their case analysis for a ≥ 1/2 vs 0 < a < 1/2 is exhaustive, while Gemini 3 Pro's 'boundary case a=0' discussion is irrelevant since a>0 is already forced by growth conditions."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) provides a cleaner, more elegant proof with better structure and tighter bounds. The SxS winner handles the case analysis more efficiently: it establishes -a < p ≤ a for the leading coefficient and directly derives the bound b²-4ac < a²+4a ≤ 9a² when a ≥ 1/2, then treats 0 < a < 1/2 separately with a concise contradiction argument using three consecutive integers. GPT 5.2's proof is correct but more verbose, with redundant sections (the a=0 case is unnecessary since the problem assumes a quadratic bound ak²+bk+c, and if a=0 the inequality |f(k)|<bk+c+1 cannot hold for large |k| anyway, making the vacuous truth argument overwrought). GPT 5.2 also uses n,n+1,n+2 where the SxS winner's u-1,u,u+1 is more symmetric. The SxS winner's equation (3) with the clever choice of m minimizing |m+b/2a| shows superior technical finesse. Both proofs correctly identify the core idea—forcing f to have three integer zeros—but the SxS winner executes it with less redundancy and sharper quantitative control."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit strategy outline', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a clearly labeled 'Strategy' subsection that outlines the proof approach before executing technical steps, while the SxS winner dives directly into calculations without upfront strategic framing.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of approach, 3-4 = clear multi-step strategy outlined, 5-6 = comprehensive strategic roadmap with numbered steps', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Second finite difference analysis', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses the discrete second difference operator Δ² to rigorously derive the constraint a ≥ |A| ≥ 1, while the SxS winner only establishes -a < p ≤ a without connecting to the integer nature of A.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = mentions finite differences without full derivation, 3-4 = applies operator correctly with partial justification, 5-6 = complete derivation with clear constraint a ≥ 1', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Invariant discriminant under shift', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states and uses the invariance of discriminant under horizontal shifts (b² - 4ac = b̃² - 4ãc̃) as a key structural insight, while the SxS winner computes at a shifted point without highlighting this invariance.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = shifts coordinates without noting invariance, 3-4 = mentions invariance in passing, 5-6 = explicitly labels and leverages invariance as a proof technique', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Boundary case a = 0 treatment', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated section analyzing and eliminating the a = 0 case by showing the inequality becomes impossible for large |k|, while the SxS winner implicitly assumes a > 0 without addressing this edge case.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief mention of a > 0, 3-4 = some analysis of boundary, 5-6 = complete rigorous elimination of a = 0 case with growth argument', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'LaTeX display equation formatting', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper display math delimiters ($$...$$) with equation numbers and aligned environments, while the SxS winner uses inline formatting with manual spacing commands like \\medskip and \\qquad.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = basic LaTeX, 3-4 = consistent display math, 5-6 = professional mathematical typesetting with structural clarity', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Tightness discussion and generalization', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a parenthetical note discussing how the bound 9a² becomes tight if f is merely integer-valued rather than integer-coefficient, showing deeper understanding of problem boundaries, which the SxS winner lacks.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = brief remark on optimality, 3-4 = substantive discussion of bound tightness, 5-6 = explicit generalization to related problem variants', 'rating': 5}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit strategy statement', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a clearly labeled 'Strategy' section at the beginning that explicitly states the proof approach (contrapositive via three consecutive integers), while the SxS winner buries this insight within the case analysis.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if marginally better signposting, 3-4 if clearly more explicit roadmap, 5-6 if substantially superior strategic transparency', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Structural section headers', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses numbered section headers (## 1), ## 2), etc.) with descriptive titles that track logical progression, while the SxS winner uses minimal \\textbf markers with less informative labels.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if slightly better organization, 3-4 if noticeably clearer structure, 5-6 if dramatically superior navigability', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Boundary case handling', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated section (## 5) explicitly treating the a=0 boundary case with clear reasoning about vacuous truth, while the SxS winner completely omits this case.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor case added, 3-4 if significant missing case addressed, 5-6 if critical completeness gap filled', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Contrapositive structure clarity', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly frames the proof as contrapositive (assume Δ≥9a², derive contradiction) with clear logical markers, while the SxS winner uses a mixed direct/contrapositive approach with less transparent logical flow.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if slightly clearer logic, 3-4 if noticeably more transparent, 5-6 if substantially superior logical exposition', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX display equation formatting', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses proper LaTeX display environments with aligned equations and clear tag references (e.g., (1), (2), (3)), while the SxS winner has denser inline math and less consistent equation numbering.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if minor formatting improvement, 3-4 if noticeably cleaner presentation, 5-6 if substantially superior mathematical typography', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit contradiction articulation', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly states 'Contradiction' and summarizes the logical consequence, while the SxS winner marks completion with '∎' without equally explicit contradiction signaling.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 if marginally clearer, 3-4 if noticeably more explicit, 5-6 if dramatically superior logical closure', 'rating': 3}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 18927, 'output_tokens': 3933, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.08505}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.08505}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 18927, 'output_tokens': 3933, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.08505}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.08505}}] 
