 tableaux in Step 3, while Gemini's padding approach creates an invalid matching field extension and obscures the actual combinatorial structure."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) provides a superior proof through structural induction with clear geometric decomposition, while GPT 5.2's proof contains critical logical gaps. Gemini 3 Pro correctly identifies that the Grassmannian Lemma only applies to n-row tableaux, and develops a sophisticated three-step induction (pure columns, rows ≥3, first two rows) that handles the general case by reducing to the Grassmannian case through careful padding. GPT 5.2 incorrectly applies the Grassmannian Lemma directly to arbitrary tableaux T and S without verifying they have n rows—this is a fatal error since the lemma explicitly requires 'n rows' and the theorem concerns tableaux of arbitrary height. GPT 5.2's 'telescoping argument' is technically correct given its (invalid) application of the lemma, but the foundation collapses. Additionally, Gemini 3 Pro's base case n=2 is properly justified, while GPT 5.2 omits any base case or induction structure entirely, failing to address how the result propagates across different values of n and ℓ."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit padding construction', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly constructs a padding scheme with dummy variables to reduce to the Grassmannian case, while the SxS winner uses an inductive argument without this explicit reduction.', 'scale': "1-6 scale: 1-2 = padding mentioned but not fully executed; 3-4 = padding scheme described with some gaps; 5-6 = complete, explicit construction with dummy set D and extended blocks B_1', B_2'", 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Column type preservation analysis', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly analyzes that T and T' must have identical multisets of column types (pure B_1, pure B_2, or mixed) for each column length, which the SxS winner does not explicitl