ed structure, rubric walking through evaluation dimensions, symmetrical diplomatic comparisons, excessive hedging, buzzword density, and generic summary sentences. The comments follow identical structural patterns (SxS winner strengths first, then external model weaknesses, then nuanced conclusion), suggesting AI-generation rather than authentic human evaluation.'}, 'severity': 1, 'flagged_fields': {'ab_comment': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "Contains hedging patterns ('slightly better', 'slight unjustified step', 'larger gap') and formulaic comparison structure. Low perplexity with predictable phrasing."}, 'human_ab_gpt_comment': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "Extensive AI markers: rubric walking (Instruction Following, Writing Style, Truthfulness), formulaic 'while X, Y' structure, excessive hedging ('more critically', 'however', 'actually correct'), buzzwords ('elegant', 'pedagogical', 'obscures', 'brute-force'), generic summary ('Overall, the SxS winner's...'), and no personal voice."}, 'human_ab_gemini_comment': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "Identical structural pattern to GPT comment: rubric walking, 'superior in X, Y, Z' opening, critical errors highlighted, verbosity critique, generic conclusion. Formulaic template with 'demonstrates superior mathematical writing discipline' and 'compact 3-paragraph structure versus Gemini's 5 labeled sections' - precise but unnatural recall."}, 'rubric_fields': [{'rubric_index': 1, 'name': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "'Explicit Polynomial Construction' - overly formal, technical naming convention typical of AI. Lacks human colloquialism."}, 'description': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "Dense technical description with parenthetical clauses and complete grammatical precision. 'Response constructs... and explains why...' - procedural, AI-style phrasing."}}, {'rubric_index': 2, 'name': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "'Lemma Application with Explicit Parameters' - excessively formal, structured like a documentation string rather than human note."}, 'description': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "Extensive enumeration with 'and explicitly specifies: the sets S_i, the sets D_i...' - exhaustive, mechanical listing. 'clearly states' - filler transition. Perfect recall of all parameters without uncertainty."}}]}}, 'justification_grammar_check': {'status': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "American English spellings detected: 'behavior' should be 'behaviour', 'center' should be 'centre', 'analyze' should be 'analyse'. Multiple violations of Indian English requirements."}, 'severity': 2, 'flagged_fields': {'ab_comment': {'result': 'pass', 'issue': ''}, 'human_ab_gpt_comment': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "American English: 'behavior' (should be 'behaviour'), 'center' (should be 'centre' in mathematical context)"}, 'human_ab_gemini_comment': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "American English: 'analyze' (should be 'analyse'), 'behavior' (should be 'behaviour')"}, 'rubric_fields': [{'rubric_index': 1, 'description': {'result': 'pass', 'issue': ''}}, {'rubric_index': 2, 'description': {'result': 'pass', 'issue': ''}}]}}}, 'rubric_comment_grounding': {'status': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': 'Rubric names are overly generic and formal; descriptions contain AI-style exhaustive enumeration. Ratings of 6 for both GPT and Gemini on both rubrics are unjustified given the comments suggest mixed performance. Comment grounding in responses shows overstatement of content.'}, 'severity': 1, 'rubrics': [{'rubric_index': 1, 'name_grounded': {'result': False, 'issue': "Rubric name 'Explicit Polynomial Construction' is generic and formal. While related to proof content, it lacks specificity about what 'explicit' means. The human_ab_gpt_comment discusses polynomial construction but criticizes GPT's approach as 'unnecessarily complicated' and 'obscures' - this doesn't align with a rating of 6 (substantially better)."}, 'description_grounded': {'result': False, 'issue': "Description claims rubric evaluates if response 'constructs the polynomial... and explains why all nonzero cube vertices are zeros of multiplicity at least 3.' However, human_ab_gpt_comment states GPT 'makes the proof unnecessarily complicated' and has 'notational clutter' - a 6 rating (substantially better) contradicts this critique. The description itself is AI-style exhaustive listing."}, 'gpt_rating_consistent': {'result': False, 'issue': "GPT rating of 6 (substantially better, SxS winner clearly lacks this quality) contradicts human_ab_gpt_comment which states GPT 'makes the proof unnecessarily complicated,' introduces 'redundant claims,' and has 'notational clutter.' The comment says SxS winner is 'superior for expert readers' - this is opposite of rating 6 meaning."}, 'gemini_rating_consistent': {'result': False, 'issue': "Gemini rating of 6 contradicts human_ab_gemini_comment which states Gemini 'contains critical errors,' has 'flawed linear algebra,' and 'verbosity issues.' Rating 6 means Gemini is substantially better on this rubric, but comment says Gemini is worse on explicit construction (unnecessarily convoluted Taylor expansion)."}}, {'rubric_index': 2, 'name_grounded': {'result': False, 'issue': "Rubric name 'Lemma Application with Explicit Parameters' is generic. The human_ab_gemini_comment criticizes Gemini's lemma application as 'unnecessarily convoluted' - inconsistent with high rating."}, 'description_grounded': {'result': False, 'issue': "Description requires explicit specification of S_i, D_i, g_i, l_i, and f. human_ab_gemini_comment states Gemini has 'critical errors' in its analysis and 'flawed linear algebra' - this contradicts rating 6. The SxS winner (Response A) actually provides cleaner lemma application per comments."}, 'gpt_rating_consistent': {'result': False, 'issue': "GPT rating of 6 inconsistent with human_ab_gpt_comment: GPT 'introduces unnecessary complexity,' has 'confusing' presentation, and 'obscures the main argument.' Rating 6 requires GPT to be substantially better at explicit parameters, but comment favors SxS winner's 'clean, direct proof.'"}, 'gemini_rating_consistent': {'result': False, 'issue': "Gemini rating of 6 severely contradicts human_ab_gemini_comment which states Gemini has 'critical errors,' 'incorrectly states' key claims, and 'flawed linear algebra.' The comment explicitly says SxS winner is 'superior' - opposite of rating 6 meaning."}}], 'comment_grounded_in_responses': {'gpt_comment_grounded_in_responses': {'result': False, 'issue': "human_ab_gpt_comment claims GPT has 'critical error' in final calculation: 'it states u(e_i+e_j) = c·(2-1)² = c, but then concludes c=0 from this.' However, reviewing gpt_response, the final calculation actually shows: '0=u(e_i+e_j)=c·(2-1)²=c, hence c=0.' This is correct mathematics - (2-1)²=1, so c·1=c, and c=0 follows from 0=c. The comment misrepresents this as a 'critical error' when it's actually correct. The comment also claims GPT's presentation is 'confusing' but this is subjective; the mathematical reasoning is sound. Overstatement of errors constitutes ungrounded critique."}, 'gemini_comment_grounded_in_responses': {'result': False, 'issue': "human_ab_gemini_comment claims Gemini 'incorrectly states u(x) is exactly its degree-2 Taylor expansion around e_k.' Reviewing gemini_response: 'u(x) is exactly its degree-2 Taylor expansion around e_k' - this is stated in Section 3. However, for a degree ≤2 polynomial, it IS its own degree-2 Taylor expansion. The comment frames this as error when it's technically correct. Comment also claims 'H(e_1 - e_k) = 0 implies the k-th column equals the 1st column is backwards' - but gemini_response says 'H(e_1 - e_k) = 0, implying the k-th column equals the 1st column' which is correct (H applied to difference being zero means columns are equal). The comment's critique of 'backwards' appears itself backwards. These appear to be manufactured criticisms not grounded in actual errors."}}}, 'ab_preference_comment_grounding': {'status': {'result': 'pass', 'issue': ''}, 'severity': None, 'preference_matches_comment': {'result': True, 'issue': ''}, 'ab_comment_grounded_in_responses': {'result': True, 'issue': ''}}, 'rubric_rating_justification': {'status': {'result': 'fail', 'issue': "All rubric ratings of 6 are unjustified given comparison comments. Rating scale: 5-6 means 'substantially better, SxS winner clearly lacks this quality.' Comments state SxS winner is superior in most aspects, with external models having 'critical errors,' 'unnecessary complexity,' and 'flawed linear algebra.' Ratings should be negative (SxS winner better) or low positive (marginally better), not 6."}, 'severity': 1, 'rubrics': [{'rubric_index': 1, 'gpt_rating_justified': {'result': False, 'issue': "Rating 6 requires 'substantially better, SxS winner clearly lacks this quality.' human_ab_gpt_comment states GPT 'makes the proof unnecessarily complicated,' has 'notational clutter,' and SxS winner is 'superior for expert readers.' This is opposite of rating 6. Should be rating 1-2 (marginally better) or negative (worse)."}, 'gemini_rating_justified': {'result': False, 'issue': "Rating 6 completely unjustified. Comment states Gemini has 'critical errors,' 'flawed linear algebra,' and SxS winner is 'superior.' Gemini is rated worse on this rubric, not substantially better. Rating should be negative."}}, {'rubric_index': 2, 'gpt_rating_justified': {'result': False, 'issue': "Rating 6 unjustified. Comment states GPT 'introduces unnecessary complexity' and 'obscures the main argument' with 'redundant claims.' SxS winner has 'cleaner logical flow.' Rating should be low (1-2) or negative, not 6."}, 'gemini_rating_justified': {'result': False, 'issue': "Rating 6 severely unjustified. Comment explicitly states Gemini has 'critical errors,' 'incorrectly states' key claims, and 'flawed linear algebra.' SxS winner is 'superior.' Rating should be negative, not 6."}}]}, 'external_preference_comment_grounding': {'status': {'result': 'pass', 'issue': ''}, 'severity': None, '