e, verifiable, and elegantly handles the n≥5 boundary case. Gemini 3 Pro's odd-divisor class approach, while creative, contains critical flaws: the claim that 'exactly two odd classes have their maximums bounded by n+2' is unsupported and confusing (the argument conflates class maximums with actual elements), the worst-case analysis for 3q_E≤2n has a gap at n=5 for even n that requires ad hoc patching, and the overall structure buries the key construction under excessive case analysis. The SxS winner's sharpness verification in Section 4 explicitly confirms n=4 fails with X={5,6,7,8}, while Gemini 3 Pro's boundary analysis is fragmented and less convincing. Both models correctly identify n=5 as the threshold, but the SxS winner's proof is more direct, complete, and pedagogically sound."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) and GPT 5.2 both solve the problem correctly, but Gemini 3 Pro provides a cleaner, more direct argument with superior clarity. Gemini 3 Pro uses the elegant structural observation that avoiding double-pairs forces X ⊆ L = {n+1,...,2n}, then immediately constructs the pair (2k,3k) with k = ⌊(n+2)/2⌋, giving lcm = 6k ≤ 3n+6 through straightforward inequalities. GPT 5.2 introduces unnecessary complexity with its 'compression move' framework—while mathematically valid, this creates a more convoluted path to the same conclusion, requiring careful tracking of iterative replacements and separate case analysis for even/odd n in the bounds. Gemini 3 Pro's single formula for k handles all cases uniformly, and its verification that 3k ≤ 2n for n ≥ 5 is more elegant than GPT 5.2's split analysis. Both correctly identify n=5 as the sharp threshold with counterexamples for n=4, but Gemini 3 Pro's presentation is more pedagogically effective with clearer sectioning and less notational overhead."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Odd-divisor class partitioning framework', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro introduces and explicitly defines a structural framework using 'greatest odd divisor' classes to partition the set {1,2,...,2n}, whereas the SxS winner uses only the simpler double-pair observation without this more general organizational structure.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = framework mentioned briefly, 3-4 = framework developed with clear definitions, 5-6 = framework fully developed with explicit notation and used throughout proof', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit case analysis with Pigeonhole Principle', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the Pigeonhole Principle application and formally structures the proof into two exhaustive cases (same odd-divisor class vs. one from each class), while the SxS winner's case structure emerges implicitly from the argument.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = cases mentioned informally, 3-4 = clear case labels with some formal structure, 5-6 = rigorous exhaustive case analysis with explicit logical coverage', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LCM formula derivation with explicit cases', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly derives the LCM formula LCM(q·2^a, 3q·2^b) = 3q·2^max(a,b) and splits into sub-cases based on exponent comparison (a > b vs b ≥ a), whereas the SxS winner computes LCM(2k,3k) = 6k as a single calculation without this structural analysis.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = formula stated without derivation, 3-4 = formula derived with some case structure, 5-6 = complete derivation with exhaustive case analysis and clear logical flow', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Worst-case boundary analysis for threshold n=5', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly analyzes why n=5 is the threshold by checking the worst-case boundary condition 3q_E ≤ 2n for both parity cases of n, showing n=4 fails and n≥5 succeeds, while the SxS winner only verifies n=4 fails by example without explaining why n=5 is minimal.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = threshold mentioned without analysis, 3-4 = some boundary reasoning provided, 5-6 = complete worst-case analysis proving minimality of threshold', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent LaTeX delimiters ($...$ and $$...$$) throughout, while the SxS winner mixes \\(...\\) and \\[...\\] with some display math using \\tag{}; Gemini 3 Pro's formatting is more uniform.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor inconsistencies, 3-4 = mostly consistent with some issues, 5-6 = perfectly consistent delimiter usage throughout', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Explicit variable definitions and notation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines notation such as x_k, M_k, q_E, E with clear semantic meaning attached to each, whereas the SxS winner uses fewer defined variables (only k, L, a, b) with less explicit semantic structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minimal notation defined, 3-4 = key variables explicitly defined, 5-6 = comprehensive notation system with clear definitions for all structural elements', 'rating': 4}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Compression argument explicitness', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly defines and justifies the compression move X' := (X\\setminus{a})∪{2a} with clear algebraic reasoning showing lcm(2a,y) ≥ lcm(a,y), while the SxS winner merely states the extremal configuration without deriving it through an iterative process.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if compression mentioned vaguely, 3-4 if described but missing justification, 5-6 if fully formalized with inequality proof', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Case analysis structure', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses explicit labeled cases (Case 1: divisibility pair, then primitive assumption (⋆)) with clear logical markers, while the SxS winner buries the case distinction in prose without structural separation.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 if cases exist but unclear, 3-4 if labeled but logic tangled, 5-6 if pristine logical flow with explicit markers', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Parity case analysis for bounds', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly handles even and odd n s