2026-03-25 09:46:17,604 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_667', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $V:=\\{x_1+\\ldots+x_n=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb R^n.$ For any $i,j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ and $a\\in\\mathbb R,$ let $H_{ij}^a:=\\{x \\in V \\mid x_i-x_j=a\\}.$\n\nLet ${\\mathcal A}$ be any finite arrangement of hyperplanes of the form $H_{ij}^a=\\{x_i-x_j=a\\}$ with $a>0.$ The connected components of the complement to ${\\mathcal A}$ are called regions. The region $R_0$ containing the origin is called fundamental region.\n\nThe labeling of a region $R$ is a function $\\lambda_R:\\{1, \\dots, n\\} \\to \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0}$ where $\\lambda_R(i)$ equals to the number of hyperplanes $H_{ij}^l$ separating $R$ from $R_0.$\n\nLet $m_{ij}$ denote the number of hyperplanes of the form $H_{ij}^a$ in ${\\mathcal A}.$ The oriented multigraph $G=G_{\\mathcal A}$ is defined as follows. The vertex set is $V_G:=\\{1,\\ldots, n\\},$ and the edge $i\\rightarrow j$ has multiplicity $m_{ij}.$\n\n**Definition 5** A function $f:\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\to\\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0}$ is called a {$G$-parking function} if for any non-empty subset $I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ there exists a vertex $i\\in I$ such that the number of edges $(i\\rightarrow j)\\in E_G,$ count with multiplicity, such that $j\\notin I$ is greater than or equal to $f(i).$\n\n**Lemma** Let $R$ be a region of an arrangement ${\\mathcal A}$ with the corresponding multigraph $G=G_{\\mathcal A}.$ Then the label $\\lambda_R$ is a $G$-parking function.\n\n**Proof of Lemma** Consider the subarrangement ${\\mathcal A}_R\\subset {\\mathcal A}$ consisting of hyperplanes of ${\\mathcal A}$ that separate the region $R$ from the origin. Let $G_R=G_{{\\mathcal A}_R}\\subset G_{\\mathcal A}$ be the corresponding sub-multigraph. The key observation is that $G_R$ cannot contain oriented cycles. Indeed, suppose that there is a cycle $i_1\\rightarrow i_2\\rightarrow\\ldots\\rightarrow i_k\\rightarrow i_1.$ Then it follows that there exist hyperplanes $H_{i_1i_2}^{a_1},\\ldots,H_{i_ki_1}^{a_k}\\in {\\mathcal A}$ separating region $R$ from the origin. Let $r=(r_1,\\ldots,r_n)\\in R$ be a point. One gets\n$$\nr_{i_1}-r_{i_2}>a_1,\n$$\n$$\n\\vdots\n$$\n$$\nr_{i_k}-r_{i_1}>a_k.\n$$\nSumming the inequalities up, one gets\n$$\n0>a_1+\\ldots+a_k,\n$$\nwhich is impossible, because $a_i>0$ for all $i$ by construction. Contradiction.\n\nLet us verify the $G$-parking function condition for $\\lambda_R.$ Let $I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots n\\}$ be any non-empty subset. It follows that there exists $i\\in I$ such that if $i\\rightarrow j$ is an edge of $G_R,$ then $j\\notin I.$ Indeed, otherwise $I$ contains an oriented cycle of $G_R.$ Note that $\\lambda_R(i)$ is equal to the outgoing index of $i$ in $G_R,$ and all the outgoing edges from $i$ leave the subset $I.$ Therefore, $\\lambda_R$ is a $G$-parking function.\n## Theorem\nLet ${\\mathcal A}$ be a finite arrangement of hyperplanes of the form $H_{ij}^a=\\{x_i-x_j=a\\}$ with $a>0.$ Let $G=G_{\\mathcal A}$ be the associated oriented multigraph. The labeling map $R \\mapsto \\lambda_R$ is a surjective map from the set of regions of ${\\mathcal A}$ to the set of $G$-parking functions.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the instruction to provide a complete proof under the heading ## Proof. It uses the provided Lemma (cited explicitly as 'Lemma (proved in the statement)'). The proof is cleaned up and complete. Minor issue: the proof uses a deletion-contraction approach which is sophisticated but valid; no explicit 'multiple approaches' are shown, though the