 separate the region $R$ from the origin. Let $G_R=G_{{\\mathcal A}_R}\\subset G_{\\mathcal A}$ be the corresponding sub-multigraph. The key observation is that $G_R$ cannot contain oriented cycles. Indeed, suppose that there is a cycle $i_1\\rightarrow i_2\\rightarrow\\ldots\\rightarrow i_k\\rightarrow i_1.$ Then it follows that there exist hyperplanes $H_{i_1i_2}^{a_1},\\ldots,H_{i_ki_1}^{a_k}\\in {\\mathcal A}$ separating region $R$ from the origin. Let $r=(r_1,\\ldots,r_n)\\in R$ be a point. One gets\n$$\nr_{i_1}-r_{i_2}>a_1,\n$$\n$$\n\\vdots\n$$\n$$\nr_{i_k}-r_{i_1}>a_k.\n$$\nSumming the inequalities up, one gets\n$$\n0>a_1+\\ldots+a_k,\n$$\nwhich is impossible, because $a_i>0$ for all $i$ by construction. Contradiction.\n\nLet us verify the $G$-parking function condition for $\\lambda_R.$ Let $I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots n\\}$ be any non-empty subset. It follows that there exists $i\\in I$ such that if $i\\rightarrow j$ is an edge of $G_R,$ then $j\\notin I.$ Indeed, otherwise $I$ contains an oriented cycle of $G_R.$ Note that $\\lambda_R(i)$ is equal to the outgoing index of $i$ in $G_R,$ and all the outgoing edges from $i$ leave the subset $I.$ Therefore, $\\lambda_R$ is a $G$-parking function.\n## Theorem\nLet ${\\mathcal A}$ be a finite arrangement of hyperplanes of the form $H_{ij}^a=\\{x_i-x_j=a\\}$ with $a>0.$ Let $G=G_{\\mathcal A}$ be the associated oriented multigraph. The labeling map $R \\mapsto \\lambda_R$ is a surjective map from the set of regions of ${\\mathcal A}$ to the set of $G$-parking functions.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to provide a proof under '## Proof' heading. However, it adds unrequested content: the 'Deletion–contraction for parking functions' subsection with its own proof (marked with '∎'), which is a separate lemma not asked for. The prompt asks for 'a complete and correct proof' of the theorem, not auxiliary lemmas with their own sub-proofs. This addition of unrequested structural complexity caps the score at 4. The response does use the provided Lemma correctly and attempts the main theorem."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Multiple claims are questionable or unverifiable: [1] In Case (i), the claim 'If R'⊂{x_i-x_j>a} then G_{R'}∪{i→j} contains a directed cycle' is asserted without proof—this requires showing that crossing H_{ij}^a creates a cycle, but the reasoning is compressed and unverified. [2] In Case (ii), the claim 'If R'⊂{x_i-x_j<a} then G_{R'} would contain a directed path j⇝i' is stated definitively without justification—this is a significant technical gap. [3] The deletion-contraction dichotomy itself is presented as 'exactly one' holds, but the proof provided doesn't fully establish mutual exclusivity. These unverified technical claims, stated as fact without hedging, cap the score at 3."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "The proof attempt contains significant gaps that may render it incorrect. [INCORRECT/PARTIALLY CORRECT]: The handling of Case (ii) contains a logical error: the response claims 'If R'⊂{x_i-x_j<a} then G_{R'} would contain a directed path j⇝i' to avoid crossing H, but this reasoning is circular and unsupported. The existence of such a path is not guaranteed by the region's position relative to H. [MISSING]: The proof does not verify that the constructed region R in each case actually has the correct label λ_R=f—the argument jumps from 'R is a region' to 'λ_R=f' without checking that exactly the right hyperplanes are crossed. The deletion-contraction lemma, while interesting, is not part of the required theorem and distracts from completing the main proof rigorously."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 4, 'reason': "The writing has identifiable issues: [1] Inconsistent notation: 'V\\setminus I' appears where V is the ambient space, but I⊂{1,…,n} is an index set—this type mismatch is confusing. [2] The symbol '⇝' for 'directed path' is introduced 