2026-03-25 09:47:11,759 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_oph response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_12632', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\nLet $k$ and $N$ be positive integers with $k\\ge2$. Let $a_1,a_2,a_3,\\dots$ be an infinite sequence of positive integers that satisfies:\n1) The set $\\{a_1,a_2,\\dots, a_N\\}$ contains at least $k$ distinct values.\n2) For $n>N$, $a_n$ is the number of appearances of the $k$-th least frequent value in $(a_1,a_2,a_3,\\dots, a_{n-1})$. (the $k$-th element in the sorted array of the number of appearances of each value)\nProve that the sequence is eventually constant.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'All instruction elements satisfied: (1) Complete and correct proof provided under ## Proof heading, (2) Multiple approaches considered (deficit tracking, stable step analysis), (3) Honest acknowledgment of where k≥2 is needed (Section 5), (4) Cleaned up final proof structure with clear sections. No unrequested content added. Format matches requirements exactly.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Major claims verified: (1) D_n is non-increasing and non-negative — verified by case analysis in equation (2), (2) Existence of M where c_n(a_{n+1}) ≥ a_{n+1} — follows from D_n finiteness, (3) Once c_t(C) > C, the k-th smallest frequency remains C — verified in Section 3. One unverifiable claim: 'the index t+1 is independent of how ties are resolved' — this is asserted without proof in Section 4, though likely true. No false claims identified. The k=1 counterexample in Section 5 is concrete and verifiable."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Core requirement [CORRECT]: