varphi -\\frac{1}{2}(V + 2\\partial^{\\tau}\\varphi\\partial_{\\tau}\\varphi)g_{\\mu\\nu}+2(F_{\\mu\\tau}F_{\\nu}^{\\tau}-\\frac{1}{4}F^2 g_{\\mu\\nu}) +m_0^2 e^{-2\\beta \\varphi} \\chi_{\\mu\\nu} \\\\\n\\nabla^2\\varphi &= \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial\\varphi} - \\frac{\\beta}{2} e^{-2\\beta\\varphi} \\mathcal{L}_m \\\\\n\\nabla_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu} &= 0\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $\\chi_{\\mu\\nu}$ is given by the variation of the graviton action with respect to the metric $g_{\\mu\\nu}$.\n\nWe consider a static spherically symmetric black hole solution with the metric ansatz:\n$$\nds^2 = -f(r) dt^2 +f^{-1}(r) dr^2+ r^2R^2(r) d\\Omega^2,\n$$\nwhere $f(r)$ and $R(r)$ are functions of $r$, and $d\\Omega^2=d\\theta^2+\\sin^2\\theta d\\phi^2$. The electromagnetic field has a component $A=A_t(r)dt$.\n\nThe reference metric $h_{\\mu\\nu}$ is chosen to be:\n$$\nh_{\\mu\\nu}=diag(0, 0, c_0^2, c_0^2\\sin^2\\theta),\n$$\nwhere $c_0$ is a positive parameter.\n## Theorem\nFor the Maxwell-dilaton-massive gravity theory defined in the setup, a class of analytical solutions for charged black holes exists. This solution is characterized by the following functions, where $\\alpha$ and $\\delta$ are constants:\n\nThe dilaton field is given by:\n$$\n\\varphi (r) = \\frac{\\alpha}{1+\\alpha^2} \\ln\\frac{\\delta}{r}.\n$$\n\nThe metric function $R(r)$ is related to the dilaton field by:\n$$\nR(r)=e^{\\alpha \\varphi} = \\left(\\frac{\\delta}{r}\\right)^{\\frac{\\alpha^2}{1+\\alpha^2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dilaton potential $V(\\varphi)$ has the form:\n\\begin{align*}\nV(\\varphi)=& \\frac{2 \\alpha ^2 e^{\\frac{2 \\varphi }{\\alpha }}}{\\left(\\alpha ^2-1\\right) \\delta ^2} +2 \\Lambda e^{2 \\alpha \\varphi } -\\frac{2 \\alpha ^2 Q^2 e^{\\frac{4 \\varphi }{\\alpha }}}{\\left(\\alpha ^2-2\\right) \\delta ^4} \\\\\n&+\\frac{4 \\alpha c_0 \\eta_1 m_0^2 (\\alpha +\\beta ) e^{\\varphi \\left(\\frac{1}{\\alpha }-2 \\beta \\right)}}{\\delta \\left(2 \\alpha ^2+2 \\alpha \\beta -1\\right)} +\\frac{2 \\alpha c_0^2 \\eta_2 m_0^2 (\\alpha +\\beta ) e^{2 \\varphi \\left(\\frac{1}{\\alpha }-\\beta \\right)}}{\\delta ^2 \\left(\\alpha ^2+\\alpha \\beta -1\\right)}.\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $\\Lambda$ is a free parameter playing the role of a cosmological constant.\n\nThe metric function $f(r)$ is given by:\n\\begin{align*}\nf(r)= &-m r^{\\frac{\\alpha ^2-1}{\\alpha ^2+1}} -\\frac{\\left(\\alpha ^2+1\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\delta }{r}\\right)^{-\\frac{2 \\alpha ^2}{\\alpha ^2+1}}}{\\alpha ^2-1} +\\frac{2 \\left(\\alpha ^2+1\\right)^2 Q^2 \\delta ^{-\\frac{4 \\alpha ^2}{\\alpha ^2+1}} r^{\\frac{2 \\left(\\alpha ^2-1\\right)}{\\alpha ^2+1}}}{\\alpha ^4-3 \\alpha ^2+2} +\\frac{\\left(\\alpha ^2+1\\right)^2 \\Lambda \\delta ^{\\frac{2 \\alpha ^2}{\\alpha ^2+1}} r^{\\frac{2}{\\alpha ^2+1}}}{\\alpha ^2-3} \\\\\n&-\\frac{\\left(\\alpha ^2+1\\right)^2 c_0 \\eta_1 m_0^2 \\delta ^{-\\frac{\\alpha (\\alpha +2 \\beta )}{\\alpha ^2+1}} r^{\\frac{2 \\alpha ^2+2 \\alpha \\beta +1}{\\alpha ^2+1}}}{\\left(\\alpha ^2+2 \\alpha \\beta +2\\right) \\left(2 \\alpha ^2+2 \\alpha \\beta -1\\right)} -\\frac{\\left(\\alpha ^2+1\\right)^2 c_0^2 \\eta_2 m_0^2 \\left(\\frac{\\delta }{r}\\right)^{-\\frac{2 \\alpha (\\alpha +\\beta )}{\\alpha ^2+1}}}{\\left(\\alpha ^2+\\alpha \\beta -1\\right) \\left(\\alpha ^2+2 \\alpha \\beta +1\\right)}\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $m$ is an integration constant related to the mass of the black hole. The solution requires the condition $\\alpha < 1$.', 'status': 'ACCEPT', 'result': {'status': 'ACCEPT', 'rejection_reason': None, 'justification': 'The prompt is a well-formulated mathematical/physics problem asking for a proof of a specific theorem about black hole solutions in Maxwell-dilaton-massive gravity. It includes all necessary reference text (the complete setup, action, equations of motion, metric ansatz, reference metric, and the explicit form of the claimed solution). The request asks for mathematical derivation/proof, which is appropriate. No rejection reas