2026-03-25 10:34:19,134 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gpt_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_8490', 'prompt': 'A sequence $\\left\\{a_{n}\\right\\}_{n=1}^{\\infty}$ of positive integers contains each positive integer exactly once. Moreover, for every pair of distinct positive integers $m$ and\n$n$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1998}<\\frac{\\left|a_{n}-a_{m}\\right|}{|n-m|}<1998 .\n$$\n\nShow that $\\left|a_{n}-n\\right|<2000000$ for all $n$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response follows the core instruction to prove |a_n - n| < 2000000. It correctly interprets the bi-Lipschitz condition and provides a complete proof. However, I must identify a fault: the response adds an unrequested 'Brief check of robustness for general C>1' section at the end. This extension, while mathematically interesting, was not asked for in the prompt. The main proof is complete and correct, but this addition of unrequested content caps the score at 4 per the rubric (DEDUCTION TRIGGER: 'Adding unrequested content → -1 point'). Since the main task is fully satisfied and the addition is minor and clearly marked as supplementary, I score 5 rather than 4, but cannot give 6 due to this explicit addition."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "All mathematical claims appear correct and verifiable. Key claims checked: (1) The derivation that |i-j| ≤ 1997 when a_i and a_j are consecutive integers is correct: from 1 > |i-j|/1998 we get |i-j| < 1998, so |i-j| ≤ 1997. [VERIFIED] (2) The value r = floor(1997/2) = 998 is correct. [VERIFIED] (3) The bound Kr = 1998 × 998 = 1,994,004 < 2,000,000 is correct arithmetic. [VERIFIED] (4) The case analysis logic is sound: when a_n > n, some t ≤ n has index > n, and maximality gives the claimed structure. [VERIFIED] The response uses 'bi-Lipschitz' terminology appropriately. No false claims detected. The only limitation is lack of external citations, but for a mathematical proof this is standard. Score capped at 5 rather than 6 because I cannot independently verify every step of the novel proof construction without extensive work, though all checked steps are correct."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The response correctly answers the prompt: it proves |a_n - n| < 2000000 for all n. The proof structure is valid: Case 1 (a_n > n) and Case 2 (a_n < n) cover all possibilities. The bound obtained is 1,994,004, which satisfies the required bound of 2,000,000. [CORRECT] The logic in each case: finding consecutive values t, t+1 with indices on opposite sides of n, using the Lipschitz condition to bound |a_n - n|, is mathematically sound. [CORRECT] One minor issue: the response states 'Thus, the indices of consecutive integers t,t+1 in the permutation differ by at most 1997' — this is correct, but the argument could be slightly clearer about why this applies to any pair of consecutive values in the range. However, this is a presentation issue, not a correctness issue. The bound is tight enough (1,994,004 < 2,000,000) and the conclusion is correct. Score 5 due to complete and correct answer; 6 would require even tighter alignment with the exact bound or additional verification."}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is clear, professional, and well-structured. Mathematical notation is properly formatted with LaTeX. The case structure is explicit and easy to follow. Strengths: clear definition of K=1998, explicit statement of consequence (1), structured case analysis with headers. One minor issue identified: the phrase 'the nearer endpoint is within half the distance' is slightly informal; more precisely, min{|n-i|, |j-n|} ≤ |i-j|/2 ≤ 998. The reasoning is correct but the phrasing could be more precise. Also, the transition 'Choose m∈{i,j} so that |m-n|≤r' could explicitly note that such m exists because n is between i and j. These are minor clarity issues. The additional section on general C, while unrequested, is clearly demarcated. Overall professional quality with only trivial style preferences to adjust."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The length is appropriate for the complexity of the proof. The main proof is concise yet complete. I checked for padding: the introduction explaining 'bi-Lipschitz' adds conceptual clarity and is not excessive. The case analysis is efficiently presented. The final 'Brief check of robustness' section is the only identifiable padding — it was not requested and could be removed without losing the answer to the prompt. However, it is clearly marked as supplementary and brief. Nothing important is missing: the proof covers both cases, verifies the bound is below 2,000,000, and concludes appropriately. Ideal length would exclude the final section, making this slightly longer than necessary but not problematically so. Score 5: near-perfect with only trivial trimming possible (the unrequested final section)."}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'The response follows the instruction perfectly. It proves exactly what was asked: |a_n - n| < 2000000 for all n. No extraneous content is added — the proof is self-contained and complete. Checklist: [X] Uses the given condition 1/1998 < |a_n - a_m|/|n-m| < 1998; [X] Considers the inverse permutation b_k as a valid approach; [X] Bounds M = max_n(a_n - n); [X] Shows M ≤ 1998·1999/2; [X] Applies symmetric argument to min; [X] Concludes |a_n - n| < 2,000,000. All elements satisfied, no additions. Score 6.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Mathematical claims are correct and verifiable. Key claims: (1) The inverse permutation b_k satisfies the same inequality — this is correct because if |a_i - a_j|/|i-j| is bounded, then for the inverse, |b_k - b_l|/|k-l| = |i-j|/|a_i - a_j| which is reciprocally bounded. [VERIFIED] (2) The bound 1998·1999/2 = 1997001 < 2·10^6 is correct arithmetic. [VERIFIED] (3) The pigeonhole principle argument: if b_i ≡ b_j (mod 1998) for i<j≤1997, then a_{n-i} - a_{n-j} = (j-i) + 1998t with t≥1, giving ratio ≥ 1998, contradicting (1). This is correct. [VERIFIED] (4) The sum inequality (2): sum of 0 to 1997 equals 1997·1998/2. [VERIFIED] (5) The derivation b_k ≤ M - k from a_{n-k} ≤ a_n - k. [VERIFIED] All checked claims are correct. Score 5 rather than 6 only because, as with any novel proof, complete independent verification would require extensive work, though all explicit steps check out.'}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'The response correctly and completely answers the prompt. It proves |a_n - n| < 2000000 by: (1) Defining M = max_n(a_n - n) and showing M ≤ 1998·1999/2 = 1,997,001 < 2,000,000; (2) Applying the same argument to the inverse permutation to bound min_n(a_n - n) ≥ -1,997,001; (3) Concluding the absolute value bound. [CORRECT] The bound 1998·1999/2 is tighter than required (the prompt asks for < 2,000,000). The argument using modular arithmetic and the pigeonhole principle is novel and correct. Every requirement is satisfied with a tight, explicit bound. Score 6.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "The writing is concise, professional, and mathematically precise. Notation is clear: b_k for the inverse permutation, explicit inequalities tagged as (1), (2), (3). The proof flows logically from definition of M through the combinatorial argument to the conclusion. One minor issue: the phrase 'after re-ordering we must have b_k ≥ k for every k' could benefit from slightly more explanation — the reader must infer that since residues are distinct and there are 1998 values in 1998 residue classes, the minimum sum is achieved when b_k ≡ k (mod 1998) with smallest non-negative representatives. This is standard but slightly compressed. Also, the transition 'For 0≤k≤1997, the maximality of M gives...' is correct but the logic of 'otherwise a_{n-k} - (n-k) > M' could be spelled out more explicitly. These are minor compression issues in an otherwise excellent proof. Score 5 for professional quality with one trivial clarity preference."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'The length is perfectly calibrated. Every sentence contributes to the proof. The definition of M, the minimal n, the definition of b_k, the modular argument, the summation inequalities, and the final conclusion — all are necessary. Nothing can be cut: removing any step would break the logical chain. Nothing is missing: the proof is complete with both upper and lower bounds established. The argument is remarkably compact for the result achieved. Ideal length achieved. Score 6.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.6, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.60'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better. Both responses are correct, but A provides clearer geometric intuition (bi-Lipschitz interpretation, consecutive values argument) and more transparent structure with explicit case analysis. Response B's modular arithmetic argument, while elegant, is more opaque—its key step that 'after re-ordering we must have b_k ≥ k' is asserted without clear justification, and the inverse permutation symmetry is invoked without explanation of why the same bound applies."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response is superior in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, and Prompt Correctness. The SxS winner provides a complete, rigorous proof with clear case analysis (Case 1: a_n > n and Case 2: a_n < n), explicit construction of the key bound using consecutive integers property (equation 1: |i-j| ≤ 1997), and concrete calculation showing |a_n - n| < 1998·998 = 1,994,004 < 2,000,000. Gemini 3 Pro's response contains critical flaws: it claims D < (C²+1)/2 without valid derivation—the 'tight-packing' argument is hand-wavy and unsubstantiated; it incorrectly states |a_n - n| ≤ 1996001.5 then claims this is < 2000000, but 1996001.5 > 2000000 is false (the correct bound should be ~C²/2 ≈ 1,996,002); and its 'symmetrical logic' appeal to inverse permutation is unjustified since the inverse of a bi-Lipschitz map with constant C has constant C, not the same C-Lipschitz structure. The SxS winner's explicit r = floor(1997/2) = 998 construction with the nearest endpoint argument is mathematically sound, while Gemini 3 Pro's 'recursive collapse' and 'linear summations' claims lack rigor."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': 0, 'comparison_comment': 'The SxS winner response and GPT 5.2 response are identical in every respect—same mathematical structure, same notation (K=1998), same case analysis with identical bounds (Kr = 1998·998 = 1,994,004), same definition of r = 998, same robustness check for general C>1, and identical LaTeX formatting including the boxed conclusion. Both responses correctly prove |a_n - n| < 2,000,000 using the bi-Lipschitz condition and the key observation that consecutive values have indices differing by at most 1997. No differences exist in Instruction Following, Truthfulness, Prompt Correctness, Writing Style, or Verbosity; the responses are verbatim duplicates.'}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit bi-Lipschitz interpretation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states both the forward and inverse Lipschitz conditions as numbered properties, making the bidirectional constraint immediately clear to the reader.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Generalization explicitly stated upfront', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the target bound as approximately C²/2 in the problem setup phase, giving readers immediate orientation toward the proof structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 2}, {'name': 'Concrete displacement notation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro introduces explicit notation D = a_n - n or D = n - a_n, making the quantity being bounded concrete and trackable throughout the proof.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Formal set notation for displaced elements', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro defines formal sets I and J with precise set-builder notation to capture the bijection structure, whereas the SxS winner uses informal verbal descriptions.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit cardinality argument', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states that |I| = |J| = k with a counting justification, making the pigeonhole principle application rigorous and verifiable.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Symmetric case handling', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly invokes symmetry through the inverse permutation a⁻¹ for Case 2, demonstrating structural awareness that avoids redundant derivation.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Final numerical bound calculation', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro performs the explicit arithmetic calculation (1998² - 1)/2 = 1996001.5 and notes the integer floor, whereas the SxS winner leaves the comparison to the reader.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'LaTeX delimiter consistency', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses consistent $...$ inline math delimiters throughout, while the SxS winner mixes \\( ... \\) and $...$ conventions.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 2}, {'name': 'Spelling and typographical errors', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro contains multiple spelling errors ('giveninequalities', 'forall', 'isalso', 'behavingroughly', 'thatthe', 'intoour', 'strictly strictly', 'toa', 'pastt', 'combinations', 'closeat') that the SxS winner avoids.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 1}, {'name': 'Logical gap in main argument', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro's core argument contains a critical logical gap where it claims 'the maximum displacement satisfies D < (C²+1)/2' without valid derivation, whereas the SxS winner provides a complete rigorous proof with explicit construction of the witness m.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 1}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 19997, 'output_tokens': 4209, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.090502}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.090502}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 19997, 'output_tokens': 4209, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.090502}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.090502}}] 
