jor clarity problems. The writing is professional and appropriate for mathematical exposition."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The response provides: (1) Setup/observation section establishing key inequalities, (2) Detailed case analysis for a_n > n with explicit counting argument, (3) Symmetric case for a_n < n, (4) Remarks on generalization. Each section serves a purpose. Could anything be cut? The remarks section (Section 3) is not strictly required by the prompt, but provides valuable context and shows understanding. The explicit listing of b_1 < ... < b_d and the detailed derivation of (2) could potentially be compressed, but the clarity benefits outweigh the cost. Could anything be added? The proof is complete; no gaps identified. The length is appropriate for a competition-level mathematics problem. Score 5: near-perfect with only trivial trimming possible.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Checklist verification: (1) Prove |a_n - n| < 2000000 — Response B claims this at the end, but with a critical gap. (2) Use bi-Lipschitz condition — [X] Used correctly in (1) and (3). (3) Handle permutation property — [PARTIAL] Uses inverse permutation b_t, but the argument has a flaw. Major issue identified: The proof in Section 1 claims to bound D = a_n - n, but the argument is incomplete. Specifically, equation (5) claims |p-n| ≥ D, but this is not properly justified — the indices of n, n+1, ..., a_n are D+1 distinct integers between min{p,n} and max{p,n}, but this only gives |p-n| ≥ D if we know these D+1 integers are consecutive, which requires additional argument. More critically, the 'iteration' argument is vague: 'after at most ⌈n/1000⌉ steps we would obtain a non-positive index' — this assumes the sequence n_k moves in one direction, but the inequality |n_{k+1}-n_k| > 1000 doesn't specify direction. The proof doesn't actually establish that a_n - n < 1998·1000 for all n, only that unbounded growth leads to contradiction, but doesn't rule out isolated large values. The bound (6) is asserted without complete justification. This is a significant gap in fulfilling the prompt's requirement for a rigorous proof."}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 4, 'reason': "Fact-check: [VERIFIED] The bi-Lipschitz reformulation (1) is correct. [VERIFIED] Consecutive bounds (2) and index bound (3) are correct. [QUESTIONABLE] Equation (4): |p-n| > D/1998 — this follows from (1) with |a_n - a_p| = |a_n - n| = D, so D < 1998|n-p|, giving |n-p| > D/1998. Correct. [QUESTIONABLE] Equation (5): |p-n| ≥ D — the claim is that D+1 distinct indices fit in an interval of length |p-n|, so |p-n| ≥ D. This requires that the indices of n, n+1, ..., a_n are all distinct and lie strictly between min(p,n) and max(p,n). But if p < n, the indices could include values less than p. The argument is incomplete. [FALSE/UNVERIFIABLE] The iteration argument: 'If a_{n_k} > n_k + 1998·1000 then |n_{k+1}-n_k| > 1000' — this assumes a_{n_k} - n_k = D > 1998·1000, but n_{k+1} = b_{n_k} is the index of value n_k, not related to a_{n_k} - n_k in the claimed way. The recurrence is confused: n_{k+1} = b_{n_k} means a_{n_{k+1}} = n_k, not that we're tracking the deviation at n_k. The argument conflates the value n_k with its index. This is a significant logical error. The claim that the same argument applies to the 'inverse permutation' is also not fully justified — the inverse of a bi-Lipschitz permutation is bi-Lipschitz, but this requires proof. Score capped at 4 due to questionable/false claims in the core argument."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 3, 'reason': "Correctness audit: Requirement — Prove |a_n - n| < 2000000 for all n. [PARTIALLY CORRECT] Response B reaches the correct conclusion, but the reasoning is flawed. Major error in Section 1: The iteration construction n_{k+1} 