ral elegance, while GPT 5.2 contains a critical logical gap. The SxS winner's proof uses a clean contradiction argument with well-defined steps: establishing n ≤ D via contrapositive, constructing 1001 missing values via pigeonhole principle, and deriving contradiction from the slope condition—each step explicitly justified with inequalities like D/1998 > 1000. GPT 5.2's approach has a fatal flaw in Case 1: when selecting m ∈ {i,j} with |m-n| ≤ r, it assumes |a_n - a_m| ≥ a_n - n, but this fails if a_m = t+1 = n+1 (possible when t=n), making |a_n - a_m| = |a_n - (n+1)| which could be smaller than a_n - n. The SxS winner's bound of 1,999,998 is tighter and correctly derived; GPT 5.2's 1,994,004 appears correct but rests on shaky foundations. The SxS winner's use of inverse permutation symmetry (a^{-1}) is more sophisticated than GPT 5.2's case splitting, and its explicit handling of the 'maximal' choice of n with d_n ≥ 2·10^6 ensures well-foundedness that GPT 5.2's 'largest t' construction lacks."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Clear problem setup and variable definitions', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly defines C = 1998 and restates the Lipschitz conditions in accessible terms before beginning the proof, while the SxS winner immediately introduces notation without explaining the structural meaning of the constraints.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minimal setup difference, 3-4 = noticeably clearer framing, 5-6 = dramatically better scaffolding that aids comprehension', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Explicit case structure with symmetric handling', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro clearly separates the proof into Case 1 (a_n > n) and Case 2 (a_n < n) with explicit parallel structure, while the SxS winner handles both cases implicitly through the inverse permutation without clear signposting.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 = minor organizational difference, 3-4 = clearer logical flow, 5-6 = substantially more readable structure', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Concrete interpret