tic since the axioms already define the functions. SxS winner's explanation of why restrictions matter is more precise about extract requiring 'additional axioms' and concrete constants having 'implicit fixed width' that breaks the translation. Both proofs are technically correct, but SxS winner's exposition is more elegant and faithful to the paper's structure."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit handling of vacuous case in forward direction', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly addresses the case where the T_ufia interpretation does not satisfy the axiomatization, noting vacuous satisfaction, while the SxS winner omits this logical case analysis.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed termination argument for recursive axioms', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes an explicit termination argument for the recursive bitwise function definitions (k reduces by 1 each step, hits base case k=1), which the SxS winner only implicitly assumes.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Precise explanation of extract restriction', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro provides a specific technical explanation of why extract is problematic (runtime bit-width i-j+1 may violate positivity or termination conditions), whereas the SxS winner gives a more generic justification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit bijective relationship mention in reverse direction', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly states the 'bijective relationship between the interpreted mappings' as the justification for equivalence, while the SxS winner relies on the reader to infer this correspondence.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with evidence, 5-6 substantially 