2026-03-25 10:35:21,034 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_gem_sxs response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_7662', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$ be the theory of parametric bit-vectors and $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$ be the theory of uninterpreted functions and integer arithmetic. A parametric bit-vector formula $\\varphi$ is a formula over a signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ containing parametric bit-vector variables (from a set $X^\\ast$) and parametric bit-vector constants (from a set $Z^\\ast$).\n\nA mapping $\\omega = (\\omega^b, \\omega^N)$ assigns to each symbol $\\mathsf{s} \\in X^\\ast \\cup Z^\\ast$ a bit-width term $\\omega^b(\\mathsf{s})$ and for $\\mathsf{s} \\in Z^\\ast$ an integer value term $\\omega^N(\\mathsf{s})$. The formula $\\varphi$ is well-sorted under $\\omega$ if for every term, its bit-width is well-defined.\n\nWe define a translation function $\\mathcal{T}_A$ from parametric bit-vector formulas to formulas in $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$, parameterized by an axiomatization mode $A$. The translation is defined as:\n$\\mathcal{T}_A(\\varphi, \\omega) \\equiv \\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega) \\implies Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$\n\nThe auxiliary function $Conv(\\varphi, \\omega)$ converts parametric bit-vector expressions into integer expressions with uninterpreted functions:\n- Parametric bit-vector variables $\\mathsf{x} \\in X^\\ast$ are replaced by unique integer variables $\\chi(\\mathsf{x})$.\n- Parametric bit-vector constants $\\mathsf{z} \\in Z^\\ast$ are replaced by the term $\\omega^N (\\mathsf{z} ) \\mod \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}} (\\omega^b (\\mathsf{z} ))$.\n- An operator $\\bvsymbolf{f}$ from the signature $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$ is replaced by a corresponding uninterpreted function ${f}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}$. For example, $\\bvsymbolf{+}$ with sort $\\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\times \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n} \\to \\ensuremath{\\sort_{}} {n}$ is replaced by $\\natsymbol{+}$ of sort $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\times \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}} \\to \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{Int}}$, where the first argument is the bit-width.\n- Most of these uninterpreted functions are then eliminated by replacing them with equivalent expressions in integer arithmetic. For example, ${+}^{\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}}(k,x,y)$ is replaced by $(x+y) \\mod \\mathsf{pow2}(k)$.\n- After elimination, the only remaining uninterpreted functions are $\\{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}, \\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}, \\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}\\}$.\n\nThe formula $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {A}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ provides axioms for these functions. For the `full` axiomatization mode ($A=\\mathrm{full}$), $\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}_{\\kern-.2em} {\\mathrm{full}}}(\\varphi,\\omega)$ is the conjunction of:\n1.  Range constraints for variables: For each parametric bit-vector variable $\\mathsf{x}$ in $\\varphi$, the axiom $0 \\le \\chi(\\mathsf{x}) < \\mathsf{pow2}(\\omega^b(\\mathsf{x}))$ is added.\n2.  Positivity of bit-widths: For each free variable $v$ in $\\omega$, the axiom $v > 0$ is added.\n3.  Axioms for the uninterpreted functions, as defined in Table 2 from the paper.\n\n**Full Axiomatization from Table 2:**\nLet $i, j, k$ be bit-widths and $x, y$ be integers. We use $\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{i}{x}$ as shorthand for $(x \\div \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)} {i}) \\mod 2$, where $\\twotothef(k)$ is an uninterpreted function for $2^k$.\n\n- **Axioms for $\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{pow2}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\mathsf{pow2}}}$):**\n  - $\\forall k. k \\le 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 1$\n  - $\\forall k. k > 0 \\implies \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k} = 2 \\cdot \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise AND, $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\&}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intandf(1, x, y) = \\min(x, y)$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intandf(k, x, y) = \\intandf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\min(\\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x}, \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y})$\n\n- **Axioms for bitwise XOR, $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\oplus}}$ ($\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{AX}^{\\mathrm{full}}_{\\oplus}}$):**\n  - $\\forall x, y. \\intxorf(1, x, y) = \\lvert x-y \\rvert$\n  - $\\forall k, x, y. k > 1 \\implies \\intxorf(k, x, y) = \\intxorf(k-1, x \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}, y \\mod \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1}) + \\ensuremath{\\twotothef(#1)}{k-1} \\cdot \\lvert \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{x} - \\ensuremath{\\hextractf_{#2}(#3)} {k}{k-1}{y} \\rvert$\n\nThe axiomatization of $\\ensuremath{\\natsymbol{\\mid}}$ is omitted and is dual to that of $\\ensuremath{\\mathrel{\\natsymbol{\\&}\\kern-.3em}}$.\n\n**Notation from Appendix:**\n- Let $\\mathcal{J}$ be a mapping of the variables in $\\vec{v}=\\ensuremath{\\mathrm{FV}\\kern-.15em(#1)} {\\omega}$ to positive integers.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\fsbvtheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}}$.\n- Let ${#1}\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}} }$ be an interpretation of $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}}$.\n- For a bit-vector constant $a$, let ${\\brackets{a}}_{\\mathbb{N}}$ denote its value as an integer.\n- For a parametric $\\ensuremath{\\sig_{\\fsbv}}$-term $t$, let $t|_{\\omega, \\mathcal{J}}$ denote the concrete bit-vector term obtained by instantiating the parameters in $t$ according to $\\omega$ and $\\mathcal{J}$.\n\nWe use $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ as shorthand for $\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{full}}(\\varphi, \\omega)$.\n## Theorem\nLet $\\varphi$ be a parameteric bit-vector formula that is well sorted under $\\omega$ and has no occurrences of bit-vector extract or concrete bit-vector constants. Then: $\\varphi$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\kern -.1em\\fsbv}} $-valid under $\\omega$ if and only if $\\trfullf(\\varphi, \\omega)$ is $\\ensuremath{T_{\\ufia}} $-valid.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -2, 'overall_comment': "Response A is moderately better. Both proofs establish the bidirectional equivalence, but A is more rigorous and concise: it uses the standard reduction to satisfiability of negations (statement *), explicitly references 'Lemma 0' for the critical semantic property that axiomatized functions compute true bitwise operations, and cleanly handles both directions with a single induction hypothesis (equation \\eqref{eq:term}). Response B is verbose, contains distracting formatting artifacts (broken LaTeX like '{#1}\\scaleto'), and over-explains standard properties while under-formalizing the key invariant linking $Conv(t,\\omega)$ to bit-vector semantics; its 'bijective relationship' claim in the reverse direction is hand-wavy compared to A's explicit term-level induction."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides a mathematically rigorous and elegant proof using the standard approach of proving equisatisfiability via contrapositive (statement *), with clear Lemma 0 establishing that the axiomatized functions compute correct bitwise operations, and bidirectional model constructions with explicit equation (eq:term) for term equivalence. Gemini 3 Pro's response, while covering both directions, suffers from critical flaws: (1) it misstates the proof strategy—the forward direction should assume T_fsbv-validity and prove T_ufia-validity, but Gemini incorrectly describes constructing a T_fsbv-interpretation from a T_ufia-interpretation (this is backwards); (2) it contains garbled LaTeX artifacts like '{#1}\\scaleto{\\nattheory}{4pt}{\\ensuremath{{\\mathcal{I}}}}' throughout, severely degrading readability; (3) the 'Restrictions' section at the end contains plausible-sounding but technically imprecise reasoning about extract and concrete constants that doesn't match the actual restriction in the theorem (which ensures Conv only uses axiomatized symbols). The SxS winner correctly notes this restriction ensures Conv contains only symbols axiomatized in AX_full, with no spurious justification needed."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "Both models provide correct proofs establishing the equivalence, but the SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior concision and structural elegance while maintaining rigor. The SxS winner uses a clean bidirectional approach via the key equivalence (*) with a unified term correspondence lemma (Lemma 0), elegantly handling both directions through a single induction principle and avoiding redundant lemmas. GPT 5.2, while thorough and correct, suffers from excessive verbosity: it separates the proof into three redundant lemmas (Lemmas 1-3) where Lemma 1 merely restates obvious pow2 behavior, Lemma 2 provides an unnecessarily detailed inductive proof of bitwise operations that the SxS winner captures in two lines, and the structural induction in Lemma 3 duplicates work. GPT 5.2's proof spans ~120 lines versus the SxS winner's ~50 lines without adding substantive technical depth. The SxS winner's notation is also more disciplined—consistently using \\T_{\\!{\\rm fsbv}} and \\T_{\\!{\\rm ufia}}—while GPT 5.2 mixes styles (\\T_{\\fsbv} vs T_{\\fsbv}). Both correctly note the restriction purpose, though GPT 5.2's explanation is more verbose. The SxS winner's proof is more elegant and efficient without sacrificing correctness."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit bidirectional proof structure', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro clearly labels and separates Forward Direction ($\\Rightarrow$) and Reverse Direction ($\\Leftarrow$) with distinct subsections, whereas the SxS winner uses a compressed equivalence-based approach without explicit directional labeling.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=minor structural difference, 6=completely missing from SxS winner', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Detailed base case and recursive case analysis for bitwise functions', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly breaks down the verification of $\\intandf$ and $\\intxorf$ into Base Case, Recursive Case, and Termination subsections, while the SxS winner collapses this into a single Lemma 0 reference without explicit case structure.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=minor detail difference, 6=completely absent from SxS winner', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit explanation of restriction necessity', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated subsection titled 'Restrictions on Extract and Concrete Constants' that explicitly explains why concrete constants and extract operations are excluded, with specific technical reasons for each; the SxS winner only mentions this restriction in a single concluding sentence.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1=brief mention vs detailed, 6=entirely 