g a T_fsbv-interpretation from a T_ufia-interpretation; for reverse, constructing a T_ufia-interpretation from a T_fsbv-interpretation. I considered whether this deserves 6, but the strategy statement lacks explicit mention of why the restrictions matter for the proof approach (this appears only later), and the 'Proof Strategy' heading is somewhat buried in formatting issues (appears as '## Proof**Proof Strategy:**'). The content is clear but presentation slightly muddled."}, 'gpt': {'score': 6, 'reason': "The response has an excellent explicit proof strategy statement: 'We prove both directions by relating models of the parametric bit-vector theory T_fsbv (after instantiating widths) and models of T_ufia satisfying the full axiom set.' It clearly identifies the core technical step (behavior of uninterpreted functions on in-range arguments) and explicitly outlines both directions with their key ideas. The strategy also foreshadows why restrictions matter: 'Throughout, the restrictions “no extract” and “no concrete bit-vector constants” are used to ensure...' This is comprehensive and well-structured. I found no meaningful fault—this is exactly what the rubric requests."}}, {'rubric_name': 'Interpretation Construction and Verification', 'opalite': {'score': 5, 'reason': "For Direction 1, the response clearly constructs I_fsbv with explicit steps: defining J, assigning values to variables using chi(x), and handling constants. It verifies range constraints and pow2 behavior. For Direction 2, it constructs I_n with explicit assignments and verifies axioms including range constraints, positivity, and recursive axioms. I considered whether this scores 6, but the verification in Direction 2 is somewhat compressed—the claim 'The recursive axioms hold by construction' is stated but the explicit verification that the constructed interpretation satisfies the recursive equations is less detailed than in Direction 1. The 'Verification of axioms' section is present but could more