erms—adding the original binomial coefficients again after already including them in the expansion. The SxS winner's proof is rigorous and concise, while Gemini 3 Pro's approach is verbose, contains algebraic errors, and fails to establish a clean logical path to the conclusion."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (internal) demonstrates superior mathematical elegance and concision through its generating function approach, achieving the proof in three compact steps by recognizing the polynomial identity P(x) = p²(1+x)^m Q(x)². GPT 5.2 provides a correct but substantially more verbose proof requiring extensive case analysis via Vandermonde convolution, with separate lemmas for binomial coefficients modulo p² and careful splitting of summation ranges. The SxS winner's key insight—factoring ((1+x)^p - (1+x^p))² = p²Q(x)²—immediately establishes all coefficients are divisible by p², whereas GPT 5.2's approach demands roughly 3x the length with redundant numerical checks and repetitive algebraic manipulations. Both are mathematically correct, but the SxS winner exhibits superior problem-solving insight and cleaner exposition."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit case verification section', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes a dedicated section with concrete numerical examples that verify the congruence holds for specific values of p, m, n.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2=one brief example, 3-4=two examples with moderate detail, 5-6=multiple well-structured test cases with clear LHS/RHS calculations', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Modulo p^2 reduction of binomial coefficients', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro systematically derives explicit formulas for binomial coefficients modulo p^2 across all residue classes (j=0, j=p, 0<j<p, p<j<2p), whereas the SxS winner only shows the final factorization without intermediate coefficient analysis.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2=partial case analysis, 3-4=systematic coverage with some gaps, 5-6