+n) and RHS has 2C(p+m,p+n)+2C(p+m,2p+n); [CORRECT] The modulus p² is correctly handled throughout; [CORRECT] The condition m≥n≥0 is respected (ensures all binomial coefficients are well-defined with the zero convention). The proof strategy—Vandermonde expansion plus Lemma 1—is sound and correctly executed. No incorrect steps identified.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 5, 'reason': "Quality audit: Strengths: Clear structure with labeled Lemma and sections; proper use of LaTeX formatting; logical flow from lemma to main proof. Minor issues identified: (1) The phrase 'the others are p²–multiples' uses an en-dash instead of standard hyphen or word 'multiples of p²'; (2) The split of the sum at i=p uses the symmetry C(2p,p+i)=C(2p,p-i) without explicit justification (though standard); (3) The final display equation restates the conclusion but is slightly redundant given the preceding text. These are trivial style preferences rather than significant weaknesses. The proof is professional and readable."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 5, 'reason': 'Length analysis: The response is appropriately detailed for a non-trivial combinatorial congruence. Nothing can be cut because: the Lemma is essential (the main proof depends on it); the three Vandermonde expansions are all needed to set up the cancellation; the splitting of the sum at i=p is crucial for the argument. Nothing is missing because: the boundary convention is stated; the lemma is proved; all algebraic manipulations are shown; the final conclusion is explicit. The response could potentially trim the final display equation (repeating the full congruence) but this is standard mathematical practice for clarity. Ideal length ±10%.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 5.45, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 5.45'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Complete checklist: [X] Prove the stated congruence modulo p²; [X] Handle prime p and integers m≥n≥0; [X] Use proper mathematical notation. The response innovatively reformulates the problem using generating functions/coefficient extraction, which is a valid and powerful approach. All steps serve the proof: defining P(x), showing the coefficient extraction gives the desired difference, factoring P(x), and concluding. No unrequested content; the generating function machinery is necessary for this elegant approach.'}, 'truthfulness': {'score': 6, 'reason': "All claims verified with specific evidence: (1) Coefficient extraction: [x^(2p+n)]P(x) correctly computed—expanding (1+x)^m(1+x^p)² gives three terms contributing to x^(2p+n): C(m,2p+n) from x^(2p+n), 2C(m,p+n) from 2x^p·x^n, and C(m,n) from x^(2p)·x^n. The subtraction of 2(1+x)^(p+m)(1+x^p) gives -2C(p+m,2p+n)-2C(p+m,p+n). This matches the LHS-RHS of the congruence. (2) Factorization: P(x)=(1+x)^m[((1+x)^p)²+(1+x^p)²-2(1+x)^p(1+x^p)] = (1+x)^m[(1+x)^p-(1+x^p)]² is correct algebra (a²+b²-2ab=(a-b)²). (3) Key claim: (1+x)^p-(1+x^p)=∑_{k=1}^{p-1}C(p,k)x^k, and each C(p,k)≡0 (mod p), so equals p·Q(x). This is correct: by Freshman's dream, (1+x)^p≡1+x^p (mod p), and the difference has no constant or x^p term. (4) Conclusion: P(x)=p²(1+x)^mQ(x)², so all coefficients are divisible by p². Every claim is verifiable and correct."}, 'prompt_correctness': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Correctness audit: [CORRECT] The coefficient extraction yields exactly LHS-RHS of the stated congruence; [CORRECT] The factorization and divisibility argument proves this difference ≡0 (mod p²); [CORRECT] All boundary conditions handled (implicitly by polynomial coefficient extraction); [CORRECT] The condition 0≤n≤m is respected. The generating function approach is not only correct but elegantly reveals why the congruence holds: the entire polynomial P(x) is divisible by p², not just specific coefficients. This is a stronger and more insightful result than required.'}, 'writing_style': {'score': 6, 'reason': "Quality audit: Exceptional clarity and structure. The three numbered sections create clear logical progression: (1) reformulate as coefficient, (2) factor and prove divisibility, (3) conclude. The notation [x^k]F(x) is standard and well-explained. The algebraic manipulation in section 2 is particularly elegant—the 'aha' moment of recognizing the perfect square is highlighted. No grammar issues, no awkward phrasing, no redundancy. The explanation 'for 1≤k≤p-1 the binomial coefficient C(p,k) is divisible by p' is precise. The final conclusion explicitly restates the required congruence with proper quantification. Publication-ready quality."}, 'verbosity': {'score': 6, 'reason': 'Length analysis: Perfectly calibrated. Nothing can be cut because: the coefficient extraction in section 1 is minimal and necessary; the factorization in section 2 is the core insight and cannot be shortened; the explicit form of (1+x)^p-(1+x^p) is needed to establish the p-divisibility. Nothing is missing because: the coefficient notation is defined; the algebraic steps are shown; the divisibility conclusion is explicit; the final restatement confirms the result. The response achieves remarkable conciseness while maintaining complete rigor—this is the ideal length for this proof.'}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 6.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 6.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': 2, 'overall_comment': "Response B is moderately better due to superior elegance and conceptual clarity. Response A uses brute-force Vandermonde expansions with messy index manipulations and case-splitting (Lemma 1, splitting sums at i=p, using symmetry), while Response B achieves the same result through a clean generating function factorization: recognizing P(x) = (1+x)^m((1+x)^p - (1+x^p))^2 = p^2(1+x)^m Q(x)^2 immediately yields p^2 divisibility for all coefficients. Response B's approach is more general, more insightful, and avoids the tedious algebraic verification in Response A."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides an elegant, correct proof using generating functions with coefficient extraction, achieving the result through a clean factorization showing P(x) = p²(1+x)^m Q(x)². Gemini 3 Pro attempts a brute-force algebraic approach using Vandermonde expansions and Lucas theorem, but contains critical errors: the LHS expansion incorrectly doubles terms (the original LHS already includes the separated terms, so adding them again creates 2binom{m}{n} instead of binom{m}{n}), and the algebraic manipulation becomes circular rather than proving the identity. The SxS winner's proof is rigorous and complete, while Gemini 3 Pro's approach, despite being more verbose, introduces computational errors and lacks the structural insight that makes the congruence transparent."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "SxS winner (Gemini 3 Pro) demonstrates superior Instruction Following and Truthfulness through an elegant, unified proof using generating functions that directly establishes the congruence by showing P(x) = p²(1+x)^m Q(x)² has all coefficients divisible by p². GPT 5.2's approach, while ultimately correct, is significantly more verbose and fragmented, requiring separate lemmas (A and B), extensive case analysis via Vandermonde expansion, and term-by-term comparison of LHS/RHS expressions. The SxS winner's factorization P(x) = (1+x)^m((1+x)^p - (1+x^p))² = p²(1+x)^m Q(x)² is mathematically cleaner and more insightful, immediately yielding the result without the combinatorial casework that occupies GPT 5.2's lengthy response. GPT 5.2's proof contains redundant calculations (separate handling of k=0, k=p, k=2p, and 1≤k≤p-1 vs p+1≤k≤2p-1) and relies on Lucas theorem modulo p rather than working directly modulo p², making its argument less direct."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [{'name': 'Explicit verification cases', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro includes concrete numerical test cases at the end of the proof to verify the congruence holds for specific values (e.g., m=n=0 and p=2, m=n=1), while the SxS winner provides no such verification.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Modulo p² reduction formulas', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro explicitly derives and states reduction formulas for binomial coefficients modulo p² (showing when binomial coefficients equal 0, 1, 2, or 2 times another binomial coefficient), while the SxS winner uses a generating function approach that obscures these explicit congruences.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Vandermonde identity application', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly applies Vandermonde's identity to expand the key binomial coefficients and tracks the summation terms, while the SxS winner uses a compact generating function factorization that does not expose this combinatorial structure.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'Lucas theorem usage', 'description': "Gemini 3 Pro explicitly invokes Lucas's theorem to evaluate binomial coefficients modulo p in intermediate steps, while the SxS winner never mentions or uses Lucas's theorem.", 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX equation formatting', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses proper LaTeX display math environments with $$ delimiters for multi-line equations and aligned structures, while the SxS winner uses a mix of inline math and non-standard formatting with manual spacing commands like \\medskip\\noindent.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}, {'name': 'Section structure with numbered headings', 'description': 'Gemini 3 Pro uses clearly numbered sections (1., 2., 3., 4.) with descriptive titles for logical organization, while the SxS winner uses informal bold labels (1., 2., 3.) without consistent formatting or clear hierarchical structure.', 'scale': '1-6: 1-2 marginally better, 3-4 noticeably better with clear evidence, 5-6 substantially better', 'rating': 3}], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit lemma statements with clear hypotheses', 'description': "GPT 5.2 states Lemma A and Lemma B with explicit conditions (e.g., 'For 1≤k≤p-1') before proving them, while the SxS winner embeds key facts without formal lemma structure.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for informal statements, 3-4 for partial structure, 5-6 for complete formal lemma statements with hypotheses', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Numerical verification examples', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes explicit numerical checks with specific values (p=2, m=n=1 and m=n=0) to verify the congruence, while the SxS winner provides no numerical verification.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: how completely numerical examples are worked through with actual arithmetic', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Prerequisite facts listed before main proof', 'description': "GPT 5.2 begins with a dedicated section listing three specific binomial coefficient facts (Chu-Vandermonde, Lucas's theorem, key congruence) that will be used, while the SxS winner introduces tools inline without advance enumeration.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for no advance listing, 3-4 for partial listing, 5-6 for complete prerequisite enumeration with clear headings', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit handling of p=2 edge case', 'description': "GPT 5.2 explicitly verifies the case p=2 in Lemma B ('For p=2, binom{4}{2}=6≡2 mod 4 holds directly'), while the SxS winner's argument implicitly assumes odd p without addressing p=2 separately.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for no mention, 3-4 for brief mention, 5-6 for explicit verification with calculation', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Step-by-step term-by-term comparison structure', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses explicit 'Right-hand side' and 'Left-hand side' subsections with detailed term-by-term expansion and matching, while the SxS winner uses a more compact coefficient extraction approach without explicit side-by-side comparison.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for no explicit comparison, 3-4 for partial comparison, 5-6 for complete structured side-by-side term matching', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX section headers with descriptive titles', 'description': "GPT 5.2 uses descriptive Markdown headers (###, ##) with clear titles like 'Step 1: A lemma on binom{2p}{k} modulo p^2', while the SxS winner uses minimal \\textbf{1.} style numbering without descriptive headers.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1-2 for minimal headers, 3-4 for some descriptive headers, 5-6 for comprehensive descriptive header structure', 'rating': 4}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response B', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 21657, 'output_tokens': 3990, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.091194}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.091194}, 'token_usage_cumulative': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input_tokens': 21657, 'output_tokens': 3990, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.091194}, 'total_cost_usd': 0.091194}}] 
