2026-03-25 10:48:33,978 494048 INFO ? odoo.addons.preference_ranking.models.models: eval_ab response: [{'task_id': 'tv_stem_13271', 'prompt': 'You are given a setup and statement to prove or derive. Your job is to provide a complete and correct proof. Think carefully, trying multiple approaches if you are stuck, and finally give a cleaned up proof under the heading ## Proof. If you are unable to complete the proof, be honest and say so.\n\n## Setup\nLet $F$ be a field. Let $T$ be a semigroup. An associative algebra $A$ is $T$-graded if $A=\\bigoplus_{t\\in T} A^{(t)}$ as a vector space, and $A^{(t_1)}A^{(t_2)} \\subseteq A^{(t_1t_2)}$ for all $t_1, t_2 \\in T$.\n\nLet $X^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}} = \\{ x_i^{(t)} \\mid i \\in \\mathbb{N}, t \\in T \\}$. Let $F\\langle X^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}} \\rangle$ be the free associative algebra on the set of free generators $X^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}$. The elements of this algebra are called graded polynomials.\nLet $f=f(x^{(t_1)}_{i_1}, \\dots, x^{(t_s)}_{i_s}) \\in F\\langle X^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}} \\rangle$. We say that $f$ is a graded polynomial identity of a $T$-graded algebra $A=\\bigoplus_{t\\in T} A^{(t)}$ and write $f\\equiv 0$ if $f(a^{(t_1)}_{i_1}, \\dots, a^{(t_s)}_{i_s})=0$ for all $a^{(t_j)}_{i_j} \\in A^{(t_j)}$, $1 \\leqslant j \\leqslant s$. The set $Id^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}(A)$ of graded polynomial identities of $A$ is a graded ideal of $F\\langle X^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}} \\rangle$.\n\nLet $P^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}_n := \\langle x^{(t_1)}_{\\sigma(1)} x^{(t_2)}_{\\sigma(2)}\\ldots x^{(t_n)}_{\\sigma(n)} \\mid t_i \\in T, \\sigma\\in S_n \\rangle_F \\subset F \\langle X^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}} \\rangle$, $n \\in \\mathbb N$. Then the number $$c^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}_n(A):=\\dim\\left(\\frac{P^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}_n}{P^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}_n \\cap Id^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}(A)}\\right)$$ is called the $n$th codimension of graded polynomial identities or the $n$th graded codimension of $A$.\n\nLet $H$ be an arbitrary associative algebra with $1$ over a field $F$. Let $F \\langle X \\rangle$ be the free associative algebra without $1$ on the set $X := \\lbrace x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots \\rbrace$. Then $F \\langle X \\rangle = \\bigoplus_{n=1}^\\infty F \\langle X \\rangle^{(n)}$ where $F \\langle X \\rangle^{(n)}$ is the linear span of all monomials of total degree $n$. Consider the algebra $$F \\langle X | H\\rangle := \\bigoplus_{n=1}^\\infty H^{\\otimes n} \\otimes F \\langle X \\rangle^{(n)}$$ with the multiplication $(u_1 \\otimes w_1)(u_2 \\otimes w_2):=(u_1 \\otimes u_2) \\otimes w_1w_2$ for all $u_1 \\in H^{\\otimes j}$, $u_2 \\in H^{\\otimes k}$, $w_1 \\in F \\langle X \\rangle^{(j)}$, $w_2 \\in F \\langle X \\rangle^{(k)}$. We use the notation $$x^{h_1}_{i_1} x^{h_2}_{i_2}\\ldots x^{h_n}_{i_n} := (h_1 \\otimes h_2 \\otimes \\ldots \\otimes h_n) \\otimes x_{i_1} x_{i_2}\\ldots x_{i_n}.$$ We refer to the elements of $F\\langle X | H \\rangle$ as associative $H$-polynomials.\n\nLet $A$ be an associative algebra. Suppose there is a homomorphism $H \\to End_F(A)$, which we denote by $h \\mapsto (a \\mapsto ha)$. Any map $\\psi \\colon X \\to A$ has the unique homomorphic extension $\\bar\\psi \\colon F \\langle X | H \\rangle \\to A$ such that $\\bar\\psi(x_i^h)=h\\psi(x_i)$ for all $i \\in \\mathbb N$ and $h \\in H$. An $H$-polynomial $f \\in F\\langle X | H \\rangle$ is an $H$-identity of $A$ if $\\bar\\psi(f)=0$ for all maps $\\psi \\colon X \\to A$. The set $Id^{H}(A)$ of all $H$-identities of $A$ is an ideal of $F\\langle X | H \\rangle$.\n\nWe denote by $P^H_n$ the space of all multilinear $H$-polynomials in $x_1, \\ldots, x_n$, $n\\in\\mathbb N$, i.e. $$P^{H}_n = \\langle x^{h_1}_{\\sigma(1)} x^{h_2}_{\\sigma(2)}\\ldots x^{h_n}_{\\sigma(n)} \\mid h_i \\in H, \\sigma\\in S_n \\rangle_F \\subset F \\langle X | H \\rangle.$$ Then the number $c^H_n(A):=\\dim\\left(\\frac{P^H_n}{P^H_n \\cap Id^H(A)}\\right)$ is called the $n$th codimension of polynomial $H$-identities or the $n$th $H$-codimension of $A$.\n\nFor an arbitrary semigroup $T$ one can consider the semigroup algebra $FT$ over a field $F$ which is the vector space with the formal basis $(t)_{t\\in T}$ and the multiplication induced by the one in $T$. Consider the vector space $(FT)^*$ dual to $FT$. Then $(FT)^*$ is an algebra with the multiplication defined by $(hw)(t)=h(t)w(t)$ for $h,w \\in (FT)^*$ and $t\\in T$.\n\nLet $A=\\bigoplus_{t\\in T} A^{(t)}$ be a $T$-graded algebra. We have the following natural $(FT)^*$-action on $A$: $h a^{(t)}:=h(t)a^{(t)}$ for all $h \\in (FT)^*$, $a^{(t)}\\in A^{(t)}$ and $t\\in T$. For any $a \\in A$, if we write $a = \\sum_{t \\in T} a^{(t)}$ where $a^{(t)} \\in A^{(t)}$, the action is extended linearly: $ha = \\sum_{t \\in T} h(a^{(t)}) = \\sum_{t \\in T} h(t)a^{(t)}$.\n\nFor every $t\\in T$ define $h_t \\in (FT)^*$ by $h_t(g)=\\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll} 1 & \\text{if } g = t, \\\\ 0 & \\text{if } g\\ne t, \\end{array}\\right.$ for $g\\in T$. If $A$ is finite dimensional, the set $supp \\Gamma := \\lbrace t\\in T \\mid A^{(t)}\\ne 0 \\rbrace$ is finite. For any $a \\in A$, we have $a = \\sum_{t \\in supp \\Gamma} a^{(t)}$, where $a^{(t)}$ is the projection of $a$ onto $A^{(t)}$. Note that $h_t a = a^{(t)}$.\nFor any $h \\in (FT)^*$ and $a \\in A$, we have $ha = \\sum_{t \\in supp \\Gamma} h(t) a^{(t)} = \\sum_{t \\in supp \\Gamma} h(t) h_t a$.\nThis implies that for any $h \\in (FT)^*$, the polynomial $x^h - \\sum_{t\\in supp \\Gamma}h(t) x^{h_t}$ is an $(FT)^*$-identity of $A$. Indeed, for any substitution $x \\to a \\in A$, its evaluation is $ha - \\sum_{t\\in supp \\Gamma}h(t) h_t a = 0$. We denote this as:\n$$\nx^h - \\sum_{t\\in supp \\Gamma}h(t) x^{h_t} \\in Id^{(FT)^*}(A) \\quad (*).\n$$\n## Theorem\nLet $A$ be a finite dimensional algebra over a field $F$ graded by a semigroup $T$. Then $c_n^{T\\text{-}\\mathrm{gr}}(A)=c_n^{(FT)^*}(A)$ for all $n\\in \\mathbb N$.', 'evaluation_result': {'response_a': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}, 'response_b': {'instruction_following': {}, 'truthfulness': {}, 'prompt_correctness': {}, 'writing_style': {}, 'verbosity': {}, 'overall_quality': {'weighted_score': 0.0, 'reason': 'Weighted: IF×0.25 + Truth×0.25 + Correctness×0.20 + Writing×0.15 + Verbosity×0.15 = 0.00'}}}, 'comparison_ab': {'comparison_score': -1, 'overall_comment': "Response A is slightly better. Both proofs are mathematically correct and complete, but Response A has cleaner structure with explicit 'Inverse isomorphisms' section proving mutual inverses directly, while Response B's Step 2 becomes convoluted when showing surjectivity (claiming 'every class...has a representative in Φ_n(P_n^{T-gr})' rather than directly proving Ψ_n∘Φ_n = id). Response A also handles the t∉Γ case more elegantly in the inverse check, whereas Response B's notation 'S:=supp Γ' is confusing (should be supp A, not supp Γ)."}, 'comparison_vs_gemini': {'comparison_score': -2, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner response provides a cleaner, more elegant proof with superior mathematical structure and efficiency, while Gemini 3 Pro's response is verbose with unnecessary complexity and contains technical errors. The SxS winner constructs explicit mutually inverse algebra homomorphisms Φ and Ψ that directly establish isomorphism between the quotient spaces, using the key identity (*) elegantly to show Φ∘Ψ and Ψ∘Φ are identity maps modulo ideals. Gemini 3 Pro's approach separates injectivity and surjectivity into lengthy steps with redundant multihomogeneous component analysis, and contains a critical error in Step 2 where it incorrectly states 'h_t(a^{(g)}) = δ_{t,g} a^{(g)}'—actually h_t acts as h_t(a^{(g)}) = δ_{t,g} a^{(g)} only when a^{(g)} is the element, but the evaluation formula conflates the action with the coefficient extraction. The SxS winner's compact notation and direct verification that Ψ(Φ(x_i^{(t)})) = x_i^{(t)} and the conjugate identity for Φ(Ψ) using (*) is mathematically sharper than Gemini 3 Pro's expansion into five steps with excessive verbal explanation."}, 'comparison_vs_gpt': {'comparison_score': -1, 'comparison_comment': "The SxS winner provides a cleaner, more elegant proof with better structural organization, while GPT 5.2's proof is correct but unnecessarily verbose and contains minor notational issues. The SxS winner uses a bidirectional approach with mutually inverse maps Φ and Ψ, cleanly establishing isomorphism in one compact section; GPT 5.2 splits this into separate injectivity and surjectivity arguments, with the surjectivity proof requiring a cumbersome 'reduction operator' ρ_n that expands what the SxS winner handles concisely via identity (*). GPT 5.2 also contains a notational error: 'Γ:=supp Γ' should be 'Γ:=supp A', and introduces redundant section 6 on singleton support that adds no mathematical value since the general proof already covers this case. Both proofs correctly establish the key isomorphism, but the SxS winner's writing style is more precise and professionally concise for expert readers."}, 'rubrics_vs_gemini': [], 'rubrics_vs_gpt': [{'name': 'Explicit strategy outline', 'description': "GPT 5.2 includes a dedicated 'Strategy (outline)' section before the formal proof that previews the proof structure and key ideas.", 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = brief mention, 6 = comprehensive roadmap with all key structural elements', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Numbered proof sections', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 organizes the proof into numbered sections (1-6) with descriptive headers for each major step.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = unnumbered paragraphs, 6 = clear numbered sections with informative headers', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Explicit injectivity proof', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 contains a dedicated subsection explicitly proving injectivity of the induced map on factor spaces.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = injectivity mentioned in passing, 6 = standalone subsection with full argument', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Explicit surjectivity proof', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 contains a dedicated subsection explicitly proving surjectivity via a reduction operator with a formal claim and proof.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = surjectivity asserted without argument, 6 = standalone subsection with constructed operator and verified claim', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Reduction operator definition', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 explicitly defines a linear reduction operator ρ_n with formula on basis monomials.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no explicit operator, 6 = formally defined operator with domain, codomain, and action on basis', 'rating': 6}, {'name': 'Bidirectional identity preservation', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 proves both directions (⇒ and ⇐) of the equivalence between graded identities and H-identities under ξ.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = one direction or assertion only, 6 = both directions with explicit constructions', 'rating': 5}, {'name': 'Singleton support remark', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 includes an explicit remark addressing the singleton support case as a verification of robustness.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = no edge case discussion, 6 = dedicated section showing case requires no separate treatment', 'rating': 4}, {'name': 'LaTeX display math for key formulas', 'description': 'GPT 5.2 uses dedicated display math environments for the main isomorphism claim, reduction operator definition, and key equations.', 'scale': '1-6 scale: 1 = inline math only, 6 = strategic use of \\[...\\] or $$...$$ for major structural formulas', 'rating': 4}], 'sxs_winner_label': 'Response A', 'token_usage': {'gemini': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'openai': {'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0, 'cached_tokens': 0, 'cost_usd': 0.0}, 'kimi': {'input